window.OCEAN_SPOT_GUIDES_EN={"countries":{"France":{"lang":"French","hello":["Bonjour","the word that opens every door, even at the bakery"],"food":["Seafood platter","Oysters, whelks and prawns, to share on a terrace facing the sea."],"respect":"Say bonjour when you walk into a shop and respect the priority rules in the water: the surfer closest to the peak goes first."},"Portugal":{"lang":"Portuguese","hello":["Olá","“hi”, rounded off with an obrigado (thank you) or obrigada depending on who is speaking"],"food":["Grilled sardines","Served on a slice of country bread, especially in summer."],"respect":"Life moves at a gentle pace: take time for a coffee at the counter and give way to locals at busy peaks."},"Espagne":{"lang":"Spanish","hello":["¡Hola!","“hi”, followed by a buenos días in the morning"],"food":["Tapas","Small plates to share at the bar, from ham to fried squid."],"respect":"People eat late, often after 9 pm. In the water, wait your turn and avoid paddling against the flow of the locals."},"Royaume-Uni":{"lang":"English","hello":["Hiya!","“hi”, very common along the coast"],"food":["Fish and chips","Fried fish and chips, eaten down by the harbour."],"respect":"Queuing is sacred, at the counter as in the line-up. Always thank the driver when you get off the bus."},"Irlande":{"lang":"English, Irish (Gaeilge)","hello":["Dia duit","“hello” in Irish, which always raises a smile"],"food":["Seafood chowder","A creamy fish and seafood soup, served with soda bread."],"respect":"At the pub, everyone buys a round in turn. On reef breaks, listen to the locals’ advice: the sea is powerful here."},"Italie":{"lang":"Italian","hello":["Ciao!","“hi” and “bye”, best kept for people you know well"],"food":["Spaghetti alle vongole","Pasta with clams, garlic and parsley, facing the sea."],"respect":"Cover your shoulders to enter a church and avoid wandering around town in your swimwear."},"Grèce":{"lang":"Greek","hello":["Yiá sou","“hello”, said with a smile to the taverna owner"],"food":["Grilled octopus","Marinated in olive oil and oregano, dried in the sun outside the tavernas."],"respect":"Cover your shoulders and knees to visit monasteries. Don’t pick up stones or ancient fragments."},"Croatie":{"lang":"Croatian","hello":["Bok!","“hi”, relaxed and widely used"],"food":["Black risotto (crni rižot)","A cuttlefish-ink risotto, a speciality of the Dalmatian coast."],"respect":"In the old towns, don’t walk around shirtless or in swimwear: some municipalities hand out fines."},"Malte":{"lang":"Maltese, English","hello":["Bonġu","“good morning” in Maltese, a legacy of Italian"],"food":["Pastizzi","Flaky pastries filled with ricotta or peas, to munch at the village café."],"respect":"Modest dress is required in the churches, which are numerous and busy. Don’t touch the Posidonia seagrass when snorkelling."},"Norvège":{"lang":"Norwegian","hello":["Hei!","“hi”, simple and universal"],"food":["Salmon and dried cod (tørrfisk)","Two treasures of the coast, from the smokehouse to the wooden drying racks."],"respect":"Allemannsretten lets you camp in nature, away from houses, as long as you leave no trace. Fires are often banned in summer."},"Islande":{"lang":"Icelandic","hello":["Góðan daginn","“hello”, literally “good day”"],"food":["Plokkfiskur","A fish and potato stew, served with rye bread."],"respect":"Stay on marked trails: moss takes decades to grow back. Check safetravel.is before every outing."},"Danemark":{"lang":"Danish","hello":["Hej!","“hi”, pronounced “high”"],"food":["Smørrebrød","Open rye-bread sandwiches topped with herring, prawns or egg."],"respect":"The dunes are protected: stay on the boardwalks and don’t camp outside designated sites."},"Allemagne":{"lang":"German","hello":["Moin!","the northern “hi”, good at any time of day"],"food":["Fischbrötchen","A bread roll filled with herring or smoked mackerel, down at the harbour."],"respect":"Recycling is taken seriously and beaches often charge a fee in summer (Kurtaxe)."},"Pays-Bas":{"lang":"Dutch","hello":["Hoi!","“hi”, short and warm"],"food":["Raw herring (haring)","Served with onions, eaten tail-first at the market stalls."],"respect":"Cycle lanes have priority: look carefully before crossing, the bikes come fast."},"Maroc":{"lang":"Moroccan Arabic (Darija), Amazigh, French","hello":["Salam","“peace”, the everyday greeting, to which you reply wa alaykoum salam"],"food":["Fish tagine","Slow-cooked with preserved lemon, olives and vegetables, often shared from one dish."],"respect":"Cover your shoulders and knees away from the beach, ask before photographing anyone and eat with your right hand."},"Tunisie":{"lang":"Tunisian Arabic, French","hello":["Aslema","“hello”, Tunisian style"],"food":["Fish couscous","A recipe from the Tunisian coast, spiced up with harissa."],"respect":"Away from the beaches, covering up is appreciated. During Ramadan, avoid eating in public during the day."},"Égypte":{"lang":"Egyptian Arabic","hello":["Salam aleykum","“peace be upon you”, the everyday greeting"],"food":["Cumin-grilled fish","Picked from the stall and grilled in front of you, with rice and salads."],"respect":"Don’t touch or stand on the coral, use mineral sunscreen and keep your distance from the dolphins."},"Sénégal":{"lang":"French, Wolof","hello":["Nanga def?","“how are you?” in Wolof, to which you reply maa ngi fi"],"food":["Thiéboudienne","Rice with fish and vegetables, the national dish, shared from a large bowl."],"respect":"Greetings are long and important: take time to ask how people are before getting down to business."},"Cap-Vert":{"lang":"Portuguese, Cape Verdean Creole","hello":["Olá","“hi”, followed by a sorriso (smile): that’s morabeza, the local hospitality"],"food":["Cachupa","A stew of corn, beans and fish or meat, an everyday dish."],"respect":"Life here is stress-free: morabeza is met with patience. Keep your distance from the turtles nesting at night."},"Ghana":{"lang":"English, Twi, Fante","hello":["Akwaaba","“welcome” in Twi, which you’ll hear often"],"food":["Fufu and fish soup","A cassava and plantain dough to dip into a spicy soup."],"respect":"Greet people before asking for anything and use your right hand to give or receive."},"Liberia":{"lang":"Liberian English","hello":["How da body?","“how are you?”, the best-known local greeting"],"food":["Palm butter soup","A palm-nut stew, served with rice."],"respect":"The Robertsport surf community is small and close-knit: introduce yourself, support the local schools and share the waves."},"Angola":{"lang":"Portuguese","hello":["Olá","“hi”, and bom dia in the morning"],"food":["Muamba de galinha","Chicken slow-cooked in palm oil with okra."],"respect":"Ask before photographing people or official buildings. Travel with a local contact on long dirt roads."},"Namibie":{"lang":"English, Afrikaans, Oshiwambo","hello":["Hallo","“hello” in Afrikaans, understood all along the coast"],"food":["Walvis Bay oysters","Farmed in the cold waters of the Benguela, among the most renowned in Africa."],"respect":"Stay on authorised tracks: 4x4 tyre marks scar the desert for decades."},"Afrique du Sud":{"lang":"English, Afrikaans, Xhosa, Zulu","hello":["Howzit!","“hi, how’s it going?”, the South African beach greeting"],"food":["Braai","The South African barbecue, with boerewors and grilled snoek."],"respect":"Find out about shark activity and follow the Shark Spotters’ flags. Leave nothing visible in your car."},"Mozambique":{"lang":"Portuguese, Bantu languages","hello":["Olá","“hi”, and bom dia in the morning"],"food":["Piri-piri grilled prawns","Marinated in chilli and garlic, grilled over a wood fire."],"respect":"Watch whale sharks and manta rays without touching them, from a distance, with a responsible operator."},"Tanzanie":{"lang":"Swahili, English","hello":["Jambo!","“hello” in Swahili, with a hakuna matata thrown in"],"food":["Zanzibar spiced pilau","Rice fragrant with cloves and cardamom."],"respect":"In Zanzibar, which is mostly Muslim, cover your shoulders and knees away from the beaches, especially in Stone Town."},"Kenya":{"lang":"Swahili, English","hello":["Jambo!","“hello”, to which you reply jambo sana"],"food":["Fish in coconut milk (samaki wa kupaka)","Grilled, then coated in a spiced coconut sauce, a Swahili speciality."],"respect":"On the Swahili coast, dress modestly in town and ask before taking photos."},"Madagascar":{"lang":"Malagasy, French","hello":["Salama","“hello” in Malagasy"],"food":["Romazava","A meat broth with brèdes, the local leafy greens."],"respect":"Respect the fady, local taboos specific to each region: ask for advice before swimming or visiting a sacred site."},"Maurice":{"lang":"Mauritian Creole, French, English","hello":["Ki manière?","“how are you?” in Creole"],"food":["Dholl puri","Split-pea flatbreads filled with curry, the island’s favourite street food."],"respect":"Hindu temples are visited barefoot and with covered shoulders and knees. Don’t walk on the coral at low tide."},"Seychelles":{"lang":"Seychellois Creole, English, French","hello":["Bonzour","“hello” in Seychellois Creole"],"food":["Coconut fish curry (cari coco)","A mild coconut-milk curry, served with rice and chatini."],"respect":"Turtles and coco de mer are strictly protected. Don’t collect any shells from the beaches."},"La Réunion":{"lang":"French, Réunion Creole","hello":["Kossa i di?","“what’s new?” in Creole"],"food":["Rougail saucisse","Sausages simmered with tomatoes, onions and chilli, served with rice and beans."],"respect":"Swimming is regulated because of the shark risk: stick to authorised zones and the rules in force."},"États-Unis":{"lang":"English, Spanish","hello":["Hey!","“hi”, often followed by a how’s it going?"],"food":["Fish tacos","Grilled or fried fish, cabbage and a creamy sauce, the emblem of surf towns."],"respect":"A tip (15 to 20%) is part of the price at restaurants. Respect the line-up pecking order: never drop in on anyone."},"Canada":{"lang":"English, French","hello":["Hi!","“hi”, or bonjour depending on the province"],"food":["Wild salmon","Grilled on a cedar plank, a Pacific coast tradition."],"respect":"Store your food out of reach of bears and learn about the First Nations territories you pass through."},"Mexique":{"lang":"Spanish","hello":["¡Hola! ¿Qué onda?","“hi, what’s up?”, very Mexican"],"food":["Tacos de pescado","Fried or grilled fish in a corn tortilla, with lime and salsa."],"respect":"In cenotes and on reefs, use only biodegradable sunscreen, or none at all as you’re often asked to."},"Belize":{"lang":"English, Kriol, Spanish","hello":["Weh di go aan?","“what’s going on?” in Belizean Kriol"],"food":["Rice and beans","Rice and red beans cooked in coconut milk, with chicken or fish."],"respect":"The barrier reef is fragile: touch nothing, don’t feed the fish and choose a licensed operator."},"Honduras":{"lang":"Spanish, Creole English on the islands","hello":["¡Hola!","“hi”"],"food":["Baleada","A flour tortilla filled with beans, cream and cheese."],"respect":"On the Bay Islands, pay the marine park fee and don’t collect coral or conch."},"Salvador":{"lang":"Spanish","hello":["¡Hola! ¿Qué ondas?","“hi, what’s up?”"],"food":["Pupusas","Corn cakes stuffed with cheese and beans, served with curtido."],"respect":"The coast’s surf communities are welcoming: buy local, share the waves and stay humble at the point breaks."},"Nicaragua":{"lang":"Spanish","hello":["¡Hola!","“hi”"],"food":["Gallo pinto","Fried rice and beans, served morning, noon and night."],"respect":"Many spots are on private land: use the authorised access points and respect the fishermen."},"Costa Rica":{"lang":"Spanish","hello":["¡Pura vida!","“pure life”, used as hello, thank you and goodbye"],"food":["Casado","A full plate: rice, beans, plantain, salad and fish or meat."],"respect":"Don’t feed the monkeys and respect the turtle nesting beaches, which are closed at night."},"Panama":{"lang":"Spanish","hello":["¡Hola!","“hi”"],"food":["Sancocho","A chicken soup with yuca and coriander, the national dish."],"respect":"In Indigenous territories such as Guna Yala, ask permission and pay the communities’ access fees."},"Porto Rico":{"lang":"Spanish, English","hello":["¡Wepa!","the joyful Puerto Rican exclamation, alongside the classic hola"],"food":["Mofongo","Mashed fried plantain with garlic, often topped with seafood."],"respect":"On shallow reefs, go in and out through the channels the locals use to spare the coral and your feet."},"République dominicaine":{"lang":"Spanish","hello":["¿Qué lo que?","“what’s up?”, Dominican style"],"food":["La bandera dominicana","Rice, red beans and stewed meat, the flag on your plate."],"respect":"Haggle with a smile and favour small local businesses."},"Bahamas":{"lang":"English","hello":["Hello!","“hello”, said with the islands’ sing-song accent"],"food":["Conch salad","A salad of raw conch marinated in lime, with peppers and chilli."],"respect":"Don’t collect live conch and respect the protected fishing seasons."},"Îles Caïmans":{"lang":"English","hello":["Hello!","“hello”"],"food":["Cayman-style fish","Snapper or wahoo stewed with peppers, onions and tomatoes."],"respect":"The marine parks are protected: no fishing and no anchoring outside designated spots. With the stingrays, follow your guide’s instructions to the letter."},"Barbade":{"lang":"English, Bajan","hello":["Wuh gine on?","“what’s going on?” in Bajan"],"food":["Flying fish and cou-cou","Spiced flying fish with okra cornmeal, the national dish."],"respect":"Wearing camouflage clothing is banned on the island. Swimwear on the beach only."},"Aruba":{"lang":"Papiamento, Dutch","hello":["Bon dia","“good morning” in Papiamento"],"food":["Keshi yena","A ball of cheese stuffed with spiced chicken, baked in the oven."],"respect":"Sunscreens containing oxybenzone are banned to protect the reefs."},"Curaçao":{"lang":"Papiamento, Dutch","hello":["Bon bini","“welcome” in Papiamento"],"food":["Stoba","A goat or beef stew slow-cooked with spices."],"respect":"Shore diving is king: enter the water at the marked access points and don’t touch the coral."},"Bonaire":{"lang":"Papiamento, Dutch","hello":["Bon dia","“good morning” in Papiamento"],"food":["Fried fish with funchi","Grilled or fried fish with a local cornmeal polenta."],"respect":"The Nature Fee is compulsory to dive or swim in the marine park. No gloves and no contact with the reef."},"Guadeloupe":{"lang":"French, Guadeloupean Creole","hello":["Sa ka fèt?","“how are you?” in Creole"],"food":["Accras de morue","Spicy salt-cod fritters, to nibble with a drink before dinner."],"respect":"Say hello to everyone, even in passing. Don’t walk on the seagrass beds and watch turtles from a distance."},"Martinique":{"lang":"French, Martinican Creole","hello":["Ka ou fè?","“how are you?” in Creole"],"food":["Colombo","A Caribbean curry of chicken or goat, flavoured with colombo spice mix."],"respect":"Greet people when you arrive and avoid beachwear in town. Turtles are for watching, not touching."},"Venezuela":{"lang":"Spanish","hello":["¡Epa! ¿Qué más?","“hi, what’s up?”"],"food":["Arepa","A corn cake with any filling you like, from fish to cheese."],"respect":"Check the local situation before you go and choose posadas run by local people."},"Colombie":{"lang":"Spanish","hello":["¡Quiubo!","“hi”, Colombian style"],"food":["Pescado frito con patacones","Fried fish, smashed plantains and coconut rice on the coast."],"respect":"Avoid joking about the country’s violent past: Colombians are proud of their country as it is today."},"Équateur":{"lang":"Spanish, Kichwa","hello":["¡Hola!","“hi”"],"food":["Ecuadorian ceviche","Prawns marinated in lime in a tomato sauce, served with toasted corn."],"respect":"In the Galápagos, stay two metres from the animals and always follow your naturalist guide."},"Pérou":{"lang":"Spanish, Quechua","hello":["¡Hola!","“hi”, followed by a habla causa among friends"],"food":["Ceviche","Raw fish marinated in lime and ají chilli, the national dish."],"respect":"The caballitos de totora are a living heritage: admire them, and ask before photographing the fishermen."},"Chili":{"lang":"Spanish","hello":["¡Hola! ¿Cachai?","“hi, get it?”, very Chilean"],"food":["Empanadas de mariscos","Pastries filled with seafood and cheese."],"respect":"The Pacific is cold and powerful: wear a thick wetsuit and respect the locals at the points."},"Brésil":{"lang":"Portuguese","hello":["Oi! Tudo bem?","“hi, how are you?”, to which you reply tudo bom"],"food":["Moqueca","A fish stew with coconut milk and palm oil."],"respect":"Don’t bring out your valuables on the beach and share the waves: surfing is a religion here."},"Uruguay":{"lang":"Spanish","hello":["¡Hola! ¿Todo bien?","“hi, how are you?”"],"food":["Chivito","The national sandwich, filled with beef, ham, egg and cheese."],"respect":"Sharing mate is a gesture of friendship: don’t stir the bombilla and hand it back to the person serving."},"Argentine":{"lang":"Spanish","hello":["¡Che! ¿Todo bien?","“hey, how’s it going?”, Argentine style"],"food":["Asado","The Argentine barbecue, a Sunday ritual."],"respect":"Mate is shared: only take it when it’s handed to you, and say gracias only when you don’t want any more."},"Australie":{"lang":"English","hello":["G’day!","the Australian “hello”, often followed by mate"],"food":["Seafood barbecue","Grilled prawns and fish, with a cold beer at sunset."],"respect":"Swim between the red and yellow flags, patrolled by the lifesavers. Never drop in on a local’s wave."},"Nouvelle-Zélande":{"lang":"English, Māori (te reo)","hello":["Kia ora","“hello” and “thank you” in Māori, used by everyone"],"food":["Hoki fish and chips","Wrapped in paper and eaten on the beach."],"respect":"Respect Māori sacred sites (wāhi tapu) and the principle of kaitiakitanga, guardianship of nature."},"Fidji":{"lang":"Fijian, English, Fiji Hindi","hello":["Bula!","“life” and “hello”, which you’ll hear everywhere"],"food":["Kokoda","Raw fish marinated in lemon and coconut milk."],"respect":"When visiting a village, cover your shoulders and knees, take off your hat and offer kava to the chief (sevusevu)."},"Polynésie française":{"lang":"French, Tahitian","hello":["Ia ora na","“hello” in Tahitian, with a big smile"],"food":["Poisson cru (raw fish in coconut milk)","Raw tuna marinated in lime, with crunchy vegetables and fresh coconut milk."],"respect":"Marae are sacred sites: don’t climb on the stones. At Teahupoo, locals always have priority."},"Nouvelle-Calédonie":{"lang":"French, Kanak languages","hello":["Bozu","“hello” in Drehu (Lifou), one of the 28 Kanak languages"],"food":["Bougna","Yam, sweet potato, banana and chicken or fish cooked in coconut milk in banana leaves."],"respect":"On customary land, perform the coutume: introduce yourself to the chief with a small offering before settling in."},"Vanuatu":{"lang":"Bislama, English, French","hello":["Halo!","“hello” in Bislama"],"food":["Lap-lap","A yam or cassava paste cooked in leaves in a stone oven."],"respect":"Many beaches are customary land: ask permission and pay the small access fee the village asks for."},"Samoa":{"lang":"Samoan, English","hello":["Talofa!","“hello” in Samoan"],"food":["Oka","Raw fish marinated in lemon and coconut cream."],"respect":"Sunday is for church and rest: avoid noisy swimming near villages. At dusk, respect sa, the prayer time."},"Tonga":{"lang":"Tongan, English","hello":["Mālō e lelei","“hello” in Tongan"],"food":["Ota ika","Raw fish marinated in lemon and coconut milk."],"respect":"Sunday is sacred by law: no swimming and no sport. Cover your shoulders in town."},"Îles Cook":{"lang":"Cook Islands Māori, English","hello":["Kia orana","“may you live long”, the local greeting"],"food":["Ika mata","Raw fish marinated in lemon and coconut milk."],"respect":"Rāui protects certain areas of the lagoon: no fishing or collecting where it applies."},"Palaos":{"lang":"Palauan, English","hello":["Alii!","“hello” in Palauan"],"food":["Grilled fish with taro","Fresh lagoon fish, served with taro."],"respect":"The Palau Pledge, stamped in your passport, commits you to protecting the island. Reef-harmful sunscreens are banned."},"Papouasie-Nouvelle-Guinée":{"lang":"Tok Pisin, English","hello":["Moning!","“good morning” in Tok Pisin"],"food":["Mumu","Meat and vegetables steamed under hot stones."],"respect":"Reefs belong to the clans: surf camps pay a fee to the villages, which limit the number of surfers."},"Indonésie":{"lang":"Indonesian, regional languages","hello":["Selamat pagi","“good morning”; terima kasih means thank you"],"food":["Nasi goreng","Spiced fried rice, often topped with an egg, for breakfast as well as dinner."],"respect":"Wear a sarong to enter a temple, give and receive with your right hand and don’t step on offerings placed on the ground."},"Philippines":{"lang":"Filipino, English, regional languages","hello":["Kumusta!","“how are you?”"],"food":["Kinilaw","Raw fish marinated in coconut vinegar, ginger and chilli."],"respect":"In marine protected areas, pay the access fees and don’t touch turtles or coral."},"Malaisie":{"lang":"Malay, English","hello":["Selamat pagi","“good morning”"],"food":["Nasi lemak","Coconut rice with sambal, fried anchovies and a hard-boiled egg."],"respect":"Away from the beaches, cover your shoulders and knees, especially in villages and near mosques."},"Thaïlande":{"lang":"Thai","hello":["Sawasdee","“hello”, with ka for a woman and khrap for a man"],"food":["Tom yam goong","A hot and sour soup with prawns, lemongrass and galangal."],"respect":"Never touch someone’s head, don’t point your feet at a Buddha and cover up in temples."},"Vietnam":{"lang":"Vietnamese","hello":["Xin chào","“hello”, pronounced “sin chow”"],"food":["Bánh xèo","A crispy pancake with prawns, to roll up in fresh herbs."],"respect":"Take off your shoes when entering someone’s home and avoid sticking your chopsticks upright in your rice."},"Sri Lanka":{"lang":"Sinhala, Tamil, English","hello":["Ayubowan","“long life”, said with palms pressed together"],"food":["Rice and curry","Rice surrounded by several vegetable and fish curries and coconut sambol."],"respect":"Never pose with your back to a Buddha statue for a photo, and cover your shoulders and knees in temples."},"Inde":{"lang":"Hindi, regional languages, English","hello":["Namaste","“I greet you”, with palms pressed together"],"food":["Fish curry","A fish curry with coconut milk, a speciality of the west coast."],"respect":"Take off your shoes before entering a temple or a home, and eat with your right hand."},"Maldives":{"lang":"Dhivehi, English","hello":["Assalaamu alaikum","the everyday greeting; kihineh? means “how are you?”"],"food":["Garudhiya and mas huni","A tuna broth and a mix of tuna, coconut and chilli, for breakfast."],"respect":"On inhabited islands, bikinis are for bikini beaches only, alcohol is banned and covering up is expected."},"Japon":{"lang":"Japanese","hello":["Konnichiwa","“hello”, with a slight bow"],"food":["Kaisendon","A bowl of rice topped with the day’s raw fish, a harbour-town speciality."],"respect":"Leave no litter behind: public bins are rare, so carry a bag. No visible tattoos in some onsen."},"Taïwan":{"lang":"Mandarin, Taiwanese","hello":["Nǐ hǎo","“hello” in Mandarin"],"food":["Night markets","Squid skewers, oyster omelettes and bubble tea."],"respect":"In temples, enter through the right-hand door and leave through the left, without stepping on the thresholds."},"Chine":{"lang":"Mandarin, regional languages","hello":["Nǐ hǎo","“hello”"],"food":["Hainan seafood","Crab, prawns and Wenchang chicken, the stars of the island."],"respect":"Carry your passport with you and set up a mobile payment method: cash is used less and less."},"Oman":{"lang":"Arabic, English","hello":["Salam aleykum","“peace be upon you”"],"food":["Shuwa","Spice-marinated meat slow-cooked underground, for special occasions."],"respect":"Cover up away from hotel beaches, avoid public displays of affection and ask before taking photos."},"Jordanie":{"lang":"Arabic","hello":["Marhaba","“welcome”, to which you reply marhabtein"],"food":["Sayadieh","Fish and rice with caramelised onions, a speciality of Aqaba."],"respect":"Accept the tea you’re offered, it’s a sign of hospitality. Cover your shoulders and knees away from the beaches."},"Suède":{"lang":"Swedish","hello":["Hej!","“hi”, pronounced “hey”, good at any time of day"],"food":["Seafood smörgåsbord","Pickled herring, gravlax salmon and prawns on crispbread."],"respect":"Allemansrätten lets you camp for a night in nature, away from houses, leaving no trace and lighting no fires in dry spells."},"Finlande":{"lang":"Finnish, Swedish","hello":["Moi!","“hi”, short and friendly"],"food":["Salmon soup (lohikeitto)","Salmon, potatoes and dill in a creamy broth."],"respect":"In the sauna, you go in naked or in a towel depending on local custom, and shower first. Quiet and personal space are highly valued."},"Pologne":{"lang":"Polish","hello":["Cześć!","“hi”, pronounced “cheshch”"],"food":["Pierogi","Dumplings filled with curd cheese, potatoes or meat."],"respect":"On the Baltic, the dunes and pine forests are protected: stay on the marked paths down to the beach."},"Suisse":{"lang":"German, French, Italian, Romansh","hello":["Grüezi","“hello” in Swiss German; bonjour or buongiorno depending on the region"],"food":["Fondue and perch fillets","Two classics of the Swiss lakeshores, for sharing."],"respect":"Punctuality matters, and so does recycling. On the lakes, respect the swimming zones and boat traffic."},"Autriche":{"lang":"German","hello":["Servus!","“hi”, Austrian style, for both hello and goodbye"],"food":["Alpine lake trout","Caught in the lakes and grilled, often served with parsley potatoes."],"respect":"Alpine lakes are often lined with private or paying beaches: look for signposted public access."},"Slovénie":{"lang":"Slovenian","hello":["Živjo!","“hi” in Slovenian"],"food":["Bled kremšnita","A vanilla cream cake, the speciality of Lake Bled."],"respect":"Nature parks such as Triglav are strictly protected: no wild camping and no drones without a permit."},"Hongrie":{"lang":"Hungarian","hello":["Szia!","“hi” in Hungarian"],"food":["Lángos","Fried flatbread topped with sour cream and cheese, the king of Balaton’s beaches."],"respect":"On Lake Balaton, respect the storm warning lights: the wind can pick up very quickly on this shallow lake."},"Macédoine du Nord":{"lang":"Macedonian, Albanian","hello":["Zdravo","“hello” in Macedonian"],"food":["Ohrid trout","Caught in Lake Ohrid, an endemic species now protected and often replaced by farmed trout."],"respect":"Lake Ohrid is a UNESCO World Heritage Site: leave no litter and cover up to visit churches and monasteries."},"Monténégro":{"lang":"Montenegrin","hello":["Zdravo","“hello”, with a smile"],"food":["Njeguši ham","A smoked, air-dried mountain ham, served with local cheese."],"respect":"In old towns such as Kotor, people don’t walk around in swimwear."},"Chypre":{"lang":"Greek, Turkish","hello":["Yiá sou","“hello” in Greek, in the south of the island"],"food":["Grilled halloumi","A sheep’s and goat’s milk cheese that grills without melting."],"respect":"Turtle nesting beaches are closed at night in summer: no torches and no parasols planted near the nests."},"Turquie":{"lang":"Turkish","hello":["Merhaba","“hello”, to which you reply merhaba"],"food":["Balık ekmek","A grilled fish sandwich, sold right on the quays."],"respect":"Cover your shoulders and knees and take off your shoes to visit a mosque; women cover their hair."},"Israël":{"lang":"Hebrew, Arabic","hello":["Shalom","“peace”, for both hello and goodbye"],"food":["Hummus and falafel","Chickpeas mashed with tahini and fried balls, served in pitta."],"respect":"During Shabbat, from Friday evening to Saturday evening, transport and shops slow right down: plan your journeys ahead."},"Émirats arabes unis":{"lang":"Arabic, English","hello":["Marhaba","“welcome”"],"food":["Machboos","Spiced rice with fish or meat, flavoured with dried lime."],"respect":"Away from the beaches, covering up is expected, and public displays of affection are frowned upon."},"Sierra Leone":{"lang":"English, Krio","hello":["Kushe!","“hello” in Krio"],"food":["Grilled fish and jollof rice","The day’s fresh fish and spicy red rice, on the peninsula’s beaches."],"respect":"Ask before photographing anyone and support the community’s small businesses and surf schools."},"Malawi":{"lang":"English, Chichewa","hello":["Moni!","“hello” in Chichewa"],"food":["Chambo","A fish from Lake Malawi, grilled or fried, served with nsima."],"respect":"The lake is a nature reserve for its cichlids: don’t take any fish and dress modestly in the villages."},"Cambodge":{"lang":"Khmer","hello":["Sua s’dei","“hello” in Khmer, with palms pressed together"],"food":["Amok","A coconut-milk fish curry cooked in a banana leaf."],"respect":"Cover your shoulders and knees in pagodas and never touch someone’s head."},"Corée du Sud":{"lang":"Korean","hello":["Annyeonghaseyo","a polite “hello”, with a slight bow"],"food":["Hoe and market seafood","Raw fish sliced to order, eaten on the spot at the harbour market."],"respect":"Don’t start eating before your elders and don’t stick your chopsticks upright in your rice."},"Hong Kong":{"lang":"Cantonese, English","hello":["Néih hóu","“hello” in Cantonese"],"food":["Dim sum","Small steamed bites, shared over tea."],"respect":"On lifeguarded beaches, swim inside the shark-net enclosure and respect the flags."},"Îles Salomon":{"lang":"English, Pijin","hello":["Halo!","“hello” in Pijin"],"food":["Fish in coconut milk","Fresh lagoon fish cooked in coconut milk, with taro."],"respect":"Almost all land and reefs are customary: ask the village for permission and pay the diving or surfing fees."},"Micronésie":{"lang":"English, local languages","hello":["Hello!","“hello” in English, understood in every state of Micronesia, where each island has its own language"],"food":["Grilled fish with breadfruit","Lagoon fish and breadfruit, the staple of island meals."],"respect":"Yap and Chuuk have strong customs: cover your thighs and ask before entering a village."},"Cuba":{"lang":"Spanish","hello":["¡Hola! ¿Qué volá?","“hi, what’s up?”, Cuban style"],"food":["Ropa vieja","Shredded beef stewed in tomato, served with rice and black beans."],"respect":"Stay in a casa particular to support families, and carry cash: internet and bank cards remain hit-and-miss."},"Jamaïque":{"lang":"English, Jamaican Patois","hello":["Wah gwaan?","“what’s going on?” in Patois"],"food":["Jerk chicken","Chicken marinated in chilli and allspice, smoked over pimento wood."],"respect":"Always ask before photographing anyone, and haggle with a smile."},"Îles Vierges britanniques":{"lang":"English","hello":["Hello!","“hello”, said with the islands’ sing-song accent"],"food":["Fungi and fish","Okra cornmeal with fish in Creole sauce."],"respect":"Use the marine park’s mooring buoys rather than your anchor and don’t touch the coral."},"Îles Vierges des États-Unis":{"lang":"English","hello":["Hello!","“hello”"],"food":["Conch pâté","A fried pastry filled with spiced conch, sold at small stands."],"respect":"Sunscreens containing oxybenzone are banned to protect the reefs."},"Saint-Vincent-et-les-Grenadines":{"lang":"English, Vincentian Creole","hello":["Wah di scene?","“what’s up?” in Creole"],"food":["Roasted breadfruit and fried jackfish","The national dish, served at small local eateries."],"respect":"In the Tobago Cays Marine Park, pay the entry fee and let the turtles swim without approaching them."},"Grenade":{"lang":"English","hello":["Hello!","“hello”"],"food":["Oil down","A stew of breadfruit, salt fish and coconut milk."],"respect":"Nutmeg is the island’s pride: buy directly from the cooperatives. Pay the underwater park fee."},"Dominique":{"lang":"English, Creole","hello":["Bonjou","“hello” in Dominican Creole"],"food":["Callaloo","A soup of dasheen leaves and coconut milk."],"respect":"Buy the nature sites pass and leave the whales and dolphins in peace, with a licensed operator."},"Sainte-Lucie":{"lang":"English, Creole","hello":["Bonjou","“hello” in Saint Lucian Creole"],"food":["Green fig and saltfish","Green bananas and salt cod, the national dish."],"respect":"The Soufrière Marine Park charges an entry fee and bans fishing and contact with the coral."},"Îles Turques-et-Caïques":{"lang":"English","hello":["Hello!","“hello”"],"food":["Conch fritters","Conch fritters, the speciality of the archipelago."],"respect":"Don’t collect conch or coral, and don’t feed the rays or fish in the marine parks."},"Guatemala":{"lang":"Spanish, Mayan languages","hello":["¡Buenas!","“hello”, very widely used"],"food":["Pepián","A spiced stew with toasted seeds, a national dish with Mayan roots."],"respect":"Ask before photographing local people, especially in Mayan villages."},"Saint-Martin":{"lang":"French, English, Creole","hello":["Bonjou","“hello” in Creole, or hello on the Dutch side"],"food":["Accras and johnny cakes","Salt-cod fritters and small fried breads, between French and Caribbean culture."],"respect":"The nature reserve covers many of the islets: no anchoring on seagrass beds and no fishing."},"Mayotte":{"lang":"French, Shimaore, Kibushi","hello":["Jeje","“hello” in Shimaore"],"food":["Mataba","Pounded cassava leaves cooked in coconut milk."],"respect":"Mayotte is mostly Muslim: cover up away from the beach. The lagoon is a marine park, so watch turtles and dolphins from a distance."}},"spots":{"hossegor":{"v":"Basque-Landes villas tucked among the pines, a saltwater lake where oysters are farmed and surf-brand shops on every street corner: Hossegor lives for the wave, and the whole town keeps a close eye on the swell.","f":["Duck magret and confit","The classics of Landes fattened duck, served with homemade chips or in a Landes salad."],"r":"At La Gravière, the line-up is packed and very local: watch for a long time from the beach, never snake anyone’s priority and stay on the sand if the barrels are beyond your level.","s":"September to November: powerful autumn swells, offshore wind in the morning and sandbanks often at their best.","a":[["Lake Hossegor","Tidal saltwater lake lined with villas and oyster beds, ideal for a stroll."],["Marais d’Orx","Protected wetland where you can watch migrating and wintering birds from purpose-built trails."],["Bayonne","Old town of colourful half-timbered houses on the banks of the Nive, ramparts and a lively covered market."],["Basque Museum of Bayonne","Traditions, pelota, fishing and rural life of the Basque Country told in a handsome riverside mansion."],["Étang Noir Nature Reserve","Boardwalk trail on stilts through a swampy forest and a freshwater pond in Seignosse."]]},"lacanau":{"v":"A seaside resort built for the people of Bordeaux, Lacanau Océan has lived to the rhythm of surfing for decades: a lively seafront, a high dune retreating before the ocean, pine forest and a big calm lake behind.","f":["Entrecôte à la bordelaise","Grilled over vine cuttings and topped with a red wine sauce, with a glass of Médoc."],"r":"Cross the dune only via the marked paths: the chestnut fencing protects a fragile ridge that erosion nibbles away every winter. Respect the swimming zones too.","s":"September to October: consistent swells, wind often calmer in the morning, water still mild and peaks freed from the summer crowds.","a":[["Lake Lacanau","Large freshwater lake fringed with pines, perfect for paddleboarding, sailing or a wave-free swim."],["Étang de Cousseau Nature Reserve","Trails and boardwalks through marshes, heathland and forest, with shy wildlife to spot."],["Lake Hourtin-Carcans","The largest natural lake in mainland France, with sandy beaches and cycle paths."],["Hourtin Dunes and Marshes","Wooded dunes, damp dune slacks and unspoilt marshland between the lake and the ocean."]]},"latorche":{"v":"At the tip of the Pays Bigouden, land of tall lace headdresses and the fishing ports of Penmarch and Le Guilvinec, La Torche faces Audierne Bay, with its dolmen and windsurfers on the horizon.","l":"French, Breton","h":["Demat","“hello” in Breton, always well received in the Pays Bigouden"],"f":["Le Guilvinec langoustine","Landed live at the Le Guilvinec fish auction and best enjoyed simply, with homemade mayonnaise."],"r":"Rip currents are treacherous near the point: stay in your zone, give the surf schools space and don’t trample the fragile vegetation around the dolmen.","s":"September to November: well-lined-up westerly swells, water still bearable and a beach deserted after summer.","a":[["Eckmühl Lighthouse","Tall granite lighthouse on the Penmarch headland: climb to the top for the view along the coast."],["Le Guilvinec","Major small-scale fishing port: watch the trawlers come in and the bustle of the fish auction."],["Pont-l’Abbé","Capital of the Pays Bigouden, with its castle housing a museum of costumes and headdresses."],["La Torche Dolmen","Prehistoric burial mound set on the point, facing the ocean and the long sweep of Audierne Bay."],["Sainte-Marine","Charming little port at the mouth of the Odet, opposite Bénodet, with its café terraces and chapels."]]},"biarritz":{"v":"A former imperial resort of Belle Époque villas, Biarritz saw the birth of European surfing on these waves below the cliffs; longboarders, schools and regulars gather on the beach as soon as the tide drops.","l":"French, Basque","h":["Kaixo","“hi” in Basque, said with a smile"],"f":["Gâteau basque","Shortcrust pastry filled with custard or black cherry jam, perfect to take to the beach."],"r":"At high tide the beach vanishes beneath the cliffs: check the tide times, and share the waves with the schools and longboarders, who are out in force here.","s":"September to October: clean autumn swells, water still mild and fewer students in the water than in high summer.","a":[["Biarritz Aquarium","Former Art Deco sea museum, with seals and Bay of Biscay wildlife, facing the Rocher de la Vierge."],["Sare","Basque village of white houses with red shutters, at the foot of La Rhune and its caves."],["Bayonne Cathedral","Gothic cathedral with soaring spires and a cloister, in the heart of Grand Bayonne."],["Guéthary","Former fishing village perched on the coast, with its little harbour and pelota court."],["Lake Mouriscot","Small protected lake between Biarritz and Bidart, circled by a shady walking path."]]},"anglet":{"v":"Between the mouth of the Adour and the Biarritz lighthouse, Anglet lines up its sandy beaches facing the Pyrenees, linked by a promenade where joggers, surfers and families cross paths around the Chambre d’Amour.","l":"French, Basque","h":["Kaixo","“hi” in Basque, said with a smile"],"f":["Bayonne ham and Espelette pepper","Cured ham and the region’s mild chilli, to nibble as tapas with a glass of Irouléguy."],"r":"At Les Cavaliers, the locals know every sandbank: wait your turn at the peak, don’t sit inside those paddling for a wave and take your rubbish with you when you leave the beach.","s":"September to November: hollow autumn swells, frequent offshore wind in the morning and water still pleasant.","a":[["Bayonne","Old town of colourful half-timbered houses on the banks of the Nive, ramparts and a lively covered market."],["Espelette","Basque village whose façades are covered in strings of chilli peppers drying in the sun."],["Basque Museum of Bayonne","Traditions, pelota, fishing and rural life of the Basque Country told in a handsome riverside mansion."],["Biarritz Aquarium","Former Art Deco sea museum, with seals and Bay of Biscay wildlife."],["Sare","Basque village of white houses with red shutters, at the foot of La Rhune and its caves."]]},"quiberon":{"v":"A granite peninsula battered by the Côte Sauvage on one side, a sheltered bay on the other: Quiberon has always lived from the sea, between sardine canneries, fishing ports, thalassotherapy and boats to Belle-Île.","l":"French, Breton","h":["Demat","“hello” in Breton, always well received on the peninsula"],"f":["Salted butter caramel","Born on the peninsula, you can try it as sweets, on a crêpe or in a jar to take home in your suitcase."],"r":"On the Côte Sauvage, swimming is dangerous because of the currents and rocks: only go in the water with an instructor, and stay on the path without trampling the heath.","s":"May to June and September: moderate swells, warming water and beaches far quieter than in August.","a":[["Quiberon Côte Sauvage","Cliffs, coves and granite arches along a spectacular coastal path facing the open sea."],["Belle-Île-en-Mer","Large rocky island reached by boat: the Port-Coton needles, colourful harbours and coastal paths."],["Belle-Île Citadel","Imposing fortress remodelled by Vauban, towering over the port of Le Palais as soon as the boat arrives."],["Kerzérho Alignments","Long rows of Neolithic standing stones lining the roadside at Erdeven."],["Port-Haliguen","Quiberon’s old fishing port turned marina, with its little lighthouse and quays."]]},"seignosse":{"v":"A resort born among the pines, Seignosse Océan hides its holiday homes behind a high dune, with freshwater lakes further inland; here, the day is set by the tide and the sandbanks.","f":["Pastis landais","Light brioche flavoured with orange blossom and rum, nothing to do with the aperitif of the same name."],"r":"Les Bourdaines draws a crowd as soon as the swell picks up: share the peaks, and park in the designated car parks rather than in the forest or at the foot of the dune.","s":"September to November: solid autumn swells, offshore wind in the morning and a calmer line-up than in summer.","a":[["Courant d’Huchet Nature Reserve","Wild river between forest and dunes, to float down in a traditional boat all the way to the ocean."],["Étang Noir Nature Reserve","Boardwalk trail on stilts through a swampy forest and a freshwater pond."],["Lake Hossegor","Tidal saltwater lake lined with villas and oyster beds, ideal for a stroll."],["Étang Blanc","Peaceful lake surrounded by pines and reeds, popular with anglers and walkers."],["Marais d’Orx","Protected wetland where you can watch migrating and wintering birds from purpose-built trails."]]},"capbreton":{"v":"Capbreton keeps the soul of a fishing port, with its wooden pier and fish stalls, while at La Piste old graffiti-covered bunkers stand guard over barrels fed by the nearby Gouf underwater canyon.","f":["Chipirons à la plancha","Baby squid seared with garlic and parsley, served all along the Basque and Landes coast."],"r":"The bunkers at La Piste are sinking and tilting in the sand: don’t climb on them. At high tide, the shorebreak hits hard, even for swimmers.","s":"September to November: powerful autumn swells, early-morning offshore wind and water still mild.","a":[["Capbreton Pier","Long wooden jetty topped by its lighthouse, for watching the surfers and the sunset."],["Lake Hossegor","Tidal saltwater lake lined with villas and oyster beds, ideal for a stroll."],["Marais d’Orx","Protected wetland where you can watch migrating and wintering birds from purpose-built trails."],["Bayonne","Old town of colourful half-timbered houses on the banks of the Nive, ramparts and a lively covered market."],["Basque Museum of Bayonne","Traditions, pelota, fishing and rural life of the Basque Country told in a handsome riverside mansion."]]},"lafitenia":{"v":"Just north of Saint-Jean-de-Luz, a town of tuna boats and shipowners where Louis XIV married the Infanta, Lafitenia Bay opens between green cliffs and Basque farms; on swell days, you watch it peel from the path.","l":"French, Basque","h":["Kaixo","“hi” in Basque, said with a smile"],"f":["Ttoro","Fish and shellfish soup of the Saint-Jean-de-Luz fishermen, spiced with Espelette pepper."],"r":"The Lafitenia right is highly sought after: one surfer per wave, priority to whoever is on the peak, and no sneaking inside. The small car park fills up fast, so come early.","s":"September to December: westerly swells strong enough to make the right peel, with offshore wind in the morning.","a":[["Saint-Jean-de-Luz","Fishing port, shipowners’ houses, the church where Louis XIV was married and a large sheltered beach."],["Château d’Abbadia","Neo-Gothic castle-observatory perched on the Hendaye clifftop road, with a lavishly decorated interior."],["Ciboure","Fishermen’s quarter facing Saint-Jean-de-Luz, birthplace of Maurice Ravel and home to Socoa Fort."],["Sare","Basque village of white houses with red shutters, at the foot of La Rhune and its caves."],["Ascain","Typical Basque village with a Roman bridge over the Nivelle, a starting point for La Rhune."]]},"guethary":{"v":"A former fishing village clinging to the cliff, Guéthary has kept its little slipway harbour and pelota court; when the big swell arrives, the whole village settles on café terraces to watch the guns tackle Parlementia out at sea.","l":"French, Basque","h":["Kaixo","“hi” in Basque, said with a smile"],"f":["Veal axoa","Minced veal simmered with onions and Espelette pepper, a festive dish of Basque villages."],"r":"Parlementia breaks far out on a reef: when it’s holding big, stay on the shore unless you have the right experience and equipment, and give priority to the peak’s regulars.","s":"October to February: big winter swells that wake up the reef, often with offshore wind in the morning.","a":[["Saint-Jean-de-Luz","Fishing port, shipowners’ houses, the church where Louis XIV was married and a large sheltered beach."],["Château d’Abbadia","Neo-Gothic castle-observatory perched on the Hendaye clifftop road, with a lavishly decorated interior."],["Sare","Basque village of white houses with red shutters, at the foot of La Rhune and its caves."],["Arcangues","Small hilltop village gathered around its church, pelota court and cemetery facing the Pyrenees."],["Biarritz Aquarium","Former Art Deco sea museum, with seals and Bay of Biscay wildlife."]]},"sauveterre":{"v":"Between the vast Olonne state forest and the salt marshes, Sauveterre remains a wild beach a stone’s throw from Les Sables-d’Olonne, a fishing and offshore-racing port from which the Vendée Globe sailors set off.","f":["Mogettes with Vendée ham","Melt-in-the-mouth white beans on toasted bread, with a slice of Vendée ham."],"r":"Reach the beach by the marked paths through the forest and dune, and never light a fire: the pine forest is highly vulnerable to wildfires in summer.","s":"September to November: consistent westerly swells, wind often calmer in the morning and quiet forest car parks.","a":[["Les Sables-d’Olonne","The seafront promenade, the big beach, the fishing port and the channel from which the Vendée Globe yachts depart."],["Olonne Marshes","Former salt marshes where you can watch waders and migrating birds on foot or by bike."],["La Chaume","Old fishermen’s quarter of narrow lanes facing the port, with its Arundel Tower."],["Talmont Castle","Ruins of a medieval fortress overlooking the town of Talmont-Saint-Hilaire."],["Abbaye Sainte-Croix Museum","Modern and contemporary art in a former abbey, with a fine collection of works by Gaston Chaissac."]]},"latranche":{"v":"A family resort in southern Vendée, La Tranche-sur-Mer stretches its sandy beaches sheltered by pine woods, facing the Pertuis Breton strait and the Île de Ré, with a long tradition of growing flower bulbs.","f":["Vendée brioche","Plaited brioche scented with orange blossom, traditionally served at weddings in the Vendée."],"r":"Near the Belle-Henriette breach, the reserve protects a fragile dune and nesting birds: keep out of fenced-off areas and keep your dog on a lead.","s":"June to September: small, easy swells, warmer water and surf schools open; aim for June or September to have some space.","a":[["Île de Ré","Flat, luminous island with whitewashed villages, salt marshes and cycle paths."],["Saint-Martin-de-Ré","Harbour town fortified by Vauban, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, with lively quays and ramparts."],["Belle-Henriette Nature Reserve","Protected coastal lagoon and dunes, rich in birdlife, between La Tranche and La Faute."],["Saint-Michel-en-l’Herm Abbey","Former Benedictine abbey in the heart of the coastal Poitevin Marsh, with its chapter house."],["Grouin du Cou Lighthouse","Lighthouse on a wooded headland with views across the Pertuis Breton to the Île de Ré."]]},"lapalue":{"v":"At the tip of the Crozon Peninsula, in the Armorique Regional Nature Park, La Palue opens between sandstone cliffs and heather moorland, on a coast of former crayfish ports such as Camaret.","l":"French, Breton","h":["Demat","“hello” in Breton, always well received on the peninsula"],"f":["Kouign-amann","Born in Douarnenez, just across the bay: flaky pastry, salted butter and caramelised sugar."],"r":"Swimming is forbidden at La Palue because of violent currents: only go in the water if you have real experience, and stay on the paths to protect the dune and the heath.","s":"September to November: powerful westerly swells, beautiful light and a nearly empty beach after summer.","a":[["Pointe de Pen-Hir","Dizzying cliffs facing the Tas de Pois rocks, one of the finest panoramas in Brittany."],["Pointe de Dinan","Wild headland extended by a rock arch nicknamed Dinan Castle."],["Cap de la Chèvre","The peninsula’s southern cape, with heathland, a coastal path and views over Douarnenez Bay."],["Camaret-sur-Mer","Harbour of colourful houses, a boat graveyard and a UNESCO-listed Vauban tower on the shingle spit."],["Ménez-Hom","Bare, heath-covered summit with a 360-degree view over the peninsula and the bays."]]},"penhors":{"v":"The little harbour of Pouldreuzic, home of the famous Breton pâté, Penhors lines the long Audierne Bay and its shingle bank; above the beach, the chapel hosts a major Breton pardon every year.","l":"French, Breton","h":["Demat","“hello” in Breton, always well received in the Pays Bigouden"],"f":["Crêpes and buckwheat galettes","A galette complète made with buckwheat, then a salted butter crêpe, with a bowl of farmhouse cider."],"r":"The shingle bank protects the marshes behind it: don’t take any pebbles, don’t walk on the vegetation and park in the designated car parks.","s":"September to November: consistent westerly swells, wind often lighter in the morning and water still reasonable.","a":[["Eckmühl Lighthouse","Tall granite lighthouse on the Penmarch headland: climb to the top for the view along the coast."],["Port-musée de Douarnenez","Traditional boats afloat and on the quay, for a dive into Breton maritime history."],["Pont-Croix","Small historic town with cobbled lanes running down to the Goyen, and its Notre-Dame church."],["Pointe de la Torche","Rocky headland and legendary board-sports spot, with a dolmen and a view over the whole of Audierne Bay."],["Pont-l’Abbé","Capital of the Pays Bigouden, with its castle housing a museum of costumes and headdresses."]]},"lapalmyre":{"v":"Razed at the end of the war and then rebuilt in a bold modern style, Royan still has its Belle Époque villas at Pontaillac, around a sheltered cove facing the estuary and the Cordouan lighthouse.","f":["Marennes-Oléron oysters","Matured in claires in the neighbouring basin, to enjoy with a glass of Pineau or white wine."],"r":"Pontaillac cove is small and packed in summer: surf outside the marked swimming zones and give the schools space on their peak.","s":"May to June and September: small, easy swells, warming water and a less crowded cove than in summer.","a":[["Cordouan Lighthouse","Monumental lighthouse standing at sea at the mouth of the estuary, UNESCO-listed and open to visitors by boat."],["Mornac-sur-Seudre","Village of flower-filled lanes, oyster farmers’ huts and craft workshops on the Seudre."],["Meschers-sur-Gironde","Cliffs riddled with troglodyte caves overlooking the Gironde estuary."],["Soulac Basilica","Romanesque church long buried in sand, a stop on the Way of St James, on the other side of the estuary."],["Saint-Palais-sur-Mer","Resort of old villas, with a coastal path, coves and the Pont du Diable."]]},"mimizan":{"v":"Set on either side of the stream linking Lake Aureilhan to the ocean, Mimizan Plage mixes old villas, pine woods and long lifeguarded beaches, with the Landes forest as its hinterland.","f":["Landes salad","Confit gizzards, dried duck breast and foie gras on green salad, the great classic of Landes tables."],"r":"Don’t go in the water near the mouth of the Mimizan stream: the outflow from the lake creates violent currents, especially on a falling tide. Surf between the marked zones.","s":"September to November: consistent autumn swells, early-morning offshore wind and water still mild.","a":[["Mimizan Bell-Tower Porch","Romanesque remains of a priory with a carved doorway, UNESCO-listed (Way of St James)."],["Lake Aureilhan","Large lake lined with pines and a promenade, ideal for paddleboarding or family cycling."],["Contis Lighthouse","Lighthouse with a black-and-white spiral in the forest, whose top offers a view of the coast."],["Maison du patrimoine","Small museum devoted to local history, from the medieval priory to life in the Landes forest."],["Seaplane Museum","Seaplanes, models and archives in Biscarrosse, a former base from the golden age of seaplanes."]]},"capferret":{"v":"At the end of a tongue of sand and pines, Cap Ferret lives between ocean and bay: oyster villages of wooden huts, pinasse boats at anchor and, opposite, the Dune du Pilat closing off the horizon.","f":["Arcachon Bay oysters","Enjoyed in an oyster farmer’s hut with crépinettes sausages and a glass of dry white wine."],"r":"At the point, the tidal currents in the channel are extremely violent: never go in the water on a falling tide without knowing the spot, and respect the no-go zones.","s":"September to November: clean autumn swells and well-formed sandbanks, always keeping an eye on the tidal coefficients.","a":[["Dune du Pilat","Europe’s highest dune, to climb for a vast view over the ocean, the forest and the Banc d’Arguin."],["Cap Ferret Lighthouse","Red and white lighthouse whose top offers a view over the bay, the point and the Dune du Pilat."],["Île aux Oiseaux","Island in the middle of the bay, famous for its cabanes tchanquées perched on stilts, best seen by boat."],["Arcachon","Winter Town of whimsical villas, piers, seafront and a lively market."],["Banc d’Arguin","Shifting sandbanks at the entrance to the bay, a seabird refuge reached by boat."]]},"bidart":{"v":"A Basque village perched on the cliff, Bidart gathers a church, a pelota court and a half-timbered town hall around its square, while down below the beaches follow one another at the foot of green cliffs.","l":"French, Basque","h":["Kaixo","“hi” in Basque, said with a smile"],"f":["Ossau-Iraty sheep’s cheese","Matured sheep’s cheese from the Pyrenees, served with Itxassou black cherry jam."],"r":"The Pavillon Royal peak is narrow and hotly contested: respect priority and don’t paddle inside the locals. The cliffs crumble, so don’t settle at their foot.","s":"September to November: consistent autumn swells, offshore wind in the morning and sandbanks often well placed.","a":[["Saint-Jean-de-Luz","Fishing port, shipowners’ houses, the church where Louis XIV was married and a large sheltered beach."],["Château d’Abbadia","Neo-Gothic castle-observatory perched on the Hendaye clifftop road, with a lavishly decorated interior."],["Guéthary","Former fishing village perched on the coast, with its little harbour and pelota court."],["Sare","Basque village of white houses with red shutters, at the foot of La Rhune and its caves."],["Lake Mouriscot","Small protected lake between Biarritz and Bidart, circled by a shady walking path."]]},"nazare":{"v":"Nazaré keeps its fishing traditions alive, between women in layered skirts and fish drying in the sun on the beach, while below the fort, Praia do Norte has become the world stage for giant waves.","f":["Nazaré dried fish","Horse mackerel split open and sun-dried on racks by the beach, sold by the fishermen’s wives."],"r":"On big swell days, stay well back from the cliff edge and the shoreline: the waves can wash over the whole beach. Let the jet-ski and rescue teams do their work.","s":"October to March: big winter swells funnel into the underwater canyon and raise the giant waves.","a":[["Fort of São Miguel Arcanjo","Fort and lighthouse above Praia do Norte, a lookout for the giant waves with a surf exhibition."],["Batalha Monastery","UNESCO-listed Gothic and Manueline masterpiece, with its Unfinished Chapels."],["Alcobaça","Town built around a vast UNESCO-listed Cistercian monastery, with the tombs of Pedro and Inês."],["Sanctuary of Nazaré","Baroque church in Sítio, the clifftop quarter, with a plunging view over the town and the beach."],["São Martinho do Porto","Almost enclosed shell-shaped bay with calm waters, ideal for swimming."]]},"supertubos":{"v":"Peniche is a major fishing port set on a clifftop peninsula, known for its bobbin lace, its fortress turned museum of resistance to the dictatorship, and its dozens of surf camps.","f":["Caldeirada","Stew of fish and potatoes simmered in layers, a speciality of ports such as Peniche."],"r":"When Supertubos is barrelling, the line-up fills with locals and pros: wait your turn, and leave nothing visible in your car in the beach car parks.","s":"September to November: consistent autumn swells and early-morning offshore wind that hollow out the barrels.","a":[["Berlengas Islands","Protected archipelago reached by boat, with a fort perched on an islet and crystal-clear waters."],["Óbidos","Walled medieval village of white, flower-filled lanes, famous for its ginjinha."],["Peniche Fortress","Former political prison of the dictatorship, now the Museum of Resistance and Freedom."],["Cabo Carvoeiro","Headland of ocean-sculpted cliffs, with its lighthouse and a view of the Berlengas."],["Óbidos Lagoon","Portugal’s largest coastal lagoon, perfect for kitesurfing, paddleboarding and birdwatching."]]},"ericeira":{"v":"A former fishing village of white houses trimmed in blue, Ericeira has become Europe’s first World Surfing Reserve; the boats are still hauled up onto the fishermen’s beach, at the foot of the cliffs.","f":["Pão de Mafra","Country bread with a floury crust and an airy crumb, from the ovens of nearby Mafra."],"r":"Coxos breaks over sharp rocks and the line-up is very local: watch first, respect the pecking order at the peak, and don’t go in at low tide without knowing the bottom.","s":"October to March: powerful west-northwest swells and offshore wind that make the rights peel along the reefs.","a":[["Pena Palace","Brightly coloured Romantic palace perched on the Sintra hills, surrounded by a wooded park."],["Mafra","Town dominated by a vast UNESCO-listed Baroque palace-convent, with its library."],["Azenhas do Mar","White village clinging to a cliff above a natural pool carved into the rock."],["Cabo da Roca","The westernmost point of mainland Europe, wind-battered cliffs and a lighthouse."],["Moorish Castle","Medieval ramparts winding along the ridges of Sintra, with views as far as the ocean."]]},"mundaka":{"v":"A small Basque port nestled at the mouth of the Urdaibai estuary, a biosphere reserve, Mundaka lives to the rhythm of the tides; from the church forecourt on the point, the whole village scans the sandbank.","l":"Spanish, Basque","h":["Kaixo","“hi” in Basque, the everyday language in Mundaka"],"f":["Marmitako","Stew of tuna, potatoes and peppers, a dish of the fishermen of Bermeo, the big neighbouring port."],"r":"The Mundaka left is a matter of metres: priority to the locals at the peak, and beware of the estuary’s powerful current on a falling tide. If you lack the level, watch from the harbour.","s":"October to February: powerful northwest swells that draw long barrels over the estuary sandbank.","a":[["Gernika","Symbolic town of the Basque Country, with its sacred oak, its Assembly House and its Peace Museum."],["Urdaibai Biosphere Reserve","Estuary of marshes, forests and beaches, to explore on foot, by bike or by kayak."],["Santimamiñe Cave","Cave decorated with prehistoric paintings, discovered through a guided visit and a reconstruction."],["Bermeo","Major Basque fishing port, with its old quarter, colourful quays and fishermen’s museum."],["Urdaibai Bird Center","Centre for watching migrating birds in the heart of the reserve’s marshes."]]},"thurso":{"v":"At the far end of mainland Scotland, in Caithness with its flagstone fences and Norse-derived place names, Thurso looks out towards Orkney; the river pours peat-brown water right next to the wave.","f":["Cullen skink","Creamy soup of smoked haddock, potatoes and onions, perfect after an icy session."],"r":"Thurso East breaks over a flat, shallow rock slab: paddle in and out via the channel, respect the small local community at the peak, and bring something to warm up with.","s":"September to November: well-lined-up North Atlantic swells and days still fairly long; bring a thick wetsuit.","a":[["Castle of Mey","16th-century castle, former residence of the Queen Mother, with walled gardens facing the sea."],["John o’ Groats","Legendary village at the northeastern tip of Scotland, departure point for boats to Orkney in summer."],["Thurso","The northernmost town on the British mainland, with its ruined old church and its beach."],["Dunnet Forest","Forest planted behind the dunes of Dunnet Bay, criss-crossed by easy trails."]]},"bundoran":{"v":"An old-fashioned seaside resort with amusement arcades and fish and chips, Bundoran has become Ireland’s surf capital, facing Donegal Bay and the flat-topped silhouette of Benbulben.","r":"The Peak breaks over a flat reef right in front of the town: give priority to the locals, avoid the spot at very low tide, and don’t walk on the slippery rocks without boots.","s":"September to November: consistent autumn swells and the warmest water of the year, before the winter storms.","a":[["Mullaghmore","Fishing village overlooked by Classiebawn Castle, famous for its giant waves."],["Glencar Lough","Lake framed by cliffs, with a waterfall that inspired the poet Yeats."],["Inishmurray","Uninhabited island holding the remains of an early Christian monastery within a stone enclosure."],["Rossnowlagh","Long beach of soft sand, perfect for walks and beginner surfing."],["Lough Melvin","Large lake straddling the border, renowned for its wild trout and peaceful shores."]]},"rodiles":{"v":"At the head of the Villaviciosa estuary, the Asturian capital of cider, the beach backs onto a pine wood and a nature reserve where birds spend the winter; after your session, head to a sidrería to watch cider poured from a height.","l":"Spanish, Asturian","f":["Fabada and Asturian cider","White beans slow-cooked with chorizo and black pudding, with cider poured from a height."],"r":"The estuary is a nature reserve: don’t trample the mudflats where birds land, park in the pine-wood car park and let the locals run the peak at the river mouth.","s":"October to March: the big northwest swells of autumn and winter finally make the river-mouth right peel.","a":[["Villaviciosa","Small town of old lanes, the heartland of Asturian apple orchards and cider houses."],["San Salvador de Priesca Church","Small pre-Romanesque Asturian church, a stone gem amid the meadows."],["Gijón","Large port city, with its old fishermen’s quarter of Cimadevilla and its town beach."],["Museum of the Asturian People","Rural houses, hórreos and everyday objects to help you understand Asturian culture."],["Nava Cider Museum","All about apples, presses and the ritual of pouring cider from a height."]]},"somo":{"v":"Opposite Santander, you reach Somo by small ferry across the bay; the village lives from its surf schools, its long spit of dunes and the Cantabrian families who spend their summers there.","f":["Rabas","Fried strips of squid, nibbled as an aperitif in the bars around Santander Bay."],"r":"The dunes of El Puntal and Loredo are fragile: cross them on the boardwalks and leave room for the surf lessons set up on the inside sandbanks.","s":"June to October: more moderate swells and milder water for learning; September avoids the August crowds.","a":[["Magdalena Palace","Royal summer palace set on a green peninsula, with a park and views over the bay."],["Arnía Beach","Cove on the Costa Quebrada bristling with blades of rock, superb at low tide."],["Santoña, Victoria and Joyel Marshes","Vast coastal marshes where many migrating birds stop over."],["Sardinero Beach","Santander’s Belle Époque beach, lined with gardens, villas and a casino."],["Santander Cathedral","Gothic cathedral built over a medieval crypt, in the heart of the city."]]},"zarautz":{"v":"A Basque seaside resort where surf schools line the promenade, between pintxos bars and txakoli vineyards clinging to the hills; right next door, Getaria keeps its fishing port and smoking grills.","l":"Spanish, Basque","h":["Kaixo","“hi” in Basque, said with a smile"],"f":["Txakoli and Getaria grilled fish","Lively white wine poured from a height, with turbot or anchovies cooked over embers."],"r":"In summer, lifeguards mark out swimming and surfing zones: stay in yours. At the peak, priority goes to the surfer closest to where the wave breaks.","s":"September to November: consistent autumn swells, water still mild and a beach freed from the summer crowds.","a":[["San Sebastián","Old town of pintxos bars, La Concha Bay and seafront promenades."],["Getaria","Fishing village at the foot of the rocky “mouse”, a lively harbour and txakoli vineyards."],["Basque Coast Geopark","Layered flysch cliffs between Zumaia and Mutriku, to follow on foot or by boat."],["San Telmo Museum","Former convent turned museum of Basque society, from yesterday to today."],["Pagoeta Natural Park","Wooded hills, trails and old ironworks above the coast."]]},"pantin":{"v":"On the Costa Ártabra, between green cliffs, hórreos and the Valdoviño lagoon, the villages of Ferrolterra have lived from the sea and from surfing ever since the Pantín contest started stopping here.","l":"Spanish, Galician","h":["Ola","“hi” in Galician, without the Spanish h"],"f":["Cedeira percebes","Goose barnacles prised from swell-battered rocks, simply boiled in salted water."],"r":"The Ferrolterra locals care about the rules of the peak: wait your turn, and park in the car park rather than on the tracks of the neighbouring farms.","s":"September to November: powerful, well-lined-up autumn swells, before the winter storms.","a":[["Fragas do Eume Natural Park","Primeval Atlantic forest at the bottom of a valley, with ferns and a wild river."],["Monastery of San Xoán de Caaveiro","Medieval monastery perched on a rocky spur, in the heart of the Fragas do Eume."],["Cabo Prior Lighthouse","Lighthouse on a windswept cape, with views over the cliffs of the coast."],["Pontedeume","Old town on the estuary, with its long stone bridge and narrow lanes."],["Andrade Castle","Medieval keep perched on a hill, with a fine view over the estuary and the valley."]]},"lasanta":{"v":"A fishing village of white houses set on a coast of black lava where the Atlantic crashes; the whole island bears the mark of César Manrique, who wedded architecture to volcanic landscapes.","f":["Papas arrugadas with mojo","Small potatoes boiled in salted water, served with a red or green mojo sauce."],"r":"The La Santa line-up is run by demanding locals: watch for a long time, leave them the sets and don’t paddle to the peak without the level, as the lava reef is very shallow.","s":"October to March: the northwest winter swells light up the reefs, with fewer trade winds than in summer.","a":[["Jameos del Agua","Lava tube landscaped by César Manrique, with its underground lake and tiny white crabs."],["Cueva de los Verdes","Visit a vast lava tunnel with astonishing colours, beneath La Corona volcano."],["Famara Cliffs","Volcanic wall towering over the long Famara beach and the island of La Graciosa."],["Teguise","The island’s former capital, white colonial houses and a big Sunday market."],["Cactus Garden","Former quarry transformed by Manrique into a garden of cacti from around the world."]]},"anchorpoint":{"v":"A former Amazigh fishing village of white and blue houses, Taghazout lives to the rhythm of its surf houses, but the boats still come back to the harbour and argan trees cover the surrounding hills.","l":"Darija, Tashelhit (Amazigh), French","h":["Azul","“hi” in Amazigh, the Berber language of the Souss"],"f":["Amlou","Spread of roasted almonds, argan oil and honey, for breakfast."],"r":"In the village, cover your swimsuit with a T-shirt or sarong. In the water, the Anchor line-up is busy: respect priority and jump off the rocks between sets.","s":"November to March: North Atlantic winter swells make the right peel, under gentle sunshine."},"pipeline":{"v":"On the North Shore, life stays rural: roadside fruit stands, wooden houses and Hawaiian surfers who are heirs to the watermen, for whom the wave is to be respected as much as it is surfed.","l":"English, Hawaiian","h":["Aloha","“hello” in Hawaiian, a word that also carries the idea of love and kindness"],"f":["Kahuku garlic shrimp","Prawns sautéed in garlic and butter, served with rice from the food trucks."],"r":"At Pipe, the pecking order is strict and the locals make the rules: watch from the beach if you don’t have the level. Keep your distance from resting turtles and monk seals.","s":"November to February: North Pacific winter swells make the wave barrel, at the height of contest season.","a":[["Waimea Valley","Sacred Hawaiian valley, botanical garden and a trail to a waterfall."],["Puʻu o Mahuka Heiau","The largest Hawaiian temple on the island, perched above Waimea Bay."],["Waimea Bay","Legendary big-wave bay in winter, a calm lagoon for swimming in summer."],["Polynesian Cultural Center","Living museum of Pacific cultures: dances, canoes and recreated villages."],["Turtle Bay","Sheltered bay where you often see green turtles, with coastal trails."]]},"mavericks":{"v":"Around Pillar Point Harbor, fishing boats sell their catch on the docks and Pacific fog wraps the cliffs; a corner of farms and pumpkin fields that comes alive in winter when the giants rise.","f":["Dungeness crab","Big North Pacific crab, sold cooked on the harbour docks in season."],"r":"Watch the sessions from Pillar Point Bluff without going near the edge of the unstable cliffs; in the Fitzgerald reserve tide pools, take nothing and leave the seals in peace.","s":"November to March: big North Pacific winter swells wake up the wave, in cold water.","a":[["Fitzgerald Marine Reserve","Rock pools to explore at low tide: anemones, starfish and seals."],["Pillar Point Bluff","Clifftop trails with a plunging view of the Mavericks wave."],["Devil’s Slide","Former coastal road turned trail above spectacular sheer drops."],["Montara Mountain","A climb through the chaparral to a summit open to the ocean and the bay."],["Half Moon Bay beaches","Long wild beaches lined with dunes and a coastal trail."]]},"trestles":{"v":"You reach the wave on foot by a trail that follows San Mateo Creek and passes under the railway bridge; surfers fought to save this little valley from a motorway, on ancestral Acjachemen land.","r":"The trail crosses the sacred Acjachemen site of Panhe: stay on the path. In the very crowded line-up, pros and locals take the sets, so don’t sit at the peak without the level.","s":"May to October: south swells and swells from Mexican Pacific hurricanes make the rights and lefts of Lowers peel.","a":[["Mission San Juan Capistrano","18th-century Spanish mission, ruins of the stone church and gardens."],["San Onofre","Legendary beach of old-school longboarders, cliffs and coastal trails."],["Los Rios Historic District","Small adobe and wooden houses among the oldest in California."],["Casa Romantica","Spanish Colonial-style villa, gardens and a view of San Clemente Pier."],["San Clemente State Beach","Beach at the foot of the cliffs, with a trail and a campsite up on the bluffs."]]},"malibu":{"v":"Once the Chumash land of Humaliwo, the point saw the birth of Californian surf culture in the Gidget years; the first World Surfing Reserve, it mixes classic longboards, a protected lagoon and villas along the Pacific Coast Highway.","r":"At First Point, the line-up is packed and the local longboarders care about priority: don’t drop in. Malibu Lagoon is protected, so stay on the trails.","s":"June to October: south swells make the First Point right peel, with warmer water.","a":[["Point Dume","Rocky headland with a trail, views over the bay and migrating grey whales."],["Malibu Creek State Park","Wild canyon, volcanic rocks and a natural pool in the heart of the Santa Monica Mountains."],["Adamson House","1920s villa decorated with the famous Malibu tiles, facing the wave."],["Solstice Canyon","Shady trail to a waterfall and the ruins of a modernist house."],["Zuma Beach","Vast beach of golden sand, one of the busiest in Los Angeles."]]},"puerto":{"v":"A fishing port on the Oaxaca coast turned hangout for surfers from all over the world; in the morning, the boats are pulled up onto the main beach and the stalls overflow with mezcal, chocolate and Oaxacan chillies.","f":["Tlayudas","Large crispy tortilla topped with beans, quesillo and grilled meat."],"r":"Zicatela is not a swimming beach: the shorebreak and currents there are violent. Leave the peak to locals and experts, and go for a swim in a sheltered cove.","s":"May to September: Pacific southern-hemisphere swells make Zicatela barrel, in the heat of the rainy season."},"santateresa":{"v":"A former fishing and cattle hamlet at the tip of the Nicoya Peninsula, Santa Teresa stretches along a dusty track lined with surf shops, yoga studios and a jungle full of howling monkeys.","r":"Drive your quad at walking pace on the main track, which is shared with pedestrians. Depending on the tide, rocks show in front of some peaks: spot them before going in.","s":"May to November: green season, consistent south swells and fewer people, with offshore winds in the morning.","a":[["Cabo Blanco Nature Reserve","The country’s first protected area: rainforest, monkeys and a trail to a wild beach."],["Montezuma","Bohemian seaside village, known for its waterfalls in the forest."],["Malpaís","Neighbouring hamlet that has stayed peaceful, with rocky coves and fishing boats."]]},"chicama":{"v":"At the end of a windswept coastal desert, Puerto Malabrigo lives from its long fishing pier and its endless left; the La Libertad region still bears traces of the Moche and Chimú cultures.","f":["Shambar","Thick soup of wheat, pulses and pork, served on Mondays in the Trujillo region."],"r":"The pier and the harbour are still workplaces: let the fishermen through. When walking back up the point, never cut across the path of whoever is riding the wave.","s":"April to October: southern-hemisphere winter swells make the left peel for hundreds of metres."},"puntadelobos":{"v":"Pichilemu, Chile’s surf capital, keeps its wooden houses, its huasos and the hand-harvested Cáhuil salt pans; at the point, surfers and residents rally to protect the cliffs from development.","r":"The cliffs at the point are protected: stay on the marked trails and park in the designated car parks. At the peak, the locals jump off the rocks and have priority.","s":"March to October: southern-hemisphere autumn and winter swells make the left peel, in cold water.","a":[["Cáhuil","Lagoon village of traditional salt pans, where you can buy salt straight from the producer."],["Pichilemu","Seaside resort with old-fashioned charm, a meeting place for young Chilean surfers."],["Agustín Ross Cultural Centre","Early 20th-century former casino and its palm park facing the sea."],["Cerro La Cruz","Hill topped by a cross, the best viewpoint over the town and the bay."],["Petrel Lagoon","Coastal lagoon where you can watch black-necked swans and waterbirds."]]},"jbay":{"v":"A small Eastern Cape town known for its shells, its surf-brand factory shops and its aloes; when the swell comes in, the whole town crowds onto the wooden boardwalks overlooking Supertubes.","f":["Port St Francis chokka","Squid caught on the line by the neighbouring fleet, served grilled or fried."],"r":"Supertubes is packed with sharp locals: take your turn, never let go of your board and enter the water through the usual gaps between the rocks.","s":"May to September: the southern-hemisphere winter sends long southwest swells and offshore winds that groom Supertubes.","a":[["St Francis Bay","Village of white houses with thatched roofs, lined with canals and beaches."],["Seal Point Lighthouse","Historic lighthouse on the tip of Cape St Francis, with views over the rocks and the coast."],["Kromme Estuary","Calm river with dark waters, to explore by kayak or paddleboard down to the sea."]]},"bells":{"v":"At the start of the Great Ocean Road, Torquay saw the birth of surf brands that went global; at Easter, the contest winner rings the bell at the foot of the red clay cliffs and coastal bush.","r":"The clay cliffs crumble: go down to the beach by the built stairways. At the Bells peak, the locals are many and experienced, so stay humble.","s":"March to October: the southern-hemisphere autumn and winter bring southwest swells in cold water.","a":[["Australian National Surfing Museum","Boards, photos and the history of Australian surfing, from Duke Kahanamoku to today’s champions."],["Point Addis Marine National Park","Wild cliffs, a coastal trail and protected reefs to explore with a mask."],["Torquay","Surf capital of the Surf Coast, shops, cafés and lifeguarded beaches."],["Anglesea","Peaceful coastal village, famous for the kangaroos grazing on its golf course."],["Jan Juc Beach","Lifeguarded beach at the foot of the cliffs, popular with local surfers."]]},"snapper":{"v":"On the border of Queensland and New South Wales, Coolangatta has raised several world champions; sand pumped from the mouth of the Tweed feeds the Superbank and the surf lifesaving clubs set the rhythm of the beach.","r":"This is one of the most crowded line-ups in the world: to jump off the Snapper rocks, wait for a lull and never cut across the line of whoever is surfing.","s":"February to May: late-summer cyclone and easterly swells make the Superbank peel for hundreds of metres.","a":[["Point Danger","Headland straddling two states, ideal for whale watching in winter."],["Cook Island Aquatic Reserve","Protected islet where you can snorkel among sea turtles and tropical fish."],["Currumbin Wildlife Sanctuary","Historic wildlife park: koalas, kangaroos and rainbow lorikeets."],["David Fleay Wildlife Park","Australian wildlife in the forest, with platypuses and nocturnal birds."],["Kingscliff","Quiet little seaside town, with a beach, an estuary and a seafront promenade."]]},"margaret":{"v":"On Wadandi land in southwest Australia, the wave breaks close to vineyards and karri forests; at Prevelly, surfers cross paths with hikers on the Cape to Cape Track and lovers of fine wine.","f":["Marron","Large freshwater crayfish from southwest Australia, often served grilled."],"r":"Main Break is run by seasoned locals: watch before you paddle out. Stay on the Surfers Point paths to protect the dune vegetation.","s":"March to May: the southern-hemisphere autumn combines big Southern Ocean swells with offshore winds in the morning.","a":[["Mammoth Cave","Large limestone cave with spectacular formations, rich in fossils."],["Wooditjup National Park","Jarrah and marri forests along the river, with walking trails."],["Margaret River","Lively little town in the heart of the wine region, with cafés and farmers’ markets."],["Ngari Capes Marine Park","Protected coastline between the two capes, reefs, dolphins and migrating whales."],["Gracetown","Tiny surfers’ village tucked into a sheltered bay, on the Cape to Cape Track."]]},"uluwatu":{"v":"At the end of the limestone Bukit Peninsula, bamboo warungs cling to the cliff above the cave that leads to the water, and every evening the kecak dance is performed at the temple facing the sunset.","l":"Indonesian, Balinese","h":["Om Swastiastu","the Balinese Hindu greeting, palms together, wishing peace and well-being"],"f":["Babi guling","Spit-roasted suckling pig stuffed with Balinese spices, served with rice and sambal."],"r":"At Uluwatu Temple, wear a sarong and keep an eye on your glasses and phone: the macaques snatch them. In the crowded line-up, give priority to Balinese surfers.","s":"April to October: dry season, southern Indian Ocean swells and southeast trade winds blowing offshore on the west coast.","a":[["Uluwatu Temple","Hindu temple perched on the cliff edge, with a kecak dance at sunset."],["Tanah Lot","Temple set on a wave-battered rock, a Balinese pilgrimage site."],["Jimbaran","Fishing village with its fish market, and grilled seafood dinners on the beach."],["Padang Padang Beach","Small cove of golden sand nestled between the rocks, reached by a staircase."],["Museum Pasifika","Art from Asia and the Pacific, with European painters inspired by Bali."]],"lbl":"Homestay"},"padang":{"v":"You reach the little cove by a staircase that squeezes through a crack in the cliff; here surfing blends into Balinese daily life, between offerings laid on the sand, family warungs and the lanes of Pecatu.","l":"Indonesian, Balinese","h":["Om Swastiastu","the Balinese Hindu greeting, palms together, wishing peace and well-being"],"f":["Sate lilit","Skewers of spiced minced fish, wrapped around lemongrass stalks."],"r":"The reef sits just below the surface: only go out if you have the level, and wear mineral sunscreen. Leave the sets to the Balinese locals, who run this short and hotly contested peak.","s":"June to September: dry season and big southern swells, the only ones able to make this left barrel.","a":[["Uluwatu Temple","Hindu temple perched on the cliff edge, with a kecak dance at sunset."],["Tanah Lot","Temple set on a wave-battered rock, a Balinese pilgrimage site."],["Jimbaran","Fishing village with its fish market, and grilled seafood dinners on the beach."],["Sakenan Temple","Ancient temple on Serangan Island, bustling during the major religious festivals."],["Garuda Wisnu Kencana Cultural Park","Giant statue of Vishnu riding Garuda, and Balinese dance performances."]],"lbl":"Homestay"},"cloudbreak":{"v":"South of the Mamanucas, boats leave from the islands and the Viti Levu coast to reach this offshore reef; long reserved for Tavarua guests, the wave has been open to all since 2010, but ashore the culture of bula and kava still reigns.","r":"Cloudbreak is surfed from a boat: go with Fijian boatmen who know the reef and respect the rotation at the peak. The coral is razor-sharp, so reef boots are recommended.","s":"May to September: the dry season brings big southern swells and trade winds that are often favourable in the morning.","a":[["Sri Siva Subramaniya Temple","Large, colourful Hindu temple in Nadi, a reflection of the Indo-Fijian community."],["Mana Island","Mamanuca island of white beaches and clear waters, with a Fijian village."],["Malolo Island","Hilly volcanic island in the Mamanucas, with villages, beaches and reefs."],["Malolo Lailai","Small island next to Malolo, with a sheltered lagoon, a marina and sandy beaches."]]},"teahupoo":{"v":"At the end of the road on Tahiti Iti, where the asphalt stops facing the mountain, the village lives from fishing, va’a and the lagoon; you reach the wave by boat, and it has hosted the Olympic surfing events.","r":"The village has only one narrow road: drive slowly and park in the designated spots. To reach the pass, go out with a local boat rather than paddling out alone.","s":"May to September: the southern-hemisphere winter sends the big southwest swells that make the wave roar.","a":[["Tahiti Iti Peninsula","Roadless wild coast of Te Pari, with cliffs, waterfalls and valleys to explore."],["Mataiea","South-coast village where Paul Gauguin lived and painted, with gardens and a lagoon."],["Taravao","The peninsula’s crossroads, from where a road climbs to the plateau and its lookouts."]],"lbl":"Family guesthouse"},"floripa":{"v":"On Santa Catarina Island, fishing villages of Azorean origin, with their lacemakers and oyster farms, sit alongside the surf shops; behind Joaquina, the big dunes welcome sandboarders.","f":["The island’s oysters","Farmed in the bays of Santa Catarina, served raw, gratinated or with garlic."],"r":"The Joaquina dunes are protected: sandboard only in the designated areas. In the water, respect the swimming zones marked out by the guarda-vidas.","s":"April to September: the southern-hemisphere autumn and winter bring consistent south swells and less crowded beaches.","a":[["Lagoa da Conceição","Large lagoon lined with dunes and hills, with restaurants, kitesurfing and nightlife."],["Campeche Island","Protected islet with turquoise waters, rock carvings and marked trails."],["Santo Antônio de Lisboa","Azorean colonial village, colourful houses, oysters and sunsets over the bay."],["Hercílio Luz Bridge","Historic suspension bridge linking the island to the mainland, the city’s emblem."],["Praia Mole","Sandy beach framed by rocks, with powerful surf and paragliding over the hills."]]},"puntaroca":{"v":"At Puerto de La Libertad, the fishermen’s boats are hoisted onto the old pier and the fish market comes alive at dawn; the country is betting on its “Surf City”, but the port keeps its working-class soul.","f":["Cóctel de conchas","Raw black clams with lime and onion, sold near the harbour pier."],"r":"Punta Roca breaks over cobbles and rocks: go in and out through the passage the locals use, and give them priority at the peak.","s":"March to October: southern Pacific swells make the right peel, with offshore winds in the morning.","a":[["El Zonte","Small surf village at the foot of the hills, with a pebble beach and a renowned right."],["Port of La Libertad","Historic pier, fishing boats and a very lively fish market."],["Santa Tecla","Cool town at the foot of the volcano, with its lively pedestrian street and El Carmen church."]]},"raglan":{"v":"A small black-sand town on Whāingaroa Harbour, Raglan lives to the rhythm of surfers, artists and farms on the volcanic hills; Māori culture and surf spirit meet in the cafés on the main street.","r":"On the Manu Bay left, give priority to the surfer closest to the peak and never paddle into the path of those riding down the wave; rocks show at low tide.","s":"March to October: southwest swells of the southern-hemisphere autumn and winter make the point peel; bring a thick wetsuit.","a":[["Raglan","Bohemian main street, art galleries, cafés and a wharf on the calm-water harbour."],["Mount Karioi","Forested former volcano overlooking the coast, with a hiking trail and views over the Tasman Sea."],["Aotea Harbour","Large wild inlet lined with hills and small communities, far from the crowds."]]},"saquarema":{"v":"Between lagoon and ocean, the old fishing town of Saquarema lives for the waves of Itaúna, watched over from their rock by the white church of Nossa Senhora de Nazaré; here, surfing is passed down through families.","r":"At Itaúna, the wave is powerful and the line-up very local: wait your turn, don’t sit at the peak as soon as you arrive and avoid contest days if you’re a beginner.","s":"May to September: south swells of the southern-hemisphere winter wake up the Itaúna beach break, often with offshore wind in the morning."},"montanita":{"v":"Bamboo huts, cocktail stalls and hammocks: Montañita is the bohemian stopover on the Santa Elena coast, where the commune’s fishermen and travellers from all over the world meet on the beach every evening.","r":"The point breaks over rocks: wear reef boots at low tide, give priority to the peak’s regulars and, at night, stay in a group in the party streets.","s":"December to April: consistent north swells, warm water and sunshine; the atmosphere peaks at carnival time.","a":[["Olón","Neighbouring village with a long, quiet beach, overlooked by a sanctuary perched on the cliff."],["Ayampe","Peaceful hamlet at a river mouth, between coastal forest, a wild beach and small waves."]]},"lobitos":{"v":"Wooden houses from the oil era, pumpjacks in the desert and pelicans on the pier: Lobitos has traded oil for surfing without losing its fishing-village soul.","r":"The lefts break over a rock bottom: let the locals go at the peak, take your rubbish with you and don’t get in the way of the fishermen working on the pier.","s":"April to October: south swells of the southern-hemisphere winter line up the lefts, with a south wind that is often offshore.","a":[["Cabo Blanco","Fishing village famous for big-game fishing and Hemingway’s stay, with a barrelling wave."],["Punta Pariñas","The westernmost point of mainland South America, with cliffs and deserted beaches."]]},"mardelplata":{"v":"Argentina’s great seaside resort, Mar del Plata mixes a monumental rambla, a casino and a fishing port of yellow boats where sea lions lounge; this is where Argentine surfing took off.","f":["Mar del Plata alfajores","Biscuits filled with dulce de leche and coated in chocolate, made famous by Havanna, founded here."],"r":"In January, the town beaches are packed: surf in the authorised zones, away from swimmers, and respect the flags and instructions of the guardavidas.","s":"March to May: more consistent south swells, water still mild and beaches freed from the summer crowds.","a":[["Juan Carlos Castagnino Art Museum","Argentine fine art in Villa Ortiz Basualdo, an elegant Anglo-Norman-style mansion."],["House over the Brook","An icon of modern Argentine architecture: a house set like a bridge over a stream."],["Torre Tanque","Water tower that looks like a medieval manor, with a lookout over the city and the ocean."],["Mar del Plata Cathedral","Neo-Gothic brick basilica facing Plaza San Martín, in the heart of downtown."],["Laguna de los Padres","Lake surrounded by a nature reserve and hills, for picnics, pedalos or birdwatching."]]},"arica":{"v":"At the foot of El Morro, Arica blends the Atacama Desert, Aymara heritage and the Chinchorro culture, whose mummies are among the oldest in the world; surfers hunt the barrels off Alacrán Island.","f":["Azapa olives","Plump and purplish, they grow in the neighbouring oasis and turn up on every table."],"r":"El Gringo breaks over a very shallow slab: only venture out with real experience, and give priority to the locals and the bodyboarders who are regulars at the peak.","s":"April to September: southwest swells of the southern-hemisphere winter hollow out the barrel, under skies that are almost always dry.","a":[["Morro de Arica","Headland overlooking the city and the port, with a history museum and a view along the whole coastline."],["San Marcos Cathedral","Prefabricated iron church attributed to the Eiffel workshops, in the heart of the old town."],["Azapa Valley","Oasis of olive groves in the middle of the desert, with an archaeological museum devoted to the Chinchorro mummies."],["Arica House of Culture","Former 19th-century customs house turned cultural centre and national monument, near the port."]]},"itacare":{"v":"A former cocoa port at the mouth of the Rio de Contas, Itacaré keeps its colourful lanes, its capoeira rodas and its coves hidden in the Atlantic Forest, reached by trails.","f":["Acarajé","Bean fritter fried in dendê oil, filled with prawns and vatapá, typical of Bahia."],"r":"The beaches are linked by forest trails: don’t walk them alone after dark, and at Tiririca, leave the waves to the regulars if you’re a beginner.","s":"April to September: more consistent southeast swells; in summer, the sea calms down and suits very first attempts."},"shonan":{"v":"The former capital of the shoguns, Kamakura sits next to Shonan surf culture: between Zen temples, the little Enoden train running along the coast and weekend surfers from Tokyo, Sagami Bay lives at two speeds.","f":["Shirasu-don","A bowl of rice topped with tiny sardine fry, caught in Sagami Bay."],"r":"Keep your swimsuit for the beach and don’t wander the streets of Kamakura in it; at the peak, one surfer per wave, as the line-ups overflow at weekends.","s":"Late August to October: typhoon swells liven up Sagami Bay, with water still warm.","a":[["Great Buddha of Kamakura (Kōtoku-in)","Huge bronze Buddha seated in the open air since the Middle Ages, a symbol of the city."],["Enoshima Island","Small island linked by a bridge: shrines, sea caves, a lighthouse lookout and views of Mount Fuji."],["Tsurugaoka Hachimangū Shrine","Kamakura’s great Shinto shrine, at the end of a long cherry-lined avenue."],["Hase-dera Temple","Hillside temple with a wooden Kannon statue, hydrangea gardens and a view over the bay."],["Engaku-ji Temple","Large Zen monastery nestled in a cedar forest, with old buildings and a listed bell."]]},"arugam":{"v":"Fishermen’s outrigger canoes pulled up on the sand, crocodile lagoons and Tamil and Muslim villages: for decades Arugam Bay has blended surfing backpackers with the coastal life of eastern Sri Lanka.","l":"Tamil, Sinhala, English","h":["Vanakkam","“hello” in Tamil, the language of the east coast, said with palms together"],"r":"The neighbouring villages are mostly Muslim and Tamil: keep your swimsuit for the beach and cover your shoulders and knees whenever you go into town.","s":"May to September: the southwest monsoon blows offshore on the east coast and the point peels consistently.","a":[["Lahugala Kitulana National Park","Small park around a reservoir where wild elephants come to graze in the late afternoon."],["Muhudu Maha Vihara","Ancient Buddhist temple set in the dunes by the ocean, in Pottuvil."],["Magul Maha Viharaya","Ruins of an ancient monastery in the jungle, with a stupa, moonstones and statues."],["Okanda Temple (Ukanthamalai Murugan Kovil)","Hindu temple at the foot of rocks, starting point of the walking pilgrimage to Kataragama."]]},"cloud9":{"v":"Coconut groves as far as the eye can see, fishermen’s bancas and habal-habal motorbike taxis: Siargao has built its surf culture around Cloud 9 and its boardwalk, while keeping the quiet life of its fishing villages.","r":"The Cloud 9 line-up is very busy and the reef is shallow: stay on the shoulder if you don’t have the level and give priority to local surfers at the peak.","s":"September to November: Pacific swells and a wind that is often offshore hollow out the barrel; it’s contest season.","a":[["Guyam Island","Teardrop-shaped islet covered in coconut palms, a classic stop on island-hopping boat trips."],["Pilar","Fishing village known for the Magpupungko natural pools, best seen at low tide."]]},"waikiki":{"v":"The beach of Hawaiian kings and then of the beach boys, Waikiki is where Duke Kahanamoku introduced surfing to the world; his statue facing the waves and the outrigger canoes gliding on the swell keep that bond alive.","l":"English, Hawaiian","h":["Aloha","“hello”, “goodbye” and “affection” in Hawaiian, far more than a greeting"],"f":["Poke","Cubes of raw fish seasoned with soy sauce, sesame oil and onion."],"r":"Hawaii bans sunscreens containing oxybenzone: bring a mineral sunscreen, and in the packed line-up, never let go of your board among the beginners and canoes.","s":"May to September: South Pacific swells make the gentle waves of Oahu’s south shore peel.","a":[["Diamond Head","Iconic volcanic crater, climbed by a trail up to a lookout over Waikiki."],["Hanauma Bay","Marine reserve in an old crater, for snorkelling among reef fish."],["Pali Lookout","Dizzying Koʻolau cliffs, a view over the windward coast and a key site in Hawaiian history."],["Byodo-In Temple","Replica of a Japanese temple at the foot of lush green cliffs, with a pond and gardens."],["Waikiki Aquarium","Century-old waterfront aquarium devoted to the marine life of Hawaii and the Pacific."]]},"unstad":{"v":"In this remote cove on Vestvågøy, between farms and peaks, Arctic surfing was born from the daring of a few local pioneers; here you surf under the midnight sun in summer and the northern lights in winter.","r":"The hamlet is tiny: park in the designated car park, don’t camp in the farm meadows and check with the local surfers before going in the water.","s":"September to March: North Atlantic storms send the swell, with the northern lights as a bonus, but the water is icy.","a":[["Nusfjord","Very well-preserved fishing village, with its red rorbuer at the end of a narrow fjord."],["Flakstad Church","18th-century red wooden church facing the sea and the mountains of Flakstadøya."],["Ballstad","Working fishing port on a small island, with cod-drying racks and houses on stilts."],["Stamsund","South-coast fishing village, a Hurtigruten port of call, surrounded by steep peaks."]]},"skeletonbay":{"v":"On the edge of the Pelican Point peninsula, the Namib dunes plunge into the icy Benguela Atlantic: fur seals, flamingos from the Walvis Bay lagoon and sand-laden wind surround this remote wave.","r":"Fur seals and jackals prowl around the spot: leave no food behind, drive on the hard-sand track and never come alone, as help is far away.","s":"May to September: the big south-west swells of the southern winter line up the barrel, often under morning fog.","a":[["Dune 7","A huge sand dune to climb on foot for the view over the desert, or to race down on a sandboard."],["Pelican Point Lighthouse","A lighthouse at the end of a long sand spit where fur seals and pelicans laze about."],["Walvis Bay","A Namibian port and its lagoon, where flamingos feed in their hundreds along the seafront."]]},"mentawai":{"v":"On Sipora, the village of Katiet lives off coconut palms and fishing in front of the reef; the archipelago is home to the Mentawai people, known for their traditional tattoos and sikerei shamans, especially on Siberut.","r":"In these remote villages, cover up out of the water and ask before taking photos; at the peak, take turns with the other boats and camps, because the very shallow reef is unforgiving.","s":"April to October: Indian Ocean swells are at their most consistent, with winds often light in the morning.","lbl":"Island surf camp"},"dungeons":{"v":"At the foot of the Sentinel, Hout Bay lives between its snoek fishing harbour, boats to Seal Island and the Imizamo Yethu township; Dungeons itself only comes alive with the big winter storms.","f":["Bobotie","Spiced curried minced meat baked under an egg custard, a Cape Malay heritage dish."],"r":"Dungeons is for big-wave specialists only: if you’re not one, watch from the boat or the harbour, and respect the rules of the marine protected area.","s":"May to August: southern winter storms send the big south-west swells that wake the wave up, in cold water.","a":[["Chapman’s Peak","A road carved into the cliff between Hout Bay and Noordhoek, with spectacular ocean views."],["Cape Peninsula","Mountains, fynbos and wild beaches all the way to the Cape of Good Hope and Cape Point."],["Bo-Kaap","A neighbourhood of colourful houses on the slopes of Signal Hill, the historic heart of Cape Malay culture."],["Hout Bay","A lively fishing harbour with a market, fish and chips and boat trips to the fur seal colony."],["Kommetjie","A surfers’ village facing the Atlantic, with a long beach and the Slangkop lighthouse."]]},"bluehole_belize":{"v":"Made famous by Cousteau’s team, the Great Blue Hole is reached at dawn from Caye Caulker or San Pedro after a long crossing, for a dive among the stalactites of ancient drowned caves.","f":["Grilled lobster","Caught around the cayes and grilled over a wood fire, only during the fishing season."],"r":"Stick close to your guide and respect the announced depth, often around 40 m: the descent is fast and the atoll is protected, so nothing may be taken.","s":"April to June: dry season, calmer seas for the long crossing and good visibility, before hurricane season.","a":[["Half Moon Caye","An islet listed as a natural monument, with a red-footed booby colony and a wall famous among divers."],["Lighthouse Reef Atoll","A remote atoll of coral walls and turquoise lagoons, the setting for Blue Hole trips."]]},"sipadan":{"v":"Off Semporna, Sipadan has to be earned: limited daily permits and a dawn departure from Mabul; all around, the Bajau Laut, sea nomads, still live on their boats and in stilt houses.","r":"Permits for Sipadan are limited each day: book early through an approved centre and, on the island, stay within the permitted area. Ask before photographing Bajau families.","s":"April to September: calm seas and often excellent visibility, ideal for the walls and barracuda tornadoes.","a":[["Mabul Island","A low-lying island home to Bajau families, a paradise for “muck” diving and small critters."],["Kapalai","A sandbank topped with stilt bungalows, with dives over sandy bottom and reef."]]},"rasmohammed":{"v":"At the southern tip of Sinai, where the Gulfs of Suez and Aqaba meet, dive boats leave Sharm el-Sheikh for Shark and Yolanda Reef, while the Bedouin keep up their tradition of tea and hospitality in the nearby desert.","r":"In the national park, pay the entrance fee, take no shells or coral, and make sure your boat moors only to the designated buoys.","s":"June to October: the water is at its warmest and big schools of jacks and barracuda gather around Shark Reef.","a":[["Dunraven wreck","A 19th-century British cargo ship lying upside down on a reef, colonised by coral and fish."],["Sharm el-Sheikh","A Sinai seaside resort with its old market, shisha cafés and dive harbour."],["Naama Bay","Sharm el-Sheikh’s lively bay, with a beach, a promenade and a reef you can snorkel."]]},"thistlegorm":{"v":"Discovered by Cousteau’s team in the 1950s, the wreck lies in the Gulf of Suez, off Sinai; you reach it at dawn by boat from Sharm el-Sheikh or Hurghada, or on a liveaboard dive cruise.","r":"This is a war grave: bring nothing up, enter the holds only with a guide and avoid grabbing the structure, which is deteriorating.","s":"April to November: warmer water and often manageable seas, but the current on the wreck can be strong all year round.","a":[["Abu Nuhas Reef","A triangular reef nicknamed the ships’ graveyard, where several wrecks lie waiting to be explored."],["Carnatic wreck","A 19th-century British steamship run aground on Abu Nuhas, its ribs carpeted in coral."]]},"tulamben":{"v":"Beneath Mount Agung volcano, Tulamben is a fishing village of black-pebble beaches, where Balinese porter women carry dive tanks on their heads down to the water’s edge.","l":"Indonesian, Balinese","h":["Om Swastiastu","a Balinese Hindu greeting, hands pressed together, wishing peace and goodwill"],"f":["Babi guling","Suckling pig stuffed with Balinese spices and spit-roasted, served with rice and sambal."],"r":"The volcanic pebbles roll underfoot: wear booties to get into the water, never hold on to the wreck and pay the tank porters fairly.","s":"April to October: dry season, calm seas and good visibility on the wreck; come early in the morning to avoid the crowds.","a":[["Lempuyang Temple","A temple whose split gates frame Mount Agung, on the slopes of Mount Lempuyang."],["Tirta Gangga","A former royal water palace: pools, fountains and stepping stones amid gardens."],["Tenganan","A Bali Aga village with preserved traditions, famous for its double-ikat geringsing weaving."],["Amed","A string of fishing villages with colourful jukungs, for snorkelling off black-sand beaches."],["Mount Batur","An active volcano climbed at night to watch the sunrise over its crater lake."]],"lbl":"Homestay"},"greatbarrier":{"v":"From Cairns and Port Douglas, boats head out every morning to the outer reef; Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, traditional custodians of this Sea Country, pass on their stories here.","f":["Barramundi","A fish from Queensland’s tropical waters, served grilled or as fish and chips."],"r":"Never touch the coral, wear a stinger suit during stinger season (November to May) and respect the marine park zoning.","s":"June to October: dry season, clear water, moderate trade winds and few jellyfish, with slightly cooler water."},"silfra":{"v":"At Þingvellir, where the Vikings held the open-air Althing from the 10th century, meltwater from the Langjökull glacier filters through the lava for decades before filling the fissure, icy and crystal clear.","f":["Hverabrauð","Sweet rye bread baked for a whole day in the geothermal ground, as in Laugarvatn."],"r":"Silfra can only be dived with an approved guide and drysuit experience; don’t touch the walls and stay within the national park boundary.","s":"September to April: fewer groups in the fissure and superb low-angled light; the water stays at 2-4 °C all year round.","a":[["Þingvellir National Park","UNESCO-listed site of the ancient Icelandic parliament, among fissures, meadows and a lake."],["Öxarárfoss waterfall","A waterfall dropping into the Almannagjá fissure, a few minutes’ walk from the site."],["Lake Þingvallavatn","Iceland’s largest natural lake, ringed by lava and mountains, for walking or fishing."],["Laugarvatn","A lake and village with hot springs, and geothermal baths by the water."],["Hengill volcanic area","A volcano with waymarked trails, fumaroles and hot rivers you can bathe in."]]},"cenote_dosojos":{"v":"Deep in the Yucatán jungle, on land managed by a Maya community, Dos Ojos opens onto a vast network of flooded caves; for the Maya, cenotes were gateways to the underworld.","f":["Cochinita pibil","Pork marinated in achiote and bitter orange, slow-cooked in a banana leaf."],"r":"Shower before entering the cenote, with no sunscreen or repellent, and never venture into the passages without a certified cave-diving guide.","s":"December to April: dry season and clear skies, when shafts of light plunge into the water around midday.","a":[["Tulum Maya site","A fortified Maya city perched on a cliff above the Caribbean Sea."],["Xel-Há Maya site","A small archaeological site in the forest, with remains of murals and a cenote."]]},"medes":{"v":"Facing L’Estartit, the Medes Islands, once a refuge for privateers, have become a marine sanctuary: the village lives off diving, while Montgrí Castle watches over the rice fields of Pals.","l":"Catalan, Spanish","h":["Bon dia","“good morning” in Catalan, the proper greeting in the morning"],"f":["L’Escala anchovies","Salted and cured following an age-old tradition in the neighbouring port of L’Escala."],"r":"In the reserve, fishing and collecting are forbidden and dives are supervised: go through an approved club and never feed the groupers.","s":"June to October: warmer water and good visibility; September still offers mild seas and fewer people.","a":[["Montgrí Castle","An unfinished fortress atop the massif, reached on foot for a view over the whole bay."],["Púbol Castle","A medieval castle given by Dalí to Gala and decorated by the painter, now a house museum."],["Aiguamolls de l’Empordà Natural Park","Coastal marshes crossed by trails and hides for spotting flamingos, storks and waders."],["Calella de Palafrugell","A fishing village of white houses and arcades, nestled around small coves."],["Museum of the Mediterranean","A museum in Torroella de Montgrí devoted to Mediterranean landscapes, sounds and crafts."]]},"portcros":{"v":"No cars here: you land at the little harbour lined with colourful houses, then head off on foot beneath pines and holm oaks. Fishermen and park rangers together watch over Posidonia seagrass meadows that have been protected for decades.","f":["Provençal aïoli","Salt cod, steamed vegetables and whelks topped with a mayonnaise of crushed garlic and olive oil."],"r":"Don’t smoke outside the village, pick nothing and, whether by kayak or boat, never drop anchor in the Posidonia meadows: they are protected.","s":"June to September: water above 20 °C and often calm seas; June and September avoid the August crowds.","a":[["Porquerolles Island","The largest of the Hyères Islands: sandy beaches, vineyards and car-free cycle tracks."],["Fort de l’Estissac","A fort overlooking Port-Cros bay that hosts exhibitions about the national park."],["Domaine du Rayol","A garden of the world’s Mediterranean regions facing the islands, with a guided underwater trail in summer."],["Bormes-les-Mimosas","A hilltop village of flower-filled lanes and cobbled steps, with a view over the roadstead."],["Giens Peninsula","A peninsula linked by two tombolos, with a coastal path and salt pans with flamingos."]]},"calanques":{"v":"Sugiton has to be earned: you walk down from the Luminy campus, cool box on your back, like the Marseillais at the weekend. Further on, Morgiou and Sormiou keep their cabanons and fishermen’s pointus moored at the foot of the cliffs.","f":["Bouillabaisse","Marseille’s rockfish soup, served with rouille, croutons and the fish on the side."],"r":"In summer, book your free access to Sugiton on the park website and check whether the massifs are open: they close on days of high fire risk.","s":"June to October: milder water and often calm seas between bouts of mistral, with far fewer people from September.","a":[["Cassis","A fishing port with pastel façades, terraced vineyards and boats leaving for the calanques."],["Cap Canaille","Dizzying ochre cliffs above the sea, to explore along the Route des Crêtes."],["Calanque de Port-Miou","A long pine-fringed harbour calanque, the gateway to the trail towards Port-Pin and En-Vau."],["Riou Island","A wild limestone island off the calanques, renowned for its dive sites and seabirds."],["La Ciotat","Town of shipyards and the Eden cinema, with its Figuerolles calanque and old harbour."]]},"richelieu":{"v":"This horseshoe-shaped rock barely breaks the surface, in the waters of the Surin Islands park, where Moken sea nomads still live. Divers set out from Khao Lak or Khuraburi, often on a liveaboard of a few days.","f":["Kaeng som","A sour and very spicy Southern Thai curry with fish, turmeric and tamarind."],"r":"Don’t hold on to the rock covered in soft corals and anemones, and keep your distance from whale sharks, never touching or chasing them.","s":"February to April: glassy seas, crystal-clear water and the best chances of whale sharks; the park closes from mid-May to mid-October."},"rajaampat":{"v":"The Papuan stilt villages live from fishing and still practise sasi, which temporarily closes certain areas of reef to let the fish come back. You stay with local families, lulled by the lapping of water beneath the floor.","f":["Papeda with yellow-broth fish","A sticky sago porridge served with fish cooked in turmeric and lime."],"r":"Cover up when walking through the villages, which are often very devout, and buy the marine park entry card, which funds patrols and local communities.","s":"October to April: generally calm seas and good visibility; from June to September, southerly winds whip up the sea.","lbl":"Homestay"},"galapagos":{"v":"At the far end of the archipelago, these two uninhabited islets can only be reached on a dive liveaboard: you never set foot on them, it all happens underwater, in currents where cold and warm waters meet.","r":"Stay grouped behind your guide in the current, never chase the sharks and touch neither the animals nor the living rock: the park rules are strict.","s":"June to November: cooler, richer water that draws big schools of hammerhead sharks and whale sharks."},"maldives":{"v":"On the local islands, life revolves around the mosque, the harbour and the dhonis, the traditional boats; in the late afternoon, people share tea and hedhikaa, little tuna and coconut snacks.","r":"At manta ray cleaning stations, settle on the bottom without moving, and give the whale sharks in the south of the atoll space, without touching them.","s":"January to April: dry north-east monsoon, calm seas and top visibility on the walls and channels of the atoll.","lbl":"Local island guesthouse"},"hanauma":{"v":"This collapsed crater open to the ocean was once a fishing ground prized by Hawaiian chiefs. Today you enter after a short lesson about the reef, and parrotfish cross paths with the humuhumunukunukuāpuaʻa.","l":"English, Hawaiian","h":["Aloha","“hello”, “goodbye” and much more in Hawaiian: the welcoming spirit of the islands"],"f":["Poke","Cubes of raw fish marinated in shoyu, sesame oil and seaweed, served on rice."],"r":"Book your entry slot online, use mineral sunscreen and never stand on the coral, even where the water is shallow.","s":"All year round, with the warmest water from April to October; arrive at opening time for clear water before the crowds.","a":[["Makapuʻu Point","An easy trail to the lighthouse, with views of the windward coast and, in winter, whales."],["Hālona Blowhole","A sea geyser that spouts between volcanic rocks, near a small cove."],["Lanikai Beach","Fine sand and turquoise water facing the Mokulua islets, ideal at sunrise."],["Koko Crater Botanical Garden","A garden of dryland plants nestled inside an old crater."],["Waikīkī","Honolulu’s beach district, cradle of modern surfing and its beach boys."]]},"bonaire":{"v":"At Sorobon, local kids learn to windsurf from childhood and several have become world freestyle stars. At the back of the bay, the mangroves and conch-shell piles of Lac Cai recall the fishing of old.","r":"Keep out of the mangroves and seagrass at the back of the bay, where young fish and conch grow, and leave room for beginners from the Sorobon schools.","s":"January to August, peaking in June and July: strong, steady trade winds over a flat, warm lagoon.","a":[["Bonaire National Marine Park","Fringing reefs all around the island, reachable by shore diving at dozens of sites."],["Hilma Hooker wreck","A cargo ship lying on the sand near Kralendijk, the island’s signature wreck dive."],["Southern Bonaire salt pans","Pink ponds, mountains of salt and flamingos, with the slave huts along the coast."],["Kralendijk","A small capital of colourful houses, with its seafront promenade and fort."],["Rincon","The island’s oldest village, tucked in a valley and proud of its traditions and harvest festival."]]},"tarifa":{"v":"Behind its ramparts, the white old town keeps its Moorish-looking lanes facing the Rif mountains. In the strait, dolphins and orcas cross paths with the bluefin tuna still caught by the almadrabas along the coast.","f":["Almadraba bluefin tuna","Caught in spring in the strait and served as tataki, a la plancha or as tartare."],"r":"In summer, launch and land your kite only in the marked zones and corridors, away from swimmers, and beware of strong Levante winds that push you out to sea.","s":"April to October: the wind blows most often, powerful Levante or gentler Poniente, in water that is warming up.","a":[["Baelo Claudia","Ruins of a Roman garum-making town by Bolonia beach and its great dune."],["Tarifa Castle","A Caliphate of Córdoba fortress overlooking the harbour, with views over the strait and Africa."],["Strait Natural Park","Cliffs, dunes and coastal trails facing Morocco, on a major bird migration route."],["Tarifa Point","The southernmost point of mainland Europe, between the Atlantic and the Mediterranean."]]},"dakhla":{"v":"Between desert and ocean, the town lives off fishing and oyster farming in the lagoon. Under the khaima, you’ll be served tea in three glasses, a Sahrawi ritual that takes all the time it needs.","l":"Hassaniya, Darija, French","f":["Dakhla oysters","Farmed in the lagoon, they are eaten freshly opened, with a squeeze of lemon."],"r":"Avoid sailing over the oyster beds and near fishing boats, and take time for tea when it is offered: it is a gesture of welcome.","s":"April to September: the thermal wind is at its strongest and blows almost daily; winter stays mild but a little less windy."},"cabarete":{"v":"A former fishing village turned Caribbean HQ for kiters, Cabarete lives with its feet in the sand: tables on the beach, merengue and bachata in the evening, motoconchos zipping along the coast road.","r":"On busy Kite Beach, always launch and land with an assistant and keep clear of swimmers in front of the bay’s restaurants.","s":"June to August, then December to March: steady thermal trade winds in the afternoon, summer being the windiest period.","a":[["Playa Encuentro","A wild palm-fringed beach, the most consistent surf spot on the north coast."],["Sosúa","A seaside town with a sheltered bay, ideal for snorkelling, and a museum on its Jewish history."]]},"lemorne":{"v":"At the foot of the mountain, once a refuge for runaway slaves and now a place of remembrance, the Creole fishing villages live to the rhythm of séga, born on these shores. On Sundays, families picnic under the casuarina trees.","r":"At One Eye, the wave belongs first to locals and regulars: watch for a long time before paddling out. In the lagoon, stay in the kite zones, away from swimmers.","s":"May to October: southern winter and strong south-east trade winds, perfect for the lagoon as well as the wave.","a":[["Le Morne Brabant","A UNESCO-listed mountain, symbol of resistance to slavery, with a view over the lagoon from the summit."],["Seven Coloured Earths","Dunes of volcanic soil in ochre, red and purple hues, in the heart of Chamarel."],["Black River Gorges National Park","Native forest, viewpoints and trails where you can spot endemic birds and monkeys."],["Grand Bassin (Ganga Talao)","A crater lake sacred to Hindus, lined with temples and monumental statues."],["Chamarel","An upland village with its waterfall, rum distillery and Creole table d’hôte restaurants."]]},"cumbuco":{"v":"This fishing village has kept its jangadas, triangular-sailed rafts that still head out to sea. After your session, you race by buggy across the dunes to the lagoons, for fried fish with your feet in the water.","f":["Caranguejada","Mangrove crabs cooked in a spicy broth, cracked open with a little mallet."],"r":"In the lagoons, leave room for swimmers and jangadas, and before a downwinder, always tell someone your route and arrival time.","s":"July to January: powerful, steady trade winds almost every day, peaking from September to November."},"essaouira":{"v":"At the harbour, blue boats land sardines and sea bream that are grilled on the spot. In the medina, thuya woodworkers and Gnawa musicians set the tempo, beneath ramparts battered by the trade wind.","f":["Grilled sardines at the harbour","Pick your fish from the harbour stalls: it’s grilled before your eyes and served with bread and salad."],"r":"At the harbour, ask before photographing the fishermen; on the beach, launch well away from swimmers and camel drivers, and cover up to go back into the medina.","s":"April to September: the trade wind blows hard almost every day; the water stays cool, so bring a good wetsuit.","a":[["Essaouira Medina","A UNESCO-listed fortified old town, with white and blue lanes, souks and workshops."],["Sqala du Port","A cannon bastion overlooking the fishing harbour, with views of the ramparts and Mogador Island."],["Bayt Dakira","A house of memory devoted to the town’s Jewish heritage, in the mellah."],["Sidi Kaouki","A small surfers’ village by a long wild beach, around its white marabout shrine."],["Mogador Island","A protected island facing the bay, a refuge for Eleonora’s falcon, to admire from the coast."]]},"lafranqui":{"v":"Between lagoon and sea, Leucate lives to the rhythm of the tramontane: oyster farmers on the lagoon, fishermen’s huts and the old villas of La Franqui at the foot of the plateau cliffs.","f":["Leucate oysters","Farmed in the Salses-Leucate lagoon, to enjoy at the producers’ with a local wine."],"r":"When the tramontane blows, the wind pushes offshore: never sail alone, stay within the authorised zones in summer and don’t trample the dunes of the sand bar.","s":"September to June: frequent, powerful tramontane, especially in spring and autumn; in summer, the sea breeze is gentler.","a":[["Gruissan","A circular village around its tower, with salt pans, lagoons and beach chalets on stilts."],["Narbo Via Museum","A large museum of Roman Narbonne, with a spectacular wall of stone inscriptions."],["Château d’Aguilar","A ruined fortress amid the vineyards of the Corbières, with views all the way to the sea."],["Leucate","An old village at the foot of the plateau, with a cliff path above the sea."],["Narbonne","A Roman and medieval town, with a cathedral, archbishops’ palace and lively covered market."]]},"jericoacoara":{"v":"Here the streets are sand and you walk barefoot. At sunset, the whole village climbs the big dune, then capoeira breaks out on the beach before nights of forró.","f":["Tapioca","A cassava-starch crêpe filled with coalho cheese, coconut or dried meat."],"r":"Pay the environmental tax on arrival, stay on the tracks in the fragile dunes and, when kiting, keep well away from swimmers at the village beach.","s":"July to January: strong trade winds almost every day, peaking from September to November, with the lagoons nice and full.","a":[["Jericoacoara National Park","Dunes, mangroves and cliffs, with the Pedra Furada rock arch to reach on foot."],["Jijoca and Paraíso Lagoon","A small town beside a turquoise freshwater lagoon, with hammocks strung above the water."]]},"hookipa":{"v":"A stone’s throw from Paia, a former sugar town turned windsurfers’ village, the beach follows an unwritten code: surfers in the morning, sails in the afternoon when the trade wind picks up, and green turtles coming to sleep on the sand.","l":"English, Hawaiian","h":["Aloha","“hello”, “goodbye” and much more in Hawaiian: the welcoming spirit of the islands"],"f":["Plate lunch","Rice, macaroni salad and teriyaki meat or kalua pig, the Hawaiian workers’ lunch."],"r":"Follow the local rule: surfing in the morning, windsurfing only from 11 am. On the beach, keep your distance from resting turtles.","s":"March to May and October to November: frequent trade winds and still-solid north swells, the best mix for the wave.","a":[["Iao Valley","A lush valley in the West Maui mountains, dominated by the spire of the Iao Needle."],["Peʻahi (Jaws)","A legendary giant wave, visible from the cliffs on days of very big winter swell."],["Keʻanae Arboretum","A tropical garden on the Road to Hana, with native plants and taro patches."],["Bailey House Museum","A former missionary house in Wailuku that tells the history and culture of Maui."],["Kanahā Pond Sanctuary","A wetland for watching Hawaiian stilts and coots, both endangered species."]]},"pozo":{"v":"A small fishing hamlet on an arid pebble coast, Pozo has seen generations of Canarian windsurfers grow up, including women world champions. Between sessions, everyone gathers facing the waves.","f":["Papas arrugadas with mojo","Small potatoes boiled in salted water, served with red and green mojo sauces."],"r":"Leave the wave zone to experts and locals, take care launching over the pebbles and never get in the way of a rider heading back out.","s":"June to August: trade winds of rare strength, often over 30 knots, and perfect waves for jumping.","a":[["Maspalomas Dunes","A nature reserve of golden dunes between palm grove, lagoon and ocean."],["Guayadeque Ravine","A green gorge with cave houses and restaurants carved into the rock."],["Agüimes","An old town of colourful lanes around its church and shady squares."],["Cuatro Puertas","A sacred cave of the ancient Canarians, pierced by four openings facing the plain."],["Bandama Caldera","A volcanic crater with a trail and viewpoint, looking over the east of the island."]],"lbl":"Apartment"},"gardalake":{"v":"A former fishing village that long remained Austrian, where Goethe stopped on his way to Italy, Torbole lives to the rhythm of sails, among olive trees, mountain forts and Arco’s climbing cliffs.","f":["Carne salada","Beef cured with salt and herbs, served in thin slices or grilled with beans."],"r":"Always give way to ferries and scheduled boats, stay out of marked swimming areas and rig your gear in the centres’ dedicated areas.","s":"May to September: the Pelèr and Ora blow almost every day, driven by the temperature difference between lake and mountains.","a":[["Monte Baldo","A range overlooking the east shore, rich in alpine flowers, reached by cable car from Malcesine."],["Malcesine","A town of cobbled lanes at the foot of its Scaliger castle, perched on a rock above the lake."],["Arco Castle","Ruins perched above the olive groves, with views over the Sarca valley and the lake."],["Varone Waterfall","A waterfall roaring at the bottom of a narrow, damp gorge above Riva."],["Lake Tenno","A small turquoise lake amid the forests, ideal for a cool swim."]]},"hoodriver":{"v":"Among pear and apple orchards and craft breweries, the small town has reinvented itself around windsurfers. On the riverbanks, Native American fishermen still catch salmon from their wooden platforms.","r":"Respect the platforms and nets of Native American fishermen, whose fishing rights are guaranteed by treaty, and stay well away from the river’s barges and tugs.","s":"June to August: the west wind funnels into the gorge almost every afternoon, drawn in by the heat of the interior.","a":[["Columbia River Gorge","A canyon of basalt cliffs and waterfalls, to explore along the historic highway and its trails."],["Dog Mountain","A steep hike to meadows full of flowers in spring, with a plunging view of the river."],["Klickitat Trail","A former railway turned trail along a wild river, on foot or by bike."],["Western Antique Aeroplane & Automobile Museum","A large collection of vintage planes and cars, many of them still in working order."]]},"annecy":{"v":"Above the lake, paragliders take off from the Col de la Forclaz. On the water, professional fishermen still haul in féra and Arctic char, in a lake that local residents cleaned up decades ago.","f":["Lake féra","A white fish from the lake, served as fillets meunière in the waterside restaurants."],"r":"Give way to the lake company’s boats, avoid the protected reed beds along the shore and don’t go near private jetties.","s":"June to September: pleasant water in high summer and a glassy lake in the morning, before the breeze picks up in the afternoon.","a":[["Palais de l’Isle","A former prison shaped like a ship’s prow in the middle of the Thiou canal, emblem of the old town."],["Annecy Castle","The castle of the Counts of Geneva overlooking the old town, with a museum on Alpine lakes."],["Le Semnoz","A wooded mountain above the lake, whose summit offers a panorama as far as Mont Blanc."],["Talloires","A village on the east shore beside a sheltered bay, around its former abbey."],["Bauges Regional Nature Park","A limestone massif of mountain pastures and forests, criss-crossed by trails and tome cheese farms."]]},"verdon":{"v":"The lake swallowed the old village of Les Salles-sur-Verdon, rebuilt higher up the shore. Around it, the lavender fields of the plateau and the faience workshops of Moustiers tell the story of Haute-Provence.","f":["Banon","A goat’s cheese matured in chestnut leaves tied with raffia."],"r":"Wild camping, bivouacking and fires are forbidden around the lake; in the gorges, don’t land on the fragile banks and let boats heading back down pass.","s":"May to June and September: the water is already mild and the gorges far less crowded than in July and August, when pedalos jam the entrance.","a":[["Moustiers-Sainte-Marie","A village clinging to the cliff, with its suspended star and faience workshops."],["Valensole Plateau","A vast plateau of lavender and wheat, in flower from late June to mid-July."],["Museum of Prehistory of the Verdon Gorges","A museum on the human presence in the gorges since prehistory, with a reconstructed village."],["Aiguines","A hilltop village above the lake, the starting point of the Corniche Sublime."],["Tourtour","A hilltop village of stone lanes, with open views over Provence."]]},"glenan":{"v":"You come here under sail or by launch from the Cornouaille coast. The Glénan sailing school has trained sailors here since the post-war years, and Saint-Nicolas protects a narcissus that grows nowhere else.","l":"French, Breton","h":["Demat","“hello” in Breton, to call out on the quay"],"f":["Buckwheat galettes and cider","A galette complète browned in butter, washed down with a farmhouse cider from the Fouesnant area."],"r":"On Saint-Nicolas, stay on the marked paths of the reserve that protects the Glénan narcissus, don’t trample the dunes and take your rubbish back to the mainland.","s":"June to September: the clearest, mildest water of the year; aim for a day without strong wind and with a small tidal coefficient.","a":[["Ville Close of Concarneau","A walled town on an islet in the heart of the harbour, with ramparts to walk and lively lanes."],["Saint-Nicolas Nature Reserve","Dune grasslands where the endemic Glénan narcissus blooms in spring."],["Fishing Museum","A museum of Breton fishing in the walled town, with a trawler to visit at the quay."],["Fort Cigogne","A former military fort covering almost all of Cigogne island, base of the sailing school."],["Mousterlin Marshes","A protected wetland behind the dune barrier, rich in birds, with a trail."]]},"capri":{"v":"Far from the chic Piazzetta, Capri keeps its mule tracks, lemon trees and the fishermen of Marina Grande. Made-to-measure sandals and limoncello are still produced here, facing the Faraglioni.","f":["Ravioli capresi","Ravioli filled with caciotta and marjoram, topped with a basil tomato sauce."],"r":"Don’t swim in the Blue Grotto, where swimming is forbidden, and in a kayak keep your distance from the boats and shuttles crowding in.","s":"May to June and September: calm, warm seas, with fewer boats around the island than in high summer.","a":[["Blue Grotto","A sea cave lit up in intense blue, entered lying down in a rowing boat."],["Villa Jovis","Ruins of Emperor Tiberius’s palace, perched atop a cliff."],["Gardens of Augustus","Terraced gardens with views of the Faraglioni and the hairpin bends of Via Krupp."],["Monte Solaro","The island’s highest point, reached by chairlift from Anacapri, with views over the whole gulf."],["Arco Naturale","A natural rock arch above the sea, at the end of a path through the pine woods."]],"lbl":"B&B"},"palombaggia":{"v":"Between twisted umbrella pines and reddish granite rocks, facing the Cerbicale islets, the beach lives to the rhythm of its beach shacks in summer, while the hinterland keeps its stone villages and Torrean sites.","l":"French, Corsican","h":["Bonghjornu","“hello” in Corsican, always appreciated at the village bar"],"f":["Corsican charcuterie and brocciu","Figatellu, lonzu and fresh ewe’s milk cheese, to taste at an inn in the hinterland."],"r":"Stay on the marked paths to spare the dune and, on a paddleboard or boat, never drop anchor in the Posidonia meadows.","s":"June and September: water already warm or still mild, often calm seas and a beach far less crowded than in August.","a":[["Lavezzi Islands","A protected granite archipelago, coves of crystal-clear water and snorkelling among groupers."],["Porto-Vecchio","A hilltop Genoese old town, ramparts and lively lanes above the former salt pans."],["Cerbicale Islands Nature Reserve","Wild islets facing the beach, a refuge for seabirds, to paddle along by kayak."],["Castellu d’Araghju","A Bronze Age fortress clinging to the mountain, with a view over the whole gulf."],["Castellu di Tappa","A prehistoric Torrean site amid the maquis, among granite boulders."]]},"praia_marinha":{"v":"The Algarve’s golden limestone has been sculpted here into arches, caves and stacks; small colourful boats run along these cliffs to the sea caves, overlooked by a dizzying coastal path.","f":["Seafood cataplana","Clams, prawns and fish simmered in a copper pot, an Algarve speciality."],"r":"Go down by the built staircase and never settle at the foot of the cliffs: the limestone crumbles. On the path, stay well back from the edges.","s":"June to September: often glassy seas in the morning and the mildest water; aim for June or September to avoid the crowds.","a":[["Silves Castle","A red sandstone Moorish fortress overlooking the former capital of the Algarve."],["Ferragudo","A fishing village of white houses, a lively harbour and grilled sardines."],["Fort of Nossa Senhora da Rocha","A white chapel perched on a headland, with a plunging view over the coves."],["Carvoeiro","A white village nestled in a cove, with a walk over the sculpted rocks of Algar Seco."],["Lagos","A fortified old town and the spectacular cliffs of Ponta da Piedade."]]},"navagio":{"v":"On Zakynthos’s west coast, limestone cliffs drop sheer into water of an almost unreal blue; in the neighbouring villages, monasteries, olive groves and honey stalls tell of an island more rural than its beaches.","r":"The limestone cliffs are becoming fragile: stay behind the viewpoint barriers and follow the boatmen’s instructions about access to the cove.","s":"May to June and September: often calm, clear seas on the west coast, with fewer boats than in high summer.","a":[["Agios Nikolaos","A small northern harbour from which boats leave for the Blue Caves of Cape Skinari."],["Anafonitria","A mountain village and its fortified monastery, surrounded by olive trees."],["Volimes","A mountain village in the north-west, with stalls of honey, olive oil and embroidery."],["Mount Vrachionas","The island’s highest point, with views over the Ionian Sea and the mountain villages."]]},"anse_source":{"v":"On La Digue, people get around mostly by bike, among Creole houses, former copra and vanilla plantations and granite boulders polished by the rain; the pace remains that of an island village.","r":"Access is through L’Union Estate, with an entry ticket. In the lagoon, use mineral sunscreen and never put your feet on the coral.","s":"April–May and October to November: between the two monsoons, light winds, calm seas and good visibility; swim preferably at high tide.","a":[["Vallée de Mai","A UNESCO-listed primeval palm forest, realm of the coco de mer and the black parrot."],["L’Union Estate","A former coconut and vanilla plantation, with a plantation house and giant tortoises."],["Coco Island","Granite islets in a marine park, among the best snorkelling in the archipelago."],["Anse Georgette","A wild white-sand cove on Praslin, fringed with tropical vegetation."],["Cousin Island","A special reserve for seabirds, where hawksbill turtles also nest."]],"lbl":"Creole guesthouse"},"whitehaven":{"v":"In the heart of the Great Barrier Reef, the Whitsundays are best experienced by boat: yachts, kayaks and catamarans link forest-covered islands, the traditional territory of the Ngaro, a seafaring people who paddled bark canoes.","f":["Grilled barramundi","Tropical Queensland’s signature fish, served grilled or as fish and chips."],"r":"From November to May, wear a stinger suit against box jellyfish. National park: no fires, and you take all your rubbish home.","s":"June to October: dry season, clear skies, moderate trade winds and a much lower jellyfish risk than in the southern summer.","a":[["Whitsunday Island","The Hill Inlet lookout over swirling sandbanks, plus forests and trails."],["Chalkie’s Beach","A white-sand beach facing Whitehaven, with snorkelling on the nearby coral."],["South Molle Island","A national park island criss-crossed by trails leading to lookouts over the archipelago."],["Lindeman Islands National Park","Wild islands of bush-covered hills and secluded bays, with waymarked walks."]]},"wissant":{"v":"Between the two capes, Wissant keeps its flobarts, fishing boats hauled up on the sand, and its Belle Époque villas; on a clear day, the English cliffs come into view opposite, and people sail here in all weathers.","f":["Boulogne herring","Smoked or marinated, served warm with potatoes in oil, king of the Opal Coast ports."],"r":"Leave space for swimmers and beached flobarts, respect the marked kite zones in summer and don’t walk on the protected dunes.","s":"September to April: low-pressure systems bring strong, steady westerly winds; in summer, the breezes often stay lighter.","a":[["Cap Blanc-Nez","Chalk cliffs overlooking the strait, with views of the English coast on a clear day."],["Cap Gris-Nez","The closest point to England, with coastal paths and migrating birds."],["Boulogne-sur-Mer","A walled old town with a belfry and France’s leading fishing port."],["Mimoyecques Fortress","A Second World War underground base turned museum, with tunnels to explore."],["Audresselles","A fishing village of low houses, flobarts and crabs from the local catch."]]},"letouquet":{"v":"A resort born in pine woods planted on the dunes, Le Touquet mixes Anglo-Norman villas, forest and a vast foreshore where sand yachts and kites glide; in winter, the Enduropale sets the beach roaring.","f":["Welsh","Cheddar melted with beer over bread and ham, an English legacy on northern French tables."],"r":"Kiting is allowed in the marked zones, away from lifeguard posts in summer; give way to sand yachts and spare the Canche bay.","s":"April to October: steady thermal breezes over a huge foreshore at low tide, perfect for progressing in shallow water.","a":[["Montreuil-sur-Mer","A hilltop walled town with a citadel and cobbled lanes dear to Victor Hugo."],["Baie de Canche Nature Reserve","A wild estuary of dunes and salt meadows, rich in birds and sometimes seals."],["Étaples","A fishing port on the Canche, with a fish auction and quays that inspired a colony of painters."],["Le Touquet Lighthouse","A brick lighthouse open to visitors, with views over the Canche bay and the forest."],["Quentovic Museum","A museum of archaeology and local history, centred on the ancient port of Quentovic."]]},"hardelot":{"v":"A resort founded in the early 20th century by an English entrepreneur near Hardelot Castle, it mixes villas among the pines, the dunes of Mont Saint-Frieux and a long beach where kites, sand yachts and shrimp fishers cross paths.","f":["Moules-frites","Moules marinières and home-made chips, the great classic of the Opal Coast beach shacks."],"r":"The dunes of Mont Saint-Frieux are protected: stay on the marked paths, and kite in the designated zones, away from swimmers.","s":"September to May: strong westerly winds carried by low-pressure systems; at low tide, the beach offers a huge stretch of water.","a":[["Nausicaá","A large aquarium devoted to the oceans, with sharks, rays and sea lions."],["Boulogne-sur-Mer","A fortified old town, belfry and quays of France’s leading fishing port."],["Hardelot Castle","A fortified castle turned Entente Cordiale cultural centre, with an Elizabethan theatre."],["Mont Saint-Frieux","A wooded dune among the highest on the coast, with trails and views over the Channel."],["Basilica of Notre-Dame de Boulogne","A dome dominating the upper town and a vast crypt with murals."]]},"wimereux":{"v":"Colourful Belle Époque villas lined up along the sea wall: it was from here that Marconi made the first radio link across the Channel in 1899, and people still gather shrimps and mussels on the rocks at low tide.","f":["Welsh","Cheddar melted with beer over bread and ham, an English legacy on Opal Coast tables."],"r":"In summer, the lifeguarded beach is reserved for swimmers: launch your kite outside it. When foraging on the shore, respect minimum sizes and put rocks back in place.","s":"Autumn to spring: low-pressure systems bring strong, steady westerly winds; in summer, sessions mostly happen in the afternoon.","a":[["Nausicaá","A large aquarium devoted to the oceans, with sharks, rays and sea lions."],["Boulogne-sur-Mer","A walled old town, belfry and quays of France’s leading fishing port."],["Cap Gris-Nez","A headland facing England, with coastal paths and migrating birds passing through."],["Boulogne Castle Museum","A medieval fortified castle housing collections of archaeology and world art."],["Ambleteuse","A coastal village with its Vauban fort set on the beach and the Slack estuary."]]},"berck":{"v":"A town of seaside hospitals since the 19th century, where children were treated in the fresh air, Berck lives facing the wind: in spring, its kite festivals fill the sky above the immense beach.","f":["Caudière berckoise","A soup of fish, mussels and potatoes, handed down from fishing families."],"r":"Watch the seals of the Authie bay from afar, never approaching the sandbanks where they rest, and sail well away from swimmers.","s":"September to April: strong westerly winds and a huge stretch of water at low tide; in spring, the wind stays reliable and milder.","a":[["Bay of the Somme","A vast bay of sand and salt meadows, with seals, birds and the harbours of Saint-Valery and Le Crotoy."],["Authie Bay","A wild estuary lined with dunes, where harbour seals rest."],["Montreuil-sur-Mer","A hilltop walled town with a citadel and cobbled lanes dear to Victor Hugo."],["Valloires Abbey","A Cistercian abbey with remarkable woodwork and wrought-iron gates."],["Valloires Gardens","A botanical garden of old roses and rare plants, at the foot of the abbey."]]},"malolesbains":{"v":"A sea wall lined with Belle Époque villas, sand from which the soldiers of 1940 embarked: Malo lives above all to the rhythm of the Dunkirk carnival, when costumed bands sing and herrings fly from the town hall balcony.","f":["Potjevleesch","A Flemish terrine of white meats in jelly, served cold with chips."],"r":"At carnival, dress up and follow the band without shoving; on the beach, launch your kite outside the lifeguarded swimming zones.","s":"September to April: frequent west to north-west gales and an immense beach at low tide; in summer, the wind is scarcer.","a":[["Bergues","A small fortified Flemish town with ramparts, canals and a belfry."],["Dunkirk Port Museum","The history of the port, with models and boats afloat along the quays."],["Duchesse Anne three-master","A large sail training ship moored in the harbour, to visit from deck to hold."],["LAAC","A contemporary art museum in a sculpture garden, facing the sea."],["Fort des Dunes","A 19th-century fort tucked in the dunes, with a trail on the 1940 battle."]]},"lehavre":{"v":"A city rebuilt in concrete by Auguste Perret and listed by UNESCO, Le Havre watches container ships go by from its pebble beach lined with white huts, where Monet painted dawn over the harbour.","f":["Moules à la normande","Cooked in cider and topped with crème fraîche, the classic of seafront bistros."],"r":"Launch your kite outside the lifeguarded swimming zones and keep well away from the breakwaters and the harbour channel, reserved for ships.","s":"September to April: strong south-west to west winds with the low-pressure systems, sometimes with a small surfable swell.","a":[["MuMa","A glass museum facing the sea, with a rich Impressionist collection around Boudin and Monet."],["Church of Saint-Joseph","Perret’s concrete lantern tower, its interior bathed in coloured stained glass."],["Hanging Gardens","A former fort turned into gardens and greenhouses, with views over the city and the estuary."],["Trouville-sur-Mer","A fishing port and seaside resort, with a fish market and grand villas."],["Seine Estuary Nature Reserve","Reed beds and mudflats of the estuary, a major stopover for migrating birds."]]},"etretat":{"v":"Courbet and Monet painted these chalk arches, Maurice Leblanc hid Arsène Lupin’s treasure here in the Hollow Needle, and on the shingle, old boats turned upside down as caloges recall the fishing past.","f":["Teurgoule","Cinnamon rice pudding baked slowly in the oven, Normandy’s signature dessert."],"r":"Don’t pick up pebbles, it’s forbidden, and check the tide times before passing the arches: it’s easy to get trapped at the foot of the cliffs.","s":"June to September: calmer seas more often, less cold water and beautiful evening light on the cliffs; head out when the wind is light.","a":[["Holy Trinity Abbey, Fécamp","A huge Gothic abbey church, a major pilgrimage site of the Precious Blood."],["Clos Arsène Lupin","Maurice Leblanc’s house, with a playful trail in the footsteps of the gentleman burglar."],["Yport","A fishing village nestled between two cliffs, with a pebble beach and villas."],["Les Pêcheries, Fécamp","A museum on cod fishing and the history of the port, with a lookout over the town."],["Étretat Gardens","A contemporary garden of topiaries and sculptures, perched on the Amont cliff."]]},"dieppe":{"v":"A pioneer of sea bathing in France, Dieppe is still a real fishing port, a major name in scallops; its pebble beach also holds the memory of the 1942 Canadian raid.","f":["Marmite dieppoise","Fish, mussels and scallops simmered in a cream sauce, the pride of the harbour’s tables."],"r":"Give way to swimmers in the lifeguarded zone and, in a kayak, keep away from the harbour entrance and the foot of the crumbling cliffs.","s":"July and August: the least cold water of the year and lifeguarded swimming; choose a calm-sea day with no offshore wind.","a":[["Dieppe Castle Museum","A fortified castle overlooking the beach, famous for its collection of carved ivories."],["Varengeville-sur-Mer","A clifftop village with a sailors’ church and the cemetery where Georges Braque lies."],["Bois des Moutiers","An English-style park and gardens, with rhododendrons and sea views."],["Château d’Arques-la-Bataille","The imposing ruins of a medieval fortified castle on a spur above the valley."],["Ailly Lighthouse","A lighthouse on the Pointe d’Ailly, with a panorama of the Alabaster Coast cliffs."]]},"siouville":{"v":"At the tip of the Cotentin, La Hague lines up moorland, dry-stone walls and granite hamlets; at Siouville, Norman surfers take on cool water and a swell rolling straight in from the Atlantic.","f":["Cotentin spider crab","Caught in pots along the Channel coast, served simply with mayonnaise."],"r":"The line-up is small and loyal: wait your turn at the peak and say hello to the regulars. Park in the designated car parks, never on the dunes.","s":"September to November: consistent Atlantic swells, water that still holds some of the summer warmth and deserted beaches.","a":[["Mare de Vauville Nature Reserve","A freshwater pond behind the dunes, a refuge for waterbirds with observation trails."],["Cap de Carteret","A rocky headland and customs officers’ path, with views of the Channel Islands."],["Barneville-Carteret","A family resort with its harbour, its havre and its big dunes."],["Château des Ravalet","A Renaissance castle in the heart of a landscaped park with greenhouses and a pond."],["Thomas-Henry Museum","Cherbourg’s fine arts museum, rich in works by Jean-François Millet."]]},"vauville":{"v":"A great wild bay lined with dunes and a bird pond, beneath the moors of La Hague: you mostly meet walkers, shore foragers and board riders who come for wind and swell facing the Nez de Jobourg.","r":"The bay’s dunes and the Mare de Vauville are protected: stay on the marked paths and don’t park your van on the dune verges.","s":"October to March: big westerly swells and strong winds, for experienced surfers and kiters only; autumn often offers the best compromise.","a":[["Mare de Vauville Nature Reserve","A freshwater pond behind the dunes, a refuge for waterbirds with observation trails."],["Cité de la Mer","An ocean museum in the former transatlantic terminal, with a nuclear submarine to visit."],["Raz Blanchard","One of the most powerful tidal currents in Europe, to watch from Cap de la Hague."],["Château des Ravalet","A Renaissance castle in the heart of a landscaped park with greenhouses and a pond."]]},"hauteville":{"v":"On the Côte des Havres, the sea goes out a very long way, revealing oyster beds and mussel posts; the wind sends sand yachts and kites gliding between dunes and small havres where the rivers flow out.","f":["Oysters and bouchot mussels","Farmed on the foreshore of the Cotentin’s west coast, to enjoy facing the sea."],"r":"At low tide, never cross the oyster beds and mussel posts on a sand yacht or kite, and watch out for the incoming tide, which is very fast here.","s":"March to June and September to November: steady westerly winds and spring tides that uncover a huge foreshore.","a":[["Chausey Islands","A granite archipelago whose landscapes change with the tide, with seals and seabirds."],["Granville","A privateers’ town perched on its rock, with a fortified upper town and fishing port."],["Hambye Abbey","The romantic ruins of a Benedictine abbey in a wooded valley."],["Regnéville Castle","The ruins of a fortified castle on the edge of the Sienne havre."],["Fours à chaux du Rey","Former lime kilns and a maritime museum on the history of the havre."]]},"granville":{"v":"A privateers’ town perched on its rock, Granville fitted out Newfoundland cod boats, saw Christian Dior grow up and holds its carnival every winter; at the foot of the upper town, the sea goes out as far as the eye can see.","f":["Granville Bay whelks","Served warm with mayonnaise, the pride of Normandy’s great shellfish port."],"r":"The tide comes in very fast here: check the tide times before setting off by kayak or on foot, and beware of the currents around the Pointe du Roc.","s":"June to September: kinder seas and long days for reaching Chausey, timing your outing with the tides.","a":[["Mont-Saint-Michel","A rock crowned by its abbey, in the middle of a bay with immense tides."],["Chausey Islands","A granite archipelago whose landscapes change with the tide, with seals and seabirds."],["Christian Dior Museum","The couturier’s childhood villa and its clifftop garden, with fashion exhibitions."],["Granville upper town","Ramparts and granite lanes of the old town, with views over the bay."],["La Lucerne Abbey","A restored Premonstratensian abbey, nestled in a peaceful valley of the bocage."]]},"saintmalo":{"v":"Granite ramparts, memories of the privateers and of Jacques Cartier: facing the Sillon and its oak breakwaters, Saint-Malo lives by the sea, from where the Route du Rhum yachts set off every four years.","f":["Cancale oysters","Rock or flat, enjoyed at the port of La Houle facing the oyster beds."],"r":"Watch the tide if you walk to the Grand Bé: the causeway is quickly submerged. At high spring tides, waves break over the Sillon sea wall.","s":"July and August: the mildest water of the year and long evenings; at low tide the sea goes out a long way, so aim for high tide.","a":[["Grand Bé","An islet reachable at low tide, with Chateaubriand’s tomb facing the open sea."],["Cancale","The oyster port of La Houle, with oyster beds and tastings on the quays."],["Pointe du Grouin","A wild headland on the Emerald Coast, with views over the bay of Mont-Saint-Michel."],["Dinan","A walled medieval town with half-timbered houses and a port on the Rance."],["Fort la Latte","A fortified castle standing on a rocky spur, facing Cap Fréhel."]]},"saintlunaire":{"v":"A resort born in the Belle Époque around villas perched on rocky points, Saint-Lunaire gazes over the bay of Saint-Malo from the Pointe du Décollé; at Longchamp, the schools introduce you to surfing on the Emerald Coast.","f":["Buckwheat crêpes and galettes","A complète with egg, ham and cheese, washed down with a bowl of cider at the village crêperie."],"r":"At Longchamp, priority goes to the surfer closest to the peak: keep clear of surf lessons and never let go of your board among beginners.","s":"September to November: more consistent but reasonably sized westerly swells, still-mild water and a beach free of holidaymakers.","a":[["Dinard","Belle Époque villas, the Clair de Lune promenade and views of the Saint-Malo ramparts."],["Fort la Latte","A fortified castle standing on a rocky spur, facing Cap Fréhel."],["Cap Fréhel","Pink sandstone cliffs, heather moorland and seabird colonies."],["Dinan ramparts","A medieval wall walk around the old town, with views over the Rance valley."],["Saint-Briac-sur-Mer","A village of sailors and painters, with small coves and a sheltered harbour."]]},"erquy":{"v":"Pink sandstone once cut into paving stones, heather and gorse moorland on the cape: Erquy also lives off its scallop fishing fleet, which makes its harbour one of the major ones in the bay of Saint-Brieuc.","f":["Erquy scallops","Fished in winter in the bay of Saint-Brieuc, simply pan-fried in salted butter."],"r":"On the cape, stay on the marked paths: the heather moorland is fragile and protected. In a kayak, keep your distance from the cliff birds.","s":"June to September: calmer seas more often, clear water and long days for exploring the cape’s coves.","a":[["Cap Fréhel","Pink sandstone cliffs, heather moorland and seabird colonies."],["Fort la Latte","A fortified castle standing on a rocky spur, facing the sea."],["Îlot Saint-Michel","An islet topped by a chapel, reached on foot along a shingle bar at low tide."],["Dahouët","A small fishing and pleasure harbour tucked at the end of a ria."],["Château de Bienassis","A fortified castle remodelled in the Renaissance, surrounded by moats and gardens."]]},"perros":{"v":"Pink granite rocks shaped into strange silhouettes, chapels and pardons of the Trégor: at Perros, you read the sea from the customs officers’ path, facing the Sept-Îles where gannets and puffins nest.","l":"French, Breton","h":["Demat","“hello” in Breton, always well received at the Trégor market"],"f":["Buckwheat crêpes and galettes","A galette complète and a salted-butter crêpe, with a bowl of Breton cider."],"r":"In the Sept-Îles, landing is only allowed on Île aux Moines: watch the birds from a distance. On the path, don’t climb on the protected rocks.","s":"July to early September: the least cold water of the year, long days and warm light on the pink granite.","a":[["Sept-Îles Nature Reserve","A protected archipelago with colonies of gannets, puffins and grey seals."],["Île Renote","A peninsula of jumbled pink granite, for a walk among sculpted rocks."],["Tréguier Cathedral","A Gothic cathedral and its cloister, in the heart of a small town full of character."],["Château de Tonquédec","The imposing ruins of a medieval fortified castle above the Léguer valley."],["Pleumeur-Bodou Radome","A huge telecommunications dome turned museum, on the history of satellite links."]]},"imsouane":{"v":"A fishing village where the blue boats come back into harbour among the surfers, Imsouane lives to the rhythm of the fish auction and the tide; you eat the catch of the day grilled facing the bay.","f":["Grilled fish from the harbour","Picked as the boats come in and grilled on the spot, with tomato salad and bread."],"r":"The fishing boats pass close to the peak: always give them priority, and cover up as soon as you leave the beach to walk through the village.","s":"October to March: north-west winter swells wrap into the bay and peel off long, consistent rights."},"safi":{"v":"A town of potters and a major sardine port, Safi keeps its sea fortress from the Portuguese occupation; its right-hander, surfed by a handful of experts on big-swell days, remains a wave apart.","f":["Safi sardines","Grilled or as meatballs in tomato sauce, in one of the sardine capitals."],"r":"An expert wave over a shallow rock slab: if you’re not at that level, watch from the shore. When it’s working, give priority to the locals.","s":"November to February: only big north-west winter swells, with a favourable wind, make the wave work.","a":[["Kechla Fortress","A fortress overlooking the medina and housing the national ceramics museum."],["Ksar el Bahr","A 16th-century Portuguese sea castle, standing face to the ocean."],["Safi","Medina, fishing port and the potters’ hill with its kilns and glazed ceramics."]]},"sidikaouki":{"v":"A hamlet of low houses set in Haha country, where the little white marabout that gave the place its name watches over the beach; between sessions, dromedaries and horses amble along the sand and argan trees cover the hills.","f":["Amlou and argan oil","Almond paste, argan oil and honey, spread on warm bread at breakfast."],"r":"The marabout of Sidi Kaouki is still a place of pilgrimage: admire it from outside. In the water, leave plenty of room between kites, windsurfers and surfers.","s":"October to March: steady Atlantic winter swells and lighter trade winds, which often leave clean mornings.","a":[["Essaouira Medina","UNESCO-listed walled town, ramparts facing the ocean, souks and thuya-wood workshops."],["Skala du Port, Essaouira","Cannon-lined bastion above the fishing harbour and its blue boats."],["Mogador Island","Bird reserve off Essaouira, a refuge for Eleonora’s falcon, best watched from the shore."],["Bayt Dakira","House of memory devoted to Essaouira’s Jewish past, in the heart of the mellah."],["Borj El Baroud","Ruined watchtower half buried in sand, near the mouth of the Oued Ksob."]]},"ngor":{"v":"The Lebou village of Ngor lives from fishing: painted pirogues pulled up on the sand, nets hauled in at the end of the day, and a pirogue shuttle to the island where Senegalese surfing found one of its founding waves.","r":"The crossing is run by the village boatmen: pay for your seat before you board. At the peak, the wave is short and prized by locals: wait your turn.","s":"November to April: well-angled North Atlantic winter swells and a northerly trade wind often favourable on the point.","a":[["Ngor Island","Car-free lanes, colourful houses and little coves a stone’s throw from the coast."],["Îles de la Madeleine National Park","Wild volcanic islets teeming with seabirds, reached by pirogue."],["African Renaissance Monument","Giant bronze statue on one of the Mamelles hills, with views over the whole peninsula."],["Mamelles Lighthouse","Lighthouse perched on a volcanic mound, with a panorama over Dakar and the ocean."],["Pointe des Almadies","Westernmost tip of the African continent, swell-battered rocks and sunsets."]]},"ouakam":{"v":"Tucked into the corniche, the old Lebou village of Ouakam keeps its pirogues and sandy lanes, overlooked by the white minarets of the Mosque of the Divinity, which seem to rise out of the waves.","r":"The area around the mosque is a place of prayer: cover your shoulders and legs as you walk through. In the water, the left is held by experienced locals: keep a low profile.","s":"November to April: steady North Atlantic winter swells, dry skies and cooler water.","a":[["Gorée Island","UNESCO-listed island, colonial houses, flower-filled lanes and the House of Slaves."],["African Renaissance Monument","Monumental bronze statue towering over Ouakam, with views over the whole Cap-Vert peninsula."],["Mamelles Lighthouse","Lighthouse on top of a volcanic mound, with a panorama over the ocean and the city."],["Îles de la Madeleine National Park","Protected volcanic islets, seabird colonies and clear water."],["Ngor Island","Small car-free island, sheltered beaches and colourful houses facing the village."]]},"elandsbay":{"v":"A West Coast hamlet between the icy ocean and the Verlorenvlei lagoon, rich in birdlife; crayfishing sets the rhythm of local life, and rock paintings adorn the nearby caves.","f":["West Coast crayfish","The local “kreef”, grilled on the braai with garlic butter, star of West Coast tables."],"r":"The paintings in Elands Bay Cave are fragile: do not touch them. At the point, the line-up is small and close-knit: greet the locals and wait your turn.","s":"May to September: big South Atlantic winter swells that make the point peel; bring a thick wetsuit.","a":[["Elands Bay Cave","Prehistoric shelter decorated with rock paintings, with a view over the bay."],["Verlorenvlei Lagoon","Protected wetland where flamingos, pelicans and herons feed among the reeds."],["Bird Island Nature Reserve","Cape gannet colony watched up close from a hide, at the end of the harbour."],["Lambert’s Bay","West Coast fishing harbour, crayfish and open-air seafood restaurants."]]},"muizenberg":{"v":"A Victorian seaside resort on False Bay, with its brightly painted beach huts and its promenade; this is where South African surfing was born and where the Shark Spotters programme was launched.","f":["Kalk Bay fish and chips","Fried snoek or hake at the neighbouring fishing harbour of Kalk Bay, eaten facing the boats."],"r":"At Surfer’s Corner, the surf schools take over the whitewater: steer clear of beginner groups and always keep hold of your board near swimmers.","s":"March to May: the south-easter eases, moderate swell and False Bay water still mild for learning.","a":[["Boulders Beach","Cove among granite boulders, with an African penguin colony to watch from the boardwalks."],["Cape Peninsula","Mountains, fynbos and cliffs all the way to the Cape of Good Hope and Cape Point."],["Chapman’s Peak","Road carved into the cliff and a summit trail, with a plunging view over Hout Bay."],["Zandvlei Estuary Nature Reserve","Lagoon and reed beds behind the beach, waterbirds and kayak outings."],["Kommetjie","Surf village on the Atlantic coast, Slangkop Lighthouse and Long Beach."]]},"dahab":{"v":"A former Bedouin fishing village turned freediving capital, Dahab faces the Sinai mountains; at the Blue Hole, plaques fixed to the rock remember the divers the sea has kept.","f":["Bedouin herbal tea","Brewed over embers with desert herbs and served very sweet under Sinai tents."],"r":"Never attempt the Arch without technical training. Enter the water through the marked entry points to spare the reef flat, and leave the memorial plaques untouched.","s":"October to November: water still very warm, excellent visibility and the summer heat has eased.","a":[["Blue Desert","Granite rocks painted blue by a Belgian artist in the middle of the Sinai desert."],["Dahab","Seafront promenade, Bedouin cafés, kitesurfing lagoon and starting point for Sinai treks."]]},"nosybe":{"v":"Off Nosy Be, the perfume island lined with ylang-ylang fields, Sakalava fishermen in outrigger canoes cross paths with dive boats heading for the little lighthouse on Tanikely.","f":["Fish in coconut","Fresh fish simmered in coconut milk, a classic of Nosy Be’s street eateries."],"r":"Tanikely is a marine protected area: do not touch the turtles or the coral, do not feed the fish and do not take any shells home.","s":"September to December: calm sea, clear warm water, and whale sharks often spotted off Nosy Be.","a":[["Nosy Komba","Volcanic island of lemurs, a village of embroiderers and forest trails."],["Nosy Be","Large island with ylang-ylang distilleries, crater lakes and the Hell-Ville market."]]},"watamu":{"v":"A small town on the Swahili coast where dhows sail past coral coves; volunteers watch over the turtle nests here, and the remains of the city of Gedi recall the ancient trade of the Indian Ocean.","r":"On the beach, do not disturb the marked turtle nests and avoid torches and fires at night; in the marine park, do not pick up shells or coral.","s":"December to March: gentler north-east monsoon, calm sea and clear water in the lagoon.","a":[["Watamu Marine National Park","Protected lagoon with coral heads, reef fish and turtles, by glass-bottom boat."],["Gedi Ruins","Medieval Swahili city swallowed by the forest, coral mosques and palaces."]]},"keramas":{"v":"On Gianyar’s black-sand coast, the rice fields run almost down to the wave; Hindu processions come to purify their effigies in the sea, and the nearby villages keep painting, dance and carving alive.","l":"Indonesian, Balinese","h":["Om Swastiastu","Balinese Hindu greeting, hands joined in front of the chest"],"f":["Babi guling","Spit-roasted suckling pig stuffed with spices, the pride of Gianyar’s warungs."],"r":"Make way for ceremonies coming down to the sea and do not step over offerings placed on the sand. At the narrow peak, wait your turn.","s":"October to March: the west monsoon blows offshore on the east coast and southern swells keep the right hollow.","a":[["Klungkung Palace (Kerta Gosa)","Royal hall of justice with ceilings painted in the Kamasan style, surrounded by a moat."],["Tirta Empul Temple","Holy water temple where Balinese purify themselves under the spouts of spring-fed pools."],["Penglipuran","Traditional village with gateways lined up along a paved street, and a bamboo grove."],["Goa Lawah Temple","Temple built in front of a cave home to thousands of bats, facing the sea."],["Batuan","Village renowned for its meticulous school of painting and its ancient temple."]],"lbl":"Homestay"},"nias":{"v":"In southern Nias, hilltop stone villages keep their great houses on stilts and the tradition of stone jumping; below, Lagundri Bay, reshaped by the 2005 earthquake, lives to the rhythm of the surfers’ losmen.","l":"Indonesian, Nias","h":["Ya’ahowu","greeting and blessing in the Nias language, offered to everyone"],"r":"In the traditional villages, introduce yourself to the elders and leave a contribution. At low tide the reef flat is exposed: do not walk on the reef.","s":"April to October: drier season and long southern Indian Ocean swells that keep the right holding.","lbl":"Losmen"},"gland":{"v":"At the end of the Blambangan peninsula, the Alas Purwo forest, sacred to Javanese Hindus and home to monkeys and peacocks, stops dead at the reef: here, you live in a surf camp cut off from the world.","f":["Banyuwangi rujak soto","Vegetable salad in peanut sauce drowned in a broth, a speciality of East Java."],"r":"Alas Purwo is a national park: do not feed the macaques, take all your rubbish away with you and stay inside the camp grounds at night.","s":"May to September: dry season, offshore south-east trade winds and long southern swells that keep the left holding.","lbl":"Jungle surf camp"},"komodo":{"v":"Bare hills where monitor lizards prowl plunge into waters churned by currents; in the stilt villages, Bajo fishermen have lived from the sea for generations.","r":"On the islands, never walk without a ranger because of the dragons. Underwater, the currents are fierce: follow your guide and only hold on to bare rock.","s":"April to June and September to November: calmer sea, good visibility and fewer boats than in high summer.","a":[["Komodo Island","Ranger-led hike to meet Komodo dragons in the savannah and forest."],["Komodo National Park","Padar and Rinca islands, viewpoints over the bays and pink-sand beaches."],["Komodo Village","Fishing village on stilts, colourful boats and everyday life by the water."]]},"bunaken":{"v":"Facing the cone of the Manado Tua volcano, Bunaken’s fishing villages live among coconut palms, mangroves and little churches; here, the Minahasa and Christian culture of North Sulawesi blends with that of the Bajo seafarers.","f":["Tinutuan (bubur Manado)","Rice porridge with vegetables, corn and pumpkin, Manado’s signature breakfast."],"r":"Bunaken is a fee-paying marine park: keep your entry tag, do not touch the wall and never walk on the reef flat at low tide.","s":"April to November: calmer sea outside the north-west monsoon and often excellent visibility along the walls.","a":[["Manado","Capital of North Sulawesi, lively waterfront and fiery Minahasa cuisine."],["Ban Hin Kiong Temple","Chinese temple with red and gold roofs, among the oldest in eastern Indonesia."]],"lbl":"Homestay"},"similan":{"v":"Nine uninhabited granite islands off the Phang Nga coast, visited only by rangers, divers and boats from Khao Lak; giant boulders form arches and swim-throughs underwater.","f":["Kaeng som","Sour, very spicy fish and tamarind curry, typical of southern Thailand."],"r":"The park limits visitor numbers: do not touch the coral or the sea fans, do not feed the fish and keep well away from moored boats.","s":"December to April: north-east monsoon, glassy sea and peak visibility while the park is open."},"weligama":{"v":"In the bay, fishermen’s outrigger canoes sit alongside rental boards; the little islet of Taprobane and its villa watch over the beach, and stilt fishermen still perch along the nearby coast.","f":["Ambul thiyal","Sour tuna curry with goraka, cooked almost dry, a speciality of the south coast."],"r":"Surf schools and fishermen share the bay: keep clear of the canoes and fishing lines, and cover up when you leave the beach.","s":"November to April: north-east monsoon, offshore wind on the south coast and small, regular swells for learning.","a":[["Galle Fort","UNESCO-listed fortified town, ramparts, colonial lanes and a lighthouse facing the ocean."],["Mirissa","Crescent beach under coconut palms and boat trips to watch whales."],["Koggala Lagoon","Mangrove-fringed lagoon, boat rides to islets and a temple."],["Dondra Head Lighthouse","Tall white lighthouse at the southern tip of the island, facing the Indian Ocean."],["Taprobane Island","Rocky islet crowned by a villa, to admire from Weligama beach."]]},"ichinomiya":{"v":"A small town on the Kujūkuri coast, both surf town and shrine town: boards strapped to bikes, surf shops along the coast road and worshippers coming to pray at Tamasaki Shrine.","f":["Namerō","Raw fish finely chopped by knife with miso, ginger and spring onion, a recipe of the Bōsō fishermen."],"r":"Park only in the paid beach car parks, do not get changed in the street and respect the lifeguarded swimming zones in summer.","s":"September to November: swells from typhoons and Pacific lows, water still mild and fewer people than in summer.","a":[["Tamasaki Shrine","Ancient Shinto shrine in the heart of Ichinomiya, black lacquer and great sacred trees."],["Kasamori-ji Temple","Kannon hall built on stilts atop a rock, deep in the forest."],["Kujūkuri Beach","Huge sandy beach running north, fishermen and surfers as far as the eye can see."],["Cape Taitō","Cliffs facing the Pacific and a lookout over the long curve of Kujūkuri."],["Hoki Museum","Museum devoted to contemporary Japanese realist painting, with bold architecture."]],"lbl":"Surfers’ guesthouse"},"kerama":{"v":"Islands of villages with coral-stone walls and red-tiled roofs, where Ryukyu culture is still alive; locals make their living from fishing, diving and, in winter, the passing humpback whales.","l":"Japanese, Okinawan","h":["Haisai","“hello” in Okinawan, which the elders love to hear"],"f":["Okinawa soba","Wheat noodles in a light broth, topped with braised pork, to try in the island’s little restaurants."],"r":"Green turtles graze the seagrass close to shore: keep your distance, do not touch them and never chase them.","s":"May to October: warm, clear water and a sea often calm between typhoons.","a":[["Zamami Island","White-sand beaches, lookouts over the archipelago and whale watching in winter."],["Tokashiki Island","The largest of the Kerama Islands, Aharen and Tokashiku beaches at the foot of the hills."],["Aka Island","Quiet little island home to Kerama deer, with reefs right by the shore."],["Geruma Island","Village of traditional houses surrounded by stone walls, linked by a bridge."]],"lbl":"Campsite or minshuku"},"rincon":{"v":"In the north-west corner of Puerto Rico, Rincón has lived to the rhythm of the swell ever since a world surfing championship was held there in the late 1960s; in winter, surfers and humpback whales share the open water.","r":"Tres Palmas is a marine reserve: do not walk on the elkhorn coral, do not fish, and give locals priority when the wave wakes up.","s":"December to March: only the big North Atlantic winter swells wake up the Tres Palmas right.","a":[["Punta Higüero Lighthouse","Historic lighthouse above the waves, a whale-watching spot in winter."],["Desecheo Island","Protected uninhabited island offshore, walls and exceptionally clear water for diving."],["Domes Beach","Surf beach below the former nuclear reactor dome, with a view of the lighthouse."],["Balneario de Rincón","Sheltered public beach, calm water and sunsets over the Mona Passage."]]},"pavones":{"v":"A fishing and surfing village at the end of a long dirt road, south of the Golfo Dulce, where the rainforest runs down to the pebbles and all of life revolves around the swell and the tides.","r":"The line-up is like the village: small and close-knit. Say hello, wait your turn and do not sit at the peak on big days if you are a beginner.","s":"April to October: green season and South Pacific swells that make the left peel along its full length.","a":[["Playa Zancudo","Long wild beach between mangroves and the Golfo Dulce, calm water and offshore fishing."],["Punta Banco","Hamlet at the end of the coast road, between forest and reef, popular with surfers."]],"lbl":"Cabinas"},"tamarindo":{"v":"A former Guanacaste fishing village turned cosmopolitan surf town, Tamarindo keeps its sabanero culture of cowboys and rodeos inland; right opposite, leatherback turtles come to nest on Playa Grande.","f":["Rosquillas and chorreadas","Cheesy corn rings and sweet corn pancakes, traditional treats of Guanacaste."],"r":"Crocodiles live in the estuary: do not surf the river mouth at dusk. At night, Playa Grande belongs to the turtles.","s":"December to April: dry season, offshore wind in the morning and moderate swell, ideal for learning.","a":[["Las Baulas National Marine Park","Leatherback turtle nesting site, guided night visits with the park rangers."],["Playa Grande","Wild beach across the estuary, with a more powerful beach break."],["Tamarindo Estuary","Mangroves to explore by kayak or boat, birds, iguanas and crocodiles."],["Playa Negra","Rocky cove to the south, famous reef right and a village feel."],["Conchal Refuge","Beach made of crushed shells and turquoise water great for snorkelling."]]},"sayulita":{"v":"Dirt streets bright with papel picado, shops selling Huichol art in beads and wool yarn, fishermen’s pangas on the sand: Sayulita blends a Nayarit coastal village with a surfing community.","f":["Pescado zarandeado","Whole butterflied fish, marinated in chillies and grilled over a wood fire, the pride of Nayarit."],"r":"At the right-hand peak, give priority to the local longboarders and stay with the other beginners in the whitewater, on the beach side.","s":"November to March: gentle, regular north-west swells, mild water and humpback whales in the bay."},"todossantos":{"v":"Off Ensenada, a fishing port and port of call in Baja California, Todos Santos Island keeps its lighthouse and its cold waters; big-wave hunters head out by boat from the marina.","f":["Ensenada fish tacos","Battered, fried fish in a tortilla with cabbage and cream: Ensenada claims to have invented them."],"r":"Go with a captain who knows the peak, an impact vest and a jet-ski partner; do not land on the island without permission.","s":"November to March: big North Pacific winter swells that stand up the walls of Killers.","a":[["Ensenada","Lively port, fish market and gateway to the Valle de Guadalupe vineyards."],["Hussong’s Cantina","The oldest cantina in Baja California, sawdust on the floor and an old-time atmosphere."],["Our Lady of Guadalupe Cathedral","Twin-towered cathedral in the heart of Ensenada, a venue for religious festivals."]]},"islamujeres":{"v":"A fishing island of colourful houses where people get around by golf cart; at its southern tip, the remains of a Maya shrine dedicated to Ixchel overlook the sea, and fish grilled with achiote is still the local pride.","f":["Tikin xic","Fish marinated in achiote and bitter orange, grilled in a banana leaf."],"r":"With whale sharks, the rules are strict: two swimmers at a time with a guide, no contact and no flash.","s":"June to September: whale sharks gather offshore and the Caribbean stays warm and calm.","a":[["Cancún Maya Museum","Modern archaeology museum on the Maya civilisations, with an open-air site."],["El Rey Maya Site","Maya ruins in the middle of the hotel zone, roamed by iguanas."],["Mercado 28","Popular market in downtown Cancún, crafts and small eateries."]]},"tofino":{"v":"At the end of the road on Vancouver Island, Tofino lives on Nuu-chah-nulth territory, between forests of giant cedars and sea inlets; surfers share the coast with fishermen, kayakers and grey whale watchers.","r":"Wolves and bears roam the beaches: leave no food or rubbish on the sand and keep your dog on a lead.","s":"September to November: well-lined-up North Pacific autumn swells, before the winter storms.","a":[["Clayoquot Sound","Maze of fjords, islands and old-growth forests, by kayak or water taxi."],["Meares Island","Boardwalk trail among giant cedars several centuries old."],["Long Beach","Huge beach in Pacific Rim park, between rocks, driftwood and rainforest."],["Vargas Island Provincial Park","Wild island reached by kayak, deserted beaches and rustic camping."]]},"newsmyrna":{"v":"Founded by Minorcan, Greek and Italian settlers who came to clear land in Spanish Florida, New Smyrna keeps its oak-shaded streets; at the inlet, local kids surf the jetty before school.","r":"On the stretch of beach open to cars, drive at walking pace in the marked lanes. In summer, turn off your lights near loggerhead turtle nests marked with stakes.","s":"September to November: swells from tropical storms offshore, water still warm and a quieter beach after summer.","a":[["Ponce de Leon Inlet Lighthouse","Tall red-brick lighthouse turned museum, with a view over the inlet from the top."],["Sugar Mill Ruins","Coquina remains of a 19th-century sugar mill, among the palm trees."],["Marine Science Center","Rehabilitation centre for sea turtles and birds, with aquariums on local wildlife."],["Green Mound","One of the largest pre-Columbian shell middens in Florida, left by Native Americans."]]},"montauk":{"v":"At the tip of Long Island, Montauk remains a sport-fishing port far from the chic of the Hamptons; at Ditch Plains, locals and weekend New Yorkers share the peak, and autumn brings back storms and striped bass.","f":["The lobster roll","Lobster in a brioche bun, cold with mayonnaise or hot with butter, served at the clam shacks."],"r":"The Ditch Plains car park is reserved for town permit holders: come by bike or early. At the peak, there are plenty of regulars, so wait your turn.","s":"September to November: swells from hurricanes and autumn storms, water still mild and the summer crowds gone.","a":[["Montauk Point Lighthouse","Oldest lighthouse in New York State, perched on the point facing the Atlantic."],["Camp Hero State Park","Cliffs, wooded trails and a former military base with its huge radar."],["Shadmoor State Park","Clay cliffs above the ocean and Second World War bunkers."],["Hither Hills State Park","Pine forest, wild beach and “walking dunes” on Napeague Bay."]],"lbl":"Hither Hills campground"},"mancora":{"v":"A former fishing village turned meeting place for South American surfers, Máncora lives between colourful boats on the sand, lively evenings and small-scale fishing off Los Órganos.","f":["Seco de chabelo","Green plantain and dried meat stewed together, a typical dish of the Piura region."],"r":"At El Ñuro, where you swim with green turtles near the fishermen’s pier, do not touch or feed them. At the peak, give locals priority.","s":"November to March: north-west swells that make the left peel, bright sunshine and the warmest water of the year."},"huanchaco":{"v":"Below its hilltop sanctuary, the bay where the Chimú already fished watches fishermen come home skimming past the old pier; neighbouring Trujillo, cradle of the marinera, dances on the edge of the desert.","f":["Shambar","Thick soup of wheat, pulses and smoked meat, served on Mondays in Trujillo and the surrounding area."],"r":"Do not climb on the caballitos drying upright on the beach. At Chan Chan, stay on the walkways: the mud-brick friezes are fragile.","s":"April to October: the most consistent southern swells on the left; bring a wetsuit, as the water stays cool.","a":[["Chan Chan","Vast Chimú adobe city, walls carved with fish and seabirds."],["Huaca de la Luna","Moche temple with polychrome murals still vivid, excavations ongoing."],["Trujillo","Colonial centre with colourful façades and wrought-iron grilles around the Plaza de Armas."],["Huanchaco Wetlands","Wetlands where the totora reed used to build the caballitos is grown."],["Huaca del Dragón","Small, well-restored Chimú temple with friezes of rainbows and creatures."]]},"iquique":{"v":"A former saltpetre port, Iquique keeps its wooden houses with verandas along Calle Baquedano; paragliders take off from the dunes above the city, and Chilean bodyboarders have made El Colegio a proving ground.","f":["Chumbeque","Layered biscuit filled with honey or fruit, an Iquique treat with Chinese roots."],"r":"El Colegio breaks over a barely submerged reef shelf: only go if you have mastered barrels, wear reef booties and give priority to local bodyboarders.","s":"April to September: the most powerful south-west swells, cool but bearable water under almost always dry skies.","a":[["Iquique Historic Centre","Wooden houses from the saltpetre era, a theatre and the Calle Baquedano promenade."],["Clock Tower","White wooden tower, a national monument in the heart of Plaza Arturo Prat."],["Esmeralda Corvette","Ship sunk off the port during the Battle of Iquique, a replica of which has become a museum."]]},"praiadorosa":{"v":"A fishing hamlet of Azorean origin discovered by surfers and backpackers, Rosa has kept its pousadas in the hills and its trails to the Ibiraquera lagoon; in winter, the mullet-fishing nets return to the sand.","f":["Sequência de camarão","Prawns served in a succession of dishes, fried, with garlic or in pastel, a ritual of the Santa Catarina coast."],"r":"Southern right whales are protected: no drones and no approaching on a paddleboard. During the traditional mullet fishing, respect the zones closed to surfing.","s":"March to June: consistent southern swells, offshore wind in the morning and quiet beaches after the Brazilian summer.","a":[["Garopaba","Former fishing village with a white church, surf beaches and the Siriú dunes."],["Imbituba","Former whaling port turned surfers’ haunt, with lookouts for spotting whales."]]},"noronha":{"v":"A former prison island bristling with Portuguese forts, Noronha now lives to the rhythm of turtles and dolphins; every visitor pays a preservation fee, and the whole village gathers at sunset at the Boldró fort.","r":"At the Atalaia natural pool, sunscreen and fins are banned and access is limited; the Bay of Dolphins can only be viewed from the lookout.","s":"September to February: dry season and peak visibility; when the swell hits the Mar de Dentro, dive on the Mar de Fora side.","lbl":"Family pousada"},"ilhabela":{"v":"Brazil’s sailing capital, the island also hides dozens of shipwrecks along its shores and caiçara communities, such as Bonete or Castelhanos, which still live from fishing, far from any road.","f":["Azul-marinho","Fish stewed with green bananas that turn the sauce blue, a caiçara dish of the coast."],"r":"In the caiçara coves, take your rubbish away with you and ask before taking photos. Bring repellent against the borrachudos that bite near the rivers.","s":"April to September: drier season, the channel often glassy in the morning and coves deserted after summer.","a":[["Ilhabela State Park","Atlantic Forest, waterfalls and trails to the wild beaches of the east coast."],["São Sebastião","Small colonial centre of low houses, facing the island across the channel."],["Maresias","Long white-sand beach renowned for surfing, with the forest as a backdrop."]]},"puntadeleste":{"v":"A former fishing hamlet on the Arroyo Maldonado, La Barra comes alive in summer with galleries and bars; you arrive over the wavy bridge, while at the Punta harbour, sea lions wait for the fishermen’s scraps.","r":"At the harbour, keep your distance from the sea lions waiting for fish: they can bite. At the peak, local surfers have priority, especially in winter.","s":"March to May: more frequent southern swells, water still mild and the resort emptied after the big January rush.","a":[["Casapueblo","Carlos Páez Vilaró’s white sculpture-house, clinging to the cliff facing the sunset."],["Isla de Lobos and its lighthouse","Rocky islet home to a huge sea lion colony, approached by boat."],["Gorriti Island","Wooded island facing the harbour, small sheltered beaches and remains of Spanish batteries."],["La Mano","Giant fingers rising from the sand of Playa Brava, the resort’s emblem."],["Museo del Mar","Whale skeletons, shells and seaside history, in the woods of La Barra."]]},"ayampe":{"v":"On the Manabí coast, Ayampe lines up its bamboo and palm-thatch houses between dry forest and a river mouth; the village lives from small surf schools, far from the bustle of Montañita.","f":["Fish viche","Thick fish soup with ground peanuts, plantain and corn, the pride of Manabí cooking."],"r":"To see the whales or Isla de la Plata, choose a licensed Machalilla park operator who respects approach distances.","s":"December to April: consistent northern swells, warm water and sunshine, despite a few tropical showers.","a":[["Machalilla National Park","Dry forest, Los Frailes beach and Isla de la Plata, nicknamed the “poor man’s Galápagos”."],["Agua Blanca","Community that guides visitors around a Manteño site and a sulphurous lagoon you can bathe in."],["Puerto López","Crescent-shaped fishing port, boats returning in the morning and departure point for whale trips."],["Montañita","Party surf village, a renowned point and lanes of bars and restaurants."]]},"burleigh":{"v":"Under the pandanus palms of the headland, Burleigh hill fills up at sunset; on the point, Gold Coast surfers watch for cyclone swells, on Aboriginal land whose story is told by the Jellurgal cultural centre.","r":"The line-up is one of the most contested in the country: do not jump off behind the point without the skills and respect priority. On the headland, stay on the path.","s":"February to May: cyclone swells and south-east trade winds blowing offshore on the point.","a":[["Burleigh Head National Park","Trail around the headland, rainforest, basalt boulders and a view of the point."],["Springbrook National Park","Upland rainforest, waterfalls, lookouts and the glow-worms of Natural Bridge."],["Currumbin Wildlife Sanctuary","Kangaroos, koalas and rainbow lorikeet feeding."],["David Fleay Wildlife Park","Wildlife park that pioneered platypus breeding, deep in the forest."],["Jebribillum Bora Park","Ancient Aboriginal ceremonial ring preserved in the middle of the city."]]},"byronbay":{"v":"A former whaling and abattoir town, Byron was adopted by the surfers of the shortboard revolution and then by the hippies; on Arakwal land, organic markets and longboards sit alongside sunrise at the lighthouse.","r":"The Pass is crowded and shared with the schools: one person per wave, and paddle out around the side rather than through the take-off zone. In the marine park’s sanctuary zones, fishing is banned.","s":"March to June: cyclone swells, then southern swells that wrap around the cape, water still warm and the summer crowds gone.","a":[["Cape Byron Lighthouse","White lighthouse at the end of a coastal path, the most easterly point of the mainland and whales in winter."],["Julian Rocks","Islets off the bay, a dive site renowned for its leopard sharks and turtles."],["Minyon Falls","Waterfall plunging off a rhyolite cliff, rainforest hike."],["Broken Head Nature Reserve","Coastal rainforest running down to small wild beaches."],["Bangalow","Hinterland village with a heritage main street, cafés and a monthly market."]]},"manly":{"v":"Lined with Norfolk Island pines, Manly lives to the rhythm of the ferries and the volunteer surf lifesaving clubs; it was at Freshwater, just to the north, that Duke Kahanamoku gave his surfing demonstration, still legendary in Australia.","r":"In the Cabbage Tree Bay Aquatic Reserve, do not touch or feed the blue gropers; little penguins nest near the wharf, so keep your distance.","s":"March to June: more consistent south and east swells, water still temperate and a less crowded beach than in summer.","a":[["North Head Quarantine Station","Former quarantine station on the headland, carved inscriptions and night tours."],["Manly Dam Reserve","Bushland and a freshwater lake for swimming, walking or mountain biking."],["Middle Head Fortifications","19th-century batteries and tunnels on a wooded headland facing the harbour entrance."],["The Gap","Dizzying sandstone cliffs above the ocean, coastal walk to Watsons Bay."],["Hornby Lighthouse","Small red-and-white striped lighthouse at the tip of South Head."]]},"ningaloo":{"v":"Here the red bush of the cattle stations meets the turquoise lagoon; between Coral Bay and Exmouth, you camp at the end of sandy tracks and live to the rhythm of whale sharks, manta rays and turtles nesting on the beaches.","r":"Never block a whale shark’s path and keep your distance, do not fish in the sanctuary zones and never put your fins down on the coral.","s":"April to July: whale shark season, often calm seas and heat more bearable than the scorching summer."},"piha":{"v":"Below Lion Rock, a former Māori pā, Piha lines up its wooden baches at the foot of forested hills; a culture of surfing and volunteer lifeguards was born here, made famous by the series Piha Rescue.","r":"Clean your shoes at the kauri dieback stations and respect closed tracks. In the water, swim between the flags: the currents are treacherous.","s":"March to May: consistent westerly swells, calmer winds and water still mild after summer.","a":[["Waitākere Ranges","Hills of native forest, waterfalls and trails between Auckland and the Tasman Sea."],["Karekare","Wild black-sand beach, waterfall and cliffs, the setting for the film The Piano."],["Kitekite Falls","Three-tiered waterfall at the end of a short forest walk, with a pool for swimming."],["Muriwai","Long black beach and a gannet colony nesting on the rocks."],["Te Henga (Bethells Beach)","Wild beach, black-sand dunes and Lake Wainamu, reached on foot."]]},"shipwreck":{"v":"At the foot of the plateau where kauri gum was once dug, Ahipara is a village of the Te Rarawa iwi; the beach of Te Oneroa-a-Tōhē still serves as a road and as a place to gather tuatua.","r":"If you drive on the beach, stay on the hard sand and off the dunes. For shellfish, respect the quotas and the rāhui set by the iwi.","s":"April to September: south-west swells wrapping around the points and offshore south-east winds.","a":[["Lake Ngatu","Dune lake with brown waters and a sandy beach, ideal for a freshwater swim."],["Whangapē Harbour","Remote estuary lined with hills and mangroves, a small Māori hamlet on the shore."],["Kaitaia","Far North town, with its regional museum on kauri gum and Māori history."]]},"restaurants":{"v":"In the heart of the Mamanucas, the Restaurants left is surfed from Namotu or Tavarua; the reefs fall within the villages’ customary fishing grounds, and since the end of the resorts’ exclusive rights, boats from Viti Levu share the peak.","r":"The reef is exposed at low tide: wait for mid-tide, wear booties or a helmet and respect the rotation at the peak. Choose a mineral sunscreen.","s":"May to October: consistent southern swells of the austral winter and offshore trade winds on the left in the morning.","a":[["Sri Siva Subramaniya Temple","Large Dravidian-style Hindu temple with painted ceilings, heart of the Indo-Fijian community."],["Malolo Island","Volcanic island with Fijian villages, grassy hills and reef-fringed beaches."],["Mana Island","Mamanuca island with clear waters, reef snorkelling and a Fijian village."]],"lbl":"Surf camp on nearby island"},"borabora":{"v":"Around Mount Otemanu, Bora lives between Vaitape, the va’a outrigger canoes training on the lagoon and the cannons left by the American base of the Second World War; in July, the Heiva rings with song and dance.","r":"Never walk on the coral heads and avoid feeding the rays and sharks. On a paddleboard or kayak, stay out of the channels used by boats.","s":"May to October: dry season, refreshing trade winds and an exceptionally clear lagoon, with fewer showers.","a":[["Vaitape","The island’s main village, local crafts, little food trucks and the start of the round-island bike ride."],["Mount Otemanu","Iconic volcanic peak towering over the lagoon, best admired from the motus."]],"lbl":"Family guesthouse"},"jardimdomar":{"v":"A village of car-free cobbled lanes clinging above terraced banana plantations, Jardim do Mar saw surfers rally against the seawall promenade that ate into its legendary wave.","f":["Espada with banana","Fillet of black scabbardfish caught offshore, pan-fried and served with Madeiran banana."],"r":"You get in and out over boulders pounded by the swell: watch for a long time before going and give locals priority. Park at the top of the village.","s":"November to February: big north-west swells that finally wake up the right, in air that is always mild.","a":[["25 Fontes Levada","Trail along an irrigation channel through laurel forest, to a pool fed by waterfalls."],["Porto Moniz","Natural pools carved into black lava, pounded by Atlantic waves."],["Cabo Girão","Dizzying cliff with a glass platform suspended above the cultivated terraces."],["Paul do Mar","Neighbouring fishing village at the foot of the cliffs, another powerful right and fresh fish."],["Pico do Areeiro","Summit above the clouds, start of the ridge trail to Pico Ruivo."]]},"santabarbara":{"v":"On the north coast of São Miguel, between tea plantations and fishing villages such as Rabo de Peixe, Ribeira Grande lives in summer to the rhythm of the Holy Spirit festivals and surf contests on the black sand.","f":["Cozido das Furnas","Stew of meats and vegetables slow-cooked for hours in the hot volcanic ground."],"r":"The beach break is shared with surf schools: keep clear of their zone and give locals priority. Outside lifeguarded hours, the currents are powerful.","s":"September to November: consistent autumn swells, water still mild and gentler winds than in midwinter.","a":[["Sete Cidades Lagoon","Twin lakes, one green and one blue, at the bottom of a vast caldera."],["Furnas","Village of fumaroles and hot springs, iron-rich pools in the Terra Nostra garden."],["Lagoa do Fogo","Wild crater lake at the top of the Água de Pau massif, a fine hike."],["Vila Franca Islet","Sea crater forming an almost round natural pool, reached by boat in summer."],["Ribeira Grande","Old centre of basalt and whitewash, Baroque churches and a bridge over the river."]]},"capomannu":{"v":"On the Sinis peninsula, between flamingo lagoons and beaches of quartz grains, the fishermen of Cabras make mullet bottarga; when the mistral blows, surfers from all over the island converge on the cape.","l":"Italian, Sardinian","f":["Cabras bottarga","Mullet roe from the nearby lagoons, grated over pasta or sliced as an aperitivo."],"r":"Do not take quartz sand from the Sinis beaches: it is forbidden and fined. Around Mal di Ventre, the marine protected area regulates anchoring and fishing.","s":"October to April, when the mistral blows for several days: well-lined-up swell on the reef and a line-up far from the summer crowds.","a":[["Tharros","Punic and later Roman city on a peninsula, columns standing against the sea."],["Giants of Mont’e Prama","Stone statues of Nuragic warriors, displayed in the Cabras museum."],["Mal di Ventre Island","Flat, wild island in the marine protected area, crystal-clear waters for snorkelling."],["S’Archittu","White limestone arch plunging into a turquoise cove."],["Capo San Marco","Lighthouse at the tip of the Sinis, cliffs and a view over the Gulf of Oristano."]]},"isolabella":{"v":"Below Taormina, perched on its hill since Greek antiquity, the bay of Mazzarò keeps its fishing boats; the islet, once the garden of an eccentric English aristocrat, is now a reserve where you swim facing Etna.","f":["Granita with brioche","Almond, lemon or mulberry granita, served with a brioche for a Sicilian breakfast."],"r":"The islet is a nature reserve: enter only by the official access, pick nothing and leave the pebbles and shells of the sandbar where they are.","s":"June to September: calm, warm sea and crystal-clear water; aim for June or September to avoid the crowds on the sandbar.","a":[["Ancient Theatre of Taormina","Greco-Roman tiers open to the sea and Etna, a concert venue in summer."],["Taormina","Lively Corso, medieval palaces and gardens hanging above the sea."],["Castelmola","Hilltop village above Taormina, castle ruins and a plunging view over the coast."],["Alcantara Gorges","Canyon of basalt columns carved by an icy river, to explore on foot."],["Ancient Naxos","Archaeological park of the first Greek colony in Sicily, with its small museum."]]},"zlatnirat":{"v":"Bol stretches between houses of white Brač stone and a Dominican monastery facing Hvar; for decades, boards and sails have watched for the afternoon maestral here, while the vines climb the mountainside.","f":["Vitalac","Brač lamb offal wrapped in caul fat and spit-roasted, a speciality of the island."],"r":"On the point, respect the buoys separating swimmers from boards: never sail among swimmers, and come in through the designated lanes.","s":"June to September: an almost daily thermal maestral in the afternoon, ideal for windsurfing as well as kiting.","a":[["Blaca Hermitage","Monastery clinging to a wild ravine, reached by a stone path."],["Stari Grad Plain","Ancient Greek fields still farmed, dry-stone walls and vines, on the island of Hvar."],["Stari Grad","Small old town at the head of a bay, with the fishpond castle of Tvrdalj."],["Dragon’s Cave","Cave decorated with reliefs carved by monks, above Bol, visited with a guide."],["Škrip","Oldest village on Brač, ancient walls and the island museum in a tower."]]},"naxos":{"v":"An island more agricultural than glamorous, Naxos grows potatoes, graviera cheese and citrons for kitron; at Mikri Vigla, sailing schools and family tavernas are scattered between dunes and junipers.","f":["Naxos graviera","Hard cheese with a nutty flavour, to try with the island’s potatoes and a glass of kitron."],"r":"In the protected juniper dunes of Alyko, stay on the paths. When kiting, keep to the corridor in the windy bay and leave the southern bay to swimmers.","s":"June to September: the meltemi blows strong and steady from the north; June and September bring gentler wind and fewer people.","a":[["Portara","Huge marble doorway of an unfinished temple, facing the harbour, at sunset."],["Naxos Chora","Venetian kastro, vaulted whitewashed lanes and a lively seafront."],["Apeiranthos","Mountain village with marble lanes, Venetian towers and small museums."],["Mount Zas","Highest peak in the Cyclades, a trail to the Cave of Zeus and views over the Aegean."],["Temple of Demeter","Restored archaic marble temple among fields and olive trees."]]},"biscarrosse":{"v":"Between two large lakes and the ocean, Biscarrosse watched the giants of the skies take off: its lake was once home to a seaplane base. At the beach, life follows the pines, the dunes and the baïnes.","f":["Arcachon Bay oysters","Close by, the oyster farmers serve them in their huts with a glass of dry white."],"r":"Baïnes create currents that drag you out to sea: surf away from the swimming zones and reach the beach only by the marked paths through the dune.","s":"September to October: consistent autumn swells, offshore wind in the morning and water still mild.","a":[["Dune du Pilat","Highest dune in Europe, with a vast view over the ocean, the forest and the Banc d’Arguin."],["Lake Cazaux-Sanguinet","Large clear-water lake lined with pines, perfect for swimming, sailing or paddleboarding."],["Seaplane Museum","Seaplanes, models and archives telling the story of the Biscarrosse base’s heyday."],["Banc d’Arguin","Shifting sandbanks at the mouth of the bay, a seabird refuge reached by boat."],["Arcachon","Ville d’Hiver with its whimsical villas, piers, seafront and lively market."]]},"carcans":{"v":"At the end of a straight road through the pine forest, Carcans-Plage keeps the feel of a small Médoc resort: a few streets behind the dune, cycle paths through the pines and the largest lake in France close by.","f":["Bordeaux canelé","Small cake with a caramelised crust and a soft centre, flavoured with rum and vanilla."],"r":"Baïnes are common on this coast: spot the currents from the dune before going in, and keep your distance from the lifeguarded swimming zones.","s":"September to October: consistent autumn swells, wind often calm in the morning and a line-up far less crowded than in summer.","a":[["Lake Hourtin-Carcans","The largest natural lake in mainland France, with sandy beaches and cycle paths."],["Étang de Cousseau Nature Reserve","Trails and boardwalks through marshes, heathland and forest, with shy wildlife to spot."],["Hourtin Dunes and Marshes","Wooded dunes, wet dune slacks and unspoilt marshes between the lake and the ocean."],["Lake Lacanau","Large freshwater lake lined with pines, ideal for paddleboarding, sailing or a wave-free swim."],["Hourtin Lighthouses","Two square brick towers hidden in the pine forest, behind the line of dunes."]]},"hourtin":{"v":"Separated from the village by miles of forest, Hourtin-Plage lives in summer to the rhythm of surfers and families, while Hourtin’s harbour, on the lake, brings together sailing boats, windsurfers and fishing boats.","f":["Pauillac lamb","Milk-fed lamb raised with its mother in the Médoc, simply roasted and served with a local wine."],"r":"The dunes of the neighbouring nature reserve are protected: stay on the marked paths and never cut through the vegetation to reach the beach.","s":"September to October: well-lined-up autumn swells, early-morning offshore wind and a beach almost empty after summer.","a":[["Lake Hourtin-Carcans","The largest natural lake in mainland France, with its harbour, beaches and cycle paths."],["Hourtin Dunes and Marshes","Wooded dunes, wet dune slacks and unspoilt marshes between the lake and the ocean."],["Hourtin Lighthouses","Two square brick towers hidden in the pine forest, behind the line of dunes."],["Vertheuil Abbey","Former Romanesque abbey among the Médoc vineyards, with its church and carved portal."],["Tour de l’Honneur","Medieval keep of Lesparre, the last remnant of its castle, overlooking the little town."]]},"montalivet":{"v":"Montalivet is the birthplace of family naturism in France: the pioneers settled in the forest here just after the war. The village also revolves around its covered market and the long beach facing the dunes.","f":["Grenier médocain","Médoc charcuterie made from rolled, spiced pork belly lining, served cold in thin slices."],"r":"On either side of the central beach, long stretches are naturist: respect this custom, and never take photos of bathers.","s":"September to October: solid autumn swells, early-morning offshore wind and water still mild.","a":[["Cordouan Lighthouse","UNESCO-listed Renaissance lighthouse standing at sea at the mouth of the estuary, visited by boat."],["Soulac-sur-Mer","Seaside resort with colourful brick and stone villas and pedestrian streets."],["Soulac Basilica","Romanesque church long buried under sand, a UNESCO-listed stop on the Way of St James."],["Pointe de Grave","Tip of the Médoc between the ocean and the Gironde estuary, with its lighthouse and a view of Cordouan."]]},"soulac":{"v":"At the tip of the Médoc, Soulac lines up its brick and stone villas, a legacy of the first sea-bathing days, around a basilica long swallowed by the sand. Here, the ocean is eating away at the coast and everyone talks about it.","f":["Lamprey à la bordelaise","Caught in the estuary, it simmers for hours in a red wine and leek sauce."],"r":"The coast is retreating fast here: do not go near collapsed dune foots and cross the dune line only at the designated access points. Watch out for currents near the estuary.","s":"September to October: consistent autumn swells, water still mild and beaches quiet again.","a":[["Cordouan Lighthouse","UNESCO-listed Renaissance lighthouse standing at sea at the mouth of the estuary, visited by boat."],["Soulac Basilica","Romanesque church long buried under sand, a UNESCO-listed stop on the Way of St James."],["Pointe de Grave","Tip of the Médoc between the ocean and the Gironde estuary, with its lighthouse and a view of Cordouan."],["Talmont-sur-Gironde","Village bright with hollyhocks on a promontory, with its Romanesque church facing the estuary."],["Barzan Gallo-Roman Site","Remains of an ancient port, baths and a great temple above the estuary."]]},"penermalo":{"v":"On the doorstep of Lorient, a major fishing port and capital of Celtic music, Guidel-Plages stretches to the wooded mouth of the Laïta; surfers, kitesurfers and walkers share the sand depending on the wind.","l":"French, Breton","h":["Demat","“hello” in Breton, always well received on the coast"],"f":["Crêpes and buckwheat galettes","A galette complète and a bowl of cider in a crêperie, the Breton ritual after a session."],"r":"Near the mouth of the Laïta, the tidal currents are strong: stay out of the water on the ebb, and leave room between kites, boards and swimmers.","s":"September to November: consistent westerly swells, water still bearable and beaches deserted after summer.","a":[["Île de Groix","Island of cliffs and coves, with the convex beach of Les Grands Sables and its coastal paths."],["Quimperlé","Small town where two rivers meet, with its old lower town and half-timbered houses."],["Sainte-Croix Abbey","Romanesque church with a circular plan inspired by the Holy Sepulchre, in the heart of the lower town."],["Carnoët Forest","Large state forest along the Laïta, criss-crossed by shady hiking trails."],["Maison-Musée du Pouldu","Reconstruction of the inn where Gauguin and his painter friends stayed at Le Pouldu."]]},"porscarn":{"v":"Between La Torche and the rocks of Saint-Guénolé, Pors Carn opens onto the Bay of Audierne in the heart of Bigouden country, land of tall lace headdresses, fishing ports and old granite chapels.","l":"French, Breton","h":["Demat","“hello” in Breton, always well received in Bigouden country"],"f":["Buckwheat galettes","Filled with egg, ham and cheese, with a bowl of cider in a Bigouden crêperie."],"r":"Do not venture onto the rocks of Saint-Guénolé in a big swell: rogue waves sweep across them. On the beach, give the surf schools space.","s":"September to November: well-lined-up westerly swells, water still bearable and the bay deserted after summer.","a":[["Eckmühl Lighthouse","Tall granite lighthouse at the Penmarch point: climb to the top for the view over the coast."],["Le Guilvinec","Major small-scale fishing port: watch the trawlers come home and the bustle of the fish auction."],["Pont-l’Abbé","Capital of Bigouden country, with its castle housing a museum of costumes and headdresses."],["Finistère Prehistory Museum","Menhirs, stelae and objects found in Finistère, displayed in the museum and its garden."],["La Torche Dolmen","Prehistoric burial mound on the point, facing the ocean and the long Bay of Audierne."]]},"tanchet":{"v":"A fishing port and the start of great offshore sailing races, Les Sables-d’Olonne lives facing the sea: the La Chaume district with its low houses, the lively Remblai and, to the south, the rocky peaks of Tanchet.","f":["Les Sables sardines","Landed at the Les Sables fishing port and served simply grilled on the quayside terraces."],"r":"At Tanchet, the wave breaks over a rocky bottom: wear booties at low tide, wait your turn at the peak and respect the regulars.","s":"September to November: consistent autumn swells, water still mild and a quieter town than in summer.","a":[["La Chaume","Old fishermen’s quarter of narrow lanes, with its Arundel Tower facing the channel."],["Abbaye Sainte-Croix Museum","Modern and contemporary art in a former abbey, with a large Gaston Chaissac collection."],["Talmont Castle","Ruins of a medieval fortress, with a view from the keep and events in summer."],["Olonne Marshes","Former salt marshes and pools where you can watch many birds, on foot or by bike."],["Saint-Jean d’Orbestier Abbey","Remains of a medieval abbey near the coast, peaceful and south of the town."]]},"baleal":{"v":"A former island linked to the coast by a sandy isthmus, Baleal looks across to Peniche, a fishing port renowned for its bobbin lace; on either side of the peninsula, two beaches let you surf in almost any wind.","f":["Óbidos ginjinha","Sour cherry liqueur served in a little chocolate cup, in the lanes of Óbidos."],"r":"There are lots of surf schools in Baleal: if you surf independently, move away from beginner groups and leave the waves at the rocky peaks to the regulars.","s":"September to November: consistent autumn swells, water still mild and beaches free of the summer crowds.","a":[["Berlengas Islands","Protected granite archipelago, with clear waters, seabirds and a fort on an islet, reached by boat."],["Óbidos","White medieval village encircled by ramparts, with flower-filled lanes to explore on foot."],["Peniche Fortress","Fortress turned political prison under the dictatorship, now a museum of the resistance."],["Cabo Carvoeiro","Cliffs sculpted by erosion and a lighthouse at the end of the peninsula, facing the Berlengas."],["Óbidos Lagoon","Large lagoon with calm waters, ideal for paddleboarding, sailing or birdwatching."]]},"arrifana":{"v":"Below the Arrifana cliffs, a small fishing harbour still shelters its boats at the foot of a winding road, while on the nearby plateau lie the remains of a ribat, a fortified monastery from the Islamic period.","f":["Aljezur sweet potato","Grown in the sandy soils of the coast, it is enjoyed roasted or in cakes."],"r":"You are in the Costa Vicentina natural park: sleeping in a van or camping outside authorised areas is banned and enforced. Reach the beach by the road, not down the cliff.","s":"September to November: consistent autumn swells that the bay shelters from the wind, water still mild.","a":[["Ribat of Arrifana","Remains of a 12th-century Islamic fortified monastery, on a promontory facing the ocean."],["Costa Vicentina Natural Park","Wild cliffs, coves and Rota Vicentina trails along the Atlantic."],["Aljezur Castle","Ruins of a castle of Islamic origin overlooking the white village and the valley."],["Arrifana Fort","Ruins of a fort on the cliff, a viewpoint over the bay and the surfers below."],["Serra de Monchique","Wooded mountains of the Algarve, with the summit of Fóia and its stone villages."]]},"amado":{"v":"Nestled in a valley between cliffs of colourful strata, Amado brings together surf schools and travellers’ vans; close by, Carrapateira remains a small farming village and goose-barnacle fishermen still climb down the rocks.","f":["Medronho","Strawberry-tree brandy distilled in the Algarve hills, served in a small glass after the meal."],"r":"The beach is inside the natural park: keep to the existing tracks by car, do not sleep in the car park and leave the cliff vegetation intact.","s":"September to November: well-lined-up autumn swells and an open beach that picks up almost every direction.","a":[["Cape St Vincent","South-western tip of Portugal, dizzying cliffs and a lighthouse, famous for its sunsets."],["Sagres Fortress","Fortress on a windswept promontory, associated with Henry the Navigator."],["Islamic Fishing Village","Remains of a medieval fishing hamlet on the Pontal, above the ocean."],["Lagos","Old walled town with cobbled streets, a marina and the golden cliffs of Ponta da Piedade."],["Padrão Menhirs","Group of prehistoric standing stones in the countryside near Vila do Bispo."]]},"bordeira":{"v":"A river winds between the dunes before meeting the ocean at the foot of the cliffs: Bordeira is a vast, wild beach, revealed from the wooden boardwalks and the Carrapateira scenic road.","f":["Seafood cataplana","Clams, prawns and chouriço simmered in a copper pot, the great Algarve classic."],"r":"Reach the beach by the boardwalks over the protected dunes, and beware of the current at the river mouth when the tide is going out.","s":"September to November: consistent autumn swells, calmer wind in the morning and an almost deserted beach.","a":[["Cape St Vincent","South-western tip of Portugal, dizzying cliffs and a lighthouse, famous for its sunsets."],["Sagres Fortress","Fortress on a windswept promontory, associated with Henry the Navigator."],["Islamic Fishing Village","Remains of a medieval fishing hamlet on the Pontal, above the ocean."],["Ribat of Arrifana","Remains of a 12th-century Islamic fortified monastery, on a promontory facing the ocean."],["Aljezur Castle","Ruins of a castle of Islamic origin overlooking the white village and the valley."]]},"carcavelos":{"v":"Lisbon’s surf beach, reachable by train from the capital, Carcavelos stretches out below the fort of São Julião da Barra; students and office workers come here to ride a few waves after class or work.","f":["Pastel de nata","A small custard tart in flaky pastry dusted with cinnamon, born near the Jerónimos Monastery in Belém."],"r":"At weekends the line-up is packed: always keep hold of your board, never let go of it under a wave, and stay clear of the surf lessons set up near the shore.","s":"October to March: fairly powerful winter swells wrap around the coast and finally get this south-facing beach working.","a":[["Pena Palace","A brightly coloured Romantic palace perched on the Sintra hills, surrounded by wooded parkland."],["Moorish Castle","Medieval ramparts snaking along the Sintra ridges, with views all the way to the ocean."],["Sintra National Palace","A royal palace in the heart of the town, recognisable by its two large conical chimneys."],["Cascais","A former fishing village turned elegant resort, with its bay, citadel and narrow lanes."],["National Museum of Ancient Art","Lisbon’s great museum, rich in Portuguese and European painting, with a garden above the Tagus."]]},"matosinhos":{"v":"A fishing and canning port right next to Porto, Matosinhos smells of charcoal: outside the restaurants, fish grills on braziers in the middle of the street. To the north, in Leça, Álvaro Siza slipped tidal pools into the rocks.","f":["Matosinhos grilled fish","Sardines, sea bass or red mullet grilled over charcoal outside the restaurants near the port."],"r":"This is Porto’s beginner beach: if you surf independently, keep away from the groups of beginners and respect the lifeguarded swimming zones in summer.","s":"September to November: consistent autumn swells, and the harbour wall shelters the beach when the sea gets bigger.","a":[["Serralves Foundation","A contemporary art museum designed by Álvaro Siza, in a large park with an Art Deco villa."],["Livraria Lello","A neo-Gothic bookshop with a famous carved red staircase, in the heart of Porto."],["Casa da Música","An angular concert hall designed by Rem Koolhaas, open for tours and full of life."],["Soares dos Reis National Museum","A palace housing the country’s oldest art museum, with Portuguese sculpture and painting."],["Vila do Conde","A shipbuilding town with a large monastery, an aqueduct and a replica caravel."]]},"espinho":{"v":"A seaside town with numbered streets laid out on a grid, Espinho still has its arte xávega fishermen, who haul their big nets up the beach by hand, next to surfers watching the right-hander by the jetty.","f":["Francesinha","A Porto sandwich filled with meats, smothered in melted cheese and a spicy beer sauce."],"r":"When the xávega fishermen are working, keep clear of the nets and ropes on the beach and in the water, and let the tractors through.","s":"September to November: consistent autumn swells, wind often calmer in the morning and water slightly less cold.","a":[["Serralves Foundation","A contemporary art museum designed by Álvaro Siza, in a large park with an Art Deco villa."],["Soares dos Reis National Museum","The country’s oldest art museum, with Portuguese sculpture and painting in a Porto palace."],["Casa da Música","An angular concert hall designed by Rem Koolhaas, open for tours and full of life."],["Porto Botanical Garden","Romantic gardens, glasshouses and camellia hedges around a former merchant’s mansion."],["Aguda","A small fishing village right on the beach, with its boats and a peaceful atmosphere."]]},"buarcos":{"v":"A former fishing village backed by the Serra da Boa Viagem, Buarcos runs on from the resort of Figueira da Foz and its huge beach; along the sea wall, a right-hander peels towards the bay when the winter swell comes in.","f":["Leitão da Bairrada","Roast suckling pig with crackling skin, a speciality of the neighbouring Bairrada, served with a sparkling wine."],"r":"The right breaks along a sea wall and over rocks: wear booties, don’t paddle into the path of surfers coming off the peak, and respect the regulars.","s":"October to March: big winter swells wrap around Cape Mondego and produce long peeling rights.","a":[["Montemor-o-Velho Castle","A large medieval castle on a hill, overlooking the rice fields of the Mondego valley."],["Serra da Boa Viagem","A small wooded mountain above the sea, with trails and viewpoints over the coast."],["Cape Mondego","A headland with cliffs rich in Jurassic fossils, topped by a lighthouse above the ocean."],["Figueira da Foz","A lively seaside resort with an exceptionally wide beach, a casino and a fort."]]},"saotorpes":{"v":"Below the big port of Sines, birthplace of Vasco da Gama, São Torpes long enjoyed water warmed by the neighbouring power station, now closed; further south begins the wild coast of the Alentejo.","f":["Carne de porco à alentejana","Marinated pork pieces sautéed with clams, the Alentejo’s marriage of land and sea."],"r":"Just to the south begins the Vicentine Coast natural park: don’t camp outside the authorised areas, don’t sleep in a van on the clifftops, and stay on the paths.","s":"September to November: consistent autumn swells, water still mild and a quiet beach after summer.","a":[["Porto Covo","A white village trimmed in blue around a square, above small coves among the rocks."],["Pessegueiro Island","A rocky islet topped by a ruined fort, facing a beach watched over by another fort."],["Sines Castle","A medieval castle above the bay, with a small museum and the statue of Vasco da Gama."],["Vila Nova de Milfontes","A white town at the mouth of the Mira, between river and ocean beaches."]]},"cordoama":{"v":"You reach it by a track across the Vila do Bispo plateau before it drops down to a wild beach below high cliffs; here, goose barnacle gatherers still brave the swell-battered rocks.","f":["Percebes","Goose barnacles prised from the swell-battered rocks, the pride of Vila do Bispo, boiled in salted water."],"r":"Don’t settle at the foot of the cliffs: rockfalls happen regularly. The beach is inside the natural park, so don’t sleep in the car park.","s":"September to November: consistent autumn swells, calmer wind in the morning and an almost deserted beach.","a":[["Cape St Vincent","The south-western tip of Portugal, with dizzying cliffs and a lighthouse, famous for its sunsets."],["Sagres Fortress","A fortress on a windswept promontory, associated with Henry the Navigator."],["Chapel of Our Lady of Guadalupe","A small, isolated Gothic chapel in the countryside, linked to the time of Henry the Navigator."],["Padrão Menhirs","A group of prehistoric standing stones in the countryside near Vila do Bispo."],["Lagos","An old walled town with cobbled streets, a marina and the golden cliffs of Ponta da Piedade."]]},"elpalmar":{"v":"A long beach with no tower blocks, lined with chiringuitos, vegetable plots and low houses, El Palmar lies below Vejer, a white hilltop village; further south, the Trafalgar lighthouse watches over the waters of the famous naval battle.","f":["Tortillitas de camarones","Thin, crispy fritters with tiny shrimps, a treat from the Bay of Cádiz."],"r":"In summer, surf only in the zones marked out by the lifeguards, and reach the beach by the existing paths without trampling the dunes.","s":"October to March: Atlantic winter swells, often groomed by the Levante, which blows offshore here.","a":[["Vejer de la Frontera","A white village perched on a hill, with Moorish lanes, ramparts and views over the coast."],["Cape Trafalgar","A low headland topped by a lighthouse, between dunes and beaches, facing the site of the 1805 battle."],["Los Caños de Meca","A small bohemian village below wooded cliffs, with its coves and freshwater springs."],["Conil de la Frontera","A fishing port of white houses, with its old tower, narrow lanes and long beaches."]]},"hierbabuena":{"v":"For centuries Barbate has lived from almadraba tuna fishing and salting; beyond the port, Hierbabuena beach hides below the pine-covered cliffs of La Breña natural park.","f":["Almadraba bluefin tuna","Caught in spring off Barbate, it’s eaten as tataki, a la plancha or cured as mojama."],"r":"The beach is inside La Breña natural park: walk there along the path from the port, take your rubbish home and stay on the clifftop trails.","s":"October to March: only well-angled winter swells reach this sheltered beach, far from the summer crowds.","a":[["La Breña Natural Park","Pine forest on high cliffs, coastal trails and marshes where many birds nest."],["Torre del Tajo","A watchtower perched on the cliff edge, a lookout over the coast and as far as Morocco on clear days."],["Vejer de la Frontera","A white village perched on a hill, with Moorish lanes, ramparts and views over the coast."],["Zahara de los Atunes","A tuna-fishing village turned quiet resort, with the ruins of its castle."],["Cape Trafalgar","A low headland topped by a lighthouse, between dunes and beaches, facing the site of the 1805 battle."]]},"salinas":{"v":"Avilés’s seaside resort, Salinas unrolls its long beach as far as the El Espartal dunes; on the point, an open-air museum displays old anchors, and in the evening people share cider poured from a height in the sidrerías.","l":"Spanish, Asturian","f":["Fabada and Asturian cider","A stew of beans and cured meats, with cider poured from high up to aerate it."],"r":"To the east, the beach continues into the El Espartal dunes: don’t trample them and use the boardwalks. At the peak, give priority to the local club regulars.","s":"September to November: consistent autumn swells from the Bay of Biscay, water still mild and a less crowded beach.","a":[["Cudillero","A fishing village of colourful houses clinging in an amphitheatre around its little harbour."],["Santa María del Naranco","A UNESCO-listed pre-Romanesque palace on the slopes of Mount Naranco above Oviedo."],["Avilés","An old town of arcaded streets, palaces and lively squares, carefully restored."],["Niemeyer Centre","A white dome and curving forms designed by Oscar Niemeyer, facing the Avilés estuary."],["Philippe Cousteau Anchor Museum","An open-air museum on the Salinas point, where old anchors face the sea."]]},"loslocos":{"v":"A former fishing port at the mouth of the San Martín de la Arena estuary, Suances has turned to surfing; its beaches, lined up below the cliffs, draw Cantabrian surfers as soon as the swell arrives.","f":["Cocido montañés","A Cantabrian stew of white beans, cabbage and cured meats, perfect after a winter session."],"r":"The peak is very local and the beach is narrow below the cliffs: go down by the built access, watch before paddling out and leave the best waves to the regulars.","s":"September to November: consistent autumn swells, water still mild and a calmer line-up than in summer.","a":[["Altamira Museum","A faithful replica of the cave with its painted bison, plus a museum on life in the Palaeolithic."],["Collegiate Church of Santillana del Mar","A Romanesque collegiate church with a carved cloister, in the heart of a medieval stone village."],["El Castillo Cave","A cave decorated with hand stencils and signs among the oldest in Europe, visited in small groups."],["Comillas","A village of Modernista palaces, including Gaudí’s El Capricho, between sea and hills."],["Arnía Beach","A Costa Quebrada cove bristling with rock blades, superb at low tide."]]},"canallave":{"v":"On the edge of the Liencres Dunes natural park, where the Pas meets the sea, Canallave opens the Costa Quebrada, a coast of layered cliffs and islets well known to geologists and Cantabrian surfers alike.","f":["Sobaos pasiegos","Soft butter cakes from the Pas valleys, perfect with a coffee after your session."],"r":"The Liencres dunes are a natural park: stay on the marked paths. Watch out for currents near the Pas estuary and for rocks at low tide.","s":"September to November: consistent autumn swells, water still mild and car parks free of the summer crowds.","a":[["Arnía Beach","A Costa Quebrada cove bristling with rock blades, superb at low tide."],["Altamira Museum","A faithful replica of the cave with its painted bison, plus a museum on life in the Palaeolithic."],["Collegiate Church of Santillana del Mar","A Romanesque collegiate church with a carved cloister, in the heart of a medieval stone village."],["Magdalena Palace","A royal summer palace set on a green peninsula, with a park and views over the bay."],["El Castillo Cave","A cave decorated with hand stencils and signs among the oldest in Europe, visited in small groups."]]},"loredo":{"v":"Facing Mouro Island and the entrance to the Bay of Santander, Loredo continues the Somo sand spit below the meadows of Trasmiera; in summer, surf schools set up among the Cantabrian families.","f":["Santoña anchovies","Bay of Biscay anchovies salted then filleted by hand in the Santoña canneries, on good bread."],"r":"Surf schools take over the shoreline in summer: if you surf independently, look for peaks further along and let beginners learn safely. Cross the dunes on the boardwalks.","s":"September to October: consistent autumn swells, water still mild and a beach freed from the August crowds.","a":[["Magdalena Palace","A royal summer palace set on a green peninsula, with a park and views over the bay."],["Santoña, Victoria and Joyel Marshes","Vast coastal marshes where many migratory birds stop over."],["El Sardinero Beach","Santander’s Belle Époque beach, lined with gardens, villas and a casino."],["Laredo","A sloping medieval old town above a huge golden sand beach."],["Santander Cathedral","A Gothic cathedral built over a medieval crypt, in the heart of the city."]]},"fistral":{"v":"Newquay has lived to the rhythm of surfing for decades: shops, schools and vans line up above Fistral. On the neighbouring headland, the whitewashed Huer’s Hut recalls the days when lookouts watched for shoals of pilchards.","l":"English, Cornish","h":["Dydh da","“hello” in Cornish, the revived Celtic language of Cornwall"],"f":["Cornish pasty","A golden pastry filled with beef, potato, swede and onion, to eat by the sea."],"r":"Fistral is very busy: surf between the black-and-white chequered lifeguard flags, never in the red-and-yellow swimming zone, and respect priority at the peak.","s":"September to November: consistent Atlantic swells, water still mild and a beach free of the summer crowds.","a":[["Bedruthan Steps","Spectacular cliffs and huge rock stacks planted in the sand, seen from the coast path."],["Truro Cathedral","A neo-Gothic cathedral with three spires, in the heart of Cornwall’s only city."],["Trerice","An Elizabethan manor house with carved gables, surrounded by peaceful gardens."],["Penhale Dunes","A vast stretch of wild dunes overlooking Perran Bay, ideal for walking."],["Crantock","A village of slate roofs and a wide beach at the mouth of the Gannel."]]},"polzeath":{"v":"Facing the Camel estuary, Polzeath is a bay for families and surfers where you walk down to the water in your wetsuit straight from the village. The coast path heads off towards Pentire and its Atlantic-battered headlands.","l":"English, Cornish","h":["Dydh da","“hello” in Cornish, the revived Celtic language of Cornwall"],"f":["Cornish crab and lobster","Caught off the north coast and enjoyed in sandwiches or grilled in the nearby harbours."],"r":"At high tide the beach shrinks and the line-up tightens: share the waves with beginners and leave your car in the car parks rather than on the sand.","s":"September to October: clean autumn swells in the bay, water still mild and a quieter village.","a":[["Port Isaac","A fishing harbour of narrow lanes and whitewashed cottages huddled around the quay."],["The Rumps","A twin headland topped by an Iron Age fort, with vast views over the ocean."],["Port Quin","A tiny unspoilt cove tucked into the cliffs, a peaceful stop on the coast path."],["Rock","A village on the Camel estuary, linked to Padstow by a small ferry."],["National Lobster Hatchery, Padstow","A centre that raises young lobsters before releasing them into the sea, open to visitors."]]},"perranporth":{"v":"Legend has it that St Piran, patron saint of tin miners, landed here from Ireland. His black flag with a white cross flies everywhere, and the cliffs, riddled with old tunnels, tell of the coast’s mining past.","l":"English, Cornish","h":["Dydh da","“hello” in Cornish, the revived Celtic language of Cornwall"],"f":["Cornish cream tea","Warm scones, strawberry jam and then clotted cream on top, the Cornish way."],"r":"Rip currents are common on this long beach: surf in the chequered lifeguarded zone, and stay well away from the foot of the cliffs undermined by old mine tunnels.","s":"September to November: frequent Atlantic swells, water still mild and vast stretches almost empty.","a":[["Penhale Dunes","Huge dunes hiding the remains of St Piran’s Oratory."],["St Agnes","A mining village with engine houses perched on the cliffs, overlooked by St Agnes Beacon."],["Holywell Bay","A beach between dunes and cliffs, facing the twin rocks of the Gull Rocks."],["Crantock","A village of slate roofs and a wide beach at the mouth of the Gannel."],["Porthtowan","A small surf village tucked into a valley that runs down to a sandy beach."]]},"gwithian":{"v":"At the far end of St Ives Bay, the Towans dunes face Godrevy Lighthouse, the one that inspired Virginia Woolf. Here you surf with views of seals, cliffs and the light that drew generations of painters.","l":"English, Cornish","h":["Dydh da","“hello” in Cornish, the revived Celtic language of Cornwall"],"f":["Cornish pasty","A golden pastry filled with beef, potato, swede and onion, to take down to the beach."],"r":"Grey seals rest in the coves below Godrevy: watch them quietly from the clifftops, without climbing down or flying a drone, especially during the pupping season.","s":"September to November: autumn swells that fill the bay, water still mild and fewer people in the water.","a":[["St Michael’s Mount","An islet topped by a castle, reached on foot by a causeway at low tide."],["St Ives","A fishing harbour with cobbled lanes, art galleries and beaches bathed in famous light."],["Godrevy Point","Clifftop paths facing the lighthouse, with grey seals in the coves below."],["Leach Pottery","A historic ceramics workshop turned museum, where potters still work."],["Zennor","A granite hamlet between moorland and cliffs, known for the legend of its mermaid."]]},"sidiifni":{"v":"A former Spanish enclave, Sidi Ifni keeps its white-and-blue Art Deco façades, its central square and its lighthouse above the ocean. The town lives slowly, between the fishing port, paragliders above the cliffs and the Atlantic mists.","l":"Tashelhit (Amazigh), Darija, French","h":["Azul","“hello” in Amazigh, the Berber language spoken in the Souss and the Anti-Atlas"],"f":["Amlou","A spread of roasted almonds, argan oil and honey, for breakfast on warm bread."],"r":"In the evening, the beach is Ifni families’ living room: cover up when you leave the sand and, towards Legzira, never climb on the remaining arch, which is fragile.","s":"October to March: consistent Atlantic winter swells, water still pleasant and skies often clear in the afternoon.","lbl":"Guesthouse"},"mirleft":{"v":"A village perched above a string of coves, Mirleft has long drawn travellers, anglers and surfers in search of peace and quiet. Below the ochre cliffs, each beach has its own character and its own wave.","l":"Tashelhit (Amazigh), Darija, French","h":["Azul","“hello” in Amazigh, the Berber language spoken in the Souss and the Anti-Atlas"],"f":["Amlou","A spread of almonds, argan oil and honey, served with bread and mint tea."],"r":"The tracks to the coves cross farmland: stay on the existing tracks, don’t wild camp near houses and take all your rubbish with you.","s":"October to March: well-angled winter swells in the coves, water still mild and a quiet village."},"tamraght":{"v":"Between Aourir and Taghazout, Tamraght is a former Amazigh village that has become a base for surfers from all over the world. Sloping lanes, rooftop terraces and the neighbouring banana groves of Aourir mingle with the surf schools.","l":"Tashelhit (Amazigh), Darija, French","h":["Azul","“hello” in Amazigh, the Berber language spoken in the Souss and the Anti-Atlas"],"f":["Amlou","A spread of almonds, argan oil and honey made in the hinterland, for breakfast."],"r":"The local point breaks are very busy with schools: give priority to whoever is on the peak, and stay discreet and covered up when walking through the village.","s":"October to March: Atlantic winter swells peeling along the points, offshore wind in the morning and water still mild.","a":[["Paradise Valley","Atlas gorges with palm trees, clear rock pools and boulders to jump from."],["Agadir Oufella Kasbah","Restored ramparts on the hilltop, with a view over the whole Bay of Agadir."],["Aourir","A small town nicknamed “Banana Village”, with its fruit stalls and lively souk."]]},"durban":{"v":"Durban is one of the birthplaces of South African surfing: on the Golden Mile, waves peel between the piers in front of the seafront towers. The city blends Zulu and Indian cultures, spice markets and joggers at dawn.","l":"English, Zulu","h":["Sawubona","“I see you”, the Zulu greeting of KwaZulu-Natal"],"f":["Bunny chow","Half a loaf of white bread hollowed out and filled with curry, born in Durban’s Indian community."],"r":"The beaches are protected by shark nets: surf in the lifeguarded sections between the piers, and never leave your things unattended on the sand.","s":"April to August: more consistent south swells, frequent offshore winds in the morning and water that stays warm.","a":[["Juma Mosque","A large mosque with golden minarets, in the heart of the Indian quarter and its markets."],["Durban Botanic Gardens","Africa’s oldest botanical garden still in operation, with rare cycads and orchids."],["uShaka Marine World","A large seafront aquarium set in a reconstructed shipwreck, with sharks and rays."],["Krantzkloof Nature Reserve","Wooded gorges, waterfalls and hiking trails in the hills above the city."]]},"ballito":{"v":"On the Dolphin Coast, Ballito stretches out between sugar cane fields and natural pools carved into the rocks. The resort hosts a stop on the pro tour every year, and local surfers meet here from dawn.","l":"English, Zulu","h":["Sawubona","“I see you”, the Zulu greeting of KwaZulu-Natal"],"f":["Bunny chow","Half a hollowed-out loaf filled with spicy curry, the Indian speciality of the Durban area."],"r":"Only surf in front of beaches with shark nets and lifeguards, and leave the natural pools to swimmers and children.","s":"April to August: consistent south swells, offshore winds in the morning and the warm water of the Indian Ocean.","a":[["Sibudu Cave","A rock shelter where archaeologists have found traces of very ancient human occupation."],["King Shaka Memorial","A monument to the Zulu king Shaka, raised on the site of his grave in KwaDukuza."],["Salt Rock","A small coastal village with a natural pool and rocky coves."],["Shaka’s Rock","A rocky promontory and quiet beaches just north of Ballito."]]},"umhlanga":{"v":"Umhlanga revolves around its red-and-white lighthouse and its seafront promenade. It’s also home to the organisation that manages the coast’s shark nets, an essential part of sea culture in KwaZulu-Natal.","l":"English, Zulu","h":["Sawubona","“I see you”, the Zulu greeting of KwaZulu-Natal"],"f":["Bunny chow","Half a hollowed-out loaf filled with curry, a legacy of Durban’s Indian community."],"r":"Reef and rocks break the surface in front of the promenade: get in and out over the sand, surf in the lifeguarded zone and follow the lifeguards’ instructions.","s":"April to August: more frequent south swells, offshore winds at sunrise and water that’s always warm.","a":[["Umhlanga Lagoon Nature Reserve","Boardwalks through the dune forest to the lagoon and a wild beach."],["Hawaan Forest","A remnant of native coastal forest, rich in birds and indigenous trees."],["Durban","A big port city with Indian markets, a seafront and Zulu cultural life."],["Juma Mosque","A large mosque with golden minarets, in the heart of Durban’s Indian quarter."]]},"umdloti":{"v":"A small holiday village on a rocky point, Umdloti has kept a laid-back feel away from the coast’s tower blocks. Its big natural pool draws families and swimmers, while surfers keep an eye on the nearby sandbanks.","l":"English, Zulu","h":["Sawubona","“I see you”, the Zulu greeting of KwaZulu-Natal"],"f":["Bunny chow","Half a hollowed-out loaf filled with curry, the speciality born among Durban’s Indians."],"r":"Rocks and currents are treacherous around the point: spot the channels from the beach, stay close to the lifeguards and leave the natural pool to swimmers.","s":"April to August: more consistent south swells, offshore winds in the morning and warm water all season.","a":[["Umhlanga Lagoon Nature Reserve","Boardwalks through the dune forest to the lagoon and a wild beach."],["Beachwood Mangroves","A mangrove at the mouth of the Umgeni, with crabs, fish and birds."],["Hawaan Forest","A remnant of native coastal forest, rich in birds and indigenous trees."],["Umhlanga","A seaside resort with a lighthouse, a long promenade and cafés facing the ocean."]]},"capestfrancis":{"v":"Cape St Francis became legendary with the film The Endless Summer and its perfect wave. Around it, the white houses with black thatched roofs of St Francis Bay and the squid-fishing fleet set the tone.","f":["Port St Francis squid","Caught on lines by the local fleet, it’s eaten grilled or fried, freshly landed."],"r":"Seal Point is a powerful reef break surfed by locals: wait your turn, don’t sit on the inside, and respect the penguin rehabilitation centre near the lighthouse.","s":"May to August: big southern winter swells and a westerly wind that often blows offshore on the point.","a":[["Seal Point Lighthouse","A tall white-and-red lighthouse on the cape’s rocks, facing the surf spot."],["Jeffreys Bay","South Africa’s surf capital, famous for the Supertubes wave and its surf shops."],["St Francis Bay","A village of white houses topped with black thatch, criss-crossed by canals."],["Kromme River","A peaceful estuary for paddleboarding, kayaking and birdwatching."],["Oyster Bay","An isolated hamlet on a long beach backed by big dunes."]]},"jaco":{"v":"The closest surf town to San José, Jacó fills up at weekends with Ticos from the Central Valley. Surf schools, sodas (local eateries) and nightlife line a long dark-sand beach, with the forest as a backdrop.","r":"Rip currents are powerful at Jacó: check the signposted zones before going in, and leave nothing valuable on the sand or in the car.","s":"April to October: consistent south swells, mornings often windless before the afternoon showers.","a":[["Carara National Park","A transitional forest where you can spot scarlet macaws, monkeys and tropical birds."],["Tárcoles River","A river famous for its huge crocodiles, visible from the bridge on the coast road."],["Playa Herradura","A sheltered horseshoe bay, calmer for swimming and paddleboarding."]]},"dominical":{"v":"On the Costa Ballena, Dominical is still a surfers’ village with dirt roads, where the jungle comes right down to the sand. Offshore, humpback whales pass by every year, and travellers often stay longer than planned.","r":"Dominical’s beach break is powerful and its currents are strong: don’t go out if you’re a beginner, and follow the local surfers’ advice on the channels.","s":"April to October: powerful, consistent south swells, warm water and mornings often without wind.","a":[["Uvita","Gateway to Ballena Marine National Park, with its sand spit shaped like a whale’s tail."],["Hacienda Barú Wildlife Refuge","Rainforest trails, mangrove and a wild beach, rich in birds and monkeys."]]},"playagrande":{"v":"Playa Grande is above all the beach of the leatherback turtles, protected by a national park that has held back building. Houses stay hidden behind the vegetation, and the Guanacaste of the sabaneros is never far away.","f":["Guanacaste rosquillas","Small crunchy rings of corn and cheese, to nibble with a black coffee."],"r":"During nesting season, the national park beach closes at night: don’t go there after sunset, and never with a torch or flash, unless you’re with an accredited guide.","s":"April to August: consistent south swells on this exposed beach break, mornings often calm and a less crowded line-up.","a":[["Las Baulas National Marine Park","Leatherback turtle nesting beaches, to be visited at night only with a guide."],["Tamarindo Estuary","A mangrove to explore by kayak or boat, with crocodiles and water birds."],["Playa Conchal","A beach made of tiny crushed shells, with clear turquoise water."],["Playa Negra","A rocky beach known for its reef right-hander, made famous by surf films."]]},"langosta":{"v":"Just south of Tamarindo, Langosta keeps a quieter feel, between a rocky point and the mouth of a small river. Its beach is part of the turtles’ protected area, and evenings are spent watching the sun sink into the Pacific.","f":["Guanacaste rosquillas","Small baked rings of corn and cheese, a classic of the province’s bakeries."],"r":"The beach is a protected nesting site: no fires or torches at night, and never touch the nests or the baby turtles heading for the sea.","s":"April to August: well-angled south swells on the point and the river mouth, mornings often without wind.","a":[["Las Baulas National Marine Park","Leatherback turtle nesting beaches, to be visited at night only with a guide."],["Tamarindo Estuary","A mangrove to explore by kayak or boat, with crocodiles and water birds."],["Playa Conchal","A beach made of tiny crushed shells, with clear turquoise water."],["Playa Negra","A rocky beach known for its reef right-hander, made famous by surf films."]]},"avellanas":{"v":"At the end of dusty tracks, Avellanas lines up several sand and reef peaks along a tree-lined beach. There’s no town here: a few houses, beach restaurants and the rural spirit of Guanacaste, between cows and dry forest.","f":["Guanacaste rosquillas","Small crunchy rings of corn and cheese, sold in the village bakeries."],"r":"The reef peaks are surfed by a close-knit local community: wait your turn, respect priority and drive slowly on the tracks so you don’t cover the houses in dust.","s":"April to August: consistent south swells on the reefs and sandbanks, with mornings that are often glassy.","a":[["Playa Negra","A rocky beach known for its reef right-hander, made famous by surf films."],["Las Baulas National Marine Park","Leatherback turtle nesting beaches, to be visited at night only with a guide."],["Tamarindo Estuary","A mangrove to explore by kayak or boat, with crocodiles and water birds."]]},"junquillal":{"v":"A long, wild and barely built-up beach, Junquillal attracts those fleeing the bustle of Tamarindo. Turtles come here to nest, and life follows the rhythm of the farms and tracks of rural Guanacaste.","f":["Guanacaste rosquillas","Small rings of corn and cheese, best with a Costa Rican coffee."],"r":"Turtles nest on this beach: avoid any light at night, keep your distance from the marked nests and take your rubbish with you, as there are hardly any bins.","s":"April to August: consistent south swells on an open beach break, warm water and an almost empty beach.","a":[["Playa Negra","A rocky beach known for its reef right-hander, made famous by surf films."],["Las Baulas National Marine Park","Leatherback turtle nesting beaches, to be visited at night only with a guide."]]},"nosara":{"v":"Nosara grew up away from the main roads: forest separates the houses, the tracks are still dirt and yoga studios sit alongside surf schools. The Nicoya Peninsula is also known for the longevity of its inhabitants.","r":"The village tracks are dusty and shared with pedestrians and cyclists: drive at walking pace, and don’t disturb the howler monkeys that live in the trees above the beach.","s":"April to October: consistent south swells on the Guiones beach break, warm water and mornings often without wind.","a":[["Ostional Wildlife Refuge","The beach of the arribadas, mass nestings of olive ridley turtles on certain nights."],["Nosara River","A mangrove-lined estuary to paddle up by kayak for birdwatching."],["Isla Chora","A protected islet in Sámara Bay, reachable by kayak from the beach."]]},"esterillos":{"v":"Between Jacó and Quepos, Esterillos unrolls long, almost empty beaches lined with oil palms and small farms. People come here to surf without crowds and live to the slow rhythm of the Central Pacific.","r":"The beaches are rarely lifeguarded and the currents are strong: never surf alone, and keep a low profile near the homes of the families who live here all year round.","s":"April to October: consistent south swells on the sandbanks, warm water and deserted beaches on weekdays."},"bejuco":{"v":"A grey-sand beach between Jacó and Quepos, Bejuco draws surfers looking for consistent waves away from the bustle. Here the coast road runs through palm plantations and peaceful hamlets.","r":"A quiet beach with powerful currents: don’t surf alone, and respect the turtles that come to nest on the neighbouring coast.","s":"April to October: powerful, consistent south swells on the beach break, warm water and few people.","a":[["Playa Hermosa-Punta Mala Wildlife Refuge","A protected beach where sea turtles come to nest, near a renowned surf spot."],["La Cangreja National Park","Little-visited hill forest, with trails and rivers in the hinterland."]]},"huntington":{"v":"Huntington Beach claims the nickname “Surf City USA”: around the pier, the Duke Kahanamoku statue, legends’ handprints on the pavement and major contests set the town’s rhythm. Over at the State Beach, campfires light up the evenings on the sand.","r":"At the mouth of the Santa Ana, a fenced area protects the nests of terns and plovers: never cross it, and only light fires in the designated fire rings.","s":"September to October: the last south swells, more frequent offshore winds, water still warm and less crowded beaches.","a":[["Huntington Beach Pier","A long historic pier from which you can watch surfers riding on either side."],["Bolsa Chica Ecological Reserve","Coastal wetlands crossed by trails, rich in migratory birds."],["Newport Beach","A marina, the Balboa Peninsula and surf beaches south of Huntington."],["Balboa Pavilion","A Victorian wooden pavilion on the harbour, departure point for ferries and boat trips."]],"lbl":"Motel"},"oceanbeachsf":{"v":"Ocean Beach is San Francisco’s cold, uncompromising surf: fog, a thick wetsuit all year round and long paddles against the sandbars. Surfers from the Sunset and the Richmond form a tough, low-key community here.","f":["Clam chowder in a sourdough bowl","A creamy clam soup served in a sourdough loaf, the city’s signature dish."],"r":"Rip currents here are formidable and the beach isn’t lifeguarded the way others are: don’t go without solid experience, and keep dogs away from the plovers in the dunes.","s":"September to November: more frequent offshore winds, less fog and the first clean north-west swells.","a":[["Lands End","A clifftop trail through cypresses and the ruins of the Sutro Baths, with views of the Golden Gate."],["Golden Gate Park","A huge urban park of lakes, gardens, museums and windmills that runs all the way to the beach."],["Japanese Tea Garden","A landscaped garden with a pagoda, an arched bridge and a teahouse, in the heart of Golden Gate Park."],["Sutro Heights","A former estate perched on the cliff, with a clear view over Ocean Beach."],["Mori Point","A Pacifica headland with ridge trails, facing the ocean."]]},"steamerlane":{"v":"Santa Cruz is one of the birthplaces of surfing on the American mainland: Hawaiian princes are said to have ridden waves here as early as the late 19th century, and Jack O’Neill developed his wetsuits here. Below the lighthouse, Steamer Lane peels at the foot of the cliffs.","r":"Steamer Lane is a very localised, rocky peak: only go out if you can handle the entry from the cliffs, respect priority scrupulously and keep your distance from the sea otters.","s":"September to November: the first north-west swells, more frequent offshore winds and less fog than in summer.","a":[["Santa Cruz Surfing Museum","A small museum set in the lighthouse, telling the story of local surfing."],["Natural Bridges","A natural arch in the sea, rock pools at low tide and a grove where monarch butterflies overwinter."],["Henry Cowell Redwoods State Park","Trails beneath giant coast redwoods, along the San Lorenzo River."],["Santa Cruz Beach Boardwalk","A historic beachfront amusement park, with the Giant Dipper wooden roller coaster."],["Wilder Ranch","A former ranch with trails along wild cliffs and unspoilt coves."]]},"lapuntilla":{"v":"A chic seaside resort from the early 20th century, with its old casino and palm park, Pichilemu has become Chile’s surf capital: schools, boards and fishing boats share the point.","r":"La Puntilla hosts the surf schools: give priority to locals at the peak and note the town’s tsunami evacuation signs.","s":"March to May: consistent southern swells, wind often calm in the morning and beaches emptied after the Chilean summer.","a":[["Cáhuil","A lagoon-side village known for its traditional salt pans and sea salt."],["Agustín Ross Cultural Centre","The old Ross casino, a Belle Époque building converted into a cultural centre."],["Ross Park","A garden of palms and clipped hedges facing the ocean, the historic heart of the resort."],["Cerro La Cruz","The town’s highest point, topped by a cross, with views over the bay and the points."],["Petrel Lagoon","A coastal lagoon behind the beach, frequented by water birds."]]},"infiernillo":{"v":"At the foot of Cerro La Cruz, Infiernillo shows Pichilemu’s other face: a more exposed, less urban beach, where you come to surf away from the schools and watch the sun sink into the Pacific.","r":"The current is strong and the beach rarely lifeguarded: don’t surf alone and follow the tsunami evacuation signs to higher ground.","s":"March to May: consistent southern swells, wind often calm in the morning and beaches emptied after the Chilean summer.","a":[["Cáhuil","A lagoon-side village known for its traditional salt pans and sea salt."],["Cerro La Cruz","The town’s highest point, topped by a cross, with views over the bay and the points."],["Agustín Ross Cultural Centre","The old Ross casino, a Belle Époque building converted into a cultural centre."],["Ross Park","A garden of palms and clipped hedges facing the ocean, the historic heart of the resort."]]},"matanzas":{"v":"A small fishing village in the commune of Navidad, Matanzas has seen windsurfers and kitesurfers arrive, drawn by the south-westerly afternoon wind. Here the dry hills plunge straight into the ocean.","r":"On the shore, the fishermen’s boats have priority: rig well away from their caleta and avoid sailing among swimmers in summer.","s":"March to May: consistent southern swells, windless mornings for surfing and a calm beach after summer.","a":[["Rapel River","A river whose mouth forms a dune-lined lagoon, good for birdwatching."],["Navidad Formation","Sea cliffs rich in fossils, a true geological archive of the coast."]],"lbl":"Campsite or cabaña"},"puertecillo":{"v":"At the end of a long track through the hills, Puertecillo is still an isolated hamlet of huts and surf camps, with no town around it: people come for its left-hander and the silence of the coast.","r":"Services are almost non-existent: bring water and food, take all your rubbish with you and respect the private land the track crosses.","s":"March to May: consistent southern swells, wind often calm in the morning and a track more passable than in winter."},"renaca":{"v":"Viña del Mar’s iconic beach, Reñaca lines up its stepped apartment blocks clinging to the hillside. In summer, Santiago’s youth gather here; the rest of the year, surfers and bodyboarders take the beach back.","r":"In summer, swimming is supervised by lifeguards: surf outside the marked zones and give priority to local bodyboarders at the peaks.","s":"April to September: more consistent south-west swells and a beach handed back to locals outside summer.","a":[["Quinta Vergara","A wooded park home to the Vergara Palace and the amphitheatre of the Viña festival."],["Ascensor Polanco","A vertical lift in Valparaíso cut into the hillside, with views over the city."],["Wulff Castle","A small 1906 castle set on the rocks, facing the waves of Viña del Mar."],["Lago Peñuelas National Reserve","Forests and trails around a reservoir lake, a bird refuge near the coast."],["Concón","A neighbouring resort known for its dunes and seafood restaurants."]]},"elabanico":{"v":"Maitencillo stretches out below cliffs from which paragliders take off. A resort of second homes and cabañas, it keeps a family-friendly, sporty feel, far from the concrete of Viña del Mar.","r":"El Abanico breaks close to the rocks: watch the line-up before going in and park in the designated spaces, not in front of residents’ entrances.","s":"April to September: consistent south-west swells and quiet beaches outside the summer season.","a":[["Zapallar","An elegant resort with villas nestled in greenery, and a coastal path over the rocks."],["Church of Our Lady of Mercy, Papudo","A church listed as a historic monument in the seaside town of Papudo."],["Papudo","A small seaside port at the head of a bay, with its beaches and seafront."]],"lbl":"Cabaña"},"canggu":{"v":"Between rice fields nibbled away by cafés and black-sand beaches, Canggu blends an international surf scene with Balinese village life: on Batu Bolong beach, a small temple still receives offerings and ceremonies.","l":"Indonesian, Balinese","h":["Om Swastiastu","a Balinese Hindu greeting that wishes peace and blessings"],"f":["Babi guling","Suckling pig roasted with Balinese spices, served with rice and spicy vegetables."],"r":"Ceremonies take place on the beach near Batu Bolong temple: stay back, and at the peak let Balinese surfers go first.","s":"April to October: dry season, south-east trade winds blowing offshore on the west coast and consistent southern swells.","a":[["Taman Ayun Temple","Mengwi’s royal temple surrounded by moats, with tiered meru pagodas."],["Uluwatu Temple","A sea temple perched on a cliff, famous for its kecak dance at sunset."],["Bali Museum","Palace-style pavilions showcasing Balinese art, costumes and ritual objects."],["Batuan","A village renowned for its meticulous painting style and its carved temple."],["Le Mayeur Museum","The Sanur home of Belgian painter Le Mayeur, with his canvases and Balinese objects."]]},"kuta":{"v":"A former fishing village turned Bali’s tourist heart, Kuta is the beach where generations of travellers caught their first wave. At sunset, schools, vendors and Balinese families take over the sand.","l":"Indonesian, Balinese","h":["Om Swastiastu","a Balinese Hindu greeting that wishes peace and blessings"],"f":["Babi guling","Suckling pig roasted with Balinese spices, served with rice and spicy vegetables."],"r":"Surf in the zones marked out by lifeguards, away from the schools. On Nyepi, the Balinese New Year, the whole island observes silence: nobody on the beach.","s":"April to October: dry season and a south-easterly wind blowing offshore on the west coast beach breaks.","a":[["Jimbaran","A fishing village known for its fish market and its beach barbecues."],["Bali Museum","Palace-style pavilions showcasing Balinese art, costumes and ritual objects."],["Sakenan Temple","A Hindu temple on Serangan Island, a major pilgrimage site during the great festivals."],["Le Mayeur Museum","The Sanur home of Belgian painter Le Mayeur, with his canvases and Balinese objects."],["Garuda Wisnu Kencana Cultural Park","A park on the Bukit dominated by a giant statue of Vishnu riding Garuda."]]},"medewi":{"v":"Far from the bustle of the south, Medewi is a village in west Bali where the rice fields almost touch the pebble beach. Fishermen haul their outrigger canoes up the sand here, next to the surfers.","l":"Indonesian, Balinese","h":["Om Swastiastu","a Balinese Hindu greeting that wishes peace and blessings"],"r":"The bottom is made of pebbles and rocks: bring booties, and make room for the fishermen launching their canoes.","s":"April to October: dry season, offshore wind in the morning and southern swells that make the left peel.","a":[["Rambut Siwi Temple","A sea temple perched above the ocean, linked to the priest Nirartha."],["Perancak Temple","A sea temple marking the spot where Nirartha is said to have landed in Bali."],["Negara","The capital of Jembrana, known for its buffalo cart races, the makepung."]],"lbl":"Homestay"},"selongbelanak":{"v":"A big white-sand bay ringed by hills, Selong Belanak lives to the rhythm of the Sasak villages: at the end of the day, buffalo cross the beach while the last students come out of the water.","l":"Indonesian, Sasak","f":["Ayam taliwang and plecing kangkung","Fiery grilled chicken and water spinach with sambal, two Lombok classics."],"r":"Lombok is mostly Muslim: cover your shoulders and knees away from the beach, especially in the villages and during Ramadan.","s":"April to October: dry season, offshore trade winds and southern swells that roll gently into the bay.","lbl":"Homestay"},"kohtao":{"v":"“Turtle Island” has become one of the world’s great dive training centres. Between coconut groves, granite coves and dive boats, island life revolves around the morning trips.","r":"Several of the island’s clubs are restoring the coral: keep your fins away from the reef, don’t feed the fish and don’t pick up shells or coral.","s":"March to September: calm sea and good visibility; from October to December, the monsoon churns up the gulf."},"kohlipe":{"v":"In the heart of Tarutao Marine Park, Ko Lipe is home to a village of the Urak Lawoi, sea nomads who have long lived by fishing. Longtail boats link the reefs of the neighbouring islets.","r":"You’re in a national marine park: pay the entrance fees, never walk on the coral at low tide and respect the Urak Lawoi village.","s":"November to April: calm Andaman Sea and crystal-clear water; during the monsoon, boats become scarce.","lbl":"Bungalow"},"moalboal":{"v":"Panagsama is just one street of dive clubs and simple eateries along the reef, but a few metres from shore, huge schools of sardines swirl. Fishermen’s bangkas still set off from the shore every morning.","l":"Filipino, Cebuano, English","h":["Maayong buntag","“good morning” in Cebuano, the language of the Visayas"],"f":["Cebu lechon","Roast suckling pig with crispy skin, stuffed with lemongrass, the island’s pride."],"r":"Don’t chase the sardine school and let the turtles surface to breathe: no touching, and pay the small municipal reef protection fee.","s":"December to May: dry season, calm sea and good visibility; the sardines are there all year round.","a":[["Pescador Island","A protected limestone islet ringed by coral walls teeming with fish."],["Kawasan Falls","Tiered waterfalls with turquoise pools, deep in the jungle above Badian."],["Simala Shrine","A castle-like monastery, Cebu’s great Marian pilgrimage site."]]},"malapascua":{"v":"With no cars or tarmac roads, Malapascua lives between fishing villages and dive clubs. Before dawn, bangkas head out to a shoal where thresher sharks come to be cleaned.","l":"Filipino, Cebuano, English","h":["Maayong buntag","“good morning” in Cebuano, the language of the Visayas"],"f":["Cebu lechon","Roast suckling pig with crispy skin, stuffed with lemongrass, the island’s pride."],"r":"With thresher sharks, no flash and no torch pointed at them: stay behind the line set by the guides and don’t approach the cleaning station.","s":"March to May: calm sea and visibility at its best; thresher sharks appear at dawn all year round."},"apoisland":{"v":"A small volcanic fishing island, Apo was one of the first marine reserves managed by the community itself, with support from Silliman University. The result: intact corals and turtles with every fin kick.","l":"Filipino, Cebuano, English","h":["Maayong buntag","“good morning” in Cebuano, the language of the Visayas"],"r":"The sanctuary is managed by the islanders: pay the fee on arrival, stay out of the no-go zone and keep your distance from the turtles.","s":"March to May: calm sea, warm water and excellent visibility, before the monsoon winds arrive.","a":[["Dauin","A coastal village with marine sanctuaries renowned for black-sand macro diving."],["Dumaguete","A lively university town with its seafront promenade, Rizal Boulevard."],["Dumaguete Cathedral","St Catherine’s Cathedral, flanked by an old stone bell tower."],["Silliman Hall","An American-style wooden building, now Silliman University’s museum."]]},"coron":{"v":"Coron Bay holds the wrecks of a Japanese fleet sunk in 1944. Above the water, the limestone cliffs of Coron Island belong to the ancestral domain of the Tagbanwa, who guard its lakes.","r":"Take nothing from the wrecks and only go inside them with the right training. On Coron Island, follow the rules set by the Tagbanwa.","s":"November to May: dry season, calm sea in the bay and the best visibility on the wrecks.","a":[["Kayangan Lake","A brackish lake with crystal-clear water nestled between cliffs, managed by the Tagbanwa."],["Coron Island","A jagged karst island with turquoise lagoons and hidden lakes."]]},"bondi":{"v":"Bondi saw the birth of one of Australia’s first lifesaving clubs, and its lifesavers in red and yellow are part of the scenery. Between the old pavilion and the seawater pool cut into the rocks, Sydney comes to swim, surf and run.","r":"Surfers have their own zone, marked separately from the swimming flags: respect it, and beware of the powerful rip current at the south end of the beach.","s":"March to June: more consistent south and east swells, offshore westerlies in the morning and water still warm.","a":[["Kamay Botany Bay National Park","Headlands and coastal trails around Cook’s 1770 landing site."],["Bare Island","A fortified islet at La Perouse linked by a footbridge, popular with divers."],["Malabar Headland","A former military site turned natural area, with a clifftop trail."],["Cronulla Sand Dunes","A vast dune field south of Botany Bay, the last of its kind in Sydney."]]},"freshwater":{"v":"This is where Hawaiian Duke Kahanamoku gave a legendary surfing demonstration in the summer of 1914–1915: his board is kept at the surf lifesaving club and his statue watches over the headland.","r":"The bay is small and the line-up close-knit: wait your turn at the peak and leave the area between the flags to swimmers and families.","s":"March to June: consistent south and east swells, offshore westerlies in the morning and water still warm.","a":[["North Head Quarantine Station","Historic buildings and passenger graffiti, on a headland facing the harbour."],["Long Reef","A headland joined by a tombolo, with protected rock platforms and seabirds."],["Barrenjoey Lighthouse","A sandstone lighthouse at the tip of Palm Beach, reached by a trail with panoramic views."],["Manly Dam Reserve","Bushland and a reservoir surrounded by walking and mountain-bike trails."],["Middle Head Fortifications","19th-century batteries and tunnels at the entrance to Sydney Harbour."]]},"noosa":{"v":"Recognised as a “World Surfing Reserve”, Noosa is a temple of longboarding: its right-handers wrap along the national park, in front of the pandanus trees. Longboard surf culture still reigns here.","r":"On the crowded points, paddle around the line-up rather than through the wave’s path, and don’t cut in front of those waiting. Leave the park’s koalas in peace.","s":"February to May: cyclone and south-east swells that light up the points, with water still warm.","a":[["Noosa National Park","A coastal trail between coves and forest, where you may meet koalas and dolphins."],["Noosa Biosphere Reserve","The lakes and meanders of the Noosa Everglades, to explore by canoe."],["Teewah Beach","A long wild beach below cliffs of coloured sands, reached by 4WD."],["Mount Coolum National Park","A steep climb to a summit with views over the whole Sunshine Coast."]]},"cottesloe":{"v":"Perth’s iconic beach, Mudurup in Noongar, Cottesloe brings together swimmers, surfers and sunset crowds on the Indian Ocean. Every year, the Sculpture by the Sea exhibition sets up its works on the sand.","r":"A very busy swimming beach: surf away from swimmers and get out of the water straight away when the shark alarm sounds.","s":"May to September: stronger southern winter swells and mornings without the sea breeze, the “Fremantle Doctor”.","a":[["Rottnest Island","A car-free island of turquoise coves, to explore by bike among the quokkas."],["Point Walter","A large sandbar reaching out into the Swan River, good for swimming."],["Leighton Battery","A Second World War coastal battery and its tunnels, on Buckland Hill."],["Aquarium of Western Australia","An underwater tunnel and tanks showcasing the wildlife of the state’s coasts."]]},"whangamata":{"v":"A resort of baches, the Kiwi holiday homes, Whangamatā lives to the rhythm of its bar, a renowned left at the mouth of the estuary. In summer the little town fills up; the rest of the year, surfers and anglers share it.","r":"On the bar, wait your turn behind the locals. On the beaches, walk around the marked nesting areas of the New Zealand dotterels.","s":"February to May: cyclone and east swells, water still warm and beaches free of holidaymakers.","a":[["Waihi","A former gold town whose huge Martha Mine pit can be seen from a lookout."],["Opoutere","A wild pine-fringed beach and protected sand spit, a refuge for shorebirds."],["Slipper Island","An island off the coast, popular with boaters and anglers."],["Tairua","An estuary village overlooked by Mount Paku, which you can climb for views of the coast."]]},"mountmaunganui":{"v":"At the foot of Mauao, an extinct volcano sacred to the iwi of Tauranga, “the Mount” blends Kiwi beach culture, a surf lifesaving club and joggers circling the mountain at dawn.","r":"Mauao is an ancestral Māori site: stay on the marked trails. At the beach, surf outside the lifeguarded swimming flags.","s":"March to June: more consistent east and north-east swells, frequent offshore winds and a quiet beach after summer.","a":[["Mauao","A volcanic dome to climb or walk around, with views over the Bay of Plenty."],["Moturiki Island","A rocky islet linked to the beach, with a blowhole that roars in big swells."],["Tauranga","A harbour city on the lagoon, with a lively waterfront and markets."],["Classic Flyers Museum","A museum of vintage aircraft at Tauranga airport."]]},"kealakekua":{"v":"A sacred bay on the Kona coast, Kealakekua is home to Hikiau temple and the white obelisk marking the spot where Captain Cook was killed in 1779. Spinner dolphins come to rest here above the protected reef.","l":"English, Hawaiian","h":["Aloha","“hello”, “love” and “goodbye” in Hawaiian"],"f":["Kona coffee","Grown on the volcanic slopes above the bay, to taste at the growers’ farms."],"r":"Stay more than 45 metres from the dolphins, as federal rules require. Paddling to the Cook monument requires a permit: check before you go.","s":"April to October: generally calm sea on the Kona side and crystal-clear water in the morning, before the afternoon wind.","a":[["Puʻuhonua o Hōnaunau National Historical Park","An ancient Hawaiian place of refuge, with a reconstructed temple and carved wooden tiki statues."],["Painted Church","A small wooden church whose interior walls are entirely painted with biblical scenes."],["Kona Coffee Living History Farm","A restored coffee farm telling the story of Japanese farming families."],["Kahaluʻu Bay","A shallow bay protected by a breakwater, ideal for swimming among turtles."],["Huliheʻe Palace","A former summer residence of Hawaiian royalty, now a museum in Kailua-Kona."]]},"poipu":{"v":"On the sunny south coast of Kauaʻi, land of Kōloa’s old sugar plantations, Poʻipū sees green turtles and Hawaiian monk seals come to rest on the sand, next to the remains of an ancient Hawaiian village.","l":"English, Hawaiian","h":["Aloha","“hello”, “love” and “goodbye” in Hawaiian"],"f":["Poke","Cubes of raw fish marinated in soy sauce and sesame oil, a Hawaiian speciality."],"r":"If a monk seal is sleeping on the beach, stay well back behind the marked-off area. Hawaii bans sunscreens containing oxybenzone: choose a mineral sunscreen.","s":"November to March: the south coast stays calm while the north takes the winter swells, ideal for snorkelling.","a":[["Spouting Horn","A lava blowhole that shoots up plumes of water with every wave."],["Allerton Garden","A tropical garden in a valley running down to the sea, by guided tour."],["Māhāʻulepū Beach","A wild coast of fossilised dunes and coves, followed by a coastal trail."],["Makauwahi Cave","Hawaii’s largest limestone cave, a true archive of vanished wildlife."],["Hanapēpē","A small town of colourful wooden façades, known for its art galleries and swinging bridge."]],"lbl":"Park campsite (permit)"},"labaule":{"v":"A seaside resort born with the railway, La Baule lines up Belle Époque villas and pines behind its long beach, a stone’s throw from the salt workers who harvest Guérande salt.","f":["Guérande fleur de sel","Harvested by hand by the salt workers, it lifts fish, galettes and caramels."],"r":"In summer, launch and come back in through the marked channels: the strip along the shore is reserved for swimmers.","s":"June to September: a sheltered bay, often calm sea in the morning and the water at its mildest.","a":[["Guérande salt marshes","A mosaic of salt pans where the salt workers harvest salt the traditional way."],["Medieval town of Guérande","Complete ramparts, fortified gates and cobbled lanes around the collegiate church."],["Wild coast of the Guérande peninsula","A coastal path between rocks, coves and sea caves."],["Le Croisic","A fishing port with shipowners’ houses and quays buzzing at the fish auction."],["Saint-Nazaire submarine base","A Second World War bunker turned into a place for visits and exhibitions."]]},"hendaye":{"v":"A border town set on the Bidassoa bay, Hendaye lives on Basque time: a shuttle boat to Hondarribia, pelota at the fronton and a gentle wave where many learned to surf.","l":"French, Basque","h":["Kaixo","“hi” in Basque, said with a smile"],"f":["Gâteau basque","Shortcrust pastry filled with pastry cream or black cherry jam."],"r":"The beach hosts lots of schools: give beginners space and surf away from the lifeguarded swimming areas.","s":"September to November: more consistent autumn swells, still-mild water and a quieter line-up.","a":[["Château d’Abbadia","The neo-Gothic castle of a scholar and explorer, perched on the cliff road."],["Hondarribia (Fontarrabie)","A walled old town with colourful balconies, reached by the bay shuttle boat."],["San Sebastián","La Concha bay, the old town and renowned pintxos bars."],["Mount Jaizkibel","A coastal ridge with sculpted sandstone cliffs, a fine hike facing the ocean."],["Sare","A Basque village of white houses with red shutters, at the foot of La Rhune."]]},"morgat":{"v":"A former sardine port turned seaside resort, Morgat nestles at the head of its bay; the cliffs of coloured rock and the sea caves reveal themselves from water level.","l":"French, Breton","h":["Demat","“hello” in Breton, always well received"],"f":["Kouign-amann","Born in Douarnenez, just across the bay, this flaky cake oozes butter and sugar."],"r":"Only enter the caves when the sea is calm and take no rocks: the coastline is protected for its geology.","s":"May to September: a bay sheltered from westerly winds, often calm sea and long days for following the cliffs.","a":[["Pointe de Pen-Hir","Dizzying cliffs facing the Tas de Pois rocks, at the tip of the peninsula."],["Cap de la Chèvre","Wild heathland and a coastal path above the Atlantic."],["Camaret-sur-Mer","A crayfish port with a shingle spit, a chapel and a listed Vauban tower."],["Ménez Hom","A heathland summit with a 360° view over the Bay of Douarnenez."],["Landévennec Abbey","Remains of a medieval abbey and a museum on the banks of the Aulne."]]},"saintjeandeluz":{"v":"A tuna port of red and green half-timbered houses, Saint-Jean-de-Luz saw the wedding of Louis XIV; its bay, closed off by sea walls, offers calm water right in the heart of town.","l":"French, Basque","h":["Kaixo","“hi” in Basque, said with a smile"],"f":["Ttoro","A fish and shellfish soup handed down from the town’s fishermen."],"r":"On a paddleboard, keep clear of the harbour channel used by fishing boats, and of the lifeguarded swimming areas.","s":"June to September: the bay, protected by its sea walls, stays calm and the water reaches its mildest.","a":[["Ciboure","Colourful quays, Ravel’s birthplace and the Socoa fort facing the bay."],["Espelette","Façades hung with drying chillies and shops selling Basque produce."],["Château d’Abbadia","The neo-Gothic castle of a scholar and explorer, on the Hendaye cliff road."],["Ascain","A Basque village at the foot of La Rhune, with a fronton and an old bridge."],["Guéthary","A former whaling port with understated charm, known for its big wave."]]},"bourget":{"v":"France’s largest natural lake, sung by Lamartine, Le Bourget brings together lavaret fishermen, a spa town and a lakeside abbey, beneath the cliff of Mont du Chat.","f":["Lavaret fillet","A white fish caught in the lake, served meunière by the water."],"r":"Steer round the reed beds along the shore, which are protected nesting areas, and keep an eye on the wind, which can quickly whip up a chop.","s":"June to September: pleasant water for swimming, calm mornings before the afternoon breeze.","a":[["Hautecombe Abbey","A lakeside abbey, burial place of the princes of the House of Savoy."],["Mont Revard","A balcony over the Bauges with a plunging view of the lake and Mont Blanc."],["Aix-les-Bains","A spa town with Belle Époque grand hotels and Roman remains."],["Chambéry","An old town, the Elephant Fountain and the castle of the Dukes of Savoy."],["Bauges Regional Nature Park","Alpine pastures, forests and hiking trails in an unspoilt massif."]]},"serreponcon":{"v":"Created by a dam on the Durance, the lake swallowed the old villages of Ubaye and Savines; the Saint-Michel chapel rises from its islet, facing the peaks.","f":["Tourtons","Small filled fritters, with potato or prunes, typical of the Hautes-Alpes."],"r":"The lake level varies with the running of the dam: avoid exposed muddy banks and pick out your exit point in advance.","s":"June to early September: a full lake, pleasant water and a lighter thermal breeze in the morning.","a":[["Boscodon Abbey","A restored Romanesque abbey in a wooded valley above the lake."],["Boscodon Forest","A great forest of larch and fir, with trails up to the Morgon cirque."],["Embrun","A small town perched on its rock, with old lanes and the Tour Brune."],["Embrun Cathedral","A Romanesque cathedral of two-tone stone with a lion porch."],["Savines-le-Lac","A village rebuilt on the shore, the starting point for boat trips."]]},"saintecroix":{"v":"A reservoir lake created on the Verdon, which drowned the old village of Les Salles, rebuilt higher up; its turquoise waters border a land of lavender and faience.","f":["Lavender honey","Harvested beside the fields of the Valensole plateau, to buy at the markets."],"r":"On the park’s shores, no fires and no wild camping; in the lower gorges, paddle in single file on your right and don’t stop in the narrow passages.","s":"June to September: pleasant turquoise water; set off early to avoid the crowds and the afternoon wind.","a":[["Verdon Gorge","A spectacular canyon of limestone cliffs, viewpoints and trails."],["Moustiers-Sainte-Marie","A village of faience makers clinging to the cliff, beneath its hanging star."],["Valensole plateau","Lavender fields in bloom in early summer, among almond trees and wheat."],["Aiguines","A hilltop village with a glazed-tile castle and a view over the whole lake."],["Ancient remains of Riez","Columns of a Roman temple and an early Christian baptistery."]]},"donnant":{"v":"On the wild coast where Monet painted the needles of Port-Coton, Donnant tucks its beach between dunes and cliffs; the island’s surf community gathers here as soon as a westerly swell rolls in.","l":"French, Breton","h":["Demat","“hello” in Breton, always well received"],"f":["Crêpes and buckwheat galettes","A galette complète or a salted-butter crêpe, with a bowl of cider."],"r":"The Donnant dunes are protected: use the marked access paths, and beware of strong currents near the rocks.","s":"September to November: consistent westerly swells, still-mild water and a calmer island after summer.","a":[["Pointe des Poulains","A wild headland pounded by the swell, with a lighthouse and flowering heath."],["Vauban Citadel at Le Palais","A star-shaped fortress overlooking the harbour, with ramparts and a museum."],["Sauzon","A small harbour of colourful houses, tucked at the end of its ria."],["Goulphar Lighthouse","The great lighthouse of the wild coast, near the needles of Port-Coton."],["Fort Sarah-Bernhardt","A small fort where the great tragic actress used to stay, facing the ocean."]]},"laconcha":{"v":"A Basque city of Belle Époque elegance, Donostia lives facing its bay: year-round swimming, rowing clubs and trainera regattas that bring the whole city together in September.","l":"Spanish, Basque","h":["Kaixo","“hi” in Basque, said with a smile"],"f":["Pintxos","Small bites on bread, to graze on from bar to bar in the old town."],"r":"Launch outside the marked swimming areas; at the pintxos bar, say what you had when you pay.","s":"June to September: a bay sheltered by Santa Clara Island, warmer water and a sea often glassy in the morning.","a":[["Mount Urgull and La Mota Castle","A wooded hill and fortress with views over the bay and the city."],["San Telmo Museum","A museum of Basque society in a former convent in the old town."],["Santa Clara Island","A small island in the middle of the bay, reachable by boat in summer."],["Getaria","A fishing port of fish grills, home town of Elcano and Balenciaga."],["Mount Ulía","A wooded coastal path between Donostia and Pasaia, above the cliffs."]]},"macarella":{"v":"On an island designated a biosphere reserve, Macarella reveals its white sand at the end of a pine-filled ravine; all around, navetas and talayots recall a unique prehistory.","l":"Spanish, Catalan","h":["Bon dia","“good morning” in Catalan, the island’s language"],"f":["Mahón cheese","A cow’s milk cheese with an orange rind, mild or aged, the emblem of the island."],"r":"In summer, road access is restricted: come by bus or on foot along the Camí de Cavalls, and don’t trample the posidonia seagrass meadows.","s":"June to September: warm, clear water; arrive early to enjoy the cove before the crowds.","a":[["Camí de Cavalls","A historic path that circles the island from cove to cove."],["Cala Mitjana","A cove of pale sand between cliffs and pines, with turquoise water."],["Naveta d’Es Tudons","A prehistoric tomb shaped like an upturned boat hull."],["Torre d’en Galmés","A large Talayotic village with towers, an enclosure and hypostyle halls."],["Monte Toro","The island’s highest point, with a sanctuary and a view over all of Menorca."]]},"arrabida":{"v":"Limestone cliffs covered in scrub plunging into turquoise water: the Romans were already salting fish here, and the dolphins of the Sado estuary cruise offshore.","f":["Choco frito","Cuttlefish fried in strips, the speciality of Setúbal’s taverns."],"r":"The coastline is a marine reserve: take nothing from underwater and, in summer, use the shuttle bus, as car access is limited.","s":"June to September: a south-facing coast sheltered from the north wind, with clear, calm water for following the cliffs.","a":[["Arrábida Natural Park","A serra covered in scrub, panoramic roads and a convent on the slopes."],["Cape Espichel","Dizzying cliffs, a remote sanctuary and dinosaur footprints."],["Sesimbra","A lively fishing port, a town beach and grilled fish on the seafront."],["Monastery of Jesus of Setúbal","A church with twisted columns, a milestone of Manueline art."],["Creiro Roman ruins","Remains of a Roman fish-salting factory."]]},"maspalomas":{"v":"At the tip of the island, a desert of golden dunes spills into the Atlantic at the foot of a century-old lighthouse; behind the touristy seafront, ravines and Canarian villages keep their calm.","f":["Papas arrugadas with mojo","Small potatoes boiled in salted water, served with red and green mojo."],"r":"In the dune reserve, stay on the marked trails and don’t build stone shelters: it is forbidden.","s":"May to October: southerly swells wake up the south coast and the water reaches its mildest.","a":[["Maspalomas Dunes","A nature reserve of shifting dunes and the Charca lagoon."],["Roque Nublo","A volcanic monolith at the heart of the island, with a hike and a view of Mount Teide."],["Fataga","A white village at the bottom of a ravine of palms and ochre rock."],["Puerto de Mogán","A harbour of flower-decked houses and canals, nicknamed Little Venice."],["Guayadeque Ravine","A green gorge with cave houses and Canarian caves."]],"lbl":"Apartment or guesthouse"},"balos":{"v":"A lagoon of clear water and pink-tinged sand, facing the islet of Gramvousa and its Venetian fort, a former pirate hideout; you get there by dirt track or by caique from Kissamos.","f":["Dakos","A Cretan barley rusk topped with tomato, mizithra and olive oil."],"r":"A Natura 2000 site: take neither sand nor shells, and stay off the dunes and the marked-off areas of the lagoon.","s":"June to September: warm, clear water; in June and September, fewer excursion boats.","a":[["Falasarna","A big sandy beach facing the sunset and the remains of an ancient harbour."],["Polyrrhenia","Ruins of a hilltop ancient city, with a view over the Gulf of Kissamos."],["Gonia Monastery","A seaside Orthodox monastery with icons and a small museum."],["Kissamos","A small coastal town with seafront tavernas and boat departures."]],"lbl":"Studio or room"},"brela":{"v":"Below the cliffs of Biokovo, Brela lines up pebble coves and pines leaning over a crystal-clear Adriatic; higher up, the old stone village recalls a life of shepherds and fishermen.","f":["Peka","Meat or octopus slow-cooked under a cast-iron bell covered in embers."],"r":"The pines come right down to the water: no fires or barbecues on the coast, the fire risk is very high in summer.","s":"June to September: warm, clear sea, glassy in the morning before the afternoon maestral.","a":[["Biokovo Nature Park","A limestone massif towering over the coast, with mountain roads and trails."],["Sveti Jure","Biokovo’s summit, with a view of the Dalmatian islands on a clear day."],["Omiš","A former pirate hideout at the mouth of the Cetina gorge."],["Pučišća","A village of Brač white-stone masons, at the head of a bay."],["Baška Voda","A seaside resort with a small harbour and a promenade along the beaches."]]},"diani":{"v":"On the Swahili coast, Diani lines up white sand and a lagoon protected by the reef; the outrigger canoes of Digo fishermen cross paths with kites, and colobus monkeys live in the coastal forest.","f":["Viazi karai","Potatoes fried in a turmeric batter, a street snack of the Swahili coast."],"r":"Don’t feed the colobus and vervet monkeys, and don’t walk on the reef at low tide.","s":"June to September (kusi) and December to March (kaskazi): steady monsoon winds over the lagoon.","a":[["Shimba Hills National Reserve","Hills of forest and savannah, home to elephants and sable antelope."],["Mwaluganje Elephant Sanctuary","A community corridor for elephants, among baobabs and a river."]]},"tofo":{"v":"A fishing village turned divers’ stopover, Tofo lives between the fish market, beach bars and trips offshore to meet manta rays and whale sharks.","r":"With whale sharks and manta rays, no contact: keep your distance, don’t chase them and avoid using a flash.","s":"All year round for mantas and whale sharks; from June to October, humpback whales pass offshore.","a":[["Inhambane","A peaceful old colonial town, with a market and dhows on the bay."],["Barra Point and lighthouse","A sandy point between ocean and mangroves, topped by a lighthouse."]]},"paje":{"v":"A Swahili village of coral-stone houses, Paje lives by the tides: at low tide, women farm seaweed on the lagoon that kites cross at high tide.","r":"Paje is a Muslim village: cover your shoulders and knees away from the beach, and kite around the seaweed farms.","s":"June to September (kusi) and December to February (kaskazi): steady trade winds over the lagoon.","a":[["Jozani National Park","Forest and mangroves, home to the red colobus monkey, endemic to Zanzibar."],["Kizimkazi","A fishing village in the south, known for the dolphins offshore."],["Kizimkazi Mosque","One of the oldest mosques in East Africa, with Kufic inscriptions."],["Jambiani","A neighbouring coastal village with a turquoise lagoon and seaweed farms."]]},"anselazio":{"v":"Sculpted granite boulders, leaning takamaka trees and translucent water: Anse Lazio sums up the granite coast of the Seychelles, on an island where the legendary coco de mer grows.","r":"Don’t collect shells or coral, which are protected in the Seychelles, and don’t feed the fish when snorkelling.","s":"April to May and October to November: between the two monsoons, a calm sea and the clearest water.","a":[["Vallée de Mai","A primeval forest of coco de mer palms, where you may hear the black parrot."],["Curieuse Island","A national park with giant tortoises, mangroves and a coastal trail."],["Anse Source d’Argent","A beach on La Digue with pinkish granite boulders and shallow water."],["Cousin Island","A seabird reserve where hawksbill turtles also nest."],["Anse Georgette","A wild neighbouring beach of white sand and granite rocks."]]},"balandra":{"v":"On the Sea of Cortez, a shallow bay of turquoise water, fringed by mangroves and desert hills, watched over by the mushroom-shaped rock that is the emblem of La Paz.","f":["Almejas chocolatas","Big clams from the Sea of Cortez, baked au gratin or cooked in their shells."],"r":"Don’t climb the fragile mushroom rock; access to the protected area is limited, so come early and take your rubbish away with you.","s":"April to June and October to November: the sea is often calm, outside the winter north winds.","a":[["Espíritu Santo Island","A protected desert island: coves, red cliffs and a sea lion colony."],["Playa El Tecolote","A long beach facing Espíritu Santo Island, with fish palapas."],["La Paz","A malecón buzzing at sunset, a cathedral and marisquerías."]]},"lajolla":{"v":"Between the Scripps Institution of Oceanography and the cliffs of La Jolla, a neighbourhood turned towards the sea: surf schools, divers exploring the underwater canyon and sea lions on the rocks.","f":["California burrito","Carne asada and fries rolled up in a tortilla, a San Diego invention."],"r":"In the underwater reserve, nothing may be taken; keep your distance from the sea lions and seals at the Cove.","s":"July to September: the warmest water, an often calm sea and harmless leopard sharks in the shallows.","a":[["La Jolla Underwater Park","A marine reserve of kelp forests, garibaldis and a deep canyon."],["Birch Aquarium","The Scripps Institution’s aquarium, with kelp tanks and ocean views."],["Children’s Pool","A small beach sheltered by a sea wall, taken over by seals."],["Windansea Beach","A historic surf spot with sandstone reefs and a palm-frond shack."],["Coast Walk Trail","A clifftop path above the sea caves of La Jolla."]]},"cabopulmo":{"v":"A fishing village that switched to protecting its reef, Cabo Pulmo has seen schools of jacks, rays and sharks return since its marine park was created on the Sea of Cortez.","r":"In the national park, no fishing and no contact with the reef; dive only with an authorised operator.","s":"August to November: warm water and visibility often at its best; in spring, the sea can be windy."},"akumal":{"v":"Akumal means “place of the turtles” in Maya: its reef-sheltered bays hold seagrass meadows where green turtles graze, and cenotes hide in the jungle.","f":["Cochinita pibil","Pork marinated in annatto and bitter orange, slow-cooked in its own steam."],"r":"In the bay, follow the marked routes wearing a life jacket, without touching or chasing the turtles, and with a sunscreen free of chemical filters.","s":"November to April: the dry season, an often clear sea and less sargassum than in summer.","a":[["Xel-Há","A natural inlet where fresh water and sea mingle, set up as a water park."],["Xpu-Há","A long white-sand beach lined with palms, and quieter."]]},"cozumel":{"v":"An island where Maya women came on pilgrimage to the goddess Ixchel, Cozumel has become a legendary name in diving, with its drifts along coral walls.","f":["Tikin-xic","Fish marinated in annatto and cooked in a banana leaf, a Maya recipe."],"r":"The marine park requires a licensed guide: don’t touch the reef, no gloves, and let yourself drift without holding on to the coral.","s":"April to July: calm sea, warm water and excellent visibility, before the hurricane season.","a":[["Palancar Reef","Coral walls and arches among the island’s flagship dive sites."],["Cozumel Reefs National Park","A marine protected area covering the great reefs of the southwest."],["Punta Sur","A wild point with a lighthouse, a lagoon and turtle nesting beaches."],["San Miguel de Cozumel","The island’s small town, with a malecón, a main square and evening tacos."]]},"holbox":{"v":"A car-free island of sandy streets and mural-painted façades, Holbox lives between fishing, a lagoon with pink flamingos and, in summer, whale sharks passing offshore.","f":["Lobster pizza","A thin crust topped with local lobster, the island’s culinary curiosity."],"r":"In the Yum Balam reserve, don’t go near the flamingos; on the island, get around on foot, by bike or by golf cart.","s":"March to June: lighter wind for paddling in the lagoon and the mangroves, before the summer rains."},"buzios":{"v":"A former fishing village put on the map by Brigitte Bardot in the 1960s, Búzios mixes colourful boats, lanes that come alive at night and some twenty beaches on its peninsula.","r":"Geribá gets very busy in summer: surf away from swimmers, respect the regulars at the peak and leave nothing unattended on the sand.","s":"May to September: more consistent southern swells on Geribá and a far less crowded beach than in summer."},"arraialdocabo":{"v":"Nicknamed Brazil’s diving capital, Arraial do Cabo still lives from small-scale fishing, with its boats hauled up on the sand and its cool waters rising from the depths.","r":"In the marine reserve, fishing is reserved for small-scale fishermen: take nothing, don’t feed the fish and keep your fins well away from the bottom.","s":"April to June: the summer crowds are gone and the sea is often calm; bring a wetsuit, as the upwelling of cold water cools the sea.","a":[["Cabo Frio Island","A protected island with turquoise water and Farol beach, reachable by boat."],["Arraial do Cabo Marine Reserve","A protected coastline where the dive sites and small-scale fishing are concentrated."],["Cabo Frio","A seaside town with its colonial fort, dunes and long white-sand beaches."],["Costa do Sol State Park","Unspoilt lagoons, dunes and restinga along the Lakes Region."]]},"ilhagrande":{"v":"A former prison island long kept apart, Ilha Grande has no roads for cars: you get around on foot or by boat, between Atlantic forest and fishing villages.","r":"Stay on the park’s marked trails, carry your rubbish back down to Abraão and don’t feed the monkeys.","s":"April to June and September: less rain than in summer, an often calm sea and quieter trails.","a":[["Ilha Grande State Park","Atlantic forest crossed by trails to the beaches and Papagaio Peak."],["Praia do Aventureiro","A remote fishing village and wild beach on the coast facing the open sea."],["Vila do Abraão","The island’s main village, with its church, harbour and boat departures."]]},"sanandres":{"v":"On the island, the Raizal culture, English-speaking Creole and Protestant, sits alongside the duty-free bustle of the centre; all around, the sea of seven colours changes shade above the reef.","l":"Spanish, San Andrés Creole, English","f":["Rondón","Fish, conch and root vegetables stewed in coconut milk, the quintessential Raizal dish."],"r":"The archipelago forms the Seaflower Biosphere Reserve: collect neither coral nor shells and don’t touch the rays on boat trips.","s":"February to April: the dry season, very clear water and blue skies; October and November are the rainiest months."},"amed":{"v":"A string of fishing villages below Mount Agung, Amed lives to the rhythm of the jukung returning at dawn and the salt makers who still harvest sea salt the traditional way.","l":"Indonesian, Balinese","h":["Om Swastiastu","Balinese Hindu greeting, palms pressed together in front of the chest"],"f":["Sate lilit","Skewers of spiced minced fish, wrapped around lemongrass stalks."],"r":"Don’t walk on the coral as you get into the water, and make way for the fishermen’s jukung landing on the pebbles.","s":"April to October: the dry season, a sea often glassy in the morning and good visibility in the bays.","a":[["Lempuyang Temple","A temple whose split gates frame Mount Agung, at the foot of Mount Lempuyang."],["Tulamben","A dive village famous for the wreck of the USAT Liberty, accessible from the shore."],["Mount Agung","Bali’s sacred volcano, climbed at night to see the sunrise from the summit."],["Tenganan","A Bali Aga village of ancient traditions, known for its double ikat weaving."],["Ujung Water Palace","A former royal palace of Karangasem, with pavilions and pools facing the sea."]]},"nusapenida":{"v":"A dry limestone island that has stayed agricultural, Nusa Penida lives between seaweed plots farmed at the water’s edge, temples renowned for their spiritual power and cliffs plunging into the strait.","l":"Indonesian, Balinese","h":["Om Swastiastu","Balinese Hindu greeting, palms pressed together in front of the chest"],"r":"Currents are powerful around the island: dive with a local centre, never touch the manta rays and keep your distance.","s":"July to October: sunfish season and regular mantas, but cooler water and strong currents.","a":[["Nusa Lembongan","A neighbouring island of mangroves, beaches and surf reefs, a few minutes away by boat."],["Goa Lawah Temple","A temple built in front of a cave home to thousands of bats."],["Padangbai","A small ferry port with white-sand coves, a good snorkelling spot."],["Sanur","A quiet seaside resort with a lagoon-side promenade and boats to the islands."]]},"pemuteran":{"v":"A dry, peaceful fishing village on the north coast, Pemuteran has reinvented itself around its reef, which locals protect and regrow together with the dive centres.","l":"Indonesian, Balinese","h":["Om Swastiastu","Balinese Hindu greeting, palms pressed together in front of the chest"],"f":["Sate lilit","Skewers of spiced minced fish, wrapped around lemongrass stalks."],"r":"Don’t touch the metal structures in the bay: these artificial reefs, carrying a weak current, help the coral grow back.","s":"April to November: the dry season, a very calm sea on the north coast and excellent visibility towards Menjangan.","a":[["Menjangan Island","An islet in the national park with coral walls, Bali’s benchmark for snorkelling."],["West Bali National Park","Forests, savannah and mangroves, refuge of the rare Bali starling."],["Pulaki Temple","A temple backed against the cliff facing the sea, frequented by macaques."]]},"nusalembongan":{"v":"A small island of fishermen and seaweed farmers, Lembongan keeps its mangroves in the north and its colourful jukung, while the reefs of its west coast draw surfers.","l":"Indonesian, Balinese","h":["Om Swastiastu","Balinese Hindu greeting, palms pressed together in front of the chest"],"r":"On the shallow reefs, give locals priority; at low tide, don’t trample the farmers’ seaweed plots.","s":"April to October: consistent southern swells and easterly trade winds, often offshore on the west-coast reefs.","a":[["Kertha Gosa Pavilion","A former royal court of justice with a ceiling painted in the Kamasan style."],["Goa Lawah Temple","A temple built in front of a cave home to thousands of bats."],["Le Mayeur Museum","The Belgian painter’s beachside house and studio, with canvases and Balinese objects."],["Sanur","A quiet seaside resort with a lagoon-side promenade and boats to the islands."]]},"elnido":{"v":"El Nido’s name recalls the swiftlet nests gathered from its limestone cliffs; the fishing town now lives to the rhythm of the bangkas that set off each morning for the lagoons.","f":["Tamilok","A mangrove worm served raw in vinegar, a Palawan curiosity for the curious."],"r":"Keep the eco-tourism fee receipt on you during trips and don’t put your feet or paddle on the coral in the lagoons.","s":"March to May: the dry season, a sea often glassy in the Bacuit archipelago and crystal-clear water in the lagoons."},"phuquoc":{"v":"A fishing island facing the Gulf of Thailand, Phú Quốc smells of pepper and of fish sauce fermenting in its wooden barrels, between the port of An Thới and the islets of the south.","f":["Phú Quốc nước mắm","Anchovy sauce fermented in wooden barrels, the pride of the island."],"r":"Don’t take starfish out of the water for photos and don’t stand on the coral around the An Thới islets.","s":"November to April: the dry season, a calm sea in the south of the island and the best visibility around the islets."},"abeltasman":{"v":"Golden granite sand, clear water and fern forest: this coast bears the name of the Dutch navigator who sailed past in the 17th century, and you explore it by kayak, on foot or by water taxi.","r":"Book the park’s campsites and huts with DOC, take all your rubbish away and keep your distance from the seals on the rocks.","s":"February to April: still-mild water, a sea often calm in the morning and fewer people than in the summer holidays.","a":[["Abel Tasman Coast Track","A great coastal walk from bay to bay, to combine with kayaking."],["Tonga Island Marine Reserve","A marine protected area where seals rest on the islet’s rocks."],["Kaiteriteri","A beach of golden sand and turquoise water, the southern gateway to the park."],["Harwoods Hole","A dizzying shaft in the karst of Takaka Hill, at the end of a short forest walk."],["Tōtaranui","A long beach backed by a large campsite, in the north of the park."]]},"cathedralcove":{"v":"In the heart of Mercury Bay, named by James Cook, who came to observe the transit of Mercury, the coast of the Ngāti Hei lines up white cliffs, arches and islets sculpted by the sea.","r":"In the marine reserve, fishing and collecting are forbidden; land without blocking the beach and respect any closures of the access track.","s":"December to March: the warmest water and an often calm sea; set off early, before the boats and the afternoon wind.","a":[["Hot Water Beach","A beach where you dig your own pool in sand warmed by springs, at low tide."],["Cooks Beach","A long white-sand beach facing Mercury Bay, ideal for swimming."],["Whitianga","A small seaside town with a marina, a passenger ferry and boat departures."],["Castle Rock","A volcanic peak with a rocky summit, a short climb for a view over the peninsula."]]},"rottnest":{"v":"A car-free island you criss-cross by bike, Wadjemup keeps the painful memory of its former Aboriginal prison and is home to quokkas, the small marsupials that have become its emblem.","r":"Don’t feed or touch the quokkas, it is forbidden, and when snorkelling don’t walk on the shallow reefs in the protected zones.","s":"December to April: the warmest water and an often calm sea in the morning, before the afternoon sea breeze.","a":[["The Basin","A natural pool of turquoise water protected by the reef, perfect for swimming."],["Wadjemup Lighthouse","A historic lighthouse in the centre of the island, with guided tours and views of the salt lakes."],["Thomson Bay","The main settlement with colonial buildings, ferry arrivals and bike hire."],["Arthur Head and the Round House","A Fremantle headland and its round prison, one of the oldest buildings in the state."],["Cottesloe Beach","Perth’s iconic beach, with Norfolk pines and sunsets over the ocean."]]},"cablebeach":{"v":"A former mother-of-pearl capital where pearl divers from Asia once worked, Broome lives on Yawuru land, between red cliffs, huge tides and camel rides at sunset.","r":"Swim between the flags, especially from November to May when box jellyfish and crocodiles may come close, and only drive a 4x4 in the permitted zone.","s":"May to October: the dry season, a clear sea and outside jellyfish season; waves stay small and infrequent.","a":[["Gantheaume Point","Red ochre cliffs above a turquoise sea, with dinosaur footprints at very low tide."],["Sun Pictures","A historic open-air cinema where you watch films under the stars."],["Roebuck Bay","Vast mudflats for migratory birds and the “Staircase to the Moon” on full-moon evenings."],["Broome and its Chinatown","The pearl town, with its historic quarter and mother-of-pearl shops."],["Broome Bird Observatory","A centre on the bay for watching shorebirds that fly in from Asia."]]},"herveybay":{"v":"A peaceful Butchulla town facing K’gari, Hervey Bay owes its reputation to the humpback whales that rest here with their calves on the way to Antarctica.","r":"If a whale approaches, stop paddling and let it pass: approaching whales and dolphins is strictly regulated in the marine park.","s":"July to October: a sheltered bay, a sea often flat in the morning and humpback whales stopping over.","a":[["Urangan Pier","A long historic pier reaching out into the bay, ideal at sunrise."],["Great Sandy Biosphere Reserve","Mangroves, dugong seagrass beds and sand islands, including K’gari."],["Woody Island lighthouses","Two historic lighthouses on a wooded island in the strait."]]},"noumea":{"v":"Facing a UNESCO-listed lagoon, Anse Vata is where Nouméa’s residents play after work: boards, kites and paddleboards wait here for the trade wind that picks up in the afternoon.","f":["Bougna","Chicken or fish, yams and bananas cooked in coconut milk in banana leaves."],"r":"Keep out of the shuttle-boat channel to Îlot Canard and the marked swimming areas; the lagoon’s turtles and dugongs are protected.","s":"September to December: steady, well-established southeasterly trade winds and lagoon water that is warming up.","a":[["Tjibaou Cultural Centre","Renzo Piano’s huts dedicated to Kanak culture, with exhibitions and a botanical trail."],["Amédée Lighthouse","A metal lighthouse on a lagoon islet, with a climb to the top and snorkelling around it."],["Lagoons Aquarium","Living corals, nautiluses and fish of the New Caledonian lagoon."],["Museum of New Caledonia","Kanak and Oceanian collections: sculptures, traditional money and a great hut."],["Îlot Canard","A small islet off the beach with a marked underwater trail."]]},"vieuxboucau":{"v":"Its name recalls the old mouth of the Adour, diverted towards Bayonne in the 16th century; the Landes village now lives between pine forest, a tidal lake and long family sessions.","f":["Magret de canard","Duck breast grilled over embers and served with sautéed potatoes, Landes style."],"r":"Stay between the flags, as the baïnes create strong currents, and use only the official access paths: the dune is protected.","s":"June and September: moderate swells suited to beginners, mild water and a less crowded beach than in August.","a":[["Courant d’Huchet Reserve","A trip by boat or canoe down a wild river lined with bald cypresses."],["Lake Hossegor","A tidal saltwater lake lined with Basque-Landes villas and oyster beds."],["Étang Noir Reserve","A boardwalk trail through a swamp forest rich in birds."],["Marais d’Orx","A protected wetland and major stopover for migratory birds, with hides."],["Port de Capbreton","A fishing and leisure harbour with its fish auction and wooden jetty."]]},"moliets":{"v":"A resort nestled in the pine forest, Moliets recalls the days of the resin tappers who harvested the pines’ resin; many arrive by bike along the forest cycle paths.","f":["Pastis landais","A brioche flavoured with rum and orange blossom, queen of Landes afternoon snacks."],"r":"Beware of the currents near the mouth of the Courant d’Huchet and stay on the boardwalks: the dunes and riverbanks are a nature reserve.","s":"September to November: more consistent autumn swells, offshore wind in the morning and a quieter line-up.","a":[["Courant d’Huchet Reserve","A trip by boat or canoe down a wild river lined with bald cypresses."],["Étang de Léon","A large pine-fringed lake where the Courant d’Huchet begins, for swimming and sailing."],["Lake Hossegor","A tidal saltwater lake lined with Basque-Landes villas and oyster beds."],["Port de Capbreton","A fishing and leisure harbour with its fish auction and wooden jetty."]]},"espiguette":{"v":"Between lagoons, salt pans and bull ranches, the Gard Camargue lives here to the rhythm of the Grau-du-Roi trawlers and the white horses grazing behind the dunes.","f":["Gardiane de taureau","Camargue bull slow-cooked in red wine, served with Camargue rice."],"r":"Rig and ride only in the zones set aside for board sports, away from swimmers; cross the protected dune only by the marked paths.","s":"Spring and autumn: frequent mistral and tramontane, an uncrowded beach; in offshore wind, stay close to the shore.","a":[["Aigues-Mortes","A medieval walled town, with the Tour de Constance and pink salt pans all around."],["Le Grau-du-Roi","A lively fishing port where the trawlers come in through the channel in the late afternoon."],["Maguelone Cathedral","A Romanesque cathedral standing alone on a former island between lagoons and sea."],["La Grande-Motte","A resort born in the 1960s, famous for its pyramid-shaped buildings."],["Tour Carbonnière","A medieval watchtower guarding the approach to Aigues-Mortes, with views over the marshes."]]},"almanarre":{"v":"On the western tombolo of Giens, the windsurfers’ beach runs alongside the Pesquiers salt pans and their pink flamingos, facing the remains of the Greek city of Olbia.","f":["Tapenade","Crushed black olives, capers and anchovies, to spread at a Provençal apéritif."],"r":"Check the rules for the riding zone, which is regulated in summer, and don’t trample the fragile dune ridge that protects the road.","s":"Spring and autumn: frequent mistral and easterly winds, and a beach far less busy than in summer.","a":[["Hyères salt pans","Former protected salt marshes where you can watch pink flamingos and water birds."],["Port-Cros Island","An island in the national park, with wooded trails and a marked underwater trail."],["Olbia archaeological site","Remains of a fortified Greek trading post by the sea, with streets and baths."],["Hyères old town","Medieval lanes climbing to the castle ruins, with palm trees and villas."],["Giens peninsula","A coastal path between rocky coves and pines, with views of the Îles d’Or."]]},"porquerolles":{"v":"A car-free island you explore by bike, Porquerolles keeps its village of military origin around the Place d’Armes, its vineyards and its botanical conservatory among the pines.","r":"Fires and cigarettes are banned in the island’s natural areas; by kayak or paddleboard, don’t land on the seagrass beds and leave the banks of posidonia in place.","s":"June and September: warm, clear water above the seagrass beds, and far fewer people than in high summer.","a":[["Port-Cros Island","An island in the national park, with wooded trails and a marked underwater trail."],["Fort du Grand Langoustier","An isolated fort at the western tip of the island, facing cliffs and coves."],["Fort de l’Alycastre","A small fort perched above the sea, on the path leading to the beach."],["Hyères salt pans","Former protected salt marshes where you can watch pink flamingos and water birds."]],"lbl":"Campsite on the mainland"},"rondinara":{"v":"Amid the scrub and granite of southern Corsica, the round bay of Rondinara opens onto the Strait of Bonifacio, where free-roaming cows sometimes wander onto the sand.","l":"French, Corsican","h":["Bonghjornu","“hello” in Corsican, always appreciated"],"f":["Fiadone","A light cake made with brocciu and lemon zest, the quintessential Corsican dessert."],"r":"Wild camping is forbidden: don’t sleep on the beach, take your rubbish away and keep your distance from the free-roaming cows.","s":"June and September: already-warm water, a calm bay and a car park far less packed than in high summer.","a":[["Lavezzi Islands","An archipelago of sculpted granite and turquoise water, by boat from Bonifacio."],["Porto-Vecchio","A Genoese old town perched on its ramparts, with lanes and lively terraces."],["Cap de Pertusato","The southern tip of Corsica, a path along limestone cliffs facing Bonifacio."],["Castellu d’Araghju","A Bronze Age fortress perched above the Gulf of Porto-Vecchio."],["Ospedale Forest","Laricio pines, a lake and the Piscia di Gallo waterfall above Porto-Vecchio."]]},"scandola":{"v":"A roadless coast watched over only by Genoese towers, Scandola is followed along the shore as the fishermen of Galéria and Girolata once did, beneath the flight of ospreys.","l":"French, Corsican","h":["Bonghjornu","“hello” in Corsican, always appreciated"],"f":["Brocciu","A fresh ewe’s or goat’s milk cheese, in omelettes, in fritters or in fiadone."],"r":"In the strict reserve, landing, fishing and diving are forbidden: follow the coast without going ashore and stay well away from osprey nests.","s":"May, June and September: a sea often calm in the morning, crystal-clear water and fewer boats than in summer.","a":[["Calanques de Piana","A jumble of red granite sculpted above the sea, with trails and a panoramic road."],["Girolata","A roadless fishing hamlet with its Genoese tower, reached on foot or by boat."],["Capu Rossu","A wild headland topped by a Genoese tower, a fine hike facing the open sea."],["Gulf of Porto","A UNESCO-listed gulf, with Porto’s marina and a Genoese tower on its rock."],["Galéria","A fishing village at the foot of the mountains, near the wild delta of the Fango."]]},"saintebarbe":{"v":"At the start of the great dune ridge running towards Quiberon, Sainte-Barbe lives between dolmens, sardine canneries and surf shops, facing an ocean that is often cold and powerful.","l":"French, Breton","h":["Demat","“hello” in Breton, a word that always goes down well"],"f":["Quiberon sardines","Prepared by hand in the peninsula’s canneries, sometimes sold as vintages."],"r":"The dunes of the Gâvres-Quiberon site are fragile: stay on the marked access paths and park your van only in authorised car parks.","s":"September to November: consistent westerly swells, still-temperate water and a quieter line-up.","a":[["Carnac","Some of the world’s most famous menhir alignments, and sheltered beaches."],["Quiberon wild coast","A coastal path between cliffs, rock arches and coves battered by the ocean."],["Saint-Cado Island","An islet linked by a causeway, with a Romanesque chapel and fishermen’s houses on the ria."],["Great Broken Menhir of Locmariaquer","The largest known menhir, lying near the tumulus and the Table des Marchand."],["Kerzérho alignments","Rows of menhirs by the roadside, less visited than those at Carnac."]]},"oleron":{"v":"In the north of Oléron, colourful oyster huts, fish weirs and shore fishing set the pace of life; you surf facing the Pertuis d’Antioche, between marshes and vineyards.","f":["Marennes-Oléron oysters","Matured in the nearby claires, to enjoy on the spot at the oyster hut."],"r":"When shore fishing, respect the minimum sizes and put stones back where you found them; stay on the marked access paths to protect the dunes.","s":"September to November: consistent autumn swells, still-mild water and a far calmer line-up than in summer.","a":[["Chassiron Lighthouse","A black-and-white striped lighthouse at the tip of the island, with views over the straits and the fish weirs."],["Fort Boyard","A stone fort set in the sea, to admire from the coast or on a boat trip."],["Saint-Martin-de-Ré","A harbour fortified by Vauban, with flower-filled lanes and lively quays on the Île de Ré."],["Île d’Aix","A small car-free island to explore by bike, between fortifications and coves."],["Port de la Cotinière","The island’s main fishing port: trawlers coming home and a lively fish auction."]]},"beauduc":{"v":"At the end of the track, the fishermen’s cabins of Beauduc form a timeless hamlet, among salt flats, pink flamingos and Camargue bulls; here, life follows the wind.","f":["Gardiane de taureau","Camargue bull slow-cooked in red wine, served with Camargue rice."],"r":"The track crosses protected areas: never drive off the tracks, keep your distance from the flamingos and take all your rubbish away.","s":"April to October: frequent mistral and sea winds over flat water; in spring and autumn, fewer people and fewer mosquitoes.","a":[["Étang de Vaccarès","A vast lagoon in the delta, haunt of pink flamingos and water birds."],["Salin-de-Giraud","A salt workers’ village of brick houses, facing the salt pans and their salt mounds."],["Gacholle Lighthouse","A small, isolated lighthouse on the sea wall, reached on foot or by bike."],["Camargue Nature Reserve","Protected lagoons, salt flats and dunes, rich in migratory birds."]]},"ribeiradilhas":{"v":"Ericeira, Europe’s first World Surfing Reserve, keeps the soul of a fishing village: white houses trimmed with blue, boats pulled up on the slipway and sea urchins in pride of place.","f":["Sea urchins and grilled fish","Specialities of Ericeira’s tascas, where the catch of the day comes straight from the harbour."],"r":"The peak is busy with skilled local surfers: wait your turn, don’t let go of your board and beware of the rock shelf at low tide.","s":"September to March: consistent North Atlantic swells that make the right-hander peel, with fewer people than in summer.","a":[["Mafra National Palace","A vast Baroque palace-convent with a monumental library."],["Pena Palace","A brightly coloured Romantic palace perched on the Serra de Sintra."],["Moorish Castle","Medieval ramparts on the ridge, with views over Sintra and the ocean."],["Azenhas do Mar","A white village clinging to the cliff, with a natural pool below."],["Ericeira","A cobbled old centre, small squares and a fishing harbour at the foot of the cliffs."]]},"guincho":{"v":"Between shifting dunes and the cliffs of the Serra de Sintra, Guincho lives to the rhythm of the nortada; the fish restaurants on the coast road welcome kiters and weekending Lisboetas.","f":["Travesseiros de Sintra","Puff pastries filled with almond cream, a speciality of Sintra’s pastry shops."],"r":"The currents are violent: don’t swim unsupervised, give surfers room and stay on the boardwalks across the protected dunes.","s":"June to September: the nortada blows hard almost every afternoon, ideal for kiting and windsurfing in strong wind.","a":[["Cabo da Roca","The westernmost point of mainland Europe, with windswept cliffs and a lighthouse."],["Pena Palace","A brightly coloured Romantic palace perched on the Serra de Sintra."],["Monserrate Palace","An Oriental-inspired villa surrounded by a lush botanical garden."],["Capuchos Convent","A tiny cork-lined convent hidden in the Sintra forest."],["Cascais","A former fishing port turned chic resort, with its citadel and bay."]]},"tonel":{"v":"On the Romans’ Promontorium Sacrum, anglers cast their lines from the clifftops and percebes gatherers brave the backwash; Sagres lives on surf, wind and raw sea.","f":["Percebes","Goose barnacles prised from the rocks of the Costa Vicentina, served simply boiled in salted water."],"r":"You are in the Costa Vicentina Natural Park: no bivouacking or sleeping in your van outside designated areas, and stay well back from the edge of the unstable cliffs.","s":"September to November: consistent westerly swells, often lighter wind and still-temperate water after summer.","a":[["Cape St Vincent","The southwestern tip of Europe, with a lighthouse and dizzying cliffs facing the sunset."],["Sagres Fortress","A walled enclosure on the headland, with its stone wind rose and cliffs."],["Carrapateira","A west-coast village, gateway to the wild beaches of Amado and Bordeira."],["Salema","A small fishing village where boats still rest on the beach."]]},"caparica":{"v":"Lisbon’s favourite popular beach, Caparica still has its arte xávega fishermen who haul the nets in from the sand, while a little train runs along the dunes to the southern beaches.","r":"Surf between the groynes, away from the rocks and the zones reserved for schools; in summer, swim only between the lifeguarded flags.","s":"April to June and September to October: moderate, consistent swells for beginners, without the summer weekend crowds.","a":[["Jerónimos Monastery","A Manueline masterpiece in Belém, with a finely carved cloister and Vasco da Gama’s tomb."],["Belém Tower","A fortified Manueline tower on the banks of the Tagus, symbol of the Age of Discovery."],["Monument to the Discoveries","A monumental prow on the Tagus, with a view over Belém from the top."],["National Coach Museum","A collection of gilded royal coaches, among the richest in the world."]]},"razo":{"v":"On the Costa da Morte, a land of shipwrecks, lighthouses and percebeiros, the villages of Bergantiños line up granite hórreos and fishing harbours battered by the Atlantic.","l":"Spanish, Galician","h":["Ola","“hi” in Galician, the everyday language on the Costa da Morte"],"f":["Pulpo á feira","Octopus sliced on a wooden board, drizzled with olive oil and paprika."],"r":"The Razo dunes are fragile: use the wooden walkways and leave the rocks to the percebeiros who work there.","s":"June to October: gentler swells for progressing and often calmer wind; bring a thick wetsuit, the water stays cool.","a":[["Buño","A potters’ village renowned for its traditional ceramics and old kilns."],["Santiago de Mens Church","A Romanesque country church, near the medieval towers of Mens."],["Castro de Elviña","An Iron Age hillfort, with the foundations of round houses."],["Barizo","A tiny fishing harbour on a rocky cove of the Costa da Morte."]]},"bolonia":{"v":"At Bolonia, the Romans salted tuna and made garum facing Africa; today, cows, a giant dune and chiringuitos keep company with the levante and poniente winds.","f":["Almadraba bluefin tuna","Caught in spring by the almadraba traps of Zahara and Barbate, served as tataki or a la plancha."],"r":"The dune is a protected natural site: use the marked paths, don’t camp on the beach and keep your lines well away from swimmers in summer.","s":"May to September: the levante and poniente of the strait blow hard and often, with milder water than in spring.","a":[["Baelo Claudia","A beachside Roman town: forum, theatre and fish-salting tanks."],["Tarifa","A walled white old town, Europe’s kitesurfing capital, facing Morocco."],["Zahara de los Atunes","A tuna-fishing village with long beaches and a laid-back feel."],["Strait Natural Park","Cliffs, pine woods and bird migrations over the Strait of Gibraltar."],["Mirador del Estrecho","A viewpoint over the strait and Morocco’s Rif mountains."]],"lbl":"Hostal"},"sotavento":{"v":"On the Jandía peninsula, between pale dunes and bare mountains where majorera goats graze, the tide shapes a lagoon each day that windsurfers have known for decades.","f":["Majorero cheese","The island’s goat’s cheese, often rubbed with paprika or gofio."],"r":"You are in Jandía Natural Park: don’t drive off the tracks, keep off the dunes and respect the separation between kite and windsurf zones.","s":"June to September: strong, almost daily trade winds, accelerated along the coast, with pleasant water.","a":[["Cofete","A remote hamlet at the end of a dirt track, facing a vast wild beach pounded by the ocean."],["Jandía peninsula","A protected volcanic massif and dunes, with the Pico de la Zarza to climb."]],"lbl":"Apartment"},"cotillo":{"v":"El Cotillo keeps its old fishing harbour, its defence tower and its low houses; surfers cross paths with fishermen here at sunset, far from the bustle of Corralejo.","f":["Majorero cheese","The island’s goat’s cheese, often rubbed with paprika or gofio."],"r":"To the south, the shorebreak and currents are powerful: swim in the northern lagoons instead and let the fishermen work in peace at the old harbour.","s":"October to March: consistent, powerful northwesterly swells, with more moderate trade winds than in summer.","a":[["Lobos Island","A protected islet with turquoise coves and a trail to the volcano and the lighthouse."],["Tindaya Mountain","The sacred mountain of the ancient Majoreros, adorned with foot-shaped rock carvings."],["Tostón Lighthouse","Lighthouses facing the waves and a small museum of traditional fishing."],["Tostón Tower","A round defence tower overlooking the old harbour and the coast."],["Corralejo","A lively northern harbour, gateway to the great protected dunes."]]},"medano":{"v":"A village of sand rather than concrete, El Médano lives to the rhythm of the wind beneath Montaña Roja; windsurfers, kiters and locals meet on the square facing the beach.","f":["Papas arrugadas with mojo","Small salt-wrinkled potatoes, served with red and green sauces."],"r":"Montaña Roja is a nature reserve: stay on the marked trails and keep the launch zones clear for other riders.","s":"June to August: trade winds accelerated by the terrain blow hard almost every day, with mild water as a bonus.","a":[["Roque Cinchado","An iconic volcanic rock at the foot of Mount Teide, in the Roques de García."],["Mount Guajara","A summit on the rim of the Cañadas caldera, with an immense view of Mount Teide."],["Barranco del Infierno","A deep gorge to hike up to a waterfall, above Adeje."],["Cave of Brother Peter","A cave sanctuary dedicated to the first Canarian saint, a place of pilgrimage."]]},"famara":{"v":"Caleta de Famara, with its sandy streets and white houses, lives to the rhythm of surfers beneath the Risco cliff; the whole island bears the stamp of the artist César Manrique.","f":["Papas arrugadas with mojo","Small salt-wrinkled potatoes, served with red and green sauces."],"r":"Currents are strong at Famara: swim only near the schools, don’t camp on the beach and give the paragliders space.","s":"September to November and spring: consistent northerly swells, but gentler than in the depths of winter.","a":[["Jameos del Agua","A lava tube transformed by César Manrique, with an underground lake and blind crabs."],["La Graciosa","An island without paved roads, with deserted beaches and sandy tracks to explore by bike."],["Teguise","The former capital, with white lanes and a big Sunday market."],["Cueva de los Verdes","Underground lava galleries explored on a guided tour."],["Cactus Garden","A former quarry transformed by Manrique into a garden of cacti from around the world."]],"lbl":"Surf house"},"calodesmoro":{"v":"In south-east Majorca, villages of golden marès stone, fishing harbours where llaüts bob on the water and dry-stone walls line coves carved into the limestone.","l":"Spanish, Catalan","h":["Bon dia","“good morning” in Majorcan Catalan, called out at any time of the morning"],"f":["Pa amb oli","Bread rubbed with tomato and olive oil, topped with cheese or sobrassada."],"r":"The cove is tiny and very popular: come early, park only in authorised car parks and take your rubbish home.","s":"June and September: warm, clear water for swimming and fish-watching, with smaller crowds than in August.","a":[["Es Trenc","A long white-sand beach lined with dunes and protected salt flats."],["Cap de ses Salines","Majorca’s southern tip, with a lighthouse and wild coast facing Cabrera."],["Na Nova Talayotic site","Remains of the Majorcan Iron Age, bearing witness to the talayot culture."],["Portopetro","A quiet little harbour of white houses, tucked at the end of an inlet."]]},"goloritze":{"v":"On the Baunei coast, shepherds and goatherds traced the paths running down from the Supramonte to the sea; Ogliastra keeps a strong pastoral culture where Sardinian is still spoken.","l":"Italian, Sardinian","f":["Culurgiones","Ogliastra ravioli filled with potato, pecorino and mint, sealed in the shape of an ear of wheat."],"r":"The cove is a natural monument: boats stay beyond the buoys, don’t take any pebbles and carry all your rubbish back up, as there are no facilities.","s":"June to September: calm seas and warm water; in June and September the climb back up the path is less punishingly hot.","a":[["Cala Gonone","The harbour where boats and kayaks set off for the coves of the Gulf of Orosei."],["Ispinigoli Cave","A karst cave home to one of the tallest limestone columns in Europe."],["Nuraghe Mannu","A Nuragic site perched above the sea, looking out over the Gulf of Orosei."],["Santa Maria Navarrese","A small harbour, an ancient church and thousand-year-old olive trees facing the islet of Ogliastra."]]},"sarakiniko":{"v":"Since ancient times Milos has lived off its volcanic rock, from obsidian to modern mines; in its inlets, the syrmata, boathouses with colourful doors, watch over the fishermen’s boats.","f":["Pitarakia","Small Milos cheese pasties, fried or baked, to nibble at the taverna."],"r":"The white rock is soft and slippery: don’t carve into it, avoid risky jumps and take everything away with you, as there is no shade and no bins.","s":"June to September: warm, clear water in the channels; pick a day without a strong meltemi for a relaxed swim.","a":[["Plaka","A white village perched at the top of the island, famous for its sunsets."],["Archaeological Museum of Milos","Objects from the island, including prehistoric obsidian and a copy of the Venus de Milo."],["Kimolos","A peaceful neighbouring island, reached by boat from Pollonia."],["Pollonia","A quiet fishing village with tavernas on the harbour and a sheltered bay."],["Phylakopi","A seaside Bronze Age site, among the most important in the Cyclades."]]},"pounta":{"v":"Facing Antiparos, Pounta is first and foremost a small ferry landing; Paros, the island of ancient marble, lines up whitewashed chapels and fishing harbours swept by the meltemi all summer.","r":"The ferry to Antiparos crosses the channel constantly: stay well clear of its lane and don’t rig up in the middle of swimmers.","s":"June to September, especially July–August: the meltemi blows strong and steady through the channel almost every day.","a":[["Panagia Ekatontapiliani","A large Byzantine church in Parikia, known as the church “of a hundred doors”."],["Antiparos","A laid-back neighbouring island, with its village and its cave full of rock formations."],["Parikia","The main port, with whitewashed lanes and a Venetian kastro."],["Archaeological Museum of Paros","Parian marble sculptures and remains from the island’s ancient history."],["Despotiko","An uninhabited islet with an ancient sanctuary of Apollo still being excavated."]]},"stiniva":{"v":"Long a military base closed to foreigners, Vis has kept its vineyards and stone villages, and in Komiža the memory of the falkuša, the sardine fishermen’s boats.","f":["Viška pogača","A flatbread filled with salted fish, onions and tomato, a speciality of Komiža and Vis."],"r":"The path is steep and stony: go down in closed shoes with water, and by kayak only pass through the opening when the sea is calm.","s":"June to September: often calm seas and warm water; arrive early in the morning, before the excursion boats.","a":[["Blue Cave of Biševo","A sea cave lit up in blue by the midday sun."],["Komiža","A fishing port at the foot of Mount Hum, with its Venetian fort and narrow lanes."],["Vis","A port town of stone houses and remains of the Greek colony of Issa."],["Mount Hum","The island’s highest point, with views over the whole Vis archipelago."]],"lbl":"Room in a local home"},"comino":{"v":"Almost uninhabited, Comino takes its name from cumin and was once a smugglers’ hideout; the Knights of Malta’s St Mary’s Tower still keeps watch over the channel between Malta and Gozo.","f":["Ġbejniet","Small sheep’s cheeses from Gozo, fresh, dried or peppered."],"r":"The lagoon is packed in summer: come early or late in the day, avoid single-use plastic and stay out of the marked boat lanes.","s":"May to June and September–October: warm, very clear water, with far fewer boats than in August.","a":[["Ġgantija Temples","Megalithic temples on Gozo, among the oldest stone monuments in the world."],["Victoria Citadel","A fortified citadel in the heart of Gozo, with views over the whole island."],["Mdina","The old fortified capital with silent lanes, nicknamed the Silent City."],["Majjistral Park","Protected garrigue, cliffs and coastal paths in north-west Malta."]]},"gozo":{"v":"At Dwejra, fishing boats rest in the shelters of the Inland Sea, facing Fungus Rock once guarded by the Knights; the Azure Window collapsed in 2017, but diving still reigns supreme.","f":["Gozitan ftira","A flatbread topped with potatoes, tomatoes and anchovies, baked in the village oven."],"r":"Dwejra is a protected site: don’t collect fossils or rocks, enter the water at the usual access points and give it a miss if the north-westerly swell is pounding the rocks.","s":"June to October: warm water, excellent visibility and often calm seas for descending into the chimney.","a":[["Victoria Citadel","A fortified citadel in the heart of Gozo, with views over the whole island."],["Ġgantija Temples","Megalithic temples among the oldest stone monuments in the world."],["Dwejra Inland Sea","A lagoon linked to the open sea by a tunnel through the cliff, to pass by boat or on a dive."],["Għasri Valley","A narrow sea gorge cut into the cliff, with steps down to the water."],["Qbajjar Bay","A small bay lined with salt pans cut into the rock, still in use."]]},"croyde":{"v":"Between whitewashed cottages, village pubs and the Saunton dunes, Croyde has lived for surfing for decades; here, a post-session cream tea is part of the ritual.","f":["Devon cream tea","Warm scones, clotted cream and strawberry jam, with the cream spread first."],"r":"At low tide the wave is hollow and crowded: respect priority, and park in the pay car parks rather than in the village lanes.","s":"September to November: consistent Atlantic swells, water still mild for Devon and far fewer people.","a":[["Baggy Point","A National Trust headland, with a coastal path and views over the bay."],["Braunton Burrows","A vast dune system, the heart of the North Devon Biosphere Reserve."],["Museum of British Surfing","Vintage boards and the history of surfing in Britain."],["Appledore","A seafaring village with colourful lanes, at the mouth of the Torridge."],["Woolacombe","A seaside resort on a large golden-sand beach."]]},"rhossili":{"v":"At the end of the Gower Peninsula, sheep and horses graze on the clifftops, the wreck of the Helvetia pokes out of the sand and Worm’s Head can only be reached at low tide.","l":"English, Welsh","h":["Shwmae","“hi” in Welsh, always a welcome touch"],"f":["Penclawdd cockles and laverbread","Cockles from the estuary and seaweed purée, served as part of a Welsh breakfast."],"r":"For Worm’s Head, check the tide times: the causeway is only passable around low water and the tide comes back in fast.","s":"September to November: consistent westerly swells, water at its warmest and car parks less busy.","a":[["Oxwich Bay","A sheltered bay lined with dunes and woods, ideal for swimming and paddling."],["Weobley Castle","A fortified medieval manor house overlooking the salt marshes of north Gower."],["Kidwelly Castle","A remarkably well-preserved Norman castle above the Gwendraeth."],["Llangennith","A Welsh surf village, the gateway to the northern end of the bay’s beach."],["Port Eynon","A former smugglers’ village beside a sheltered beach."]]},"lahinch":{"v":"A promenade facing the Atlantic, links golf and surf schools: Lahinch lives off the sea all year round, and in the evening traditional music rings out in the pubs of Clare.","r":"The nearby reefs are powerful and reserved for experienced locals; start out on the beach break, near the lifeguards, and watch out for currents.","s":"September to November: consistent autumn swells and water at its mildest; a thick wetsuit is still essential.","a":[["Cliffs of Moher","Dizzying cliffs above the Atlantic, with bird colonies and a coastal path."],["Doolin","A village renowned for its traditional music sessions in the pubs."],["Burren Geopark","Lunar limestone plateaus, rare flora and prehistoric sites."],["Doolin Cave","A cave home to a huge hanging stalactite."],["Kilfenora Cathedral","A small medieval cathedral surrounded by carved Celtic crosses."]]},"mullaghmore":{"v":"A small fishing port in Yeats Country, Mullaghmore looks out on Ben Bulben and the Atlantic; in winter, the village gathers on the headland to watch the best big-wave surfers.","r":"The wave is reserved for big-wave surfers with jet-skis and safety cover: if that’s not you, watch from the headland, well away from the rocks.","s":"October to March: big North Atlantic winter swells, surfable only with an offshore wind.","a":[["Drumcliff","A village at the foot of Ben Bulben, with Yeats’s grave and a Celtic cross."],["Glencar Lough","A valley lake framed by cliffs, with a waterfall celebrated by Yeats."],["Inishmurray","An uninhabited island with the ruins of an early Christian monastery."],["Sligo Abbey","The ruins of a medieval Dominican friary in the heart of Sligo."]]},"klitmoller":{"v":"A former fishing village on the Thy coast, Klitmøller has kept its low houses among the dunes and heather; surfers and windsurfers have built a real community here.","r":"The stone reefs are shallow and the water cold: wear boots and a thick wetsuit, and respect the pecking order of a small, local line-up.","s":"September to November: autumn North Sea storms bring consistent swell and wind; bring a 5 mm wetsuit.","a":[["Thy National Park","Dunes, heaths and lakes along the North Sea, to explore on foot or by bike."],["Stenbjerg","A fishing village with tarred huts on the landing beach."]]},"sylt":{"v":"Thatched Frisian houses, wicker beach chairs lined up on the sand and Biike bonfires in February: Sylt cherishes its Frisian traditions facing an often rough North Sea.","f":["Sylt oysters","Farmed in the north of the island, around List, best enjoyed simply with a squeeze of lemon."],"r":"The dunes are protected and fragile: stay on the wooden boardwalks and respect the marked zones separating swimmers, surfers and kiters.","s":"September to October: autumn storms and more consistent swells, water still bearable and empty beaches.","a":[["Kampen","An upmarket village of thatched houses above the red cliff of the Rotes Kliff."],["Denghoog","A Neolithic passage grave where you descend into a chamber of great stones."],["St Severin’s Church, Keitum","An old Frisian church watching over Keitum and its former sea captains’ houses."],["Königshafen","A shallow bay at the very north of the island, among mudflats, dunes and birds."]]},"scheveningen":{"v":"A former fishing village of The Hague, Scheveningen still celebrates the arrival of the new herring in its harbour, while surfers meet between the groynes beneath the facade of the Kurhaus.","r":"Between the groynes the peaks are tight: wait your turn, give priority to the regulars and in summer respect the areas reserved for swimmers.","s":"September to November: autumn storms on the North Sea, more consistent swells and a beach handed back to the locals.","a":[["Kunstmuseum Den Haag","An art museum in an Art Deco building, famous for its large Mondrian collection."],["Peace Palace","A neo-Renaissance palace housing the International Court of Justice, set in a park."],["Beelden aan Zee","A sculpture museum tucked into the dunes, with terraces facing the sea."],["Madurodam","A miniature park that sums up the Netherlands: canals, windmills, ports and towns on a small scale."]]},"hoddevik":{"v":"At the end of the Stad Peninsula, a cape feared by Norwegian sailors for centuries, this hamlet of farms and sheep at the head of a deep bay has become home to a small Nordic surf culture.","r":"The access road is narrow and steep: park only in the designated spaces and don’t cross the farm meadows where the sheep graze.","s":"September to October: consistent autumn swells, water still around 12 °C and days long enough to fit in session after session."},"taghazout":{"v":"White and blue houses, fishing boats hauled up on the sand and argan trees on the hills: Taghazout keeps the soul of an Amazigh fishing village, where surfers have flocked since the hippie years.","l":"Colloquial Arabic (Darija), Tashelhit (Amazigh), French","h":["Azul","“hello” in Tashelhit, the Amazigh language of the Souss"],"f":["Amlou and argan oil","A paste of almonds, argan oil and honey, spread on warm bread at breakfast."],"r":"At point breaks such as Anchor Point, wait your turn and give priority to the locals; in the village, cover up again when you leave the beach.","s":"October to March: North Atlantic winter swells get the points peeling, with offshore winds in the morning.","a":[["Paradise Valley","Palm-lined Atlas gorges with pools of clear water to swim in."],["Agadir Oufella Kasbah","The ramparts of the old kasbah on the hill, with a panorama over the whole Bay of Agadir."]]},"oualidia":{"v":"Around its lagoon, Oualidia lives from oyster farming and fishing: colourful boats, oyster beds and tables where you eat shellfish facing the water, in a resort popular with Moroccan families.","f":["Oualidia oysters","Farmed in the lagoon and served freshly opened, with a squeeze of lemon, facing the water."],"r":"The lagoon is home to oyster beds and many waterbirds: don’t trample the beds and keep your distance from the flamingos.","s":"June to September: a calm lagoon, warmer than the ocean, ideal for swimming; August is the busiest month."},"salkite":{"v":"Santa Maria was born from the salt trade: on its old pier, fishermen still land tuna and wahoo when the boats come in, and in the evening morna drifts out of the seafront bars.","r":"Pump up and launch away from swimmers, steer around the marked turtle nests in summer and never ride alone: the beach is isolated.","s":"December to April: north-easterly trade winds at their strongest, steady almost every day, with a warm sea all year round.","a":[["Pedra de Lume","Salt pans set in an old crater, where you float in very salty water."],["Santa Maria salt pans","Old salt basins where the town began, frequented by waterbirds."],["Ponta Preta","A black lava point and legendary wave, to watch when the windsurfers take it on."],["Santa Maria","A colourful little town with a fishermen’s pier, fish market and live-music bars."]]},"pontapreta":{"v":"At the foot of a desert of rock and sand, this black lava point has hosted wave windsurfing world cup events, while Santa Maria lives to the rhythm of the returning fishing boats and morabeza.","r":"Shallow reef and strong current: only paddle out if you can handle big waves, and give priority to those already in position.","s":"November to March: North Atlantic winter swells meet the trade winds, and the right-hander lives up to its reputation.","a":[["Pedra de Lume","Salt pans set in an old crater, where you float in very salty water."],["Buracona","A natural pool in the black lava and a cave where the light makes a “blue eye” shine."],["Santa Maria salt pans","Old salt basins where the town began, frequented by waterbirds."],["Santa Maria","A colourful little town with a fishermen’s pier, fish market and live-music bars."]]},"busua":{"v":"Brightly painted pirogues and nets spread out on the sand: Busua is still an Ahanta fishing village, surrounded by old European forts, where local young people learned to surf before going on to teach.","l":"English, Ahanta, Fante","f":["Kenkey with fried fish","A ball of fermented maize cooked in leaves, served with fried fish, chilli and onion."],"r":"Fishermen haul their pirogues and nets up the beach: don’t get in the way of their work and always ask before photographing them.","s":"November to March: the dry season, with gentler, more consistent swells, ideal for learning in the bay.","a":[["Fort Metal Cross","A whitewashed British fort perched on a headland above Dixcove harbour."],["Butre","A village between river and sea, overlooked by the ruins of Fort Batenstein on the hill."],["Cape Three Points","Ghana’s southernmost point, with a lighthouse, forest and fishing coves."],["Fort Gross Friedrichsburg","Remains of the Brandenburg fort at Princes Town, facing the ocean."],["Dixcove","A lively fishing port where you can watch the pirogues come in and the fish being sold."]]},"robertsport":{"v":"At the foot of Mount Cape Mount, among houses from the days of the first settlers and the pirogues of Vai fishermen, Robertsport saw a young community of Liberian surfers take shape after the civil war.","l":"Liberian English, Vai","r":"The points break over rocks: work out the ways in and out with a local before your session and let the local kids have their share.","s":"April to October: consistent southern swells that get the lefts peeling, even though it is also the rainy season.","a":[["Lake Piso","A large lagoon lined with mangroves, crossed by fishermen’s pirogues and full of birds."],["Mount Cape Mount protected area","Tropical forest on the slopes of the mountain overlooking the town and the coast."]]},"cabroledo":{"v":"South of Luanda, beyond Quiçama Park, this fishing village on an arid coast has become the weekend getaway for surfers from the capital, far from any mass tourism.","r":"The bay belongs to the fishermen too: don’t surf in among the nets and the boats coming in, and drive carefully on the coast road.","s":"May to September: the cool dry season, with the most consistent southern swells to get the long left peeling."},"aliwal":{"v":"At Umkomaas, dive boats launch from the beach through the shorebreak, and every winter KwaZulu-Natal’s South Coast watches out for the sardine run, with dolphins and Cape gannets in its wake.","l":"English, Zulu","h":["Sawubona","“I see you”, the Zulu greeting, to which you reply yebo"],"f":["Bunny chow","Half a loaf of white bread hollowed out and filled with curry, a speciality of Durban’s Indian community."],"r":"In the marine protected area, don’t touch or chase the ragged-tooth sharks: stay calm near the bottom and let them pass.","s":"June to November: ragged-tooth sharks gather on the reef, and the sardine run sometimes livens up the coast in winter.","a":[["Scottburgh","A South Coast seaside resort with a large lifeguarded beach and a seawater pool."],["Green Point Lighthouse","A provincial heritage lighthouse standing above the coastal rocks."]]},"sodwana":{"v":"In the heart of Maputaland, dive boats launch from the beach through the waves, and it was in the offshore canyons that divers came across coelacanths, fish once thought extinct.","l":"English, Zulu","h":["Sawubona","“I see you”, the Zulu greeting, to which you reply yebo"],"r":"In iSimangaliso Park, never drive on the beach outside the launch ramp and only approach nesting turtles with an accredited guide.","s":"November to April: the warmest water of the year and turtles coming to nest on the nearby beaches.","a":[["Lake Sibaya","The country’s largest freshwater lake, among wooded dunes, hippos and birds."],["Sodwana Bay National Park","Coastal dune forest and small lakes to explore on foot, among monkeys and birds."]]},"langebaan":{"v":"On the Afrikaans West Coast, Langebaan combines white houses, beach restaurants where crayfish grills over a wood fire and a springtime burst of flowers in the nearby park, beside a waveless lagoon.","f":["West Coast crayfish","Rock lobster from the cold Atlantic waters, grilled over a wood fire in the beach restaurants."],"r":"The lagoon is a zoned marine protected area: only ride in the sector open to kites and avoid the sandbanks where birds feed.","s":"November to March: the south-easterly blows hard almost every afternoon over a flat, shallow lagoon.","a":[["West Coast National Park","Lagoon, fynbos and turquoise coves, carpeted with wildflowers in the southern spring."],["West Coast Fossil Park","A fossil bed of extinct animals, with guided tours of the excavations."],["Saldanha Bay","A vast natural harbour with cold waters, fishing ports and seabird islands."]]},"bigbay":{"v":"Facing Table Mountain across the bay, Bloubergstrand keeps its fishermen’s cottages and the memory of the battle that handed the Cape to the British; in summer, kiters from all over the world meet here.","f":["Bobotie","Minced meat spiced with mild curry, topped with egg and baked, a legacy of Cape Malay cooking."],"r":"Launch and land only in the kite zones, away from swimmers, and give priority to surfers on the peaks when the wind drops.","s":"November to March: the south-easterly blows hard almost every day over cold Atlantic waves.","a":[["Robben Island","The prison island where Nelson Mandela was held, a World Heritage Site."],["Devil’s Peak","A summit next to Table Mountain, a hike with views over the city and the bay."],["Blaauwberg Nature Reserve","Dunes, fynbos and a hill with one of the finest views of Table Mountain."],["Rietvlei Wetland Reserve","A protected wetland for watching flamingos, pelicans and waterbirds."]]},"pontadoouro":{"v":"A village of sandy tracks right on the South African border, Ponta do Ouro lives to the rhythm of RIBs launched from the beach and lively weekends around the marisqueiras.","r":"In the marine reserve, swimming with dolphins is only allowed with an accredited operator, without touching them, chasing them or cutting across their path.","s":"June to November: often better visibility and humpback whales migrating along the coast."},"bazaruto":{"v":"Off Vilankulo, lateen-sailed dhows glide between giant dunes and sandbanks, and island fishermen still live by the tides and nets hauled in by hand.","r":"In the national park, don’t walk on the coral at low tide, don’t collect any shells and keep your distance from dugongs and turtles.","s":"May to November: the dry season, with often calm seas and clear water, and humpback whales migrating in the southern winter.","a":[["Benguerra Island","The archipelago’s second island, with dunes, freshwater lakes and reefs to explore by snorkel."],["Bazaruto Island","A large island of dunes to climb, with views over the sandbanks and turquoise lagoons."],["Santa Carolina","A small rocky island with crystal-clear coves, nicknamed Paradise Island."]]},"ifaty":{"v":"The Vezo, a people of nomadic sea fishermen, criss-cross the lagoon in their outrigger pirogues, while on land the spiny forest and its baobabs grow almost down to the sand.","r":"Don’t walk on the reef at low tide, don’t bring back coral or shells, and agree the price of the pirogue trip before you board.","s":"April to November: the dry season, with a clearer lagoon, and humpback whales offshore from July to September."},"lavanono":{"v":"At the far end of Antandroy country, a dry land of cacti, zebu and decorated tombs, a few fishermen’s huts face reefs where you can surf almost alone.","r":"Don’t go near the tombs unless invited and don’t photograph them; fresh water is scarce here, so use it sparingly.","s":"March to October: the southern winter swells arrive powerful and consistent on the reefs."},"mnemba":{"v":"Facing Matemwe, fishermen’s outrigger canoes cross paths with the dhows taking divers out, and at low tide women farm seaweed on the reef flat.","r":"The islet is private: you can’t land there. In the conservation area, turn down boats that chase dolphins and never touch the turtles.","s":"October to March: the warmest water, often calm seas in the morning and very good visibility on the reef.","a":[["Matemwe","A fishing village facing the atoll, with a long beach and seaweed farms at low tide."],["Kiwengwa-Pongwe Forest Reserve","Forest on ancient coral, with caves, birds and monkeys, to explore with a guide."],["Tumbatu ruins","Remains of a medieval Swahili city, with a mosque and coral-stone houses."]]},"bluebay":{"v":"Right next to Mahébourg, a former French town where the Battle of Grand Port lives on in memory, fishermen in pirogues share the lagoon with glass-bottom boats.","r":"In the marine park, no fishing, no feeding the fish and no collecting anything: swim at the surface, without touching the coral, within the marked area.","s":"October to December: calmer trade winds, water already warm and clear, before the cyclone season.","a":[["Île aux Aigrettes","An islet reserve where the original dry forest, giant tortoises and pink pigeons are thriving again."],["Mahébourg","A small Creole town on the bay, with a seafront and a lively market."],["National History Museum","A colonial mansion tracing the island’s history and the naval Battle of Grand Port."],["Frederik Hendrik Museum","Ruins of the Dutch fort at Vieux Grand Port, the island’s first settlement."],["Ferney Valley","Protected forest in the Bambous Mountains, with trails and rare endemic species."]]},"tamarinmu":{"v":"Between salt pans still in use and Rempart Mountain, Tamarin is a Creole fishing village whose left-hander was revealed to the world’s surfers by a cult film from the 1970s.","r":"In the line-up, stay humble and give priority to the locals; on a paddleboard, don’t approach the bay’s dolphins, which are already under heavy pressure from boats.","s":"May to September: the southern winter sends in southerly swells, and the trade wind often blows offshore across the bay.","a":[["Le Morne Brabant","A UNESCO-listed mountain, once a refuge for runaway slaves, with a hike overlooking the lagoon."],["Black River Gorges National Park","Forests and deep gorges, with trails, viewpoints and endemic birds."],["Seven Coloured Earths","Dunes of volcanic earth in shifting hues, near a beautiful waterfall."],["Chamarel","An upland village among sugar cane and forests, with viewpoints over the west coast."],["La Preneuse Martello Tower","A 19th-century British defensive tower turned into a small museum."]]},"beauvallon":{"v":"One evening a week, the Bazar Labrin fills the beach with Creole stalls and music, while fishermen bring their boats in under the shade of the takamaka trees facing Silhouette Island.","f":["Palm heart salad","Raw heart of palm, thinly sliced, nicknamed millionaire’s salad, served as a starter."],"r":"Swim between the buoys of the bathing area, as boats and jet skis run offshore, and follow the lifeguards’ flags when the sea picks up.","s":"May to October: under the south-east trade wind, the north-west-facing bay stays sheltered and calm.","a":[["Sainte Anne Marine Park","Eight islets and their reefs, for snorkelling among turtles and lagoon fish."],["Venn’s Town mission ruins","Remains of an old mission school in the forest, with a viewpoint over the west coast."],["National Botanical Gardens","Coco de mer, endemic palms, orchids and giant tortoises."],["Victoria Market","The capital’s colourful market hall, with the catch of the day, tropical fruit and spices."]]},"saintleu":{"v":"A former sugar town of Creole houses, Saint-Leu saw the world’s best surfers battle it out on its left-hander before the shark crisis emptied the line-ups; every day, paragliders glide down from the uplands.","r":"Very shallow reef and a risk of sharks: only surf in the zones and time slots authorised by official decree, never alone and never in murky water.","s":"April to October: the southern winter sends in south-westerly swells that get the left peeling.","a":[["Kélonia","A sea turtle observatory: pools, a care centre and the history of turtle fishing."],["Stella Matutina Museum","A former sugar factory telling the story of sugar cane, vanilla and the island."],["Mascarin Botanical Garden","An upland Creole estate with collections of plants endemic to the Mascarenes."],["Pointe au Sel","A wild basalt cape, with traditional salt pans and a path above the ocean."],["Piton des Neiges","The island’s highest point, climbed by night to watch the sunrise from the summit."]]},"ermitage":{"v":"On Sundays, Réunionese families settle under the filao trees with pots of carri and mats for long picnics, while the children splash about in the lagoon.","r":"The lagoon is part of the marine nature reserve: don’t walk on the coral, don’t feed the fish and stay inside the reef.","s":"May to November: the dry season, with clear water and a calm lagoon, away from the rains and cyclones of the southern summer.","a":[["Le Maïdo","A viewpoint at over 2,000 m above the Cirque de Mafate, best seen early before the clouds."],["Villèle Museum","A former sugar plantation, with its master’s house and the memory of slavery."],["Boucan Canot beach","A white-sand beach lined with coconut palms and terraces, very popular at sunset."],["Jardin d’Éden","A private botanical garden devoted to sacred, medicinal and tropical plants."],["Grotte des Premiers Français","A cave where the island’s first inhabitants are said to have lived, near Saint-Paul."]]},"elgouna":{"v":"A new town built on lagoons and canals north of Hurghada, El Gouna combines marinas, domed Nubian-inspired houses and a large community of kiters based on the windward beaches.","r":"The shallow lagoons hide coral and sea urchins: wear boots, stay in the centres’ marked zones and give learners plenty of space.","s":"May to September: the strongest, most consistent northerly wind, and warm water that doesn’t call for a thick wetsuit."},"abudabbab":{"v":"On the still-wild coast of Marsa Alam, between bare mountains and fringing reefs, the bay lives to the rhythm of turtles grazing on the seagrass, far from the bustle of Hurghada.","r":"Never chase turtles or the dugong: keep your distance, swim at the surface above the seagrass and don’t put your fins down on the bottom.","s":"June to October: the warmest water and often calm seas in the morning, ideal for swimming above the seagrass.","lbl":"Dive camp"},"djerba":{"v":"A flat island of whitewashed houchs and small fortified mosques, Djerba still catches octopus with gargoulettes, clay jars shaped by the potters of Guellala.","l":"Tunisian Arabic, French, Berber","f":["Djerbian rice","Steamed rice with meat, chard, herbs and spices, the island’s own recipe."],"r":"At El Ghriba, modest clothing is required. In the villages, always ask before photographing people, especially women.","s":"May to October: steady breezes and warm water in the shallow lagoons, with more people in July–August.","a":[["El Ghriba Synagogue","An ancient synagogue with blue tiles, a place of Jewish pilgrimage in spring."],["Houmt Souk","Souks, fondouks and the Borj el-Kebir fort facing the fishing harbour."],["Mosque of the Turks","A whitewashed Ottoman-era mosque in the heart of Houmt Souk."],["Midoun","The island’s second town, with its market and lively lanes."]],"lbl":"Guest houch"},"sunsetbeach":{"v":"On the North Shore, everything follows the winter swells: surfers’ houses, food trucks and rural towns, with the Triple Crown drawing the pros to this coast every season.","l":"English, Hawaiian","h":["Aloha","“hello” in Hawaiian, full of goodwill"],"f":["Kahuku garlic shrimp","Shrimp sautéed in garlic and butter, served with rice from roadside trucks."],"r":"Here, locals and pros own the peak: watch for a long time from the beach, only paddle out if you can handle big surf, and leave the honu turtles in peace on the sand.","s":"November to February: the most powerful north-westerly swells; in summer, the ocean goes almost flat.","a":[["Pipeline","A legendary reef barrel, to watch from Ehukai beach on swell days."],["Waimea Bay","The bay for really big days in winter, with calm water and rock jumping in summer."],["Waimea Valley","A sacred Hawaiian valley, with a botanical garden and a path to a waterfall."],["Puʻu o Mahuka Heiau","Oahu’s largest ancient temple, perched above Waimea Bay."]]},"jaws":{"v":"Maui’s north coast of former pineapple and sugar cane fields: Paia, once a sugar town, lives for windsurfing and surfing, and on the days of really big swell everyone heads off to watch Jaws from the cliff.","l":"English, Hawaiian","h":["Aloha","“hello” in Hawaiian, full of goodwill"],"f":["Poke","Cubes of raw fish marinated in soy sauce and sesame oil, often served on rice."],"r":"Jaws is only surfed with a safety team: watch from the cliff, stay on the paths and respect the private farmland on the plateau.","s":"November to March: only big north-westerly swells wake it up, a few days each winter.","a":[["Hoʻokipa","A world-famous windsurfing spot, with turtles hauling out on the beach in the evening."],["Hosmer’s Grove (Haleakalā)","A forest trail at the entrance to Haleakalā National Park, rich in birdlife."],["Keʻanae Arboretum","A tropical garden and taro patches beside the Road to Hana."],["Bailey House Museum","A missionary house turned museum of Hawaiian history and crafts."],["Sugar Museum","The history of Maui’s sugar cane plantations and immigrant workers."]]},"kona":{"v":"On the Big Island’s leeward coast, lava flows, upland coffee farms and Hawaiian royal sites line calm bays where manta rays come at night to filter plankton.","l":"English, Hawaiian","h":["Aloha","“hello” in Hawaiian, full of goodwill"],"f":["Kona coffee","Grown on the volcanic slopes above the coast, best tasted straight from the farm."],"r":"Let the mantas come to you: never touch them, keep your hands on the light board or on the bottom and don’t fin towards them.","s":"All year round: the mantas come almost every night, and the sea is often calmer from April to October.","a":[["Puʻuhonua o Hōnaunau","A former royal place of refuge, with a stone wall, a temple and wooden statues."],["Kealakekua Bay","A protected marine bay, with reef snorkelling and a monument to Captain Cook."],["Kahaluʻu Bay","A sheltered bay ideal for a first snorkel among turtles and fish."],["Huliheʻe Palace","A former summer residence of Hawaiian royalty, now a museum."],["Kona Coffee Living History Farm","A restored coffee farm that tells the story of the planters’ families."]]},"hanalei":{"v":"At the end of the north coast road, Hanalei lines up flooded taro fields, one-lane bridges and an old pier, facing a bay where locals grew up on big winter days.","l":"English, Hawaiian","h":["Aloha","“hello” in Hawaiian, full of goodwill"],"f":["Poi","Taro purée, often fermented; the taro grows in the valley just behind the bay."],"r":"At the one-lane bridges, cars cross in groups, taking turns. On the beach, let the monk seals rest at a good distance.","s":"October to March: northerly swells get the bay working, for seasoned surfers; in summer, it’s paddleboarding on calm water.","a":[["Nā Pali Coast","Dizzying cliffs and hanging valleys, reached on foot or by sea."],["Kalalau Trail","A demanding coastal trail along the Nā Pali cliffs."],["Kīlauea Point","A lighthouse and seabird colonies on a cliff facing the open sea."],["Limahuli Garden","A terraced garden of Hawaiian plants at the foot of the mountains."],["Waiʻoli Mission","A mission house and green wooden church in the heart of Hanalei."]]},"kailua":{"v":"On Oahu’s windward coast, Kailua keeps traces of the Hawaiian past, such as the Kawainui fishpond and Ulupō Heiau, behind a beach where the trade winds carry kites and outrigger canoes.","l":"English, Hawaiian","h":["Aloha","“hello” in Hawaiian, full of goodwill"],"f":["Malasadas","Portuguese-style doughnuts rolled in sugar, a speciality of Oahu’s bakeries."],"r":"The Mokulua islets are a seabird sanctuary: only land on the beach of Moku Nui and keep away from the nesting areas.","s":"April to September: the most consistent easterly trade winds, warm water and a lagoon often glassy in the morning.","a":[["Lanikai Beach","Fine sand and turquoise water facing the Mokulua islets."],["Pali Lookout","A cliff in the Koʻolau Mountains with a vast view over the windward coast."],["Hanauma Bay","An old crater open to the sea, a marine reserve for snorkelling."],["Diamond Head","A volcanic cone to climb to the summit, with views over Waikīkī."],["Byodo-In Temple","A replica of a Japanese temple at the foot of the cliffs, with a koi pond."]]},"rinconca":{"v":"On the border of Santa Barbara and Ventura counties, this cobblestone point is a place of pilgrimage for Californian surfing, between the avocado orchards of Carpinteria and a coast once home to the Chumash.","f":["Santa Barbara sea urchin","Hand-harvested by divers in the channel and eaten raw at the fishmongers on the harbour."],"r":"The line-up is crowded and very local: wait your turn at the peak, never paddle inside others and park only in authorised spaces.","s":"November to March: north-westerly winter swells wrap around the point in long rights.","a":[["Santa Barbara","A town of white facades and tiled roofs, with a Spanish mission and a pier."],["Carpinteria beach","A sheltered family beach, with seals resting on the nearby rocks."],["Ojai","A small artists’ town in a valley of orange groves at the foot of the mountains."],["Lake Casitas","A reservoir surrounded by hills, for peaceful kayaking and fishing."]]},"pointlobos":{"v":"Between Carmel and Big Sur, Point Lobos keeps traces of its whalers and abalone fishermen, on a coast of twisted cypresses where Monterey long lived to the rhythm of its sardine canneries.","f":["Clam chowder","Creamy clam soup served in a sourdough bread bowl, down on the wharf."],"r":"Dives in the reserve must be booked in advance and the number of dive teams is limited. Take nothing and keep your distance from otters and seals.","s":"September to November: the upwelling eases, the water clears and the sea often stays calm before the winter storms.","a":[["Monterey Bay Aquarium","A huge kelp forest tank, sea otters and local Pacific wildlife."],["Cannery Row","The old street of sardine canneries, celebrated by Steinbeck."],["Carmel-by-the-Sea","A village of fairy-tale cottages, galleries and a white-sand beach beneath the cypresses."],["Tor House and Hawk Tower","A stone house and tower built by hand by the poet Robinson Jeffers."]]},"catalina":{"v":"Avalon moves at the pace of golf carts, beneath the Art Deco casino that overlooks the bay; the island owes a great deal to the Wrigley family and keeps bison in its hills.","r":"Casino Point park is a marine reserve: take nothing, don’t feed the garibaldis and stay within the marked area, away from boats.","s":"July to October: the warmest water of the year and often better visibility in the kelp.","a":[["Wrigley Memorial and Botanic Garden","A garden of plants endemic to the island around a memorial overlooking the valley."],["Avalon","A small town rising like an amphitheatre above the bay, with its promenade and harbour."],["Catalina Isthmus (Two Harbors)","A narrow strip of land between two bays, the starting point for wild hikes."],["Mount Ada","The Wrigleys’ former summer residence, perched above Avalon Bay."]]},"keylargo":{"v":"The first of the Keys on the Overseas Highway, Key Largo lives off diving and fishing, among mangroves, wooden marinas and the archipelago’s own “conch” culture.","f":["Key lime pie","A Key lime tart on a biscuit base, the emblem of the archipelago."],"r":"On the reef, tie the boat to the mooring buoys provided, never drop anchor on the coral and don’t put your hands or fins on the coral.","s":"April to June: often calm seas, warm water and good visibility, before the height of hurricane season.","a":[["Molasses Reef","A reef very popular with divers, rich in fish and coral spurs."],["Spiegel Grove wreck","A large naval ship deliberately sunk to form an artificial reef."],["Key Largo Hammock","Trails through a tropical dry forest typical of the Keys, rich in birdlife."],["African Queen","The boat from the 1951 film, moored in a Key Largo marina."]]},"cocoabeach":{"v":"On the Space Coast, you sometimes surf while watching a rocket lift off from nearby Cape Canaveral; the town lives between its fishing pier, giant surf shops and a laid-back beach culture.","r":"Around the pier, stay well away from anglers’ lines. In summer, don’t touch the marked sea turtle nests on the beach.","s":"September to November: more frequent hurricane and nor’easter swells, with the water still warm.","a":[["Kennedy Space Center","Rockets, the Space Shuttle Atlantis and the history of NASA’s lunar missions."],["Thousand Islands","Mangrove islets in the lagoon to explore by kayak, with manatees and dolphins."],["Cape Canaveral Space Museum","Old launch pads and rockets from the early days of space exploration."]]},"hatteras":{"v":"At the elbow of the Outer Banks, the fishing villages of Buxton and Hatteras face the “Graveyard of the Atlantic”, where currents and shoals have swallowed countless ships.","f":["Hatteras clam chowder","A clear broth with clams, potatoes and bacon, with no cream or tomato."],"r":"Only drive on the beach via authorised access points, with a permit, and respect areas closed for turtle and bird nesting.","s":"September to November: tropical storm and nor’easter swells, water still warm and quieter beaches.","a":[["Cape Hatteras Lighthouse","A black-and-white striped lighthouse, moved back from the shore to escape erosion."],["Cape Hatteras National Seashore","Long wild beaches, dunes and unspoilt marshes along the barrier islands."],["Graveyard of the Atlantic Museum","Wrecks, shipwrecks and the maritime history of the Outer Banks."]]},"lawrencetown":{"v":"On the Eastern Shore, very close to Halifax, a small community of surfers takes on the North Atlantic all year round, not far from the Acadian villages of Chezzetcook and their salt marshes.","f":["Nova Scotia lobster","Boiled and served whole or in a roll, in the shacks and restaurants along the coast."],"r":"The beach is a provincial park: park in the car parks, keep off the fragile dunes and give priority to the locals at the peak.","s":"September to November: swells from Atlantic storms and hurricanes, water still bearable in a thick wetsuit.","a":[["Maritime Museum of the Atlantic","Halifax’s naval history, from the Titanic to the 1917 explosion."],["Pier 21 Museum of Immigration","The former terminal where immigrants arriving in Canada by sea came ashore."],["Acadian House Museum","An old house that brings to life the Acadian families of Chezzetcook."],["Georges Island","A fortified islet with a lighthouse in the middle of Halifax Harbour."]]},"puntamita":{"v":"At the tip of Banderas Bay, the small fishing village of El Anclote watches the pangas set off for the Marietas Islands, away from the luxury residences that occupy the point.","f":["Pescado zarandeado","A fish split in half, marinated in chillies and grilled over embers, a speciality of Nayarit."],"r":"The Marietas Islands are a national park with limited access: go with an authorised operator and never put your feet on the coral.","s":"May to October: southerly swells get the points working; in winter, smaller waves ideal for beginners."},"scorpionbay":{"v":"At the end of long dirt tracks, the fishing village of San Juanico shares its bay with nomadic surfers in vans, under an exceptionally clear desert sky.","r":"The village is short of water and rubbish collection: save fresh water, take your rubbish away with you and drive at walking pace through San Juanico.","s":"May to October: southerly swells peel along the points, with warmer water at the end of summer."},"laventana":{"v":"A small fishing village facing Cerralvo Island, La Ventana lives to the rhythm of North American kiters in winter, while the pangas still head out to fish in the Sea of Cortez.","r":"The fishermen’s pangas have priority on the beach: don’t rig up in their launch area and drive at walking pace through the village.","s":"December to February: the north wind is at its most consistent; the water is cooler, so bring a wetsuit."},"ikkil":{"v":"Deep in Maya country, near Chichén Itzá, the cenote is a reminder that these natural wells were a vital water source for the Maya and a sacred gateway to the underworld.","l":"Spanish, Yucatec Maya","f":["Cochinita pibil","Pork marinated in achiote and bitter orange, slow-cooked in banana leaves."],"r":"Take the required shower before entering the water, without sunscreen or insect repellent, to protect this freshwater well.","s":"November to March: the dry season and bearable heat; come early to avoid the groups coming from Chichén Itzá.","a":[["Chichén Itzá","A great Maya city with the Pyramid of Kukulcán, the ball court and the observatory."],["Sacred Cenote","The Maya offering well, at the end of a causeway at Chichén Itzá."],["Great Museum of Chichén Itzá","Maya objects found at the site, including offerings from the Sacred Cenote."]]},"soupbowl":{"v":"On the Atlantic-battered east coast, Bathsheba lines up mushroom-shaped rocks, fishermen’s houses and rum shops, far from the chic beaches of the west coast.","r":"Shallow reef and a local line-up: watch first, don’t hog the waves and only swim in the sheltered pools, as the currents are fierce.","s":"August to March: hurricane and then winter swells from the North Atlantic, which keep the right-hander working.","a":[["Harrison’s Cave","A limestone cave with stalactites and underground rivers, toured by little train."],["Andromeda Botanic Gardens","A terraced tropical garden overlooking the Bathsheba coast."],["Hunte’s Gardens","A lush garden tucked in the bottom of a gully, planted with palms and flowers."],["Cherry Tree Hill","A hill beneath mahogany trees with a sweeping view over the wild north-east coast."],["Flower Forest","A former plantation turned botanical garden in the heart of the hills."]]},"hadicurari":{"v":"Nicknamed Fisherman’s Huts after the fishermen’s shacks that once lined the beach, Hadicurari combines colourful sails and diving pelicans, beneath divi-divi trees bent by the trade wind.","r":"Windsurfers and kiters share the water: respect the launch lanes and give way to fishermen heading back to shore.","s":"May to August: the trade winds at their strongest and very consistent; the wind drops noticeably from September to November.","a":[["Arikok National Park","Cactus-covered hills, caves with Amerindian paintings and wild coves."],["California Lighthouse","A stone lighthouse on a limestone plateau, with a fine view over the northern tip."],["Antilla wreck","A large cargo ship scuttled during the war, a wreck popular with divers."],["Arashi Beach","A calm white-sand beach for swimming and snorkelling."],["Bubali Bird Sanctuary","A wetland for watching herons, cormorants and other waterbirds."]]},"playalagun":{"v":"In rural Bandabou, the cove still shelters the boats of Lagún’s fishermen, not far from the old plantations where the slave revolt led by Tula began.","r":"Leave room for the moored fishing boats, enter the water from the beach without walking on the reef and watch the turtles without following them.","s":"September to November: weaker trade winds, a very calm sea and often excellent visibility.","a":[["Christoffel Park","A national park of cacti and hills, with the climb up Mount Christoffel."],["Grote Knip (Playa Abou)","A large white-sand beach with turquoise water, surrounded by cliffs."],["Playa Kalki","A cove renowned for diving, with mushroom-shaped corals."],["Watamula","A wild point on the north coast, where the swell spouts up through holes in the rock."]]},"stingraycity":{"v":"It all supposedly began with fishermen cleaning their catch in the shelter of the North Sound reef: the rays got into the habit of coming, and the sandbar became a Caymanian meeting place.","r":"Never take a ray out of the water and only feed one with your guide. Shuffle your feet in the sand so you don’t step on one.","s":"April to June: calmer trade winds and crystal-clear water over the sandbar, before hurricane season.","a":[["Seven Mile Beach","A long coral-sand beach with calm water on the west coast."],["Queen Elizabeth II Botanic Park","Forest trails, flower gardens and endemic blue iguanas."],["Pedro St. James","The island’s oldest stone house, the cradle of its political life."],["Mastic Reserve","A trail through primary dry forest and unspoilt wetlands."],["Kittiwake wreck","A rescue ship sunk as an artificial reef, accessible by snorkel."]]},"roatan":{"v":"A sandy street lined with bars on stilts and dive centres, West End mixes the islanders’ Creole English, mainland Spanish and travellers from all over the world, with the reef just a few strokes away.","f":["Coco bread","A soft little coconut milk bread roll, a legacy of island and Garifuna cooking."],"r":"Choose a dive centre that ties up to the marine park’s buoys and work on your buoyancy: the reefs close to shore are very busy.","s":"March to September: less rain, calm seas and better visibility before the wet autumn season."},"bocasdeltoro":{"v":"Wooden houses on stilts, calypso and English Creole on the jetties: the archipelago blends Afro-Caribbean heritage, Ngäbe villages and memories of the banana era, and everything is done by water taxi.","l":"Spanish, English Creole, Ngäbere","f":["Rondón","A Caribbean stew of fish and root vegetables simmered in coconut milk."],"r":"On Bastimentos, look at the little red frogs without touching them, and never take the starfish out of the water at Boca del Drago.","s":"December to March: Caribbean winter swells on the reefs, with a small second season in June and July.","a":[["Isla Bastimentos National Marine Park","Reefs, mangroves and the islets of Cayos Zapatilla, to explore by boat and with a mask."],["Bastimentos Island","The Afro-Caribbean village of Old Bank, rainforest and wild beaches such as Red Frog Beach."],["Bocas Town","The archipelago’s little capital, with colourful wooden houses and restaurants on stilts."],["Carenero Island","A wooded island facing Bocas Town, with a few reef waves and bars right on the water."],["San San-Pond Sak Wetland","A protected lagoon and marshes on the mainland, a refuge for manatees and sea turtles."]]},"popoyo":{"v":"Dirt tracks, cattle ranches and fishermen hauling their boats onto the sand: Popoyo is still a hamlet on the Rivas coast, living to the rhythm of the tides and the wind that comes down from the lake.","r":"The reef is exposed at low tide: check the tide times, wear booties and leave the big Outer Reef sessions to seasoned surfers.","s":"April to October: more powerful South Pacific swells, offshore wind in the morning and fewer people than in the dry season."},"elzonte":{"v":"A fishing village of volcanic sand turned “Bitcoin Beach”, where you sometimes pay for your pupusas in cryptocurrency and local kids learn to surf on the cobblestone right.","f":["Mariscada","A creamy seafood soup, a speciality of the comedores on the La Libertad coast."],"r":"On the point, the bottom is made of big slippery cobbles: paddle in and out through the channel, and let the village surfers take their waves.","s":"March to October: consistent southern swells on the right, warm water and showers mostly in the afternoon.","a":[["La Libertad","A lively fishing port with its big pier, its fish market and the Punta Roca wave."],["Tamanique","A village in the coastal hills known for its waterfalls, reached on a guided hike."],["El Palmarcito","A quiet little cove with cobbled streets and a gentle wave for beginners."]]},"cousteau":{"v":"On the leeward coast, fishermen bring their boats in onto the black sand of Malendure, and Bouillante owes its name to the hot springs that bubble up right into the sea.","f":["Fish blaff","Fresh fish poached in a broth with lime, chilli and herbs."],"r":"In the reserve, fishing is banned and mooring is on buoys only: touch neither corals nor turtles, and fin well away from the bottom.","s":"December to May: dry season, calm sea on the leeward side and often excellent visibility around the islets.","a":[["Saut de l’Acomat","A waterfall in the rainforest, with a pool for swimming at the foot of the falls."],["Deshaies","A fishing village around a bay, with a botanical garden and Grande Anse beach close by."],["Fort Delgrès","A colonial fort overlooking Basse-Terre, a landmark of the resistance to the reinstatement of slavery."],["Guadeloupe Zoo","Parc des Mamelles, deep in the rainforest, with a rope bridge above the canopy."]],"lbl":"Gîte or B&B"},"ansedufour":{"v":"Fishing yawls pulled up on the sand, nets drying and a little restaurant at the back of the cove: Anse Dufour lives from fishing, a stone’s throw from the dark sand of Anse Noire.","f":["Grilled fish with sauce chien","The catch of the day, grilled and topped with a sauce of herbs, lime and chilli."],"r":"Keep your distance from the turtles, don’t chase them and swim outside the fishing boats’ channel. The car park is tiny: come early.","s":"December to May: dry season, calm sea and clear water for watching the turtles on the seagrass.","a":[["Les Anses-d’Arlet","A picture-postcard village with its church facing the sea and its jetty over turquoise water."],["Cap 110 Memorial","White stone statues facing the sea, in memory of the wreck of a slave ship."],["La Savane des Esclaves","A reconstructed village telling the story of slavery and rural life in Martinique."],["Fort Saint-Louis","A Vauban-style fort on the bay of Fort-de-France, still a navy base."],["Les Trois-Îlets","A peaceful small town, birthplace of Empress Joséphine, surrounded by beaches and mangroves."]]},"thunderball":{"v":"A string of turquoise cays you get around by small boat: on Staniel Cay, a few colourful houses, an airstrip and a yacht club keep the island going between fishing and sailing.","r":"Enter the grotto at slack low tide, as the current is strong the rest of the time, and don’t touch the corals on the ceiling.","s":"April to June: often calm sea, already warm water and the hurricane season not yet started.","a":[["Pig Beach","A beach on Big Major Cay famous for its pigs that swim out towards the boats."],["Staniel Cay","A small inhabited island in the Exumas, a base for boat trips to the neighbouring cays."],["Compass Cay","An island with a sheltered natural harbour, where you swim with nurse sharks used to the dock."]]},"itamambuca":{"v":"Atlantic forest tumbling into the ocean and caiçara culture, that of the fishermen of the São Paulo coast: Ubatuba calls itself the state’s surf capital, and Itamambuca is its heart.","f":["Azul-marinho","A caiçara dish of fish stewed with green bananas that turn blue as they cook."],"r":"At the peak near the river mouth, the local line-up is close-knit: wait your turn and give priority to those who have surfed here since childhood.","s":"April to September: cold fronts and consistent south swells, cooler water and a quiet beach outside the holidays.","a":[["Ilha Anchieta State Park","A wooded island with the ruins of an old penal colony, trails and coves for swimming with the fish."],["Serra do Mar State Park","A vast massif of Atlantic forest, with waterfalls and trails above the coast."],["Ilha das Couves","An islet with clear water and white sand beaches, perfect for a snorkelling trip."],["Ubatuba","A coastal town with dozens of beaches and a sea turtle protection centre."]]},"portodegalinhas":{"v":"Sailing jangadas, fish sculptures in the streets and a name inherited, so they say, from enslaved people landed in secret under the code word “chickens”: Porto de Galinhas lives to the rhythm of the low tides.","f":["Bolo de rolo","A roll cake in very thin layers filled with guava, the pride of Pernambuco."],"r":"In the natural pools, don’t walk on the reef and don’t feed the fish: stick to the marked areas shown by the jangadeiros.","s":"September to March: dry season, crystal-clear water, and visit at the big low tides for well-formed pools.","a":[["Santo Aleixo Island","An unspoilt island with two pale sand beaches, reached by boat for a calm swim."],["Cabo de Santo Agostinho","A cape with coves such as Calhetas and Gaibu, and colonial remains on the heights."]]},"bonito":{"v":"In the heart of the fazendas of Mato Grosso do Sul, Bonito has built its tourism on protecting its rivers: every outing is guided, booked and limited in visitor numbers.","f":["Ventrecha de pacu","The belly of the pacu, a fish from the Pantanal rivers, served fried or grilled."],"r":"No sunscreen or repellent before getting in the water, and never put your feet on the bottom: you would cloud the water and damage the riverbed.","s":"May to September: dry season, rivers at their clearest and little rain to cloud the water."},"praiamole":{"v":"Florianópolis keeps the mark of its Azorean settlers: bobbin lace, fishing villages and oyster farms, while Praia Mole, young and bohemian, welcomes surfers and paragliders.","f":["Florianópolis oysters","Farmed in the island’s bays, served raw, gratinated or steamed."],"r":"The rip currents are strong: swim in front of the guarda-vidas posts and leave the peaks near the rocks to the regulars.","s":"April to August: cold fronts and well-lined-up south swells, but cool water that calls for a wetsuit.","a":[["Lagoa da Conceição","A large lagoon lined with dunes, bars and restaurants, for paddling or strolling at sunset."],["Barra da Lagoa","A fishing village at the mouth of the lagoon, with its colourful boats and little coves."],["Campeche Island","A protected island with turquoise water, known for its rock carvings and guided trails."],["Praia de Moçambique","The island’s longest beach, wild and backed by the forest of Rio Vermelho park."],["Hercílio Luz Bridge","A historic suspension bridge linking the island to the mainland, the emblem of Florianópolis."]]},"maragogi":{"v":"On the Costa dos Corais, a large marine protected area, the jangadas head out to the reef when the sea pulls back, between coconut groves and the sugar cane fields of the hinterland.","f":["Caldinho de sururu","A thick broth of small lagoon mussels, an emblem of Alagoas cooking."],"r":"Visits to the galés are limited: go with a licensed operator, don’t stand on the coral and don’t feed the fish.","s":"September to March: dry season, crystal-clear water and big low tides that uncover the pools.","a":[["Porto de Pedras","A village on the Ecological Route of the Miracles, known for its manatee protection project."],["Japaratinga","A peaceful small town with long palm-lined beaches and natural pools at low tide."],["Porto Calvo","A small colonial inland town, marked by the history of the wars against the Dutch."]]},"pipa":{"v":"A former fishing village turned bohemian haunt: cobbled streets full of bars in the evening, ochre cliffs above the sand and dolphins swimming into the bay in the morning.","f":["Carne de sol","Salted, dried beef, served grilled with cassava and clarified butter."],"r":"Don’t chase the dolphins of Baía dos Golfinhos, get there at low tide, and don’t sit at the foot of the crumbly cliffs.","s":"September to March: dry season, warm water and small, consistent waves for progressing, with crowds in January.","a":[["Tibau do Sul","A village on the edge of the Guaraíras lagoon, known for its sunsets over the water."],["Baía Formosa","A quiet fishing town at the back of a large bay, with good point waves."],["Mata da Estrela Reserve","One of the largest remnants of Atlantic forest in the Nordeste, between dunes and lagoons."]]},"prea":{"v":"A fishing hamlet with sandy streets where kite schools rub shoulders with jangadas, forró rings out in the evening and buggies race along the beach towards Jericoacoara.","f":["Baião de dois","Rice and beans cooked together with queijo coalho, a Ceará classic."],"r":"Ride well offshore of the jangadas and the fishermen’s nets, and stay clear of swimmers when you cruise along the beach at Jeri.","s":"July to January: strong, steady trade winds, dry skies and lagoons still full at the start of the season.","a":[["Jericoacoara National Park","Dunes, lagoons and the rock arch of Pedra Furada, to discover on foot or by buggy."],["Jericoacoara","A village with sandy streets, where you climb the big dune to watch the sunset."],["Jijoca de Jericoacoara","A neighbouring town by Lagoa do Paraíso, a freshwater lagoon with hammocks strung above the water."]],"lbl":"Pousada"},"atins":{"v":"A fishing village with no paved road, reached by 4x4 or by boat along the Rio Preguiças, where life unfolds between the river mouth, the white dunes and the fishing huts.","r":"In the national park, vehicles have no business in the dunes: explore on foot or with a local guide, and take all your rubbish away with you.","s":"July to December: strong, steady wind, and until September lagoons still full in the dunes.","a":[["Mandacaru Lighthouse","A lighthouse you can climb to take in the river mouth, the mangroves and the dunes."],["Rio Preguiças","A river lined with mangroves and palms, travelled by boat towards the villages and the sea."]],"lbl":"Pousada"},"puntahermosa":{"v":"A seaside resort for Lima’s residents, with summer houses on the cliff, Punta Hermosa is a cradle of Peruvian surfing: world champion Sofía Mulanovich grew up here.","r":"At Pico Alto and Punta Rocas, the big sessions belong to the local experts: watch first and strictly respect priority.","s":"April to October: big south swells that wake up the reefs, but cold water and the grey skies of the garúa.","a":[["Pachacámac Sanctuary","A vast pre-Inca and Inca archaeological site, with adobe pyramids and a Temple of the Sun."],["Pucusana","A colourful fishing port where you board a boat to see sea lions and seabirds."],["Pachacámac Islands","Rocky islets offshore, home to seabirds and sea lions, visible by boat."],["San Bartolo","A neighbouring seaside village, perched on the cliff above several point waves."]]},"paracas":{"v":"Ochre desert plunging into a cold sea teeming with fish: the Paracas culture, famous for its textiles, lived here, and the fishing boats of El Chaco port come home laden with seafood.","f":["Tejas from Ica","Little sweets filled with manjar blanco and fruit, coated in sugar."],"r":"In the reserve, drive only on the marked tracks and keep your distance from the flamingos, sea lions and seabirds.","s":"April to November: stronger afternoon wind over a flat bay; bring a wetsuit, the water stays cool.","a":[["Paracas National Reserve","Red cliffs, deserted beaches and seabird colonies on the desert peninsula."],["Paracas Candelabra","A mysterious giant geoglyph carved into a hillside, visible from the sea."],["San Gallán Island","A wild island home to sea lions, known to surfers for its right reached by boat."],["Pisco","The port town that gave its name to the grape brandy, with its Plaza de Armas."]]},"anakena":{"v":"According to tradition, this is where Hotu Matu’a, the first king of Rapa Nui, came ashore: the moai crowned with their pukao watch over the beach, where families come for Sunday picnics.","l":"Spanish, Rapa Nui","h":["’Iorana","“hello” in Rapa Nui, which will be returned to you with a smile"],"f":["Umu","Meat, fish and sweet potatoes cooked underground on hot stones, for celebrations."],"r":"Never touch a moai and don’t climb onto the ahu, the sacred platforms: it is forbidden and heavily penalised.","s":"December to March: southern summer, milder water and an often calm sea in the sheltered bay.","a":[["Rano Raraku","The moai quarry, where dozens of unfinished statues emerge from the slopes of the volcano."],["Ahu Tongariki","The island’s largest platform, with fifteen moai lined up facing inland."],["Ahu Akivi","Seven moai inland, the only ones facing the ocean."],["Mount Terevaka","The island’s highest point, to climb on foot for a view of the whole ocean around."]]},"salinasec":{"v":"The summer beach of Guayaquil’s residents, lined with tower blocks and a lively malecón, Salinas also lives from big-game fishing and sees humpback whales pass offshore in season.","f":["Encebollado","A soup of tuna, cassava and pickled onions, the morning cure on the Ecuadorian coast."],"r":"At La Chocolatera, a military zone and marine reserve, stay on the marked paths and don’t disturb the sea lions on the rocks.","s":"December to April: consistent north swells and warm water, before the cold current returns.","a":[["La Chocolatera","The westernmost point of mainland Ecuador, where waves crash against the rocks."],["Megaterio Paleontological Museum","A reconstructed skeleton of a giant sloth and fossils of prehistoric local fauna."],["Santa Elena","The provincial capital and its Lovers of Sumpa museum, the prehistoric grave of an embracing couple."]]},"kickerrock":{"v":"In Puerto Baquerizo Moreno, the archipelago’s administrative capital, sea lions sleep on the benches of the malecón and fishermen share the harbour with the dive boats.","r":"On the malecón, walk around the sea lions without waking or touching them, and dive at León Dormido only with a park-licensed operator.","s":"December to May: warmer water, calm sea and good visibility; from June to November, more hammerhead sharks."},"tayrona":{"v":"The ancestral land of the Kogi, Arhuaco and Wiwa peoples, the park closes for a few weeks each year to let the Sierra rest, and the ruins of Pueblito recall the Tayrona civilisation.","r":"Never swim at Arrecifes, with its deadly currents, and come in without alcohol or single-use plastic, both banned in the park.","s":"December to March: dry season, clearer sea for snorkelling, but crowds at the end of the year.","lbl":"Hammock or dorm"},"nuqui":{"v":"With no road to reach it, the Afro-Colombian coast of Chocó lives from canoe fishing and the marimba of currulao, between jungle, hot springs and Emberá villages.","l":"Spanish, Emberá","f":["Tapao de pescado","Fish braised in a covered pot with green plantain and Pacific herbs."],"r":"During whale season, insist on boats that keep their distance, and choose community-run accommodation.","s":"April to October: more consistent South Pacific swells, with humpback whales as a bonus from July.","lbl":"Beach cabaña"},"cabodelavela":{"v":"For the Wayuu, the cape is Jepirra, the place where the souls of the dead depart: the rancherías string up their hammocks facing the sea and the women weave colourful mochilas.","l":"Spanish, Wayuunaiki","f":["Friche","Young goat fried in its own fat, a festive dish of Wayuu families."],"r":"You are on Wayuu land: ask before taking photos, buy mochilas from the weavers and save fresh water, which is very scarce here.","s":"December to August: strong, almost daily trade winds, dry skies and flat water in the bay.","lbl":"Hammock in a ranchería"},"losroques":{"v":"On Gran Roque, the archipelago’s only village, there are no cars: sandy lanes, colourful posadas and lobster fishermen who head out to the cays every morning.","f":["Grilled lobster","Caught in the archipelago during the open season, simply grilled in the posadas."],"r":"It’s a national park: don’t collect shells or coral, and never order lobster outside the fishing season.","s":"April to June: gentler trade winds, calm lagoons and clear water, away from the end-of-year and August holidays.","a":[["Cayo de Agua","Two islets joined by a strip of white sand between two turquoise lagoons."],["Gran Roque","A village island with colourful houses, and an old lighthouse on the hill for the view."],["Crasquí","A long white sand beach and fishermen’s huts, for swimming and eating fresh fish."],["Dos Mosquises","Islets home to a research station that raises and releases sea turtles."]],"lbl":"Posada"},"lapaloma":{"v":"A small summer town nestled in the pines, where fishermen’s huts sit beside holiday homes and Uruguayan surfers stay loyal even in the cold winter wind.","f":["Buñuelos de algas","Seaweed fritters from the coast, a speciality of the Rocha beaches."],"r":"Cross the dunes on the boardwalks and leave the sea lions resting on the rocks of the point in peace.","s":"March to May: consistent autumn swells, water still mild and beaches emptied after the summer.","a":[["Laguna de Rocha","A protected lagoon separated from the sea by a sandbar, rich in swans and flamingos."],["La Pedrera","A village perched on a cliff above the ocean, popular with surfers and artists."],["Cabo Santa María Lighthouse","A lighthouse you can visit, with a view over the beaches and the rocks of the point."]]},"puertomadryn":{"v":"Born from the arrival of Welsh settlers in the 19th century, Puerto Madryn keeps Welsh teas and chapels in the nearby valley, while its long bay lives to the rhythm of sea lions, divers and the winter return of the southern right whales.","l":"Spanish, a little Welsh","f":["Welsh tea and torta negra","A hearty afternoon tea handed down from the Welsh pioneers, with a black fruit cake."],"r":"In the water with sea lions, let them come to you and never approach the pups at the colony. Whale watching is done only with the authorised operators of the Valdés Peninsula.","s":"December to March: the least cold water of the year and playful sea lions; from June to December, the southern right whales fill the gulf."},"gilit":{"v":"On this motor-free islet, horse carts replace cars, the call to prayer from the Sasak village mingles with the beach bars and turtles graze the seagrass a few strokes from the sand.","f":["Ayam taliwang and plecing kangkung","Chilli grilled chicken and water spinach with sambal, the two Lombok classics."],"r":"The island is Muslim: cover up when you leave the beach, especially during Ramadan. Underwater, don’t touch the turtles and don’t rest on the reef, even for a photo.","s":"May to September: dry season, calm sea and good visibility; rain from December to March sometimes clouds the water."},"desertpoint":{"v":"At the tip of the Sekotong peninsula, a fishing hamlet and a few bamboo huts where surfers watch for the swell for days, hammock strung facing the strait, for a few sessions in the tube.","r":"The reef sits just below the surface at low tide: booties and a helmet are no luxury. When it’s on, the line-up is packed and tense: wait your turn without dropping in on anyone, and bring enough cash.","s":"June to September: big south-west swells and offshore south-east trade winds; you need a powerful swell and the right tide.","lbl":"Surf hut"},"lakeypeak":{"v":"Near the village of Hu’u, in the Muslim, rural east of Sumbawa, a row of losmen faces the reef; between sessions, life follows the fishermen, the maize fields and the call to prayer.","r":"Sumbawa is conservative: cover your shoulders and knees as soon as you leave the beach. The peak is often busy: respect priority and beware of the sharp reef at low tide.","s":"May to September: dry season, consistent southern swells and offshore wind in the morning, before the trade wind picks up.","lbl":"Surf losmen"},"wakatobi":{"v":"A remote archipelago where the Bajau, a sea people, still live in stilt villages above the lagoon, while fishing canoes glide between untouched coral walls.","f":["Kasuami","A steamed cone of grated cassava, the staple of local meals, served with fish."],"r":"In Bajau villages, ask before taking photos and buy neither shells nor turtle. The national marine park charges a fee: pay it and don’t touch the reef.","s":"April to June and September to December: calm sea and superb visibility; in July and August, the south-east trade wind ruffles the surface.","lbl":"Homestay"},"lembeh":{"v":"Facing the tuna port of Bitung, in Christian Minahasa country, fishing boats cross paths with divers searching the black volcanic sand, under the gaze of the volcanoes at the northern tip of Sulawesi.","f":["Cakalang fufu","Skipjack tuna smoked on sticks, a Minahasa speciality, flaked and spiced with chilli."],"r":"Never move a creature for a photo and stay stable above the sand so you don’t kick up a cloud. Limit flash on pygmy seahorses.","s":"May to October: dry season, calmer sea and plenty of critters, with slightly cooler water in midsummer.","a":[["Tangkoko Nature Reserve","Rainforest where you can see tarsiers and crested black macaques at dusk."],["Mount Klabat","Sulawesi’s highest volcano, a night climb for a sunrise above the clouds."]]},"krui":{"v":"A former export trading post of the Lampung country, Krui lives between its damar resin gardens, its fishing boats and a handful of surf camps scattered along the wild coast.","f":["Seruit","Grilled fish mashed with sambal, tempoyak and vegetables, a Lampung sharing dish."],"r":"The region is Muslim and rural: cover up in town and in the villages. The local surf scene is growing, so make room for the young local surfers in the line-up.","s":"May to September: dry season, consistent Indian Ocean swells and south-east winds that are often offshore."},"tubbataha":{"v":"In the middle of the Sulu Sea there is no village: only rangers taking turns at their station, among islets where seabirds nest, and dive boats come here only a few months a year.","f":["Tamilok","A mangrove worm served raw kinilaw-style, the daring speciality of Puerto Princesa."],"r":"The park charges an entry fee and requires a ranger briefing: no gloves, no contact with the coral, and stay with your guide in the open-water currents.","s":"Mid-March to mid-June: the only window when the Sulu Sea is calm enough for the overnight crossing, with remarkable visibility.","lbl":"Before boarding"},"boracay":{"v":"The ancestral land of the Ati, Boracay sets its white-sand west coast, where paraw sailboats head out at sunset, against Bulabog on the windward side, where kite schools line up on the beach.","r":"On the island, smoking and drinking on the beach have been banned since its rehabilitation. On Bulabog, leave the launch zone to the schools and ride well away from swimmers and boats.","s":"December to March: the north-east amihan blows steadily over flat, warm water protected by the reef.","a":[["Boracay Island","White Beach and Puka Beach, white sand and paraw trips at sunset."],["Northwest Panay Peninsula Natural Park","The last large lowland forest of the Western Visayas, rich in endemic species."]]},"railay":{"v":"A world-class climbing hotspot, Railay lives to the rhythm of the long-tails landing on the sand; in a cave at the foot of the cliffs, fishermen honour a sea princess with offerings.","f":["Khua kling","Minced meat dry-fried in a fiery curry paste, typical of southern Thailand."],"r":"In Phra Nang Cave, don’t touch the offerings and keep a low profile. In a kayak, keep your distance from the long-tails and don’t feed the monkeys.","s":"November to April: north-east monsoon, calm and clear Andaman Sea, ideal for paddling between the limestone pinnacles.","a":[["Ko Poda","A white sand islet topped by a limestone pinnacle, a good snorkelling spot."],["Ko Thap","A small island linked to its neighbours by a sandbar at low tide."],["Bamboo Island","A flat islet with white sand and turquoise water, on the way to the Phi Phi Islands."],["Wat Kaew Korawaram","A white temple perched above the centre of Krabi, decorated with murals."],["Krabi","A provincial town on its mangrove estuary, with a lively night market."]]},"muine":{"v":"A former fishing village on the Cham coast, Mũi Né keeps its round basket boats in the bay and its fish sauce factories, while kites dance in front of the red sand dunes.","f":["Gỏi cá mai","Small raw fish marinated in lime, rolled in rice paper with herbs."],"r":"Launch and land in the designated zones, away from swimmers and the fishermen’s basket boats. At busy times, give priority to the kiter riding upwind.","s":"December to March: the north-east monsoon blows hard almost every afternoon, over a sea that is sometimes choppy further out."},"perhentian":{"v":"Once a stopover for merchants sailing along the coast, the two islands keep a Malay fishing village on Kecil, jungle trails between the coves and water taxis linking the beaches.","f":["Keropok lekor","Fried sticks of fish and sago, dipped in a chilli sauce, an emblem of Terengganu."],"r":"Terengganu is conservative: cover up in the village and be discreet with alcohol. In the marine park, don’t feed the fish and give the turtles and sharks space.","s":"May to August: glassy sea and excellent visibility; the archipelago almost entirely closes during the monsoon, from November to February.","lbl":"Beach chalet"},"tioman":{"v":"Tioman strings together fishing kampungs linked by concrete paths and jungle, beneath the granite peaks of the south that legend links to a dragon princess turned into an island.","f":["Gulai tempoyak ikan patin","Catfish simmered in a fermented durian curry, the pride of Pahang cooking."],"r":"The marine park charges a fee on arrival and forbids collecting coral or shells. In the kampungs, cover up and share the narrow paths carefully with the scooters.","s":"April to September: calm sea and top visibility; from November to February, the north-east monsoon batters the east coast.","lbl":"Village chalet"},"hikkaduwa":{"v":"A historic stop for travellers on the hippie trail, Hikkaduwa mixes beach bars, fishermen’s outrigger canoes and small Buddhist temples, on a coast that still remembers the 2004 tsunami.","f":["Ambul thiyal","A tangy, almost dry tuna curry with goraka, a speciality of the south coast."],"r":"Don’t feed or touch the turtles in the lagoon, even if others do. At low tide, don’t walk on the reef’s coral.","s":"December to March: north-east monsoon blowing offshore, consistent west swells and a calm sea outside the waves.","a":[["Kothduwa Temple","A Buddhist temple on an island in the Madu River, reached by boat through the mangroves."],["Polgasduwa Hermitage","A forest monastery founded by a German monk, on a wooded islet in the lagoon."],["Peraliya","The village where the 2004 tsunami struck a packed train, now a memorial site."],["Ratgama Lagoon","A brackish lagoon dotted with islets, calm and rich in birds, to explore by rowing boat."],["Seenigama","A village known for its temple dedicated to a local deity, much visited by pilgrims."]]},"kalpitiya":{"v":"On this long sandy peninsula, Tamil, Muslim and Catholic fishermen dry fish on the beaches, kite camps set up on the edge of the lagoon and pods of dolphins cruise offshore.","f":["Karawala","Salted fish dried in the sun on the beaches, fried or cooked in a curry with rice."],"r":"On the lagoon, steer around the fishermen’s nets and canoes. On a dolphin trip, ask the boat to keep its distance and not to cut across the pods’ path.","s":"May to September: the south-west monsoon blows hard and steadily; from December to March, a gentler wind takes over.","a":[["Bar Reef","The country’s largest coral reef, a marine sanctuary for snorkelling and dolphins."],["Kalpitiya","A fishing town at the end of the peninsula, with an old Dutch fort and an old church."]],"lbl":"Kite camp"},"varkala":{"v":"At the foot of the cliffs, Papanasam beach is a sacred place where Hindu families come at dawn to pay homage to their ancestors, while cafés and ayurveda centres line the clifftop path.","l":"Malayalam, English","h":["Namaskaram","“hello” in Malayalam, said with palms joined"],"f":["Karimeen pollichathu","Pearl spot fish marinated in spices and cooked in a banana leaf, the pride of Kerala."],"r":"On the part of the beach set aside for rites, dress modestly and don’t photograph the ceremonies. Keep away from the edge of the cliffs, which crumble.","s":"October to March: after the monsoon, clean swells and warm water, with a smoother sea in the morning; from June to September, it is too rough.","a":[["Janardanaswamy Temple","A very old temple dedicated to Vishnu, on the hill above the sacred beach."],["Sivagiri","A place of pilgrimage where the reformer Sree Narayana Guru rests, visited by devotees in yellow."],["Kappil","A beach where the backwaters meet the sea, between coconut palms and a peaceful lagoon."]]},"havelock":{"v":"Settled by Bengali families who arrived after Partition, the island keeps villages and beaches simply numbered, rice paddies between the coconut palms and fishing boats beached in the mangroves.","l":"Bengali, Hindi, English","h":["Nomoshkar","“hello” in Bengali, said with palms joined"],"f":["Macher jhol","A light Bengali-style fish curry, a legacy of the families who settled on the island."],"r":"Swim at lifeguarded beaches: saltwater crocodiles sometimes frequent the mangroves. Don’t collect coral or shells: taking them off the islands is forbidden.","s":"December to April: calm sea, excellent visibility and dry skies; the monsoon from May to September makes crossings rough.","a":[["Radhanagar Beach","A long white sand beach fringed by forest, famous for its sunsets."],["Neil Island","A small rural island next door, with a natural rock arch and reefs just below the surface."]]},"cokes":{"v":"The wave owes its name to the soft drink bottling plant on Thulusdhoo, a local island where surfers’ guesthouses sit beside fishermen’s homes, the football pitch and the dhonis moored in the harbour.","r":"In the village, cover shoulders and knees, and wear a bikini only on the bikini beach. The line-up is small and resort boats drop people off there: wait your turn and respect the locals.","s":"March to October: consistent southern swells; the biggest arrive from May to August, ideal for Cokes, with more wind.","lbl":"Local island guesthouse"},"hanifaru":{"v":"In Baa Atoll, a biosphere reserve, the villages still live from pole-and-line tuna fishing and lacquerware crafts, and fishermen have long known about the mantas’ seasonal gathering.","r":"In the bay, only snorkelling is allowed, in limited groups supervised by rangers: keep your distance from the mantas, don’t touch them and don’t dive beneath the group.","s":"June to November, especially July to October: monsoon currents concentrate the plankton and draw mantas and sometimes whale sharks.","a":[["Baa Atoll Biosphere Reserve","An atoll listed by UNESCO for its reefs, its seagrass beds and its manta gatherings."],["Dharavandhoo","The local island closest to the bay, with guesthouses, a village and a sandy beach."]],"lbl":"Local island guesthouse"},"miyazaki":{"v":"On the Hyūga coast, land of Japan’s founding myths, Shinto shrines and palm trees line the beaches where generations of Kyūshū surfers meet at dawn before work.","f":["Chicken nanban","Fried chicken dipped in sweet vinegar then topped with tartare sauce, born in the prefecture."],"r":"Line-up etiquette is taken seriously: one person per wave and priority at the peak. Park in the beach car parks and take all your rubbish away with you.","s":"September to November: swells from typhoons and autumn lows, water still warm and beaches quiet after the summer.","a":[["Aoshima Island","A subtropical islet with its shrine, ringed by the rock slabs of the “Devil’s Washboard”."],["Udo Shrine","A vermilion shrine tucked into a sea cave, where you throw lucky clay balls."],["Aya Biosphere Reserve","One of Japan’s largest laurel forests, crossed by a great suspension bridge."],["Miyazaki Shrine","A shrine dedicated to Emperor Jinmu, in a wooded park in town."],["Heiwadai Park","A hilltop park with its Peace Tower and its garden of haniwa figurines."]]},"ishigaki":{"v":"At the heart of the Yaeyama archipelago, Ishigaki lives to the sound of the sanshin and island songs, among red-tiled roofs guarded by shisa, Kabira fishermen and black pearl farming in the bay.","f":["Yaeyama soba","Thin, round noodles in a pork broth, seasoned with local long pepper."],"r":"At the cleaning station, hang back, settled on the sand or at the surface, and never chase or cut across the mantas’ path. Don’t touch the coral as you fin.","s":"June to October: mantas very present off Kabira and the water at its warmest, with possible typhoons in midsummer.","a":[["Kabira Bay","A turquoise bay dotted with wooded islets, to explore by glass-bottom boat; swimming is forbidden."],["Taketomi Island","A preserved Ryukyu village with sandy lanes, coral stone walls and water buffalo carts."],["Mount Omoto","The highest peak in Okinawa Prefecture, with a trail through subtropical forest."],["Miyara Dunchi","The former residence of a Ryukyu dignitary, with its rock garden made of coral."],["Yaeyama Museum","History, canoes, costumes and ritual objects of the archipelago’s culture."]]},"jialeshui":{"v":"At the tip of the Hengchun Peninsula, home of folk songs accompanied on the yueqin lute, the coast lines up coral rocks sculpted by the sea, small surf schools and winds dropping down from the mountains in winter.","f":["Gangkou tea","A robust tea grown nearby, in Taiwan’s southernmost tea garden."],"r":"In the national park, don’t collect coral or shells and stay within the area open to surfing. In winter, the offshore wind can push you out to sea: watch the current.","s":"October to March: the north-east monsoon sends a consistent swell; in summer and autumn, typhoons bring powerful sessions.","a":[["Eluanbi Lighthouse","A white lighthouse on Taiwan’s southern tip, in a park of uplifted coral."],["Hengchun Old Town walls","Gates and walls of the old fortified town, among the best preserved on the island."],["National Museum of Marine Biology","A large aquarium devoted to Taiwan’s waters, reefs and the world’s oceans."],["Longpan Park","Grassland on limestone cliffs facing the Pacific, popular at sunrise and for stargazing."],["Kenting Forest","Tropical forest on uplifted coral, with caves, trails and viewpoints."]]},"riyuebay":{"v":"Near Wanning, where Chinese returning from Southeast Asia planted Xinglong coffee, the bay has given birth to a real Chinese surf scene, among fishing villages, coconut palms and surf schools.","f":["Hele crab","A meaty crab from the Wanning area, one of Hainan’s four great dishes, steamed."],"r":"The bay is full of beginners in lessons: keep your distance from school groups and don’t surf in their zone. The rocks at either end quickly break a board.","s":"November to March: the north-east monsoon sends a consistent swell; in summer, only typhoons really make the bay work."},"masirah":{"v":"An island of fishermen and desert, Masirah lives to the rhythm of dhows and boats pulled up on the sand, flamingos in the lagoons and loggerhead turtles that come in great numbers to nest on its beaches.","r":"On nesting beaches, no lights at night and no vehicles on the sand, and stay well away from the turtles. When wild camping, take all your rubbish away and cover up in town.","s":"June to September: the south-west monsoon blows hard and relentlessly, with a swell that also wakes up the east coast waves."},"aqaba":{"v":"Jordan’s only port, Aqaba keeps the memory of the Great Arab Revolt and lives to the rhythm of cargo ships, fishing boats and shisha evenings on the corniche, facing the lights of Eilat.","r":"In the marine park, enter the water through the marked access points so you don’t trample the coral, and don’t feed the fish. On the town’s public beaches, locals swim clothed: keep a T-shirt on.","s":"March to May and October to November: warm water, excellent visibility and desert heat still bearable out of the water.","a":[["Aqaba Fortress","A Mamluk then Ottoman caravanserai by the sea, near the great flagpole of the Arab Revolt."],["Aqaba Archaeological Museum","Objects from the ancient Islamic city of Ayla and the history of the Red Sea port."],["Aqaba","A lively souk, shisha cafés and fish restaurants where you can try sayadieh after a dive."],["Pharaoh’s Island","An islet on the Egyptian side crowned by a restored medieval fortress, surrounded by reefs."]]},"lennox":{"v":"A quiet Northern Rivers village in Bundjalung country, Lennox lives between Lake Ainsworth, its water browned by the tea trees, and the point, where every big swell brings together the cream of the coast’s surfers.","r":"At the point, getting in and out over the boulders is tricky: watch the regulars before you go. The line-up is very hierarchical: stay in your place and don’t drop in on anyone.","s":"March to July: more consistent south-east swells, south-west winds that groom the point and the summer crowds gone.","a":[["Cape Byron","The easternmost point of the mainland, with its white lighthouse and a trail overlooking the whales."],["Julian Rocks","Rocky islets off Byron, famous for diving with rays, turtles and leopard sharks."],["Broken Head Nature Reserve","Coastal rainforest and small wild coves at the foot of the headland."],["Byron Bay","A surf and music town with a bohemian spirit, with its long beaches and its markets."],["Ballina Naval and Maritime Museum","Local maritime history, including a balsa raft that arrived after crossing the Pacific."]]},"kirra":{"v":"In Coolangatta, twin town of Tweed Heads sitting on the Queensland border, surfing is a family affair: Michael Peterson and Rabbit Bartholomew shaped a tube-riding culture here that is still alive at the peak.","r":"When Kirra is on, the line-up is packed with pros and locals: don’t force it on the sets if you don’t have the level, and give priority to whoever is deepest.","s":"February to May: cyclone swells from the Coral Sea and offshore south-east winds that hollow out the sandbanks.","a":[["Springbrook National Park","Gondwana rainforest, waterfalls and lookouts over the Gold Coast hinterland."],["Point Danger","A headland on the border between the two states, with views of Snapper Rocks and the whales."],["Cook Island","A protected rocky islet where you snorkel among green turtles."],["Currumbin Wildlife Sanctuary","A historic wildlife park to meet koalas, kangaroos and rainbow lorikeets."],["SkyPoint Observation Deck","A lookout at the top of a Surfers Paradise tower, over the whole Gold Coast."]]},"jervisbay":{"v":"Between the HMAS Creswell naval base and Booderee park, jointly managed with the Aboriginal community of Wreck Bay, the bay lives to the rhythm of its resident dolphins and the whales that stop here during their migration.","r":"At Hyams Beach, the car park is full in summer: come early or on foot. In the marine park, sanctuary zones ban all fishing and collecting; keep your distance from the dolphins.","s":"December to April: the warmest water of the year, a sea often glassy in the morning and top visibility for snorkelling.","a":[["Booderee National Park","Forests, white sand coves and a botanic garden, on jointly managed Aboriginal land."],["Murrays Beach","A sheltered cove with turquoise water, ideal for snorkelling facing Bowen Island."],["Hyams Beach","A small village famous for its dazzling white sand that squeaks underfoot."],["Point Perpendicular Lighthouse","A lighthouse on the edge of high cliffs at the entrance to the bay, a whale-watching spot."],["Cape St George Lighthouse ruins","The remains of a badly sited 19th-century lighthouse, amid the heathland of Booderee park."]]},"lordhowe":{"v":"On this volcanic crescent where people get around mostly by bike, island families descend from the first settlers who supplied the whalers and still live from the seeds of the kentia palm, shipped all over the world.","r":"Stay on the trails: the Lord Howe woodhen, saved from extinction by a whisker, nests on the ground. In the lagoon, mineral sunscreen only and no feet on the coral.","s":"December to April: the warmest water of the year in the lagoon, good visibility and a kinder sea than in the southern winter.","a":[["Mount Gower","The island’s highest point, a guided climb through the cloud forest to the summit."],["Admiralty Islets","A string of rocks north of the island, with seabird colonies and renowned dives."],["Blackburn Island","A small uninhabited islet in the middle of the lagoon, reached by kayak on a calm sea."],["Mount Lidgbird","A basalt peak twinned with Mount Gower, whose sheer walls tower over the south of the island."]],"lbl":"Lodge or apartment"},"coralbay":{"v":"An isolated hamlet amid the cattle stations of the Gascoyne, Coral Bay lives entirely to the rhythm of the reef: tour boats, passing anglers and the caravans of nomadic retirees escaping the southern winter.","r":"Bills Bay is a sanctuary zone: fishing is banned, and never stand on the coral. With manta rays and whale sharks, keep your distance and don’t touch them.","s":"April to July: whale sharks and manta rays offshore, milder heat and calmer winds than at the height of summer."},"luckybay":{"v":"A bay named by Matthew Flinders, who found shelter here while exploring the south coast, at the heart of a park of granite and flowering heathland; the nearby town of Esperance owes its name to a French frigate.","r":"Don’t feed the kangaroos, even when they come begging on the sand: bread and scraps make them ill. The park campground fills up fast, so book your spot.","s":"December to March: the least cold water, long days and an often calm sea in the bay, ideal for kayaking.","a":[["Cape Le Grand National Park","Granite domes, turquoise coves such as Hellfire Bay and the climb up Frenchman Peak."],["Woody Island","A wooded island in the Recherche Archipelago, with sea lions, seabirds and clear water."],["Rossiter Bay","A large wild bay where the explorer Eyre was rescued by a French whaler."]]},"yallingup":{"v":"Yallingup, whose Noongar name is often translated as “place of love”, is one of the cradles of West Australian surfing; between vineyards and eucalyptus bush, you surf the reef in the morning and visit the wineries in the afternoon.","f":["Margaret River wines","Renowned chardonnays and cabernets, to taste right at the wineries between sessions."],"r":"At Main Break, locals have surfed this reef for generations: wait your turn and beware of the shallow reef at low tide. Avoid surfing alone at dawn or dusk.","s":"March to May: big south-west swells from the Indian Ocean and offshore east winds in the morning, before the winter storms.","a":[["Ngilgi Cave","A limestone cave with white and orange formations, open to visitors just above the village."],["Canal Rocks","Granite rocks split by the sea, linked by a walkway above the waves."],["Cape Naturaliste Lighthouse","An early 20th-century lighthouse at the start of the Cape to Cape Track, overlooking Geographe Bay."],["Meelup Regional Park","Sheltered coves with calm water and a coastal trail on Geographe Bay."]]},"kaikoura":{"v":"Kaikōura means “meal of crayfish” in Māori: a former whaling station, the town now lives from sperm whale watching, started by Ngāti Kurī, and from its roadside crayfish shacks.","f":["Kaikōura crayfish","Crayfish grilled or with garlic, sold at the roadside shacks north of town."],"r":"Stay at least 20 m from the fur seals on the peninsula rocks and never put yourself between them and the sea. In the Hikurangi Marine Reserve, all taking of marine life is banned.","s":"December to March: calmer sea in the morning, the least cold water and long days for paddling among fur seals and dolphins.","a":[["Kaikōura Peninsula","A coastal trail above the limestone cliffs, with fur seal and seabird colonies."],["Fyffe House","The town’s oldest house, set on whale bones, a witness to the whaling era."],["Mount Fyffe","A long climb in the coastal range for a sweeping view over the peninsula and the ocean."],["Kaikōura Dark Sky Sanctuary","A night sky protected from light pollution, ideal for watching the Milky Way."]]},"poorknights":{"v":"Declared tapu by Ngātiwai after a deadly raid in the 19th century, the islands have remained uninhabited; boats leave from Tutukaka marina, and a warm current brings subtropical fish here.","r":"Landing on the islands is forbidden without a permit: everything happens in the water or from the boat. In the marine reserve, nothing may be taken, not even a shell.","s":"January to May: the warmest water of the year, visibility at its best and a sea often calmer than in winter."},"gisborne":{"v":"On the Tairāwhiti coast, where the ancestral waka and later James Cook came ashore, Gisborne remains one of the most Māori towns in the country; at Wainui, surfing families and early risers meet in the water with the first sun.","r":"At Wainui, the peaks are shared among local regulars: say hello and wait your turn. The dunes are being restored, so use only the marked access paths.","s":"March to June: cyclone swells followed by more consistent easterly swells, offshore winds in the morning and water still mild.","a":[["Titirangi (Kaiti Hill)","A former Māori pā overlooking the town and the bay, with a lookout over Tūranganui-a-Kiwa."],["Cook Landing Site","A historic reserve telling of the arrival of the Māori waka and of Cook, the first encounter."],["Gisborne","A small coastal town surrounded by vineyards, with its port and riverbanks."]]},"beqa":{"v":"Beqa is the island of the firewalkers, a ritual handed down by the Sawau clan; on the lagoon side, villages have given up fishing on Shark Reef in exchange for a fee paid by every diver, a model of community protection.","r":"During the shark feed, stay behind the coral wall, arms by your sides, and follow the guide’s instructions to the letter. The reserve fee keeps the villages going: don’t dodge it.","s":"May to October: dry, cooler season, clearer sea and bull sharks present in numbers on Shark Reef.","a":[["Beqa Island","A mountainous island with traditional villages, home of the Sawau clan’s firewalkers."],["Navua River","Tropical gorges and waterfalls to travel down by canoe or raft to the inland villages."],["Pacific Harbour","A small town where the dive boats leave from, the hub of the coast’s adventure activities."]]},"rainbowreef":{"v":"Taveuni, Fiji’s “Garden Island” crossed by the 180th meridian, lives to the rhythm of its villages and taro plantations; Somosomo is home to the Tui Cakau, the region’s paramount customary chief.","r":"The sites depend on the tides in the Somosomo Strait: dive with a local guide and keep your fins well away from the very fragile soft corals. In villages, cover your shoulders and knees.","s":"May to October: the dry season, top visibility and soft corals opened out by the tidal currents.","a":[["Taveuni Island","A forest-covered volcanic island, with the Bouma waterfalls and the Lavena coastal walk."],["Somosomo","Taveuni’s chiefly village, seat of the Tui Cakau, on the shore of the strait of the same name."]],"lbl":"Bure at a guesthouse"},"moorea":{"v":"Facing Tahiti, Moorea grows its pineapples deep in the Ōpūnohu valley, where the marae recall ancient Polynesian society; on the lagoon, va’a paddlers glide out at sunrise.","f":["Moorea pineapple","A small, very sweet pineapple from the island’s valleys, to enjoy fresh or as local juice."],"r":"Don’t touch the rays and never feed rays or sharks: the lagoon is covered by a management plan. Don’t walk on the coral heads and choose a mineral sunscreen.","s":"May to October: the dry season, cooling trade winds, crystal-clear water and humpback whales late in the season.","a":[["Ōpūnohu Bay","A deep bay framed by green peaks, with the lookout and the valley’s marae further up."],["Cook’s Bay","A spectacular bay at the foot of Mount Mouaroa, lined with villages and yachts at anchor."],["Mount Rōtui","A dizzying ridge between the two bays, a demanding hike for a view over the whole lagoon."],["Mount Tohiea","The island’s highest point, a jagged summit towering over the lagoon from the centre of Moorea."]],"lbl":"Family guesthouse"},"rangiroa":{"v":"Rangiroa, “immense sky” in Paumotu, lives on fishing, pearls and copra; in Tiputa, people watch from the point for dolphins playing in the waves of the pass on the rising tide.","l":"French, Tahitian, Paumotu","f":["Rangiroa wine","A vineyard planted on coral among the coconut palms, whose white and rosé you can taste on site."],"r":"In the pass, follow your guide: dives are timed with the very powerful incoming current. Don’t chase the dolphins and never touch the sharks.","s":"December to March for great hammerhead sharks in the pass, but dolphins and grey sharks are there all year round.","a":[["Rangiroa Atoll","A vast lagoon to explore by boat, out to the Blue Lagoon and the Pink Sands."],["Tiputa","The atoll’s largest village, with its church and its point on the pass, reached by water taxi."],["Avatoru Pass","The atoll’s second, more accessible pass, for drift snorkelling or swimming with mask and snorkel depending on the tide."],["Avatoru","The atoll’s main village, with its guesthouses, its church and its pearl farms."]],"lbl":"Family guesthouse"},"fakarava":{"v":"Fakarava lives to the quiet rhythm of Rotoava, its pearl farms and its bicycles; in the south, the hamlet of Tetamanu keeps a coral church, a reminder of the days when it was the capital of the Tuamotus.","l":"French, Tahitian, Paumotu","r":"In the biosphere reserve, don’t collect shells or coral and don’t grab the reef in the pass. Buy your pearls directly from the farms.","s":"June and July, during the gathering of camouflage groupers in the south pass, which draws even more sharks.","lbl":"Family guesthouse"},"poe":{"v":"In Bourail, a land of bush folk and cattle farmers whose agricultural fair brings all of New Caledonia together every year, Poé is the weekend beach: family picnics, kitesurfing in the afternoon and turtles in the lagoon.","f":["Bush venison","Stew, skewers or venison sausage, a speciality of the west coast’s farmers."],"r":"Stay in the kitesurfing zone, well away from swimmers and snorkellers. At La Roche Percée, loggerhead turtles nest in the southern summer: keep your distance and switch off your torch.","s":"September to December: steady afternoon trade winds, a flat lagoon and water warming up before summer.","a":[["Bonhomme de Bourail","A monolith sculpted by the waves at La Roche Percée, facing a long wild beach."],["Baie des Tortues","A cove shaded by tall columnar pines, reachable on foot from La Roche Percée."],["Domaine de Deva","A vast natural area between hills and lagoon, with trails on foot, by mountain bike or on horseback."],["Bourail Museum","The history of the penal colony and colonisation, in a former prison building."]]},"coolidge":{"v":"Luganville was born of the gigantic American Second World War base: at Million Dollar Point, the army dumped its surplus into the sea, and in the evening people meet at the nakamal for a shell of kava.","r":"The wreck is protected: don’t bring up any object, or you face heavy penalties. Dive with an approved guide, as the inside of the liner is a real labyrinth.","s":"April to October: the dry season, calmer sea and better visibility, outside the cyclone period.","a":[["Second World War Museum","Objects and stories from the American base on Santo and the sinking of the Coolidge."],["Luganville","The country’s second town, with its covered market, its nakamals and its wartime barracks."],["Aore Island","A wooded island facing Luganville, with calm coves and snorkelling a few minutes away by boat."],["Malo Island","A large rural island of traditional villages and cocoa gardens, south of Santo."]]},"bluecorner":{"v":"Palau created the world’s first shark sanctuary: here, a living shark is worth more than a fin, and the guides of Koror know every drop-off of the Chelbacheb islands like their own village.","r":"Hook your reef hook onto dead rock only, never onto living coral, and stay still at the edge of the drop-off. The Rock Islands permit is compulsory.","s":"December to April: the dry season, calmer sea and visibility at its best for diving in the current.","a":[["Jellyfish Lake","A marine lake in the Rock Islands where you swim among almost harmless golden jellyfish."],["German Channel","A channel cut through the reef, a cleaning station where manta rays come to be groomed."],["Peleliu","An island scarred by a terrible battle in 1944, with military remains in the jungle."],["Ngerukewid Islands (Seventy Islands)","A string of limestone islets in a strict nature reserve, to admire from the boat."]]},"jellyfishlake":{"v":"In the heart of the Chelbacheb islands, this saltwater lake, cut off from the ocean for millennia, is home to golden jellyfish that follow the sun’s path from one shore to the other; Palauans call it the “fifth lake”.","r":"Scuba diving is forbidden: swim gently at the surface, fins in slow motion, so as not to harm the jellyfish. Rinse off every trace of sunscreen before getting in; the lake permit is compulsory.","s":"December to April: the dry season and a calm sea for the crossing; check beforehand, as jellyfish numbers vary from year to year.","a":[["Blue Corner","A legendary reef point where you hook onto the drop-off to watch sharks and schools of fish go by."],["German Channel","A channel cut through the reef, a cleaning station where manta rays come to be groomed."],["Ngerukewid Islands (Seventy Islands)","A string of limestone islets in a strict nature reserve, to admire from the boat."],["Palau International Coral Reef Center","Koror’s aquarium devoted to the reefs and marine lakes of Palau."]]},"salani":{"v":"On Upolu’s south coast, life follows fa’a Samoa: open fale, a council of matai that governs the village and church singing on Sundays; the wave is surfed at the river mouth, facing the fishermen’s canoes.","r":"The reef belongs to the village: go through the surf camp or ask before going in the water. When crossing the village, slow down, say hello and cover your shoulders.","s":"May to October: regular southern swells from storms in the Southern Ocean, hitting this south coast head-on.","a":[["O Le Pupu-Puʿe National Park","Rainforest, the Togitogiga waterfalls and lava cliffs on the south coast."],["Lalomanu","A long white-sand beach lined with fale, facing the Aleipata islets."],["Namua Island","A volcanic islet off Lalomanu, with beach fale and a reef to explore with mask and snorkel."],["Fagaloa Bay","A deep bay surrounded by forests and traditional villages, a conservation area."]],"lbl":"Beach fale"},"vavau":{"v":"Around Neiafu’s Port of Refuge, a natural shelter where Pacific yachts anchor during the southern winter, Vava’u blends Tongan villages, churches with powerful choirs and wooded islets linked by calm channels.","r":"Only swim with the whales with a licensed operator: small groups, a slow approach and never any contact. On Sundays everything stops, boat trips included.","s":"July to October: the season of humpback whales coming to give birth, clear water and moderate trade winds in the channels.","a":[["Neiafu","The archipelago’s small capital, with a market, churches and sailors’ bars facing the Port of Refuge."],["Pangaimotu Island","An island next to Neiafu linked by a causeway, with calm coves for paddling."],["ʻEneʻio Botanical Garden","The country’s first botanical garden, with useful and medicinal plants of the islands."]]},"aitutaki":{"v":"The first of the Cook Islands reached by missionaries, Aitutaki keeps an old coral church and its Sunday singing in Arutanga; the Coral Route flying boats once landed on the lagoon, near Akaiami.","r":"The lagoon’s giant clams are protected: don’t touch them and never lift them. On Sundays the island lives to the rhythm of the church, so dress respectfully if you attend the service.","s":"April to November: the dry season, steady trade winds for kitesurfing and a crystal-clear lagoon, outside the cyclone period.","a":[["One Foot Island (Tapuaetai)","A white-sand motu fringed by turquoise sandbanks, where you can get your passport stamped."],["Akaiami","The motu where the Coral Route flying boats used to land, with a beach and shallow water."],["Maina","An islet in the south-west of the lagoon, with a seabird colony and snorkelling over the coral heads."],["Arutanga","The island’s small main town, with its wharf and the oldest church in the Cook Islands."]]},"kavieng":{"v":"A former German colonial town at the end of the Boluminski Highway, Kavieng is still a peaceful port of markets and coconut plantations, surrounded by islets where the villages themselves manage surfing on their reefs.","r":"The Pacific War wrecks are graves: don’t bring up any object. At the market or in a village, always ask before photographing someone.","s":"November to April: north-west monsoon swells and light winds, while the rest of the South Pacific is calmer."},"envau":{"v":"White limestone cliffs, Aleppo pines clinging to the rock and turquoise water: here you arrive on foot or by kayak, like the people of Marseille and Cassis who have always roamed the calanques.","l":"French, Provençal","f":["Bouillabaisse","Marseille’s rockfish soup, served with rouille and garlicky croutons."],"r":"Access to the massif is regulated in summer according to the fire risk: check it is open before setting off, light no fires and take all your rubbish home.","s":"May to June and September: the water is already mild, the light is bright and the trails are less crowded than in high summer.","a":[["Calanques National Park","Coves, cliffs and coastal paths between Marseille and Cassis, on land and at sea."],["Cap Canaille","Among the highest sea cliffs in France, with a huge view from the Route des Crêtes."],["Cassis","A colourful fishing port, with terraces on the quays and the terraced vineyards of Cassis wine."],["Calanque de Port-Miou","A long calanque sheltering a marina and old stone quarries."],["Château d’If","An island fortress in the harbour of Marseille, made famous by The Count of Monte Cristo."]]},"peyrefite":{"v":"On the Côte Vermeille, the terraced vineyards of Banyuls tumble down towards schist coves, and Catalan culture shows everywhere, from the place names to the colourful boats.","l":"French, Catalan","f":["Collioure anchovies","Salted and matured by hand using age-old know-how, with a glass of Banyuls."],"r":"You are in a marine nature reserve: no fishing or collecting, don’t touch the Posidonia seagrass or the wildlife, and follow the marked trail.","s":"June to September: the warmest water, an often calm sea and a guided underwater trail in summer.","a":[["Cerbère-Banyuls Marine Reserve","France’s first exclusively marine nature reserve, rich in groupers and gorgonians."],["Collioure","A painters’ village with its bell tower rising from the water, flower-filled lanes and pebble beaches."],["Royal Castle of Collioure","A massive fortress overlooking the port, once a residence of the kings of Majorca."],["Banyuls-sur-Mer","A Catalan port surrounded by terraced vineyards, famous for its naturally sweet wine."],["Sant Pere de Rodes Monastery","A Romanesque abbey perched above Cap de Creus, with a superb view of the sea."]]},"lerins":{"v":"Pine forest, shady paths and the fort where the Man in the Iron Mask was held: facing the Croisette, Sainte-Marguerite offers a car-free Côte d’Azur, next door to the monks of Saint-Honorat.","f":["Socca and pan bagnat","A chickpea pancake and a Niçois sandwich, perfect for a picnic on the island."],"r":"No fires or camping on the island, and don’t drop anchor on or trample the Posidonia seagrass meadows. Keep an eye on the time of the last boat to Cannes.","s":"June to September: warm water and a calm sea, with visibility often better in early summer.","a":[["Fort Royal of Sainte-Marguerite","A citadel where you can visit the cell of the Man in the Iron Mask, with a view over the bay of Cannes."],["Île Saint-Honorat","The island of the Cistercian monks, among vines, chapels and a still-inhabited abbey."],["Fortified Monastery of Lérins","A medieval tower-monastery standing over the sea at the tip of Saint-Honorat."],["Le Suquet","The old quarter of Cannes perched on its hill, with lanes and a view over the bay."],["Esterel Massif","Red volcanic rocks plunging into the sea, with trails and wild coves."]]},"pampelonne":{"v":"Legendary beach clubs, vines right down to the sand and a pine-covered peninsula: Pampelonne mixes the Saint-Tropez jet set with nature still unspoilt on its capes.","f":["Tarte tropézienne","A brioche filled with a light cream, born in Saint-Tropez and now known everywhere."],"r":"Beach car parks are expensive and fill up fast in summer: come early or by bike, and keep out of the marked swimming zones when you paddleboard.","s":"June and September: a warm sea, wind often light in the morning and a beach far less crowded than in August.","a":[["Saint-Tropez","A former fishing village of pastel façades, a lively port and the lanes of La Ponche."],["Citadel of Saint-Tropez","A 17th-century fortress housing a maritime history museum, with a view over the gulf."],["Musée de l’Annonciade","Pointillist and Fauvist painters who came to Saint-Tropez, in a former chapel by the port."],["Ramatuelle","A hilltop village of winding lanes, surrounded by vines and wooded hills."],["Domaine du Rayol","A garden of the world’s Mediterranean climates facing the sea, with an underwater trail in summer."]]},"gruissan":{"v":"Chalets on stilts facing the sea, an old village coiled around its tower and pink salt pans: Gruissan lives to the rhythm of the tramontane, among lagoons, fishermen and colourful sails.","l":"French, Occitan","f":["Étang de Leucate oysters","Raised in the nearby lagoon, to enjoy with a glass of La Clape white."],"r":"When the tramontane blows, the wind comes off the land and pushes you out to sea: never sail alone, stay close to shore and respect the swimming zones.","s":"April to June and September to October: frequent, strong tramontane, uncrowded beaches and water that’s still pleasant.","a":[["Old village of Gruissan","Lanes in a circle around the Barberousse tower, with salt pans and a lagoon at the village gates."],["Réserve Africaine de Sigean","A wildlife park on the edge of the lagoons, with African species seen by car or on foot."],["Bages","A fishing village perched above the lagoon, with quiet lanes and lovely views over the water."],["Leucate","A limestone plateau dropping into the sea, with cliffs to walk and major board-sports spots."]]},"salagou":{"v":"Red earth, volcanic hills and olive trees: Lake Salagou flooded part of the valley, leaving the abandoned village of Celles on its shores, in an almost Martian landscape.","l":"French, Occitan","f":["Petits pâtés de Pézenas","Small sweet-and-savoury mutton pies, a speciality of the neighbouring town."],"r":"Wild camping and driving off the tracks are forbidden around the lake: the red ruffe soils erode very quickly. Watch out for the wind, which can pick up strongly in the afternoon.","s":"June to September: pleasant water for paddleboarding, windless mornings and a calmer lake than in the middle of August.","a":[["Saint-Guilhem-le-Désert","A medieval village nestled at the end of a small valley, around a former Benedictine abbey."],["Mourèze and its cirque","A jumble of dolomite rocks to explore on trails, above a small village."],["Celles","A near-abandoned village on the lake shore, emptied when the dam was filled."],["Villeneuvette","A former royal cloth manufactory turned into a walled village, out of time."],["Hérault Gorges","A deep-cut river beneath the Pont du Diable, with swimming spots and hikes through the garrigue."]]},"monteynard":{"v":"Between the Vercors and the Trièves, this green-watered reservoir has become the meeting place for the Alps’ kitesurfers and windsurfers, drawn by an afternoon breeze that is rare in the mountains.","f":["Gratin dauphinois","Melting potatoes baked in cream, the great classic of the Dauphiné."],"r":"Only get in the water from the zones reserved for board sports and stay well away from the pleasure boat. The water stays cool: a wetsuit is a must.","s":"June to September, especially July and August: a steady thermal breeze in the afternoon and less cold water.","a":[["Vercors Regional Nature Park","Limestone cliffs, forests and mountain-pasture plateaus criss-crossed by hiking trails."],["High Plateaus of the Vercors","A vast, roadless wild plateau, for long hikes through the alpine pastures."],["Trièves","A small area of hedged farmland and villages at the foot of Mont Aiguille, with very open landscapes."],["La Mine Image","A former anthracite mine to visit to discover the work of the plateau’s miners."],["Champollion Museum","The Champollion family home, devoted to the decipherment of hieroglyphs."]]},"pontdarc":{"v":"Colourful canoes under the limestone arch, cliffs riddled with caves and sun-scorched garrigue: around the Pont d’Arc, people left the oldest paintings in the region.","l":"French, Occitan","f":["Picodon","A small goat’s cheese from the Ardèche and the Drôme, mild when young and sharp when aged."],"r":"In the gorges reserve, bivouacking is only allowed at designated sites and must be booked. Leave nothing behind on the pebble beaches.","s":"May to June and September: still enough flow, pleasant water and a river far less crowded than in summer.","a":[["Grotte Chauvet 2","A faithful replica of the Chauvet cave and its paintings of lions, horses and rhinos."],["Ardèche Gorges","A canyon of limestone cliffs to paddle down by canoe or admire from the viewpoint road."],["Aven d’Orgnac","A huge underground chamber with giant formations, plus a museum of prehistory."],["Grotte de la Madeleine","A cave of draperies and formations opening onto a balcony above the gorges."],["Grotte de Saint-Marcel","Long galleries with stepped rimstone pools and varied formations, to visit underground."]]},"roquegageac":{"v":"Golden stone houses clinging to the cliff, traditional gabarre boats on the river and castles on every promontory: the Dordogne valley keeps the memory of medieval wars.","l":"French, Occitan","f":["Périgord foie gras and walnuts","Foie gras on toast and walnut cake, two prides of the tables of the Périgord Noir."],"r":"Give way to the gabarres taking visitors on trips and only land on the pebble beaches, not on private riverbanks.","s":"May to June and September: a decent water level, mild weather and a river much calmer than in high summer.","a":[["Sarlat-la-Canéda","A medieval town of golden-stone mansions and a big, lively market."],["Château de Beynac","A fortress perched on top of a cliff, with a spectacular view over the Dordogne."],["Château de Castelnaud","A medieval fortress housing a museum of warfare in the Middle Ages and its siege engines."],["Domme","A bastide town perched on a cliff, whose clifftop offers a vast panorama over the valley."],["Château des Milandes","A Renaissance château where Josephine Baker lived, surrounded by formal French gardens."]]},"gorgestarn":{"v":"Stone villages huddled at the foot of the causses, boatmen’s punts gliding between the cliffs: in La Malène, life in the canyon has long revolved around the river.","l":"French, Occitan","f":["Aligot","Stretchy mashed potato with fresh tomme cheese, served piping hot with a sausage."],"r":"Always give way to the La Malène boatmen’s punts, especially in the narrow passage of Les Détroits, and throw nothing into the river.","s":"May to June and September: enough flow, pleasant water and a river far less busy than in summer.","a":[["Chaos de Montpellier-le-Vieux","A labyrinth of strangely shaped dolomite rocks, to explore on trails."],["Cévennes National Park","Causses, valleys and wild ridges, criss-crossed by trails and stone villages."],["Grotte de Dargilan","A cave with pink-tinted formations, hollowed out beneath the Causse Noir."],["Meyrueis","A characterful small town where three rivers meet, gateway to the causses and Mont Aigoual."],["La Canourgue","A medieval small town crossed by little canals, nicknamed the little Venice of Lozère."]]},"excenevex":{"v":"Fine sand and reeds on the shore of Lake Geneva, professional fishermen’s boats and the outline of the Swiss Alps opposite: Excenevex feels like the seaside at the foot of the Chablais.","f":["Lake Geneva perch fillets","Pan-fried in butter and served with chips, the great classic of the lakeshore."],"r":"The bise can whip up short waves very quickly on Lake Geneva: on a paddleboard, stay close to shore and avoid crossing towards Switzerland without experience.","s":"July to August: Lake Geneva’s water at its warmest, long evenings and a lake that is often calm in the morning.","a":[["Yvoire","A fortified medieval village of flower-decked houses, jutting into the lake between harbour and ramparts."],["Jardin des Cinq Sens","A garden laid out as a plant labyrinth where you discover plants through sight, smell and touch."],["Thonon-les-Bains","A spa town on a balcony above the lake, with a funicular and the fishing port of Rives."],["Évian-les-Bains","A Belle Époque spa resort with flower-lined promenades and elegant architecture facing the lake."],["Château de Coppet","Madame de Staël’s home on the Swiss shore, with its salons and its park."]]},"guerledan":{"v":"In the heart of the Argoat, far from the sea, the lake flooded part of the Nantes–Brest canal: sunken locks, a towpath and deep forests give the place the charm of inland Brittany.","l":"French, Breton","h":["Demat","“hello” in Breton, to greet people the way they do in Central Brittany"],"f":["Crêpes and buckwheat galettes","A galette complète or a salted-butter crêpe, with a bowl of cider."],"r":"On the towpath, share the space with cyclists and walkers, and on the water keep well away from the dam and its off-limits surroundings.","s":"June to September: the mildest water of the year and long days to explore the wooded coves.","a":[["Quénécan Forest","A large forest of beeches and heathland overlooking the lake, criss-crossed by trails."],["Château des Rohan","A 15th-century fortified castle with massive towers and moats, in the heart of Pontivy."],["Pontivy","A medieval old town and a Napoleonic quarter on a grid plan, on the banks of the Blavet."],["Chapelle de Quelven","A large Gothic pilgrimage chapel in the middle of a granite hamlet."]]},"vassiviere":{"v":"On the Millevaches plateau, among heathland, peat bogs and conifer forests, the lake blends swimming, sailing and contemporary art, in a rural and peaceful Limousin.","l":"French, Occitan","f":["Pâté de pommes de terre","A Limousin pie filled with potatoes and cream, served hot."],"r":"On the island, the works in the sculpture wood are part of an art trail: don’t climb on them. Swim at the lifeguarded beaches in summer.","s":"July to August: water that is finally mild on this high plateau, lifeguarded beaches and a light wind.","a":[["Île de Vassivière","A wooded island dotted with open-air sculptures, to explore on foot around the lake."],["Vassivière Art Centre","A building with a brick lighthouse housing contemporary art exhibitions."],["Millevaches Nature Park","Heathland, peat bogs and springs on a granite plateau, ideal for hiking."],["Gentioux War Memorial","A rare pacifist memorial where a child shakes his fist beneath the inscription “Cursed be war”."]]},"maubuisson":{"v":"Between the pine forest and the dunes of the Médoc, the lake’s fresh, shallow water offers the quiet flip side of the nearby ocean coast, with cycle paths and small marinas.","l":"French, Gascon","f":["Entrecôte à la bordelaise","Grilled over vine cuttings and coated in a Médoc red wine sauce."],"r":"The lake’s shores and reed beds shelter many birds: keep away from the protected areas and bring your gear ashore at the designated beaches.","s":"May to September: a steady afternoon sea breeze and water that warms up fast thanks to the shallow depth.","a":[["Étang de Cousseau Reserve","Trails through marshes, heathland and forest, with shy wildlife to watch."],["Hourtin dunes and marshes","Wooded dunes, damp dune slacks and unspoilt marshes between the lake and the ocean."],["Lac de Lacanau","Another large pine-fringed lake, for sailing, paddleboarding or a wave-free swim."],["Hourtin lighthouses","Two brick towers hidden in the pine forest, near the ocean coast."]]},"belharra":{"v":"Off the Basque corniche, on days of huge swell, the whole coast crowds onto the cliffs to watch jet skis tow surfers onto immense walls of water.","l":"French, Basque","h":["Kaixo","“hi” in Basque, said with a smile"],"f":["Ttoro","A fish soup of the Saint-Jean-de-Luz fishermen, rich and spiced with Espelette pepper."],"r":"Only venture out for supervised tow-in, with jet-ski safety cover. To watch, park in the corniche car parks without blocking the road.","s":"November to February, only during very big, well-aligned winter swells, a few days a year.","a":[["Abbadia Castle-Observatory","The neo-Gothic castle of a travelling scholar, on an estate of cliffs and heathland facing the ocean."],["Saint-Jean-de-Luz","A Basque fishing port with half-timbered houses, a sheltered bay and a lively pedestrian street."],["Ciboure","A fishermen’s quarter facing Saint-Jean-de-Luz, with Basque houses and the Fort de Socoa."],["Château d’Urtubie","A fortified manor reworked over the centuries, surrounded by a park."],["Guéthary","A small Basque village perched above its harbour and its reef waves."]]},"conchebaleines":{"v":"At the tip of the island, white houses with green shutters, salt marshes and bikes everywhere: the Conche keeps a wild side, among dunes, pine forest and a lighthouse watching over the currents.","f":["Île de Ré potatoes","Thin-skinned new potatoes harvested in spring, simply cooked with salted butter."],"r":"Only cross the dune by the marked paths, and leave the car behind: the island is easy to explore by bike on its cycle paths.","s":"September to November: regular autumn swells, water still lukewarm and an island much quieter than in summer.","a":[["Phare des Baleines","A tall lighthouse to climb for a view over the whole island, with the old Vauban tower next to it."],["Saint-Martin-de-Ré","A port fortified by Vauban, a UNESCO World Heritage site, with lively quays and lanes."],["Ars-en-Ré","A village of narrow lanes, recognisable by its black-and-white bell tower that serves as a seamark."],["Lilleau des Niges Reserve","Marshes and mudflats where thousands of birds winter, to watch from the cycle paths."],["La Flotte","A small port of white houses, a covered medieval market and quiet quays."]]},"cotesauvage":{"v":"Dunes, endless pine forest and an ocean roaring at the foot of the lighthouse: the Arvert peninsula lives between forest, the oyster ponds of the Seudre and wild waves to approach with respect.","l":"French, Saintongeais","f":["Marennes-Oléron oysters","Finished in claires in the Seudre basin, to taste straight from the oyster farmer."],"r":"The baïnes and currents here are formidable: only go in the water near lifeguard stations, never alone, and stay on the marked paths across the dune.","s":"September to November: powerful autumn swells, water still lukewarm and an almost deserted beach.","a":[["Cordouan Lighthouse","A Renaissance lighthouse standing at sea, a UNESCO World Heritage site, reached by boat at low tide."],["La Coubre Lighthouse","A tall red-and-white tower to climb for a view taking in the ocean, the forest and the estuary."],["La Coubre Forest","A huge pine forest on dunes, criss-crossed by cycle paths and trails."],["Mornac-sur-Seudre","A village of fishermen and oyster farmers with flower-filled lanes, on the edge of the marshes."],["La Palmyre Zoo","A large zoo set beneath the pines, a few minutes from the beaches."]]},"lavezzi":{"v":"Wind-sculpted granite boulders, crystal-clear water and a lonely lighthouse: in the middle of the Strait of Bonifacio, the Lavezzi recall the shipwrecks of old, while Bonifacio keeps watch from its cliffs.","l":"French, Corsican","h":["Bonghjornu","“hello” in Corsican, always appreciated"],"f":["Aubergines à la bonifacienne","Stuffed, gratinéed aubergines, a family speciality of Bonifacio."],"r":"You are in a nature reserve: no fishing, no fires, no camping, and don’t drop anchor on the Posidonia seagrass meadows.","s":"June to September: warm water and excellent visibility, but keep an eye on the often strong wind in the strait.","a":[["Bonifacio Upper Town","A fortified old town perched on limestone cliffs overhanging the sea."],["Cap de Pertusato","The southern tip of Corsica, with a clifftop path and a view of Sardinia."],["Rondinara Bay","An almost enclosed cove of white sand and turquoise water, between two headlands."],["Fazziò Beach","A small white-sand cove nestled between cliffs, reached on foot."]]},"saleccia":{"v":"Sun-scorched maquis, old pastures and dirt tracks: the Agriates, once the farming granary of Cap Corse, have become a protected desert reached by boat or 4×4.","l":"French, Corsican","h":["Bonghjornu","“hello” in Corsican, always appreciated"],"f":["Brocciu","A fresh ewe’s or goat’s milk cheese, eaten plain, in an omelette or in fiadone."],"r":"Wild camping and fires are forbidden in the Agriates: sleep at the beach campsite or head back on the shuttle boat, and bring enough water.","s":"June to September: a warm, clear sea, with calmer mornings before the boats arrive.","a":[["Désert des Agriates","Wild maquis crossed by the coastal path, from cove to cove."],["Plage du Lotu","A neighbouring white-sand beach, served by boat from Saint-Florent."],["Saint-Florent","A lively port at the head of its gulf, with colourful lanes and a Genoese citadel."],["Patrimonio","A wine village renowned for its wines, among hillsides overlooking the gulf."],["Monte Revincu","Prehistoric megaliths and dolmens scattered through the maquis."]]},"santagiulia":{"v":"White sand, a lagoon behind and fragrant maquis: around Porto-Vecchio, southern Corsica mixes postcard coves, an old Genoese citadel and Torrean sites in the hinterland.","l":"French, Corsican","h":["Bonghjornu","“hello” in Corsican, always appreciated"],"f":["Fiadone","A lemon-scented brocciu cake, light and melt-in-the-mouth, found on every Corsican table."],"r":"The bay is very busy in summer: on a paddleboard or in a kayak, keep out of the marked swimming zones and don’t trample the seagrass or the neighbouring lagoon.","s":"June and September: a warm, calm sea, clear water and a beach far less crowded than in August.","a":[["Rondinara Bay","An almost enclosed cove of white sand and turquoise water, between two headlands."],["Porto-Vecchio","A Genoese old town perched above the salt pans, with lanes and terraces buzzing in the evening."],["Castellu d’Araghju","A Bronze Age stone fortress, perched with a view over the whole gulf."],["Cerbicale Islands","A small protected archipelago off the coast, a seabird refuge to cruise past by boat."],["Ospedale Forest","A high-altitude forest of laricio pines, with a lake, trails and the Piscia di Gallu waterfall."]]},"lemoule":{"v":"Cane fields, a rum distillery and a coast pounded by the Atlantic: Le Moule looks out to sea, with local surfers who know every reef in the bay.","r":"The line-up is very local and the reefs are shallow: watch before paddling out, give priority to the regulars and say hello at the peak.","s":"November to March: North Atlantic winter swells, cleaner in the morning before the trade wind picks up.","a":[["Pointe des Châteaux","Jagged cliffs facing the ocean, a cross at the top and a view of La Désirade."],["Edgar-Clerc Museum","Collections of Amerindian archaeology tracing the history of the island’s first inhabitants."],["Plage des Raisins Clairs","A fine-sand beach protected by the reef, with a calm lagoon for swimming."],["Plage du Petit-Havre","A small sheltered cove with calm water, popular for swimming and snorkelling."]],"lbl":"Holiday cottage or studio"},"petiteterre":{"v":"Two uninhabited islets, an old stone lighthouse and a turquoise lagoon: Petite-Terre is explored on a day trip from Saint-François, among iguanas and seabirds.","r":"The reserve can only be reached on authorised boats: stay on the trails, don’t touch or feed the iguanas, and keep your distance from the lagoon’s wildlife.","s":"December to May: the dry season, steady trade winds and an often crystal-clear lagoon, even if the crossing can be rough.","a":[["Petite-Terre Lighthouse","Guadeloupe’s oldest lighthouse, rising amid the dry vegetation."],["La Désirade","A calm, wild island with deserted beaches and fishing villages far from the bustle."],["Grand-Montagne","La Désirade’s highest point, for a climb with a view over the whole archipelago."]],"lbl":"Holiday cottage or studio"},"grandeanse_deshaies":{"v":"Coconut palms, golden sand and green hills tumbling into the sea: Deshaies still feels like a fishing village, with its colourful boats and small fish restaurants.","r":"Watch the flags: the swell creates strong currents here. Turtles nest on the beach, so don’t touch the marked nests and avoid lights at night.","s":"April to June: the end of the dry season and a sea more often calm than in winter, when northern swells hit the coast.","a":[["Cousteau Reserve","Protected waters around the Pigeon islets, famous for diving and snorkelling."],["Deshaies","A colourful fishing village at the head of its bay, the filming location of a TV series."],["Îlet à Kahouanne","A wild islet off the coast, popular with divers for its fish-rich waters."],["Guadeloupe Zoo","A wildlife park deep in the rainforest, with a canopy walk."]],"lbl":"Holiday cottage or studio"},"bonneville":{"v":"At the end of the Caravelle peninsula, Tartane is still a fishing village of colourful yoles, now the heart of Martinique surfing with its schools set up facing the bay.","r":"Several schools share the bay: avoid the lesson areas, leave the peaks to the regulars and watch out for rocks and sea urchins on the sides.","s":"November to April: regular Atlantic swells, cleaner in the morning before the trade wind picks up.","a":[["Caravelle Reserve","Trails through dry forest, mangroves and cliffs battered by the Atlantic."],["Château Dubuc","The ruins of a former colonial sugar estate, in a wild setting."],["Caravelle Lighthouse","A lighthouse perched above the peninsula, with a wide panorama over the Atlantic coast."],["Saint James Rum Museum","The history of agricultural rum and old stills on the site of a distillery."],["Jardin de Balata","A lush botanical garden with suspended walkways in the trees."]],"lbl":"Holiday cottage or studio"},"pointefaula":{"v":"In Le Vauclin, small-scale fishing still sets the pace of the town and the yoles rondes, Martinique’s traditional sailing boats, train offshore. The Pointe Faula lagoon serves as a classroom for the south’s kitesurfers.","r":"Stay out of the swimming zone and don’t sail right over the seagrass at low tide: the lagoon is very shallow.","s":"December to May, during the carême dry season: strong, steady trade winds, dry weather and warm water.","a":[["Sainte-Anne Islets Reserve","Protected islets where terns and seabirds nest, to observe from the sea."],["Le François Islets","An archipelago of islets and white shallows where you can bathe standing up in the open sea."],["Îlet Chancel","Martinique’s largest islet, a refuge of the Lesser Antillean iguana."],["Montravail Rock Carvings","Amerindian petroglyphs carved on boulders deep in the forest."],["Plage des Salines","A long beach of golden sand lined with coconut palms at the island’s southern tip."]]},"salines_mq":{"v":"At weekends, Martinican families settle under the trees with cool boxes and cooking pots. All around, the lagoon and the dry savannahs of the Sainte-Anne peninsula contrast with the mountainous north.","r":"Spot the manchineel trees marked with a red band: never shelter beneath them, as their sap and fruit are toxic.","s":"December to April: the dry season, a calm sea and clear water for swimming and watching the fish.","a":[["Diamond Rock","An iconic volcanic islet, also a renowned dive site."],["Cap 110 Memorial","White stone statues facing the sea, in memory of the victims of the slave trade."],["Sainte-Anne Islets Reserve","Protected islets where terns and seabirds nest, to observe from the sea."],["Martinique Ecomuseum","The island’s history, from the Amerindians to the sugar estates."],["Anse Trabaud","A wild beach on the Atlantic coast, reached by a dirt track."]]},"orientbay":{"v":"On the French side of the island, Orient Bay mixes beach bars, fishermen and a Caribbean vibe. People switch from one language to another, and in the evening they head to Grand-Case for seafront barbecues.","f":["Grand-Case lolo barbecues","Ribs, chicken and fish grilled in the little seafront canteens."],"r":"The southern end of the beach is naturist: respect that area. When kitesurfing, stay well away from swimmers and the channel buoys.","s":"December to April: strong, steady trade winds, the dry season and outside the hurricane period.","a":[["Pinel Island","A small white-sand islet reached by shuttle boat, good for snorkelling."],["Tintamarre Island","A flat, uninhabited island in the reserve, with deserted beaches and fish-rich waters."],["Pic Paradis","The island’s highest point, with forest trails and a view of the neighbouring islands."],["Grand-Case","A seaside village renowned for its restaurants and its lolos."],["Fort Louis","An 18th-century fort overlooking Marigot and its bay."]]},"ngouja":{"v":"In the south of Mayotte, outrigger canoes pulled up on the sand and faces masked with msindzano reflect a very much alive Mahoran culture, between the lagoon and Mount Choungui.","r":"Keep your distance from the turtles, don’t touch them and don’t follow them. Don’t feed the lemurs that come down from the trees.","s":"May to October, in the dry season: less rain, clearer water and an often very calm lagoon.","a":[["Mount Choungui","A sugarloaf volcanic peak, a steep climb and a view over the lagoon."],["Îlot de Sable Blanc","A white sandbank in the middle of the lagoon, reached by boat."],["Saziley Marine Park","A marine protected area where turtles come to nest on the beaches."],["Tsingoni Mosque","An old mosque with a carved mihrab, one of the oldest in France."],["Bandrélé salt","Traditional salt harvesting on the mudflat, a skill kept alive by women."]]},"borestranda":{"v":"Jæren is a windswept farming plain, criss-crossed by dry-stone walls and round pebbles. Norwegian surfers found their first waves here, in thick wetsuits facing the North Sea.","r":"The Jæren dunes are protected: stay on the marked paths and park in the designated car parks.","s":"September to April: North Sea low-pressure systems and sandbanks in good shape, in a thick wetsuit.","a":[["Orre","A coastal village with its old church, its bird lake and its long beach."],["Obrestad","A farming hamlet with a lighthouse and a small fishing harbour facing the North Sea."],["Sola Aviation Museum","A collection of vintage aircraft and seaplanes near Stavanger airport."]]},"saltstraumen":{"v":"With the rhythm of the tides, the channel turns into a swirling river that anglers watch from the rocks for coalfish. In summer, the midnight sun lights up the strait.","r":"Only dive at slack water, with a local club that knows the current times, and give way to boats.","s":"May to September: very long days to time dives at slack water and milder weather, in a drysuit.","a":[["Keiservarden","A plateau above Bodø, with a view of the islands and the midnight sun."],["Norwegian Aviation Museum","A large museum of civil and military aviation, with Cold War aircraft."],["Nordland Museum","The history of Bodø, coastal fishing and Sámi culture."],["Bodø","A port town of the Far North, the departure point for ferries to the Lofoten Islands."]]},"apelviken":{"v":"Varberg sees itself as Sweden’s capital of surfing and boardsports. In summer, families fill the bay; as soon as the westerly gales blow in, riders in wetsuits take over.","r":"In summer, respect the areas reserved for swimmers and only launch your kite in the permitted sectors.","s":"September to November: strong westerly winds and autumn storms, with the beach deserted by holidaymakers.","a":[["Varberg Fortress","A medieval fortress by the sea, with ramparts and a view over the Kattegat."],["Halland Museum","Inside the fortress, it displays the Bocksten Man, a medieval body found in a peat bog."],["Getterön Nature Reserve","Wet meadows and lagoons rich in birds, with observation hides."],["Träslövsläge","A fishing village with its harbour and its huts by the water."]]},"toro":{"v":"At the southern tip of the Stockholm archipelago, pine forests and smooth rocks frame a pebble beach. Surfers come here in full wetsuits, sometimes between sheets of ice.","r":"The site is a nature reserve: no fires and no cars outside the car park, and don’t move the pebbles.","s":"October to March: Baltic storms raising short waves, in icy water.","a":[["Landsort","A village on the island of Öja, at the far end of the archipelago, with its lighthouse and its trails."],["Nynäshamn","A small port town, with fish smokehouses and ferries to Gotland."]]},"kosterhavet":{"v":"Off Strömstad, Bohuslän lines up pink granite islands, prawn-fishing villages and lobster boats. Below the surface, the Koster trench shelters cold-water coral reefs.","f":["Bohuslän prawns","Northern prawns cooked on board, eaten on bread with mayonnaise and dill."],"r":"In the park, some areas are off-limits during bird nesting and seal pupping: check the signs.","s":"June to August: long days, water at its mildest and a sea often calm between the islands.","a":[["Koster Islands","Almost car-free islands, to explore by bike between coves and heathland."],["Strömstad","A small seaside town and fishing port, the departure point for the boats."],["Ytre Hvaler National Park","The neighbouring Norwegian marine park, with rocky islets and rich waters."]]},"hvidesande":{"v":"Hvide Sande lives around its lock and its harbour: fish auction, trawlers and fish smokehouses. Between the North Sea and the fjord, you sail in the morning and eat smoked herring on the quay in the evening.","f":["Smoked fish from the harbour","Wood-smoked herring, mackerel or salmon, sold directly by the smokehouses."],"r":"On the fjord, stay in the marked kite zones and keep well away from fishing nets and protected bird areas.","s":"May to September: steady westerly winds and a milder fjord; autumn brings strong wind and waves on the sea side.","a":[["Ringkøbing","An old merchant town of cobbled lanes and colourful houses, on the shore of the fjord."],["Tipperne","A protected peninsula of salt meadows, a major stopover for migratory birds."],["Abeline’s Farm","A preserved fjord-side farm that tells the story of rural life in the past."],["Nymindegab Museum","Local coastal history and paintings of the dunes and the North Sea."]]},"romo":{"v":"Rømø keeps the farms of the commanders, whaling captains of old, and a beach so wide that cars drive on it. In spring and autumn, clouds of starlings darken the sky over the marshes.","r":"The beach is open to cars: drive at walking pace, park outside the traffic lane and only fly your kite in the kite zone.","s":"May to September: steady westerly winds and milder water; autumn brings stronger gales.","a":[["Wadden Sea National Park","Mudflats, sandbanks and seals, to discover on guided outings."],["Rømø Church","The sailors’ church, surrounded by the carved gravestones of the commanders."],["Mandø","A small Wadden island, reached at low tide by a road that floods."],["Marsk Tower","A double-helix observation tower, with a view over the marshes and the Wadden Sea."],["List (Sylt)","A village in the north of Sylt, in Germany, reached by the ferry from Havneby."]]},"linnansaari":{"v":"The park protects the rare Saimaa seal and still keeps up the slash-and-burn farming of the past. You paddle in silence between pine-covered islands and end the day in the sauna, facing the lake.","r":"Only light fires in the designated fireplaces and keep away from the rocks where the Saimaa seal rests.","s":"June to August: pleasant water for swimming, light nights and a calm lake for paddling from island to island."},"tiree":{"v":"A Gaelic island of crofters, Tiree lives among machair meadows, white houses with black roofs and endless beaches. The wind is such that windsurfers from all over the world have made it an autumn gathering.","l":"English, Scottish Gaelic","h":["Halò","“hi” in Scottish Gaelic, still spoken on the island"],"r":"Don’t drive on the fragile machair, close field gates behind you and let sheep and cows pass on the tracks.","s":"September to November: Atlantic low-pressure systems, strong wind and well-lined-up swell on the west coast.","a":[["Dun Mor Vaul Broch","An Iron Age stone tower on the north coast, facing the sea."],["Scarinish","The island’s main village, with its small harbour and a few shops."]]},"freshwaterwest":{"v":"In south Pembrokeshire, dunes, cliffs and fishing harbours make up a rugged landscape. At Freshwater West, a hut recalls the gathering of seaweed for laverbread, a Welsh speciality.","l":"English, Welsh","h":["Shwmae","“hi” in Welsh, casual and warm"],"f":["Laverbread","A purée of dark seaweed served at breakfast with cockles and bacon."],"r":"Respect the red flags of the nearby military firing range and beware of the currents, among the strongest in Wales.","s":"September to November: regular autumn swells, water still lukewarm and a less crowded car park.","a":[["Skomer Island","A nature reserve where puffins and shearwaters nest, reached by boat."],["Pembroke Castle","An imposing medieval fortress, birthplace of Henry VII."],["Carew Castle","Castle ruins on the edge of an estuary, with a tidal mill."],["Angle","A village at the entrance to Milford Haven, with small beaches and a coastal path."]]},"sennen":{"v":"At the far end of Cornwall, Sennen Cove keeps its fishing harbour, its lifeboat station and its granite houses. Surfers share the water with seals here when the swell comes in.","f":["Cornish pasty","A pastry filled with beef, potato and swede, born among the tin miners."],"r":"Surf between the lifeguards’ black-and-white flags and swim between the red-and-yellow flags.","s":"September to October: regular autumn swells, water at its mildest and a beach emptied after summer.","a":[["St Michael’s Mount","An island and castle reached on foot at low tide along a causeway."],["Botallack Mine","Old tin mines clinging to the cliffs, a World Heritage site."],["Mousehole","A fishing village of narrow lanes around a granite harbour."],["PK Porthcurno","A museum of undersea telegraph cables, in a turquoise bay."],["Carn Euny","An Iron Age village with its underground passage, the fogou."]]},"kynance":{"v":"On the Lizard peninsula, dark serpentine stone, worked by local craftspeople, colours the cliffs and islets. Coastal paths link fishing villages, lighthouses and turquoise coves.","f":["Cornish cream tea","Warm scones, strawberry jam and thick clotted cream."],"r":"The beach disappears at high tide: check the tide times so you don’t get stuck on the islets.","s":"June to September, at low tide: the mildest water of the year and plenty of exposed sand.","a":[["Lizard Point","The southernmost point of Great Britain, with cliffs and seals."],["Cadgwith","A fishing hamlet of thatched roofs, with boats pulled up on the shingle."],["Lizard Lighthouse","A historic lighthouse with a visitor centre on navigation."],["Coverack","A quiet fishing village on a sheltered bay."],["Cornish Seal Sanctuary","A centre that cares for stranded seal pups before releasing them."]]},"easkey":{"v":"Easkey, whose Irish name evokes an abundance of fish, is home to the Irish Surfing Association. At the water’s edge, a medieval tower keeps watch over the reef slabs and the surfers.","r":"The reefs are shallow at low tide: give priority to the locals and don’t go out if you don’t have the level.","s":"September to March: big, well-lined-up Atlantic swells on the reefs, in a thick wetsuit.","a":[["Lissadell House","A neoclassical mansion linked to the poet Yeats, on the shore of Sligo Bay."],["Ox Mountains","Mountains of moorland and lakes, with wild and little-walked hikes."],["Killala","A bayside village with its medieval round tower."],["Rosses Point","A peninsula village, with beaches and a view over Sligo Bay."]]},"inch":{"v":"On the Dingle peninsula, Irish can still be heard in the western villages. At Inch, you park on the sand, surf facing the Kerry mountains and finish at the pub with traditional music.","r":"The Inch dunes are protected: only park your car on the permitted part of the beach and stay on the paths.","s":"May to September: gentler swells for learning, less cold water and lifeguards on duty in summer.","a":[["Dingle","A colourful harbour town with traditional music pubs, gateway to the peninsula."],["Kilmalkedar","A medieval monastic site with a Romanesque church and an ogham stone."],["Slieve Mish Mountains","Sandstone ridges above Tralee and Dingle bays, with fine hikes."],["Annascaul","The home village of the explorer Tom Crean, whose pub still keeps his memory alive."],["Magharee Islands","A small archipelago off the Maharees, with the remains of a hermitage."]]},"brouwersdam":{"v":"Born of the Delta Works after the great flood, the dam has become a playground: kitesurfers on the sea side, divers on the Grevelingen side, and Zeeland mussels on the table in the evening.","f":["Zeeland mussels","Mussels farmed in the waters of the Oosterschelde, cooked with celery and onion."],"r":"Only fly your kite in the marked kite zones and, on the lake side, make way for divers along the dyke.","s":"In spring and from September to October: steady westerly winds, beaches less crowded than in summer.","a":[["Renesse","A lively seaside resort with wide beaches and wooded dunes."],["Moermond Castle","A moated castle on the edge of Renesse."],["Ouddorp","A village with a windmill and low houses, between dunes and polders."],["Scharendijke","A village on the edge of the Grevelingen, a meeting place for Dutch divers."]]},"stpeterording":{"v":"A spa resort on the Eiderstedt peninsula, Sankt Peter-Ording lives among salt meadows, lighthouses and restaurants on stilts. The beach is so vast that you drive across it to reach the water.","f":["North Sea brown shrimp","Small peeled brown shrimp, served on bread or in a roll."],"r":"Never head out alone on the tidal flats when the tide is rising: the sea comes back fast. Kitesurfing is done in the dedicated zones.","s":"May to September: steady westerly winds over the tidal flats; in spring and autumn, it blows harder.","a":[["Westerheversand Lighthouse","A red-and-white lighthouse surrounded by salt meadows, the emblem of the coast."],["Wadden Sea National Park","Vast mudflats, seals and migratory birds, on a guided walk."],["Tönning","A small port town with gabled houses and a Wadden Sea centre."],["Eiderstedt Peninsula","Polders, dykes and large farms with tall thatched roofs to explore by bike."]]},"chalupy":{"v":"On the Hel Peninsula, a Kashubian land of fishermen, the villages stretch between the Baltic and the Bay of Puck. In summer, kite schools, campsites and fish smokehouses line up along the thin strip of sand.","l":"Polish, Kashubian","f":["Baltic smoked fish","Smoked flounder, mackerel or herring, sold hot at the smokehouses."],"r":"Only cross the dunes and pine forest by the marked paths and stay in the kite corridors set by the schools.","s":"May to September: a flat, shallow bay, milder water and steady winds; crowds in July and August.","a":[["Puck","A small old town on the bay, with its square and its Gothic church."],["Coastal Landscape Park","Protected dunes, cliffs and forests along the Pomeranian coast."],["Jastarnia Lighthouse","A lighthouse on the Hel Peninsula, between pine forest and Baltic beach."],["Rzucewo Castle","A neo-Gothic manor in a park on the shore of the Bay of Puck."]]},"walchensee":{"v":"At the foot of the Bavarian Pre-Alps, the lake’s turquoise reflections inspired the painter Lovis Corinth from his house in Urfeld. Today, windsurfers and hikers meet here over a Brotzeit.","l":"German, Bavarian","h":["Grüß Gott","“hello” in Bavaria, the everyday greeting"],"f":["Bavarian Brotzeit","Pretzels, obatzda and cold meats, to share under the trees of a Biergarten."],"r":"Kitesurfing is only tolerated in specific sectors: ask locally and park in the car parks, never at the roadside.","s":"May to August: the thermal wind picks up almost every sunny afternoon, and the water is finally bearable.","a":[["Herzogstand","A summit reached by cable car, with a plunging view over the Walchensee and the Kochelsee."],["Kochelsee","A glacial lake at the foot of the mountains, fringed with reed beds."],["Franz Marc Museum","Works by the Expressionist painter and his friends of the Blue Rider."],["Benediktbeuern Abbey","A large Baroque monastery at the gateway to the Alps."],["Ettal Abbey","A Benedictine abbey with a Baroque dome, near Oberammergau."]]},"eisbach":{"v":"Where it leaves the tunnel, the river channel swells into a wave that Munich surfers ride in turn. Just behind, the English Garden and its Biergärten embody the Bavarian art of living.","l":"German, Bavarian","h":["Servus","“hi” in Bavaria, for arriving as well as leaving"],"f":["Weißwurst and pretzel","White veal sausage, sweet mustard and a pretzel, served in the morning."],"r":"Only one surfer at a time: wait your turn in the queue on the bank. The wave is for experienced surfers only, as the bottom is hard and shallow.","s":"All year round when the flow allows; the wave depends on the upkeep of the riverbed, so check it is working before you come.","a":[["Munich Residenz","The former palace of the Wittelsbachs, with state rooms and the royal treasury."],["Bavarian National Museum","Decorative arts, sculptures and nativity scenes, from the Middle Ages to the 19th century."],["Haus der Kunst","A contemporary art museum, right next to the wave."],["Hofgarten","A Renaissance garden with its small temple, at the foot of the Residenz."],["Feldherrnhalle","A monumental loggia on Odeonsplatz, inspired by Florence."]]},"silvaplana":{"v":"In the Upper Engadine, Romansh can be read on houses decorated with sgraffito and the lake slips between larches and peaks. Every summer afternoon, the Maloja wind covers the water with colourful sails.","l":"Romansh, German, Italian","h":["Allegra","“hello” in Romansh, the language of the Engadine"],"f":["Engadine nut tart","Shortcrust pastry filled with caramelised walnuts, the pride of Graubünden’s pastry chefs."],"r":"At 1,800 metres, the water stays icy even in summer: bring a thick wetsuit and respect the separation between kite and windsurf.","s":"June to September: the Maloja wind almost every afternoon and a lake free of ice.","a":[["Morteratsch Glacier","A large glacier of the Bernina, with a marked trail up to its snout."],["Lake Sils","A neighbouring lake with wild shores, lined with Engadine villages."],["Piz Corvatsch","A cable car up to a viewpoint over the lakes and the Bernina massif."],["Val Roseg","A car-free valley, on foot or by horse-drawn carriage up to the glaciers."],["St. Moritz","A chic Alpine resort on the shore of its lake, between grand hotels and trails."]]},"podersdorf":{"v":"Reed beds, vineyards and the white villages of the Seewinkel: here the Pannonian plain opens onto a flat lake where the wind blows freely, and you end your session with a glass of Burgenland wine.","f":["Lake Neusiedl pike-perch","Fish from the lake, pan-fried or grilled in the inns of the lakeside villages."],"r":"Kiting is only allowed in designated zones: check with the schools and keep away from the reed beds, a refuge for protected birds.","s":"April to June and September: more frequent wind, water that warms up quickly and a less crowded beach than in summer.","a":[["Neusiedler See-Seewinkel National Park","Salt pans, steppe and reed beds where you can watch waterfowl and waders."],["Esterházy Palace","Baroque palace of the Esterházy princes in Eisenstadt, where Haydn was Kapellmeister."],["Heidentor of Carnuntum","Large ruined Roman arch, a remnant of the ancient city of Carnuntum on the Danube."],["Haydn’s Birthplace","Small farmhouse in Rohrau turned into a museum devoted to the composer."],["Fertő-Hanság National Park","The Hungarian side of the lake: marshes, wet meadows and observation trails."]]},"bled":{"v":"No engines on this lake: it’s the pletnas, wooden rowing boats handed down from father to son among the boatmen, that take you to the island. Bled is also a stronghold of Slovenian rowing.","r":"Motorboats are banned on the lake: on a paddleboard or in a kayak, give way to the pletnas shuttling to the island.","s":"Late June to early September: the water becomes pleasant; come early in the morning to enjoy the lake before the crowds.","a":[["Vintgar Gorge","Wooden walkways above a deep turquoise river, all the way to a waterfall."],["Pokljuka Plateau","Karst plateau covered in forest and alpine pastures, a starting point for hikes towards Triglav."],["Mount Triglav","Slovenia’s highest peak and national symbol, climbed by hikers over two days."],["Vrba","Birthplace of the poet France Prešeren, with his house museum and church."]]},"soca":{"v":"The valley keeps the memory of the Isonzo Front of the Great War, among trenches and chapels. Today Bovec lives to the rhythm of rafting guides, kayakers and hikers.","f":["Bovec cheese","Sheep’s cheese from the valley’s alpine pastures, with a bold, slightly sharp taste."],"r":"Paddling on the Soča requires a permit and is only allowed on certain stretches: go through a local club and respect the anglers.","s":"April to June: snowmelt swells the river and livens up the rapids; summer is calmer and very busy.","a":[["Triglav National Park","Slovenia’s only national park: limestone peaks, glacial lakes and mountain streams."],["Kobarid","Small town known for its First World War museum and historical trail."],["Log pod Mangartom","Village at the foot of Mangart, the starting point of the scenic road towards the summit."],["Lake Bohinj","Large, wild glacial lake, more peaceful than Bled, surrounded by mountains."]]},"balaton":{"v":"Siófok is the Hungarian summer: managed beaches, lángos stands, glasses of fröccs, wine mixed with sparkling water, and lively evenings. Across the water, the north shore is lined with vineyards and volcanic hills.","r":"Most of Siófok’s beaches are managed “strand”: use the showers and bins, and stay within the marked swimming areas.","s":"June to August: the shallow water warms quickly and becomes some of the mildest of any lake in Central Europe.","a":[["Tihany Peninsula","Hilltop village on a volcanic peninsula, lavender fields and views over the lake."],["Tihany Abbey","Baroque Benedictine abbey overlooking the lake, with a medieval crypt."],["Balatonföldvár","Leafy resort on the south shore, with its marina and promenade."]]},"ohrid":{"v":"Ohrid, cradle of Slavic writing with Saint Clement, is lined with Byzantine churches and overhanging houses. In Kaneo, the old fishermen’s quarter, wooden boats still wait at the foot of the rocks.","r":"St John at Kaneo is a working church: cover your shoulders and knees to go in, and don’t order wild Ohrid trout, a protected species.","s":"June to September: the lake water becomes pleasant; June and September avoid the July–August crowds.","a":[["Ohrid Old Town","Cobbled lanes, Samuel’s Fortress, an ancient theatre and churches with Byzantine frescoes."],["Radožda","Waterside fishing village, with a small cave church in the cliff."],["Lin","Albanian village on a peninsula, known for the mosaics of an early Christian basilica."],["Peštani","Village on the east shore, pebble beaches and access to Galičica National Park."]]},"como":{"v":"At the north end of the lake, the Alto Lario lives between mountain and water: stone villages, old Spanish forts and fishermen who still dry shad in the sun, following a centuries-old tradition.","f":["Missoltini","Lake shad, dried then grilled, served with polenta."],"r":"Kitesurfing and windsurfing are only allowed in the authorised zones of Colico and Dervio: follow the clubs’ instructions and give way to the ferries.","s":"May to September: in fine weather the thermal winds are at their most reliable and the lake water warms up.","a":[["Bellagio","Elegant village at the tip where the lake’s two arms meet, stepped lanes and villa gardens."],["Piona Abbey","Waterside abbey on a peninsula, with a lovely Romanesque cloister."],["Vezio Castle","Medieval tower above Varenna, with a sweeping view down over the lake."],["Pian di Spagna Reserve","Wetland at the mouths of the Adda and Mera, rich in migratory birds."],["Fort Fuentes","Ruins of a Spanish fortress on a hill, facing the plain and the lake."]]},"portopollo":{"v":"Wind-carved granite, maquis and stazzi, the isolated farmsteads of Gallura: here windsurfers and kiters from all over the world meet between gusts blowing in from the Strait of Bonifacio.","l":"Italian, Gallurese","f":["Zuppa gallurese","Bread, cheese and broth baked until golden, a peasant dish from Gallura."],"r":"Windsurfers and kiters share the bay by zones: respect the launch corridors and don’t trample the dunes on the isthmus.","s":"May to October: the wind funnelled through the Strait often blows hard, especially outside high summer.","a":[["La Maddalena Archipelago National Park","Pink granite islands and crystal-clear water, to explore by boat from Palau."],["Caprera","Wild island linked to La Maddalena by a causeway, pine woods and coves where Garibaldi lived."],["Budelli","Protected island famous for its pink beach, admired from the sea."],["Coddu Vecchiu Giants’ Tomb","Nuragic collective tomb with a large granite stele, near Arzachena."],["Nuraghe La Prisgiona","Bronze Age stone tower surrounded by the remains of its village."]]},"pelosa":{"v":"Stintino, a fishing village founded by families driven off Asinara, long lived from its tuna trap. Facing the beach, the Aragonese tower still watches over the passage to the island.","r":"Access is limited and requires a paid booking: put your towel on a mat, as the rules require, and take away no sand or pebbles.","s":"June and September: turquoise water that’s already warm and places easier to book than in July–August.","a":[["Asinara National Park","Wild island and former penal colony, home to white donkeys; boat trips from Stintino."],["Argentiera","Former silver-mining village facing the sea, with restored industrial buildings."]]},"calaluna":{"v":"At the foot of the Supramonte, a land of shepherds and nuraghi, the cliffs hide caves where the monk seal once lived. The fishermen of Cala Gonone have become boatmen, but the coast still has no road.","l":"Italian, Sardinian","f":["Culurgiones","Ravioli sealed in a wheat-ear pleat, filled with potato, pecorino and mint."],"r":"Camping and fires are banned on the beach: in a kayak, follow the coast away from boat traffic and take all your rubbish home.","s":"May–June and September: often calm sea, mild water and a cove less overrun by boats than in August.","a":[["Tiscali Nuragic Village","Ruins of a village hidden deep in a collapsed sinkhole, at the end of a fine walk."],["Cala Goloritzé","White-pebble cove overlooked by a limestone pinnacle, reachable on foot."],["Ispinigoli Cave","Huge underground chamber around a giant stalagmite column."],["Orgosolo","Barbagia village whose façades are covered in politically charged murals."],["Su Gologone Spring","Karst spring of blue water rising from a fissure at the foot of the Supramonte."]]},"tavolara":{"v":"Tavolara nurtures the legend of a tiny island kingdom passed down in one island family. Around it, Olbia’s fishermen and dive centres share a protected sea where the grouper has returned.","f":["Olbia mussels","Farmed in the Gulf of Olbia, served sautéed with garlic and parsley."],"r":"Dive with a centre approved by the marine area: fully protected zones are off-limits, no anchoring on the posidonia and no touching the gorgonians.","s":"June to October: warm water, good visibility and a sea often calm in the island’s lee.","a":[["Basilica of San Simplicio","Romanesque granite church, the oldest in Olbia, in the heart of the town."],["Molara Island","Wild granite island in the marine area, with coves you reach by boat."],["San Teodoro","Seaside resort with long white-sand beaches, backed by a lagoon with flamingos."],["Su Mont’e s’Abe Giants’ Tomb","Nuragic collective tomb with a large stone façade, near Olbia."]]},"zingaro":{"v":"The reserve was born when locals campaigned against a coastal road. Between dwarf palms and the old tuna traps of Scopello, this coast still lives from fishing, prickly pears and mule tracks.","f":["Fish couscous","Hand-rolled semolina and fish broth, the pride of San Vito Lo Capo."],"r":"There’s an entry fee and water is scarce: bring plenty to drink, stay on the paths, no fires, and take all your rubbish home.","s":"May–June and September: clear water that’s already warm, and trails more bearable than in high summer.","a":[["Segesta","Solitary Doric temple and ancient theatre perched facing the hills."],["Erice","Medieval hilltop town, stone lanes and almond pastry shops."],["Scopello","Hamlet around an old courtyard, with its tuna trap and faraglioni sea stacks."],["San Vito Lo Capo","White village beside a long pale-sand beach, at the foot of Monte Monaco."],["Scurati Caves","Caves sheltering an old hamlet, including the Mangiapane cave and its living nativity."]]},"calarossa":{"v":"Favignana lived from its tuff quarries and the Florio family’s tuna trap, where the mattanza of tuna took place. You get around by bike between old quarries turned into gardens and coves carved into the stone.","f":["Favignana tuna","Tuna and bottarga, fresh or preserved, a legacy of the island’s tuna traps."],"r":"In the Egadi Islands marine protected area, don’t take sea urchins or shellfish and don’t drop anchor on the posidonia.","s":"June to September: warm, clear water; June and September avoid the packed boats of mid-August.","a":[["Favignana Tonnara","The Florio family’s former tuna cannery, turned into a fishing museum."],["Levanzo","Small, peaceful island, white village and a cave decorated with prehistoric paintings."],["Motya","Islet in the lagoon with the remains of a Phoenician city and a museum."],["Stagnone Lagoon","Salt pans, windmills and shallow water, magnificent at sunset."],["Marsala","Baroque town renowned for its sweet wine and cellars open to visitors."]]},"conigli":{"v":"Lampedusa, closer to Africa than to Sicily, lives from fishing and the sea. A landing point for many migrants, the island also carries a powerful human memory, beyond its coves.","r":"The beach closes at night during the loggerhead turtles’ nesting season: respect the nest enclosures, the opening hours and the limited number of visitors.","s":"June and September: warm, clear water, and fewer people than in August on a beach with limited access.","a":[["Gate of Europe","Sculpture facing the sea, dedicated to the migrants lost in the Mediterranean."],["Lampione Islet","Uninhabited offshore rock, renowned for its diving and marine life."]]},"stagnone":{"v":"Salt pans, windmills and white heaps of salt line the lagoon, with the Phoenician city of Motya opposite. Between sessions, you taste Marsala wine in the cellars that made the town’s fortune.","f":["Busiate with pesto trapanese","Twisted pasta with a pesto of tomato, almonds, garlic and basil."],"r":"The Stagnone is a nature reserve: kite only in the authorised zones, away from swimmers and from the boat route to Motya.","s":"April to October: steady wind over the lagoon and flat water, which gets really warm in summer.","a":[["Motya","Islet in the lagoon with the remains of a Phoenician city and a museum."],["Marsala","Baroque town renowned for its sweet wine and cellars open to visitors."],["Trapani","Old town on a narrow peninsula, Baroque churches and a port for the Egadi Islands."],["Sanctuary of the Madonna of Trapani","Revered basilica housing a marble statue of the Virgin, much loved by fishermen."]]},"portofino":{"v":"Between the fishermen of Camogli and the Doria abbey, the headland lives facing the sea. The submerged statue has become a symbol for divers, honoured every summer with a ceremony at sea.","f":["Focaccia di Recco","Very thin dough filled with soft melting cheese, a speciality of the neighbouring village."],"r":"In the Portofino marine area, dive with an approved centre, don’t drop anchor and don’t touch the red coral on the drop-offs.","s":"June to October: warm water and calm sea; visibility is often best in early autumn.","a":[["Abbey of San Fruttuoso","Medieval abbey at the head of the cove, with its tower and cloister facing the sea."],["Castello Brown","Fortress above Portofino harbour, gardens and a view over the bay."],["Portofino Lighthouse","A walk under the pines to the point, looking out to the open sea."],["Sanctuary of Montallegro","Hilltop sanctuary above Rapallo, reached by cable car, with a wide view."],["Rapallo","Riviera resort, seafront promenade and a small castle on the water."]]},"polignano":{"v":"Birthplace of Domenico Modugno, the singer of “Volare”, Polignano lives perched on cliffs riddled with sea caves. Its rocks also host international high-diving competitions.","f":["Orecchiette with cime di rapa","Small ear-shaped pasta with turnip tops, garlic and anchovies."],"r":"Cliff diving is reserved for supervised athletes: don’t copy it, and come early because the tiny cove fills up fast.","s":"June and September: warm water and a less crowded cove than in July–August.","a":[["Castellana Caves","Vast karst network of chambers and formations, all the way to the famous White Cave."],["Monopoli","Fishing port with blue boats, a white old town and ramparts on the sea."],["Conversano Castle","Norman castle overlooking a small historic town inland."],["Abbey of San Vito","Fortified abbey on the water’s edge, facing a small fishing harbour."]],"lbl":"B&B"},"illetes":{"v":"Formentera is best taken slowly, by bike among salt pans, dry-stone walls and propped-up fig trees. Here fishermen still dry fish in the sun, and the posidonia keeps the water so clear.","l":"Spanish, Catalan","h":["Bon dia","“good morning” in Catalan, the language of the Balearics"],"f":["Ensalada payesa with peix sec","Sun-dried fish, potatoes and peppers, a Formentera recipe."],"r":"In the natural park, stay on the boardwalks that protect the dunes and never drop anchor on the posidonia, which is protected by law.","s":"June and September: turquoise water that’s already warm and a sandspit less crowded than in July–August.","a":[["S’Espalmador","Private islet with white-sand beaches, reached by boat from Formentera."],["Cap de Barbaria","Arid plateau in the south of the island, a lonely lighthouse and cliffs at sunset."],["Ibiza, Dalt Vila","UNESCO-listed fortified old town, white lanes and ramparts."],["Sa Caleta Phoenician Site","Remains of a Phoenician village by the sea, on Ibiza’s south coast."]],"lbl":"Hostal"},"calacomte":{"v":"On Ibiza’s west coast, far from the clubs, the pagès countryside keeps its white cube-shaped houses and carob trees. In the evening, locals and travellers gather on the rocks to watch the sun sink into the sea.","l":"Spanish, Catalan","h":["Bon dia","“good morning” in Catalan, the language of the Balearics"],"f":["Bullit de peix","Fish and potato stew, followed by rice cooked in the broth."],"r":"The car park fills up quickly: come early or by bus from Sant Antoni, and don’t drop anchor on the posidonia meadows.","s":"June and September: warm, clear water, sunsets without the August crowds.","a":[["Es Vedrà","Dizzying rocky islet rising from the sea, shrouded in legends."],["Ibiza, Dalt Vila","UNESCO-listed fortified old town, white lanes and ramparts."],["Cala d’Hort","Sandy cove facing Es Vedrà, with a few fish restaurants."],["Sa Talaiassa","The island’s highest point, covered in pines, with a view over the whole coast."],["Cala Salada","Clear-water cove lined with pines and fishermen’s boat shelters."]]},"estrenc":{"v":"Behind the dunes, the Es Trenc salt pans still produce fleur de sel, and the countryside of southern Mallorca is lined with stone walls, almond trees and windmills. A beach still free of buildings, defended by the locals.","l":"Spanish, Catalan","h":["Bon dia","“good morning” in Catalan, the language of the Balearics"],"f":["Sobrassada","Raw, spreadable paprika sausage, to spread on Mallorcan country bread."],"r":"The natural park’s dunes are fragile: use the marked access paths, don’t pick any plants and don’t enter the salt pans, which are private.","s":"June and September: clear, warm water, car parks less packed than in high summer.","a":[["Cabrera Archipelago National Park","Wild protected islands, a hilltop castle and a blue cave, by boat from the coast."],["Portopetro","Small, peaceful fishing port at the head of an inlet, white houses and boats."],["Ses Salines","Rural village in the south of the island, gateway to the salt pans and Cap de Ses Salines."],["Campos","Farming town of stone houses, with its weekly market."]],"lbl":"Hostal"},"capdecreus":{"v":"Rocks twisted by the tramontane, the white houses of Cadaqués and fishing boats: this landscape fed Dalí’s imagination. Life here faces the sea, to the rhythm of the north winds.","l":"Spanish, Catalan","h":["Bon dia","“good morning” in Catalan, which people will gladly say back to you"],"f":["Suquet de peix","Rockfish and potato stew, a classic of Catalan fishermen."],"r":"The tramontane can rise suddenly: check the forecast before setting off in a kayak, and respect the fully protected reserves where diving is banned.","s":"June to September: milder water and good visibility, between bursts of tramontane.","a":[["Dalí House-Museum","The painter’s labyrinthine home and studio, facing Portlligat bay."],["Monastery of Sant Pere de Rodes","Romanesque abbey perched on the mountain, with a vast view over the Gulf of Lion."],["Citadel of Roses","Fortified enclosure housing the remains of a Greek city and a monastery."],["Basilica of Castelló d’Empúries","Large Gothic church in the heart of a medieval Empordà town."],["Cap Norfeu","Wild protected headland, a path to cliffs plunging into the sea."]]},"monsul":{"v":"The driest land in Europe, Cabo de Gata blends extinct volcanoes, old tuna traps and white farmhouses. Its Wild West landscapes have been the backdrop for many film shoots.","f":["Migas de Almería","Pan-fried flour semolina, served with fried sardines and peppers."],"r":"In summer, car access is restricted: take the shuttle from San José, don’t climb on the dunes and take your rubbish home.","s":"May–June and September: clear water, bearable heat and easier access than in high summer.","a":[["Cabo de Gata-Níjar Natural Park","Wild volcanic coast, coves, cliffs and salt pans home to flamingos."],["San José","White village on a bay, a handy base for the park’s beaches."],["Los Albaricoques","White inland hamlet, a setting for spaghetti westerns."]]},"larestinga":{"v":"A fishing village at the far end of the Canary world, La Restinga lived through the nearby undersea eruption before being reborn around diving. Black lava flows and small-scale fishing shape everyday life.","f":["El Hierro cheese","Goat’s and sheep’s milk cheese, often lightly smoked."],"r":"In the marine reserve, dive only with an approved centre: dive numbers are limited, the fully protected zone is off-limits and fishing is banned.","s":"All year round, but from June to November the water is warmer and the sea often very calm.","a":[["Orchilla Lighthouse","Isolated lighthouse on lava flows, near the old prime meridian."],["Pico de Malpaso","The island’s highest point, a hike among volcanic cones and pine forests."],["La Frontera","Municipality in the El Golfo valley, terraced vineyards beneath high cliffs."]]},"lascanteras":{"v":"The locals’ beach in Las Palmas, where people swim before work and stroll along the paseo in the evening. Card players, La Cícer surfers and swimmers all share the same water.","f":["Papas arrugadas with mojo","Small potatoes boiled in salted water, served with a chilli sauce."],"r":"Don’t walk on La Barra, the reef that shelters rich marine life; at the La Cícer peak, respect the local surfers’ priority.","s":"All year round: pleasant water even in winter; summer brings flat calm behind La Barra.","a":[["Santa Ana Cathedral","Cathedral in the historic Vegueta district, with a view over the city from its tower."],["Painted Cave of Gáldar","Cave decorated with geometric patterns by the ancient Canarians, with a museum."],["Canarian Botanical Garden","Large garden devoted to the Canaries’ endemic flora, in a ravine."],["Canarian Museum","Archaeology museum devoted to the island’s first inhabitants."],["Cenobio de Valerón","Ancient communal granary carved into the cliff by the Canarians."]]},"portomoniz":{"v":"On Madeira’s north coast, vines cling to the cliffs, sheltered from the wind by heather hedges. The villages live from fishing and the levadas, the channels that have irrigated the island for centuries.","f":["Espada with banana","Fillet of deep-sea black scabbardfish, pan-fried and served with banana."],"r":"In heavy swell, follow the lifeguards’ instructions and never swim in the nearby wild pools, which are swept by the waves.","s":"June to October: milder water and a calmer sea on this north coast, often battered in winter.","a":[["25 Fontes Levada","Path along an irrigation channel to a cirque of waterfalls."],["Seixal","North-coast village, black-sand beach and natural pools."],["São Vicente Caves","Lava tubes open to visitors, formed by an ancient eruption."],["Ponta do Pargo","Lighthouse atop high cliffs, ideal at sunset."],["Paul do Mar","Fishing village at the foot of the cliffs, known to surfers."]]},"berlengas":{"v":"Off the big fishing port of Peniche, a block of granite battered by the Atlantic, where hardly anyone lives but the reserve wardens, a few fishermen in summer and colonies of seabirds.","f":["Caldeirada de Peniche","Stew of the day’s fish, potatoes and tomatoes, simmered as it is on the boats."],"r":"Visitor numbers on the island are limited: book your crossing, stay on the paths and keep your distance from nesting birds.","s":"June to September: calmer sea for the crossing, regular boats and better visibility around the island.","a":[["Fort of São João Baptista","17th-century fortress set on an islet, linked to the island by a narrow stone bridge."],["Peniche Fortress","Seafront citadel, a former political prison now a museum of the resistance."],["Baleal Island","Peninsula linked by a sandbar, surrounded by beaches and surf spots."],["Óbidos Lagoon","Vast calm lagoon cut off from the ocean by a sand barrier, ideal for paddling."]]},"myrtos":{"v":"Here, almost every village was rebuilt after the 1953 earthquake; only a few northern harbours such as Fiskardo keep their Venetian houses, facing Ithaca.","f":["Kefalonian kreatopita","Meat pie with rice and herbs, a speciality of the island’s tables."],"r":"The road down to the beach is narrow and the car park fills up fast: arrive early. Watch out for the shore break on the pebbles when there’s a swell.","s":"June to September: warm water and a sea often calm; aim for June or September to avoid the August crowds.","a":[["Assos","Small fishing village at the foot of a peninsula crowned by a Venetian fortress."],["Fiskardo","Harbour of colourful houses spared by the earthquake, quayside tavernas facing Ithaca."],["Castle of St George","Venetian ramparts on a hill, with a wide view over the south of the island."],["Argostoli","The island’s capital: seafront, archaeological museum and loggerhead turtles in the harbour."],["Petani Beach","White-pebble cove beneath the cliffs of the Paliki peninsula."]],"lbl":"Studios"},"portokatsiki":{"v":"At the end of the wild peninsula in south-west Lefkada, you head down to the sea among olive trees, goats and white cliffs; caiques from the nearby harbours drop swimmers off here.","f":["Lefkada salami","Dry, fragrant pork sausage, the pride of the island’s mountain villages."],"r":"Don’t settle at the foot of the cliffs, which are prone to rockfalls. Arrive early: the small car park at the top of the steps fills up fast in summer.","s":"June to September: warm turquoise water; in the morning, before the boats arrive, the beach stays quiet.","a":[["Egremni Beach","Long white-pebble beach beneath the cliffs, reached by a staircase or by boat."],["Vassiliki","Southern harbour village, a windsurfers’ haunt, where the water taxis leave from."],["Agios Nikitas","West-coast fishing village, flower-filled lanes and a small sheltered beach."],["Meganissi","Small green island with secluded coves, reached by ferry from Nydri."]]},"vassiliki":{"v":"A fishing port turned windsurfers’ haunt: the thermal wind the regulars nickname “Eric” picks up in the afternoon and sets the pace of the days, between board centres and quayside tavernas.","f":["Lefkada salami","Dry, fragrant pork sausage, the pride of the island’s mountain villages."],"r":"Respect the launch corridors in front of the centres and the marked swimming areas; if you’re a beginner, sail in the morning and leave the afternoon to experienced riders.","s":"June to September: the thermal blows almost every afternoon, stronger in July and August.","a":[["Porto Katsiki","Turquoise beach at the foot of high white cliffs, reached by boat from the harbour."],["Egremni Beach","Long white-pebble beach beneath the cliffs of the west coast."],["Skorpios","Wooded islet once owned by the Onassis family, which you pass by boat."],["Meganissi","Small green island with secluded coves and quiet villages."]]},"prasonisi":{"v":"At the very south of Rhodes, far from the east-coast resorts, a windswept spit of sand where life follows the rhythm of the board-sports centres, with a few farming villages for neighbours.","f":["Pitaroudia","Chickpea fritters served as meze in Rhodian tavernas."],"r":"The centres keep kite and windsurf zones separate: ask before rigging up. Don’t drive on the sandbar.","s":"June to September: the meltemi blows strong and steady, especially in July and August."},"elafonisi":{"v":"At the south-western tip of Crete, far from the big towns, a region of olive-growing villages and small mountain roads; Cretan families come here for picnics too.","f":["Dakos","Barley rusk topped with grated tomato, mizithra cheese and olive oil."],"r":"Natura 2000 site: take no pink sand or shells and don’t walk in the islet’s dunes and junipers.","s":"June and September: a warm lagoon, less crowded than in high summer, when the buses arrive from the morning.","a":[["Chrysoskalitissa Monastery","White monastery perched on a rock above the sea."],["Falassarna","Large sandy beach facing the sunset, near the remains of an ancient harbour."],["Paleochora","Peaceful town on a peninsula, between a sandy beach and a pebble beach."],["Sougia","Seaside hamlet at the mouth of a gorge, a starting point for coastal hikes."]]},"kleftiko":{"v":"A volcanic island mined since antiquity for its obsidian and later its minerals, Milos keeps its “syrmata”, fishermen’s houses with colourful doors sheltering boats right at the water’s edge.","r":"At midday, excursion boats crowd into the arches: come early by kayak, don’t climb on the crumbly rock and leave nothing in the caves.","s":"June and September: warm water and a sea often calmer than in July–August, when the meltemi stirs up the coast."},"goldenbeach_paros":{"v":"Paros, island of the white marble sculpted in antiquity, lives between whitewashed inland villages and small harbours where fishing caiques still unload octopus, hung to dry in front of the tavernas.","r":"Head out through the schools’ marked corridors and keep your distance from swimmers, who crowd the water’s edge in summer.","s":"June to September: the meltemi often blows hard, especially in July and August.","a":[["Naoussa","Fishing port with white lanes, guarded by the ruins of a small Venetian fort."],["Antiparos","Small, quiet neighbouring island, renowned for its stalactite cave."],["Marpissa","White hilltop village, with windmills and a monastery at the top."],["Naxos","Large neighbouring island: Venetian old town, the gateway of the Temple of Apollo and mountain villages."]]},"kamenjak":{"v":"At the tip of Istria, a heath of pines, junipers and wild orchids where dinosaur footprints mark the rock; Pula’s locals come here in summer to dive off the rocks.","l":"Croatian, Italian","f":["Fuži with Istrian truffles","Hand-rolled Istrian pasta, topped with grated truffle from the peninsula’s interior."],"r":"Protected landscape: car access is paid in summer, drive slowly on the tracks, no fires and no wild camping.","s":"June and September: clear, warm water and coves less busy than in high summer.","a":[["Pula Arena","One of the best-preserved Roman amphitheatres in the world, still used for concerts."],["Temple of Augustus","Intact Roman temple on Pula’s ancient forum."],["Porta Gemina","Double-arched Roman gate, the entrance to Pula’s old town."],["Nesactium","Archaeological site of a Histrian, then Roman, town above a bay."],["Premantura","Village at the entrance to the cape, with campsites and water-sports schools."]]},"viganj":{"v":"The Pelješac peninsula, land of ocean-going captains, plavac mali vines and stone villages, faces Korčula; in Viganj, windsurfers’ campsites line the water.","f":["Ston oysters","Farmed in Mali Ston Bay, at the other end of the Pelješac peninsula."],"r":"Ferries between Orebić and Korčula cross the channel: keep your distance from boats and never sail alone in strong wind.","s":"June to September: the maestral picks up almost every afternoon, most reliably in July and August.","a":[["Korčula Old Town","Fortified town on a peninsula, herringbone lanes and ramparts."],["St Mark’s Cathedral, Korčula","Gothic and Renaissance cathedral in carved stone, in the heart of the old town."],["Franciscan Monastery of Orebić","Monastery perched above Orebić, with an open view over the channel and Korčula."],["Badija Island","Wooded islet home to a former Franciscan monastery, reached by boat."],["Lumbarda","Wine village on Korčula, sandy beaches and grk white wine."]]},"ulcinj":{"v":"Ulcinj, a mainly Albanian town, blends minarets, a fortified old town and a past as a corsairs’ lair; on the Bojana, fishing huts on stilts spread their big square lift nets.","l":"Montenegrin, Albanian","h":["Tungjatjeta","“hello” in Albanian, the majority language in Ulcinj"],"r":"Kite only in the schools’ zones, away from swimmers. In town, cover up near mosques and away from the beach.","s":"June to September: steady thermal wind in the afternoon, most established in July and August.","a":[["Ulcinj Old Town","Citadel perched above the sea, cobbled lanes and views along the coast."],["Ada Bojana","Triangular island at the mouth of the Bojana, fish restaurants on stilts."],["Buna River–Velipojë Protected Landscape","Delta, lagoons and coastal forests on the Albanian side, a refuge for waterbirds."]]},"cirkewwa":{"v":"At Malta’s northern tip, where the ferries leave for Gozo, dive clubs’ vans park in a row; the whole island lives from the sea, from luzzu boats to wrecks turned into reefs.","r":"The ferries pass very close: stay within the dive area, come up along the edge and deploy a surface marker buoy.","s":"June to October: water between 20 and 26 °C and excellent visibility; the sea stays diveable in winter, just cooler.","a":[["Comino","Small, almost uninhabited island, famous for the turquoise water of the Blue Lagoon."],["Ġgantija Temples","Megalithic temples on Gozo, among the oldest stone buildings in the world."],["Majjistral Park","Nature park of garrigue and cliffs, coastal paths in north-west Malta."],["Mellieħa Sanctuary","Marian shrine partly carved into the rock, an ancient place of pilgrimage."],["Popeye Village","Wooden village built for a film, set beside a cove."]]},"zenobia":{"v":"Larnaca lives facing the sea: palm-lined promenade, the Church of Saint Lazarus, a fishing harbour and a salt lake where flamingos winter; almost all its dive clubs revolve around the same wreck.","r":"Descend along the mooring lines and only enter the wreck with the right training. Bring nothing up: the wreck is protected.","s":"May to November: warm water, a generally calm sea and very good visibility; you can dive all year in a thick wetsuit.","a":[["Hala Sultan Tekke","Ottoman mosque and mausoleum on the shore of the salt lake, a holy site of Cypriot Islam."],["Larnaca Salt Lake","Brackish lagoon where flamingos gather in winter."],["Stavrovouni Monastery","Orthodox monastery perched on a rocky peak, with a view over the whole plain."],["Larnaca District Archaeological Museum","Collections from ancient Kition: pottery, sculptures and everyday objects."]]},"capegreco":{"v":"In the island’s south-east, between red-earth villages where potatoes are grown and the old fishing port of Ayia Napa, the cape remains a plateau of garrigue, cliffs and sea caves.","r":"Stay on the park’s paths and don’t drive off the tracks. Avoid jumping off the cliffs: rocky bottoms and swell make jumps dangerous.","s":"June to October: warm, clear water; in May or October, fewer people on the ladders and in the coves.","a":[["Ayia Napa","Former fishing village around a 16th-century Venetian monastery."],["Fig Tree Bay","Bay of pale sand and shallow water facing an islet, ideal for swimming."]],"lbl":"Apartment"},"oludeniz":{"v":"At the foot of Babadağ, paragliders drift down towards the beach while gulets set off for the coves; it’s also the starting point of the Lycian Way, a long-distance coastal trail.","r":"The lagoon is a protected park with an entry fee. On the beach, keep the paragliders’ landing zone clear.","s":"May–June and September–October: warm water, a calm lagoon and fewer crowds than in July–August.","a":[["Kayaköy","Greek village abandoned in 1923, hundreds of stone houses on the hillside."],["Butterfly Valley","Cove at the bottom of a canyon, reached by boat, with a waterfall and butterflies in summer."],["Gemiler Island","Islet covered in ruined Byzantine churches, to explore by boat or kayak."],["Mount Babadağ","Summit where the paragliders take off, with a plunging view over the lagoon."],["Tomb of Amyntas","Lycian tomb carved into the cliff above Fethiye, with a temple façade."]]},"alacati":{"v":"A former Greek village of stone houses and colourful shutters, overlooked by its windmills, Alaçatı has become a chic hideaway for Izmir; down below, the bay is still the windsurfers’ playground.","f":["Çeşme kumru","Hot sandwich of sucuk, cheese and tomato in a soft sesame bun."],"r":"The bay is shared by many schools: respect right of way, stay out of the marina channel and keep your distance from beginners.","s":"June to September: steady, strong northerly wind, especially in July and August, on flat water.","a":[["Alaçatı Village","Lanes of stone houses, windmills on the hill, cafés and shops."],["Erythrae","Ruins of an Ionian city above the sea, a theatre and an acropolis."],["Ildır","Quiet fishing village built among the remains of Erythrae."],["Ilıca","Long fine-sand beach where hot springs bubble up in the sea."]]},"kas":{"v":"Once ancient Antiphellos, Kaş keeps Lycian tombs in the middle of its streets and a theatre facing the sea; from its little harbour you can see the Greek island of Kastellorizo, a ferry ride away.","r":"At Kekova, swimming and diving are banned above the sunken ruins: look at them from your kayak without landing.","s":"June to October: warm water and often excellent visibility; September and October are calmer and still warm.","a":[["Kaleköy (Simena)","Roadless hamlet at the foot of a castle, facing the sunken ruins of Kekova."],["Kastellorizo","Small Greek island of colourful houses around a harbour, reached by ferry."],["Antiphellos","Hellenistic theatre facing the sea and Lycian tombs in the heart of Kaş."],["Aperlae","Partly submerged Lycian city, to discover by kayak or snorkelling."],["Kalkan","Former fishing village of white houses stacked above a bay."]]},"bureh":{"v":"Between forested hills and fishing pirogues pulled up on the sand, the village founded its own surf club, run by local young people who teach and welcome visitors.","r":"Rent your gear and book your lessons with the community club, and make way for the fishermen when the pirogues come in to the beach.","s":"November to April: dry season, moderate swells for learning; the rainy season brings more waves and heavy downpours.","a":[["Banana Islands","Wooded islands reached by pirogue, coves, colonial remains and diving."],["Western Area Peninsula National Park","Rainforest in the hills above Freetown, a refuge for chimpanzees and birds."],["John Obey Beach","Sandy beach and fishing village beside a river, north of Bureh."]],"lbl":"Beach bungalow"},"boavista":{"v":"Sal Rei owes its name to the salt pans of old: a small town of colourful houses around a square, where fishermen land their catch on the beach and morna fills the evenings.","r":"Sail within the schools’ zone, away from fishing boats and swimmers, and don’t go out alone when the trade wind picks up offshore.","s":"November to April: steady, strong north-east trade winds, strongest from January to March.","a":[["Cabo Santa Maria Wreck","Rusting hull of a stranded cargo ship, lying on a long wild beach in the north."],["Viana Desert","Sea of white sand dunes in the heart of the island, among palms and hills."],["Santa Mónica Beach","Huge, deserted pale-sand beach on the south-west coast."],["Chaves Beach","Long beach backed by dunes, with the ruins of an old brickworks."],["Sal Rei Islet","Islet facing the town, ruins of a fort and small coves, reached by boat."]]},"walvisbay":{"v":"A fishing port that remained South African until 1994, Walvis Bay lives among morning fog, oyster beds, flamingos and Namib dunes that run right down to the coast.","r":"The lagoon is a protected wetland: don’t ride through groups of flamingos and other birds, and stay within the usual riding zones.","s":"October to March: strong south-westerly wind almost every afternoon; the water stays cold, so bring a thick wetsuit.","a":[["Dune 7","Tall dune to climb at sunrise, with a view over the desert and the town."],["Pelican Point Lighthouse","Lighthouse at the end of a sandspit, surrounded by seal colonies."],["Dorob National Park","Coastal desert between dunes and ocean, to explore by 4x4 on the permitted tracks."]]},"victoriabay":{"v":"A row of beach houses, a railway line along the cliff and a right-hander peeling along the rocks: the cove is a haunt for surfers from George and the Garden Route.","r":"A narrow line-up busy with regulars: wait your turn, don’t paddle to the peak uninvited and exit over the rocks with care.","s":"May to September: more consistent south-westerly Southern Ocean swells; the cool water calls for a wetsuit.","a":[["Wilderness","Village among a lagoon, lakes and forests, a starting point for riverside trails."],["Montagu Pass","Historic gravel pass road through the Outeniqua Mountains."],["Outeniqua Transport Museum","Steam locomotives and vintage cars, the memory of the Garden Route’s railway."],["Herolds Bay","Small sandy bay sheltered by cliffs, calm for swimming."]]},"coffeebay":{"v":"On the Wild Coast, the round huts of Xhosa villages dot the hills, cattle wander down to the sand and life follows the rhythm of the fields, the rivers and shore fishing.","l":"Xhosa, English","h":["Molo","“hello” in Xhosa, the language of the Wild Coast villages"],"r":"Ask before photographing people, go hiking with a guide from the village and avoid driving at night, because of cattle and potholes.","s":"April to September: more consistent Southern Ocean swells and offshore winds in the morning; the water stays pleasant all year.","lbl":"Backpackers"},"nahoon":{"v":"In East London, a port city in the Eastern Cape, surf culture is long-standing and demanding: Nahoon has shaped generations of surfers and bodyboarders used to cool, powerful water.","r":"A wave for experienced surfers only: respect the locals’ priority. Don’t surf alone, at dawn, at dusk or in murky water, because of sharks.","s":"May to August: south-westerly Southern Ocean swells and offshore westerly winds that make the right hollow.","a":[["East London Museum","Natural history museum displaying the first coelacanth identified in the 20th century."],["Gonubie","Small seaside resort at a river mouth, beach and wooden boardwalk."],["Umtiza Nature Reserve","Coastal forest sheltering the rare, endemic umtiza tree, with marked trails."]]},"boulders":{"v":"Simon’s Town, a naval port since British times, keeps its Victorian façades and the statue of Just Nuisance, a Great Dane enlisted in the Royal Navy; the penguins nest at the end of the street.","f":["Bobotie","Spiced curried minced meat topped with egg, inherited from Cape Malay cooking."],"r":"Stay on the boardwalks, and don’t touch or feed the penguins, which nip hard. Check under your car before you leave.","s":"December to March: southern summer, slightly less cold water and a south-easterly wind often softened in the coves.","a":[["Cape Point","Headland with dizzying cliffs and a lighthouse, at the end of the Cape Peninsula."],["Cape of Good Hope","Legendary rocky cape of seafaring, coastal paths, ostriches and baboons."],["Simon’s Town","Port town with Victorian façades, a marina and a naval base."],["South African Naval Museum","Naval museum housed in the dockyard’s old warehouses."]]},"proteabanks":{"v":"On KwaZulu-Natal’s South Coast, divers set off at dawn from Shelly Beach by RIB, punching through the breakers to reach a remote offshore bank, the domain of big sharks.","l":"English, Zulu","h":["Sawubona","“hello” in Zulu, literally “we see you”"],"f":["Bunny chow","A curry served in a hollowed-out half loaf of bread, an Indian legacy from Durban."],"r":"Dive only with a local club: the surf launch, current and depth call for a surface marker buoy. Never touch the sharks.","s":"June to November for ragged-tooth sharks; the southern summer brings tiger and bull sharks instead.","a":[["Oribi Gorge","Wooded canyon carved by the river, with viewpoints, trails and waterfalls."],["Uvongo","Small seaside resort at the mouth of the Vungu, with its waterfall and lagoon."],["Port Shepstone Lighthouse","Cast-iron lighthouse standing near the mouth of the Mzimkhulu River."]]},"longbeach_kom":{"v":"Kommetjie lives to the rhythm of the cold Atlantic: kelp forests, Slangkop Lighthouse and a community of surfers in thick wetsuits, used to big winter swells on the nearby reefs.","f":["Bobotie","Mildly curried spiced mince, baked under an egg custard, a legacy of Cape Malay cooking."],"r":"The peak is very busy with the village surfers: greet the line-up, wait your turn and never drop in on a local already riding.","s":"April to September: consistent southern winter swells. The water stays cold all year, so bring a 4/3 mm.","a":[["Chapman’s Peak","Cliffside road between Noordhoek and Hout Bay, with dizzying ocean views."],["Boulders Beach","Coves among big granite boulders, home to a colony of African penguins."],["Table Mountain","Cape Town’s iconic plateau, reached on foot or by cable car, with a view over the whole peninsula."],["Silvermine Nature Reserve","Trails through the fynbos, a reservoir and viewpoints over both coasts."],["Hout Bay","Lively fishing harbour among the mountains, where boats leave for Seal Island."]]},"vilankulo":{"v":"In Vilankulo, lateen-sailed dhows set off every morning for the Bazaruto Archipelago, fishermen and small markets liven up the seafront, and low tide reveals vast sandbanks.","r":"When kiting, keep clear of the dhows and of nets set on the sandbanks. In Bazaruto park, keep your distance from dugongs and turtles.","s":"August to December: stronger, steadier southerly wind, warm water and sandbanks you can ride at low tide.","a":[["Magaruque Island","Small island in the Bazaruto Archipelago, fringed with reefs for snorkelling."],["Bangué Island","Sandy islet in the archipelago, among lagoons and banks exposed at low tide."],["Chibuene","Archaeological site of an ancient Indian Ocean trading post."]]},"mafia":{"v":"On Mafia, Swahili life follows the tides: dhows and outrigger canoes, octopus fishermen on the reef flats and ruined merchant towns overgrown by baobabs around Chole Bay.","r":"The marine park charges a fee: keep your receipt. Don’t put a foot or a fin on the coral, and keep your distance from whale sharks without touching them.","s":"October to March: calmer sea beyond the bay, good visibility and whale sharks near Kilindoni.","a":[["Chole Island","Swahili village among the ruins, baobabs and mangroves of the bay."],["Kua Ruins","Remains of a medieval Swahili town on Juani Island, mosques and coral-stone houses."],["Juani Island","Large wooded island facing the bay, with lagoons and mangrove channels."],["Kisimani Mafia","Ruins of an old Swahili port on the island’s west coast."]]},"kendwa":{"v":"Kendwa is still a Swahili fishing village facing the sunset: dhows at anchor, nets drying on the sand and traditional boatyards in Nungwi, just to the north.","r":"Away from the beach, cover your shoulders and knees in the village. During Ramadan, avoid eating or drinking in public during the day.","s":"June to October and December to February: dry season and crystal-clear sea; the long rains fall from March to May.","a":[["Mnemba Atoll","Private islet surrounded by a protected reef, a top spot for snorkelling with dolphins and turtles."],["Nungwi","Large fishing village at the northern tip, where dhows are still built."],["Matemwe","East-coast village, between seaweed farms and a long beach facing Mnemba."]]},"kisite":{"v":"In the south of the Swahili coast, dhows leave Shimoni for the Kisite reefs; on Wasini there are no cars, just lanes, fishermen and fossil coral gardens.","r":"The park is run by the Kenya Wildlife Service: pay the entry fee, don’t walk on the coral and let the dolphins come to you without chasing them.","s":"October to March: gentler north-east monsoon, calm sea and better visibility on the reefs.","a":[["Wasini Island","Car-free island with Swahili villages, coral gardens and a mangrove boardwalk."],["Shimoni","Fishing port known for its caves, linked to the history of the slave trade."],["Mkwiro","Fishing village at the eastern end of Wasini, facing the mainland."],["Funzi Island","Mangrove-covered islands, to explore by canoe along the channels."]]},"brothers":{"v":"Two rocks lost in the middle of the Red Sea, with an old British-era lighthouse as the only human presence: here you live aboard, to the rhythm of dawn dives and nights at anchor.","r":"Always dive with the guide: strong currents and open water. Stay grouped against the wall and never chase the sharks.","s":"May to November: warm water, thresher and hammerhead sharks in summer, then oceanic whitetips in autumn."},"elphinstone":{"v":"On the desert coast of Marsa Alam, between barren mountains and dive villages, Elphinstone Reef is the divers’ legendary trip, reached by boat across an often choppy sea.","r":"With oceanic whitetips around, stay in a group near the reef, don’t linger at the surface and get back on board quickly. No gloves and no touching the coral.","s":"October to December: oceanic whitetips are more frequent and the water still warm, despite a sometimes strong north wind."},"somabay":{"v":"A desert peninsula between red mountains and reefs, Soma Bay lives to the rhythm of the north wind: kites and sails colour the lagoon from late morning, while the dive boats head out to sea.","r":"Stay within the zone marked out by the centre, away from swimmers. At low tide, watch out for the reef and sea urchins near the shore: keep your booties on.","s":"April to October: stronger, steadier north wind and warm water; winter stays windy but cooler.","a":[["Salem Express Wreck","Ferry that sank off Safaga, a moving dive on a site of remembrance."],["Safaga","Port town turned towards the sea, with its souk and dive boats."]]},"dahablagoon":{"v":"A former Bedouin camp turned haunt of divers, freedivers and windsurfers, Dahab keeps a slow pace: tea under the tents, cushions by the water and the Sinai mountains as a backdrop.","r":"In the lagoon, respect the separation between kite and windsurf zones. Always ask before photographing a Bedouin, especially women.","s":"April to October: stronger, steadier north wind; in winter the water stays mild but the wind drops more often.","a":[["Dahab","Small Sinai seaside town, with its promenade of cafés and shore dive sites."],["Blue Desert","Sinai desert rocks painted blue by an artist, a surprising art landscape."]]},"giftun":{"v":"Hurghada was just a fishing village before becoming the Red Sea’s big port for boat trips; every morning, dozens of boats leave the marina for the Giftun reefs.","r":"The Giftun protected area charges a fee: don’t feed the fish, don’t collect shells or coral and choose boats that use mooring buoys.","s":"April to June and September to November: warm water, a sea often calm and fewer people than in high summer."},"sakalava":{"v":"In the north of Madagascar, Diego-Suarez blends the legacy of the former French naval base with Malagasy, Comorian and Indian cultures; on the bay, fishermen in pirogues and kitesurfers share the same lagoon.","r":"Give way to the fishermen’s pirogues and don’t inflate your kite in the middle of their nets. Take all your rubbish away: the beach has no collection.","s":"June to September, the heart of the varatraza season: strong wind almost every day on a lagoon sheltered by the reef.","a":[["Montagne des Français","Reserve of dry forest and baobabs, trails to caves and a view over the bay."],["Diego-Suarez Bay","Huge natural bay guarded by the Sugarloaf, a sacred islet at its centre."],["Ramena","Fishing village and white-sand beach, where pirogues leave for the Emerald Sea."],["Diego-Suarez","Port town with colonial façades, lively markets and mixed cuisine."]]},"saintemarie":{"v":"Once a pirate hideout, Sainte-Marie keeps its pirate cemetery and fishing villages; on car-free Île aux Nattes, you arrive by pirogue and get around on foot between coconut palms and lagoon.","r":"To see the whales, choose an operator that follows the whale-watching charter: a slow approach, a respected distance, and never getting into the water near a mother and her calf.","s":"September to December: a drier season and a clear lagoon; the humpback whales are still around in September.","lbl":"Bungalow"},"troubiches":{"v":"Under the casuarinas of Trou aux Biches, fishermen’s pirogues and family Sunday picnics set the rhythm of the beach, a stone’s throw from the great Hindu temple of Triolet and the villages of the north.","r":"At weekends, Mauritian families picnic under the casuarinas: share the space and leave nothing behind. In the lagoon, stay out of the boat channels.","s":"September to December: dry season, crystal-clear lagoon and warming water, before the summer rains.","a":[["Pamplemousses Garden","A historic botanical garden, famous for its giant water lilies and its palms."],["Aapravasi Ghat","The former immigration depot for indentured labourers, a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],["Triolet Temple","A large Hindu temple dedicated to Shiva, the religious heart of the village of Triolet."],["Cap Malheureux","A little red-roofed church facing Coin de Mire, at the northern tip of the island."],["Port Louis","A lively capital: central market, Chinatown and the Caudan waterfront."]]},"baieternay":{"v":"At the end of Mahé’s north-west coast, granite hills plunge into a bay that has stayed wild; Creole culture lives on in the neighbouring villages, between line fishing, curry and sega.","r":"In the marine park, fishing and collecting are forbidden: don’t feed the fish, and keep your fins well above the shallow corals.","s":"April to May and October to November: between the two monsoons, glassy sea and maximum visibility.","a":[["Mission Ruins","The remains of a mission school in the hills, with a viewpoint over the west coast."],["Sainte Anne Marine Park","Islets and reefs facing Victoria, to explore by glass-bottom boat or with a mask."],["Victoria Botanical Garden","Coco de mer palms, orchids and giant tortoises in the heart of the capital."],["Thérèse Island","A small granite island off Port Glaud, with a long white-sand beach."]]},"anseintendance":{"v":"With no reef to shelter it, Anse Intendance takes the ocean head-on: granite boulders, leaning coconut palms, local surfers at dawn and turtles coming ashore to lay their eggs in the sand.","r":"Hawksbill turtles nest here: keep away from the nests and don’t shine lights on the beach at night. No lifeguards and a powerful shorebreak: don’t swim if it’s pounding.","s":"May to October: south-east trade winds and southern swell; the wind often turns onshore, so surf early in the morning.","a":[["Domaine de Val des Prés","A craft village around a traditional Creole house and its workshops."],["La Plaine St André","A restored former colonial plantation, with a rum distillery and garden."],["Mission Ruins","The remains of a mission school in the hills, with a viewpoint over the west coast."]]},"tabarka":{"v":"Tabarka lives off red coral, long harvested here and worked by its jewellers, and off the cork oak forests of Kroumirie that run down to the sea, beneath the gaze of the Genoese fort.","r":"Red coral is strictly protected: never bring any up and don’t touch the gorgonians. Away from the beach, opt for covering clothing.","s":"June to October: warmer water and an often calm sea, with better visibility in late summer.","a":[["Les Aiguilles de Tabarka","Tall needles of rock sculpted by erosion, at the water’s edge."],["Aïn Draham","A small mountain town with tiled roofs, in the heart of the Kroumirie forests."]]},"moulaybouzerktoun":{"v":"A few fishermen’s houses, argan trees and a wind-battered bay: this hamlet on the Essaouira coast has long drawn windsurfers from all over the world, without losing its calm.","f":["Amlou","A spread of roasted almonds, argan oil and honey, eaten at breakfast."],"r":"An experts-only spot: whoever is riding the wave has priority over whoever is heading out. Watch out for the rocks along the edge, and give way to the locals.","s":"May to September: the north-east trade wind blows hard almost every day, over smaller waves than in winter.","a":[["Essaouira medina","A UNESCO-listed walled town of ramparts, souks and white lanes with blue shutters."],["Skala du Port","A cannon-lined bastion facing the fishing harbour and its blue boats."],["Bayt Dakira","A house of memory of Moroccan Judaism, in the heart of the mellah."],["Borj El Baroud","A ruined former watchtower, half buried in the beach dunes."]],"lbl":"Guesthouse"},"capemaclear":{"v":"In Chembe, the day begins with the return of dugout canoes; small fish dry on racks by the water, and in the evening the lake is dotted with the lights of the fishermen.","r":"Avoid swimming near the reeds and the village washing areas, where bilharzia can be present. To reach the islands, pay the park entrance fee.","s":"August to November: dry season, warmer and crystal-clear water, ideal for watching cichlids with a mask."},"eilat":{"v":"At the head of the Gulf of Aqaba, the red desert mountains plunge into one of the northernmost coral reefs in the world, with Egypt and Jordan visible from the beach.","r":"Enter and exit only via the reserve’s walkways, never stand on the reef and don’t feed the fish.","s":"April to November: warm water and often excellent visibility; in high summer, get in the water early to escape the heat.","a":[["Eilat Mountains","Canyons of colourful granite and sandstone, with trails looking out over four countries."],["Underwater Observatory","An aquarium and a submerged tower for seeing the gulf’s reef without getting wet."],["Pharaoh’s Island","An Egyptian islet crowned by a medieval fortress, surrounded by coral."]]},"einbokek":{"v":"At the lowest point on the planet, the shores of the Dead Sea are crusted with white salt formations; people come here to float and smear themselves with black mud, facing the mountains of Jordan.","r":"Never put your head under the water and don’t splash: the salt burns your eyes. Don’t shave the day before, rinse off in the showers and drink plenty.","s":"November to April: bearable heat and lukewarm water; in summer, the heat becomes crushing from the morning on.","a":[["Masada","Herod’s fortress perched on a plateau, reachable on foot or by cable car."],["Mount Sodom","A mountain of salt carved with caves and pillars, with trails above the Dead Sea."]]},"daymaniyat":{"v":"Boats leave the Muscat coast at sunrise, running past beaches where Omani fishermen still haul up their boats, to reach islets of bare rock ringed with coral.","r":"A nature reserve: no fishing, and some islands are closed to landing while birds and turtles are nesting. Don’t touch any turtle.","s":"October to May: calmer sea and bearable heat; in summer the water is very warm and visibility more variable."},"musandam":{"v":"At the tip of Arabia, the cliffs of Musandam enclose fishing villages reachable only by sea; in Khasab, excursion dhows rub shoulders with speedboats racing towards neighbouring Iran.","r":"In the fjord villages, ask before landing or photographing the inhabitants. Let the dolphins come to the boat without chasing them.","s":"October to April: bearable heat and calm water in the khors; summer is crushing.","a":[["Khor Ash Sham","The longest fjord in Musandam, between cliffs and isolated villages, visited by dolphins."],["Telegraph Island","An islet in the khor with the ruins of a British telegraph station, ideal for snorkelling."],["Jebel Harim","The highest peak in Musandam, a mountain road with dizzying views over the fjords."],["Bukha","A small coastal town with its traditional fort by the sea."]]},"dubaikite":{"v":"Before the towers, Umm Suqeim was a village of fishermen and pearl divers; today, Emirati families stroll here at sunset, between kites and the silhouette of the Burj Al Arab.","r":"Only ride in the marked kite zone and during the permitted hours. When you leave the sand, cover your shoulders and knees for the promenade.","s":"November to March: steadier afternoon breeze and bearable heat; summer is stifling and the wind light.","a":[["Burj Khalifa","The tallest tower in the world, with observation decks over the whole city and the desert."],["Ras Al Khor Wildlife Sanctuary","A wetland at the end of Dubai Creek, where you can watch flamingos."],["Jumeirah Archaeological Site","The remains of an old caravan stop from the Abbasid era."]]},"nemberala":{"v":"On Rote, life follows the rhythm of the lontar palm, which gives sugar and palm wine, and of the seaweed farms uncovered at low tide in front of Nemberala. The island is also the birthplace of the sasando, a palm-leaf harp.","r":"The seaweed farms are the village’s livelihood: don’t walk on them on your way to the peak, and be discreet with your board in the lanes.","s":"May to September: dry season and consistent southern swells that make the T-Land left peel.","lbl":"Homestay"},"batukaras":{"v":"A Sundanese fishing village where outrigger canoes share the bay with surf schools. The local scene is driven by village surfers who have become instructors.","l":"Indonesian, Sundanese","h":["Sampurasun","a respectful Sundanese greeting, answered with “Rampes”"],"f":["Nasi timbel","Rice cooked in a banana leaf, served with fried fish, sambal and raw vegetables, Sundanese style."],"r":"The village is Muslim: cover your shoulders and knees away from the beach. At the peak, give priority on the right to students and locals.","s":"April to October: dry season, southern swells nicely filtered by the point and often windless mornings.","a":[["Green Canyon (Cukang Taneuh)","A green-water canyon to travel up by canoe or by swimming between mossy walls."],["Pangandaran","A West Java seaside resort with a fish market and a wooded peninsula full of monkeys."]]},"gerupuk":{"v":"A Sasak village focused on fishing and farming lobsters in floating cages. The fishermen have become boatmen: every morning their boats take surfers out to the peaks in the bay.","l":"Indonesian, Sasak","f":["Ayam taliwang","Grilled chicken coated in a very fiery sambal, Lombok’s great speciality."],"r":"Agree on the boat price before setting off and pay your boatman. Avoid the lobster cages and stay covered up in the Muslim village.","s":"April to October: dry season and consistent southern swells, with lighter winds early in the morning.","a":[["Kuta Lombok","A lively small coastal town, the starting point for the bays and hills of southern Lombok."],["Tanjung Ringgit","A wild cape in eastern Lombok, with sheer cliffs and views over the Alas Strait."],["Awang Bay","A large, calm bay in south-east Lombok, lined with fishing villages and seaweed farms."]],"lbl":"Homestay"},"banda":{"v":"The Dutch and the English fought over these tiny islands for nutmeg; forts, warehouses and colonial houses still bear witness to it in Banda Neira, facing the cone of Gunung Api.","f":["Manisan pala","The flesh of the nutmeg fruit candied in sugar, nibbled all over the Banda Islands."],"r":"The corals on the lava flow grow just below the surface: stay at the surface without finning over the coral heads, and ask before entering the nutmeg groves.","s":"October to December and March to May: calm sea between the two monsoons and good visibility.","a":[["Fort Belgica","A pentagonal 17th-century Dutch fort, with views over the bay and the Gunung Api volcano."],["Banda Neira","The main island: colonial houses, old nutmeg warehouses and a lively little port."],["Run Island","A tiny nutmeg island that the English ceded to the Dutch, who gave up Manhattan."],["Fort Nassau","The ruins of the first Dutch fort on Banda Neira, overtaken by vegetation."]]},"kakaban":{"v":"The Derawan archipelago is Bajau country, a people of sailors and fishermen. Kakaban’s lake, cut off from the ocean for millennia, is home to jellyfish that have evolved safe from predators.","r":"In the lake: no sunscreen, no finning near the bottom, and don’t take the jellyfish out of the water: they are fragile and unique.","s":"April to October: a generally calmer sea for the boat crossings and good visibility around the island.","lbl":"Homestay in Derawan"},"alor":{"v":"Alor has dozens of Papuan languages and keeps its moko bronze drums, handed down as dowry. In the hilltop villages, the lego-lego dance and traditional houses carry on a culture that has stayed apart.","r":"The currents in the straits are powerful: dive with a local guide. Always ask before taking photos in the traditional villages.","s":"March to November: an often calmer sea; the currents stay strong and the water sometimes cool all year round.","a":[["Kalabahi","Alor’s main town at the end of a long bay, with a lively market and a museum of moko drums."],["Pura Island","A volcanic island in the Pantar Strait, surrounded by renowned coral walls."]]},"anilao":{"v":"On the Mabini coast, the dive centres’ outrigger bancas leave from small coves where fishermen also moor their boats. At weekends, Anilao is the playground of Manila’s macro photographers.","f":["Lomi and kapeng barako","A thick noodle soup and strong liberica coffee, two points of pride in Batangas province."],"r":"For macro, never move a nudibranch or a seahorse for a photo, and fin well away from the bottom so as not to stir up the sand.","s":"November to May: dry season and a calmer sea; the water cools down in January and February.","a":[["Taal","A colonial town of ancestral wood-and-stone houses, embroidered fabrics and balisong knives."],["Taal Basilica","A huge Spanish-era stone basilica towering over the old town of Taal."],["Villavicencio House","A colonial mansion known as the “wedding gift house”, open to visitors."]],"lbl":"Simple dive resort"},"puertogalera":{"v":"Sabang is a former fishing hamlet turned divers’ village, where bancas and dive centres crowd the shore. In the hills live the Iraya, one of the Mangyan peoples of Mindoro.","r":"Bancas pass right over the reefs: swim with a surface marker buoy and stay within the marked snorkelling areas.","s":"November to May: dry season, calm sea and good visibility; typhoons are a threat from June to October.","a":[["Puerto Galera","A port nestled at the end of a sheltered bay, with long White Beach to the west of town."],["Verde Island","A volcanic island in the middle of the passage, renowned for its coral walls for snorkelling."]]},"launion":{"v":"An Ilocano town that has become the meeting point for Manila’s surfers, between surf schools, cafés and street stalls. The monsoon swell has given rise to a real community of local surfers here.","l":"Filipino, Ilocano, English","h":["Naimbag nga aldaw","“good day” in Ilocano, the language of northern Luzon"],"f":["Pinakbet","A northern Luzon vegetable stew seasoned with bagoong, a fermented fish paste."],"r":"The Urbiztondo line-up is packed with beginners: keep your distance, don’t let go of your board and give priority to whoever is closest to the peak.","s":"October to March: north-east monsoon swells and late typhoon-season swells, warm water.","a":[["San Fernando","The capital of La Union, with its hilltop Taoist Ma-Cho Temple and its market."],["Luna","A coastal town known for its pebble beach and its old Spanish watchtower."],["Namacpacan Church","A colonial church in Luna, a Marian shrine revered throughout northern Luzon."],["Poro Point","A peninsula south of San Fernando, with a lighthouse and views over the Lingayen Gulf."]]},"baler":{"v":"Philippine surfing is said to have been born here after the filming of Apocalypse Now, when the crew left their boards to the local kids. Baler also keeps the memory of the siege of its church by a Spanish garrison.","r":"The beach break forms powerful rip currents: swim near the surf schools and ask the locals for advice before paddling out.","s":"October to February: north-east monsoon swells and Pacific typhoon swells, warm water.","a":[["Baler","A small town and its stone church, scene of the 1898–1899 siege."],["Museo de Baler","A museum devoted to Baler’s history, from the Spanish siege to the beginnings of local surfing."]]},"balicasag":{"v":"Balicasag is a small fishing island whose reef has long been protected. Every morning, outrigger bancas shuttle across from Panglao in the low light over the Bohol Sea.","l":"Filipino, Cebuano, English","h":["Maayong buntag","“good morning” in Cebuano, the language of Bohol"],"f":["Calamay","A Bohol sweet of sticky rice, coconut and sugar, sold in a coconut shell."],"r":"The sanctuary limits visitor numbers: go through an accredited operator, never stand on the reef flat and keep your distance from the turtles.","s":"March to June: glassy sea, very warm water and visibility at its best around the drop-off.","a":[["Panglao Island","A coral island linked to Bohol, with white-sand beaches, freshwater caves and an old church."],["Alona Beach","Panglao’s lively white-sand beach, the departure point for dive boats."]]},"redang":{"v":"Off Terengganu, a Malay state attached to its painted fishing boats and its traditions, Redang lives to the rhythm of the green turtles that come to nest on its beaches and of the island’s fishing villages.","f":["Keropok lekor","A fried fish-and-sago sausage, nibbled with a chilli sauce, the pride of Terengganu."],"r":"Pay the marine park fee, don’t feed the fish and stay well away from the turtle nesting beaches at night, without a torch.","s":"April to September: calm sea and crystal-clear water; the island slows right down during the north-east monsoon.","a":[["Lang Tengah Island","A small neighbouring island with quiet beaches and fringing reefs good for snorkelling."],["Bidong Island","An island that once held a camp for Vietnamese refugees, of which remains and a memorial are left."]],"lbl":"Beach chalet"},"tarpark":{"v":"Facing Kota Kinabalu, these islands live to the rhythm of the harbour’s water taxis and of the Bajau, a seafaring people of Sabah’s west coast. In town, the waterfront markets overflow with grilled fish.","l":"Malay, English, Kadazan-Dusun, Bajau","h":["Kopivosian","a greeting in Kadazan-Dusun, the language of Sabah’s first people"],"f":["Hinava","Raw fish marinated in lime, ginger and chilli, a Kadazan-Dusun recipe from Sabah."],"r":"Don’t feed the fish, stay within the marked swimming areas and keep your park entry ticket on you.","s":"March to September: calmer sea and better visibility, before the heavy year-end rains.","a":[["Kota Kinabalu","Sabah’s capital, with waterfront markets, mosques and views of Mount Kinabalu."],["Sabah Museum","The state museum on Sabah’s peoples, crafts and nature, with traditional houses."],["Gaya Island","The park’s large wooded island, with forest trails and stilt villages."],["Kota Kinabalu Wetlands","A mangrove in the middle of the city, crossed by boardwalks for watching birds and crabs."],["Mari Mari Cultural Village","A living museum showcasing the houses, skills and dances of Borneo’s peoples."]]},"kata":{"v":"Kata is the birthplace of Phuket’s small surf scene: in summer, shapers and board-hire stands take back the beach when the tourists desert it. The island keeps a Sino-Portuguese heritage and active temples.","f":["Moo hong","Pork braised with black pepper and garlic, handed down by Phuket’s Hokkien families."],"r":"During the monsoon, rip currents are deadly: respect the lifeguards’ red flags and never swim alone outside the patrolled area.","s":"May to October: the south-west monsoon pushes consistent waves into the bay, cleaner early in the morning.","a":[["Big Buddha Phuket","A white marble Buddha on a hilltop, with views over the bays of southern Phuket."],["Wat Chalong","Phuket’s most revered temple, with its great chedi decorated with murals."],["Kata Noi Beach","A small neighbouring beach at the foot of the hills, quieter than the main bay."],["Racha Islands","Two islands off Phuket, with clear water and reefs for snorkelling."]]},"sailrock":{"v":"Between Ko Pha-ngan and Ko Tao, diving keeps a host of small centres going, and fishing boats cross paths with dive boats at dawn. Hin Bai, the “sail rock”, is a landmark for the gulf’s fishermen.","r":"In the chimney, wait your turn and don’t touch the rock. If a whale shark passes by, keep your distance and don’t chase it.","s":"March to September: calm sea and better visibility, before the north-east monsoon stirs up the gulf at the end of the year.","a":[["Ko Pha-ngan","A wooded island with many coves, waterfalls and temples, well beyond its party reputation alone."],["Bottle Beach","An isolated beach in northern Ko Pha-ngan, reachable by boat or along a trail."],["Thong Sala","Ko Pha-ngan’s main port, with its night market rich in street food."]]},"honmun":{"v":"In Nha Trang Bay, fishermen still steer round bamboo basket boats, and the islands’ cliffs shelter swiftlet nests, harvested in the region for a long time.","f":["Bún chả cá","A rice vermicelli soup with fish cakes, a speciality of Nha Trang’s canteens."],"r":"Don’t stand on the coral when getting off the boat, and turn down any coral or shells offered for sale.","s":"February to September: calm sea and good visibility; from October to December, rain and rough sea.","a":[["Nha Trang","A lively seaside city with markets, Cham towers and a seafront lined with cafés."],["Nha Trang Cathedral","A neo-Gothic stone cathedral built on a mound during the French colonial era."]]},"lanha":{"v":"Among the limestone karsts, fishing families still live in floating houses and on fish farms, as in the village of Cái Bèo, one of the oldest settlements on Cát Bà.","f":["Bánh đa cua","A soup of reddish rice noodles in crab broth, a Hai Phong speciality."],"r":"By kayak, go around the floating farms without landing on them, and leave nothing behind in the enclosed lagoons.","s":"April to June and September–October: warm water and clearer skies, between the winter mists and the summer storms.","a":[["Cát Bà Island","A large island with a forested national park, hiking trails, caves and small beaches."],["Hạ Long Bay","Thousands of limestone islets in an emerald sea, with caves and floating villages."],["Ti Tốp Island","An islet in the bay with a small beach and a summit viewpoint over the karsts."]]},"mykhe":{"v":"From dawn, Da Nang’s residents swim and exercise on the beach, while fishermen haul their round basket boats up the sand. American soldiers nicknamed it China Beach.","f":["Mì Quảng","Yellow rice noodles in just a little broth, with prawns, pork and a toasted rice cracker."],"r":"Swim between the lifeguards’ flags: the currents strengthen quickly in winter. And leave room for the basket boats.","s":"May to August: calm, warm sea, perfect for swimming; the surfing waves arrive from October to March.","a":[["Marble Mountains","Five marble hills riddled with caves, pagodas and stairways leading to viewpoints."],["Museum of Cham Sculpture","The finest collection of sandstone sculptures from the kingdom of Champa, in a colonial building."],["Sơn Trà Peninsula","A wooded peninsula home to douc langurs, with a large pagoda facing the sea."],["Hội An Japanese Bridge","A covered bridge topped by a small temple, the emblem of Hội An’s old town."],["Dragon Bridge","A dragon-shaped bridge over the Hàn River that breathes fire and water on certain evenings."]]},"condao":{"v":"A former penal colony of the colonial era and then of the war, Côn Đảo remains a place of remembrance and pilgrimage for the Vietnamese. All around, the sea is protected and green turtles come back to nest.","r":"Trips to Bảy Cạnh are run by park-approved operators. To watch the turtles, follow the rangers, with no torch or flash.","s":"March to June: calm sea and better visibility; the turtles nest mainly in summer and early autumn.","a":[["Côn Sơn Island","The main island: former prisons, Hàng Dương Cemetery and a peaceful little town."],["Côn Đảo National Park","Tropical forest criss-crossed by trails leading to coves and protected reefs."]]},"kohrong":{"v":"Sok San is a Khmer fishing village at the end of a long beach, where colourful boats sit alongside a few bungalows. At night, bioluminescent plankton makes the water sparkle around swimmers.","r":"The island struggles with its waste: take your plastics back to the mainland, and cover up when walking through the fishing village.","s":"November to April: dry season, glassy sea and crystal-clear water on the west coast.","a":[["Koh Rong Sanloem","A quieter neighbouring island, with white-sand bays and turquoise water good for snorkelling."],["Koh Rong Marine Park","A marine protected area around the islands, with seagrass beds and reefs to explore with mask and snorkel."]],"lbl":"Bungalow"},"palolem":{"v":"In the south of Goa, Palolem blends Catholic and Hindu village life, fishermen’s canoes pulled up on the sand and huts taken down every monsoon. Konkani is spoken here, and the white churches recall the Portuguese past.","l":"Konkani, English, Hindi","f":["Xacuti","A Goan curry with roasted coconut and toasted spices, often made with chicken."],"r":"Nudity and topless sunbathing are banned in Goa. When kayaking to the islet, give way to fishing boats coming home to the north of the bay.","s":"November to March: end of the monsoon, calm sea and bearable heat for paddling.","a":[["Galgibaga Beach","A wild beach lined with casuarinas where olive ridley turtles come to nest."],["Cotigao Wildlife Sanctuary","A Western Ghats forest with trails and watchtowers for spotting wildlife."]]},"netrani":{"v":"Long used as a target range by the navy, Netrani is gradually opening up to divers. On the coast, Murudeshwar’s fishermen set out beneath the giant statue of Shiva that towers over the headland.","l":"Kannada, Konkani, English","h":["Namaskara","“hello” in Kannada, said with palms joined"],"f":["Neer dosa","Thin rice crêpes from the Karnataka coast, served with a fish curry."],"r":"The island lies far offshore: dive with an accredited centre in Murudeshwar and take home no coral or shells.","s":"December to May: calm sea and good visibility; the monsoon halts trips from June to September.","a":[["Murudeshwar","A temple with a tall gopuram and a giant statue of Shiva on a headland facing the sea."],["Chitrapur Math","The monastery-temple of the Chitrapur Saraswat community, in the heart of the village of Shirali."]]},"rishikesh":{"v":"A holy city where the Ganges leaves the Himalayas, Rishikesh lives among ashrams, temple bells and evening ceremonies on the ghats. The Beatles meditated here, and both yoga and white water draw the whole world.","f":["Vegetarian thali","Rice, dal, vegetables and chapatis: the holy city is vegetarian and alcohol-free."],"r":"Meat and alcohol are banned in town. Cover up on the ghats and don’t swim outside the supervised areas: the current is treacherous.","s":"March to May and October–November: good water levels; rafting trips stop during the monsoon.","a":[["Parmarth Niketan","A large ashram on the banks of the Ganges, famous for its Ganga aarti ceremony at dusk."],["Beatles Ashram","An abandoned ashram in the forest, its walls covered in murals, where the Beatles meditated."],["Lakshman Jhula","A suspension footbridge over the Ganges, surrounded by temples and shops."],["Neelkanth Mahadev Temple","A Shiva temple in the forest, the goal of a hike or pilgrimage from Rishikesh."],["Rajaji National Park","Forests in the Himalayan foothills, home to elephants, deer and tigers."]]},"covelong":{"v":"A Tamil fishing village where kattumarams, log rafts that gave us the word catamaran, now sit alongside surfboards. The village children have become the first local surfers.","l":"Tamil, English","h":["Vanakkam","“hello” in Tamil, said with palms joined"],"f":["Meen kuzhambu","A tangy tamarind fish curry, cooked every day in Tamil villages."],"r":"The beach is the fishermen’s workplace: don’t put your board down on the nets or boats, and cover up in the village.","s":"March to September: southern swells running up the Bay of Bengal, modest and consistent waves.","a":[["Shore Temple","An 8th-century granite temple standing on the Bay of Bengal at Mahabalipuram."],["Descent of the Ganges","A huge bas-relief carved into the rock, populated by gods, elephants and ascetics."],["Pancha Rathas","Five monolithic temples, each carved from a single block of granite."],["Cholamandal Artists’ Village","An artists’ community founded in the 1960s, with a gallery and studios."]]},"mirissa":{"v":"A fishing village on the south coast, where colourful boats return to the little harbour at dawn while tuk-tuks speed along the coast road lined with coconut palms and surfers’ cafés.","f":["Ambul thiyal","A dry, sour tuna curry made with goraka, a preserving recipe typical of the south coast."],"r":"For the whales, choose an operator that keeps its distance and doesn’t cut across the animals’ path. On the reef right, give priority to the locals.","s":"November to April: the monsoon has passed, the sea is smooth on the south side and the swell arrives clean on the reef.","a":[["Dondra Head Lighthouse","A tall white lighthouse on the southernmost point of Sri Lanka."],["Matara Star Fort","A small star-shaped Dutch fort, with a moat and an ornate gate."],["Taprobane Island","A rocky islet crowned by a villa, set in Weligama Bay."],["Koggala Lagoon","A lagoon dotted with islets to explore by boat, between mangroves and temples."],["Martin Wickramasinghe Folk Museum","The writer’s birthplace and a museum of Sinhalese rural traditions."]]},"pigeonisland":{"v":"The Tamil and Muslim north-east coast, where Nilaveli’s fishermen still haul nets from the beach. Offshore, the rocky islet shelters a reef, and rock pigeons nest there, which gave it its name.","l":"Tamil, Sinhala, English","h":["Vanakkam","“hello” in Tamil, said with palms joined"],"r":"In the national park, never stand on the coral and don’t feed the sharks. At Koneswaram Temple, cover your shoulders and legs and take off your shoes.","s":"May to September: the east coast is sheltered from the monsoon, the sea is calm and the water crystal clear.","a":[["Koneswaram Temple","A Hindu temple perched on Swami Rock, above the sea."],["Fort Frederick","A Portuguese and later Dutch fort where deer wander among the ramparts."],["Kanniya Hot Springs","Seven hot-water wells at different temperatures, a place of pilgrimage."],["Velgam Vehera","The ruins of an ancient Buddhist monastery, in the countryside north of Trincomalee."]]},"pastapoint":{"v":"It was in this corner of North Malé Atoll that Maldivian surfing took off, around the first surf camps. Around it, the local islands live off pole-and-line tuna fishing and the comings and goings of dhonis.","r":"Pasta Point is reserved for the resort’s guests: don’t try to surf it from another island. On the local islands, cover up outside the bikini beaches.","s":"March to October, often with the cleanest conditions in April–May and in September, when the wind drops.","a":[["Thulusdhoo","A lively local island, with surfers’ guesthouses and a neighbouring reef wave."],["Malé Old Friday Mosque","A mosque of carved coral stone, a jewel of Maldivian heritage."],["National Museum of the Maldives","Royal artefacts, pre-Islamic sculptures and the history of the archipelago."],["Huraa","A small, quiet inhabited island, fringed with mangroves and lagoons."]],"lbl":"Guesthouse on a local island"},"maeda":{"v":"On Okinawa’s west coast, land of the old gusuku castles of the Ryūkyū Kingdom, the sea draws divers and growers of umibudo, the sea grapes that the village of Onna has made its speciality.","l":"Japanese, Okinawan","h":["Haisai","“hi” in Okinawan, said cheerfully"],"f":["Gōyā chanpurū","A stir-fry of bitter melon, tofu, egg and pork, Okinawa’s family dish."],"r":"Don’t touch the coral, and don’t go down the steps when the red flag is up: the swell makes getting out of the water dangerous.","s":"May to October: warm water and good visibility, but keep an eye out for typhoons in late summer.","a":[["Zakimi Castle","The curved ramparts of a UNESCO-listed gusuku, with views over the coast."],["Ryukyu Mura","A museum village of old houses, Okinawan crafts and dances."],["Yomitan","A village renowned for its yachimun pottery workshops and old kilns."]]},"niijima":{"v":"A volcanic island administered by Tokyo, famous for its pale kōgaseki stone, used for moyai statues and glass. The village lives to the rhythm of fishermen, open-air onsen and surfers from the capital.","f":["Kusaya","Dried fish marinated in a fermented brine, the pungent pride of the Izu Islands."],"r":"The beach is long and unpatrolled outside summer: never surf alone when the swell comes in. At the onsen, wash before getting into the bath.","s":"September to October: consistent typhoon swells, still-warm water and a beach deserted after the summer.","a":[["Shikine-jima","A small island of rocky coves and hot springs by the sea."],["Niijima village","Stone moyai statues, a glass studio and a free onsen facing the open sea."],["To-shima","A cone-shaped island covered in camellias, which yield a renowned oil."]]},"yonaguni":{"v":"At the far end of Japan, the island keeps its own language, its small horses and a marlin fishing port. On clear days, the mountains of Taiwan appear on the horizon.","f":["Yaeyama soba","Thin wheat noodles in a clear broth, topped with pork and kamaboko."],"r":"The currents are powerful: dive only with a local club and stay with your guide. Yonaguni’s horses roam free; don’t feed them.","s":"December to March for the schools of hammerhead sharks, at the cost of an often rough and cool sea.","a":[["Cape Irizaki","The westernmost point of Japan, facing Taiwan, ideal at sunset."],["Yonaguni Island","Cliffs, pastures of free-roaming horses and fishing villages."]],"lbl":"Minshuku"},"yoshino":{"v":"On Miyako Island, fringed with reefs and sugar cane fields, the villages of the east coast keep a quiet life turned towards the sea and their own island language.","f":["Miyako soba","Wheat noodles in a light broth, with the pork traditionally hidden beneath the noodles."],"r":"Don’t walk on the coral at low tide, and beware of the currents at the lagoon’s exit. Wear mineral sunscreen.","s":"May to June and October: warm water, a calm lagoon and less risk of typhoons than in high summer.","a":[["Cape Higashi-hennazaki","A long, wild peninsula ending in a lighthouse, with cliffs and views over the reef."],["Yonaha Maehama Beach","A long white-sand beach facing Kurima Island and its bridge."],["Miyakojima City Museum","The history, wildlife and traditions of the Miyako Islands, in Hirara."],["Miyakojima Tropical Botanical Garden","A shady botanical garden planted with tropical species."]]},"jukdo":{"v":"On the Gangwon coast, between pine forests and fishing ports, Yangyang has become the cradle of Korean surfing, where young people from Seoul come at weekends to learn to ride waves.","f":["Songi mushrooms","Matsutake picked in Yangyang’s pine forests in autumn, grilled or in soup."],"r":"In summer, respect the marked zones separating swimmers and surfers. At weekends, the line-up is full of beginners: keep your distance.","s":"October to March: winter low-pressure systems raise more consistent swells; bring a thick wetsuit.","a":[["Odaesan National Park","A wooded mountain in the Taebaek range, with trails and ancient Buddhist temples."],["Jumunjin","A bustling fishing port and its large fish and squid market."]]},"munseom":{"v":"In the south of Jeju, Seogwipo lives among tangerine orchards, basalt cliffs and small harbours. The haenyeo, the island’s free-diving women, still harvest abalone and sea urchins here.","l":"Korean, Jeju","h":["Honjeopseoye","“welcome” in Jeju, the island’s language"],"f":["Jeju black pork","Thick slices of black pork grilled over charcoal, dipped in an anchovy sauce."],"r":"The currents around the islet are strong: dive with a local club. Don’t pick up any shellfish; these waters are worked by the haenyeo.","s":"September to November: the water is at its warmest of the year and visibility improves after the typhoons.","a":[["Jeongbang Waterfall","A waterfall that drops straight into the sea, at the foot of the cliffs."],["Cheonjiyeon Waterfall","A waterfall at the end of a wooded gorge, beautiful at nightfall."],["Hallasan National Park","Trails to the summit of the volcano, the highest point in South Korea."],["Seogwipo Olle Market","A bustling covered market: fish, tangerines and Jeju street snacks."],["Soesokkak","An estuary of green water between lava walls, to explore by boat."]]},"greenisland":{"v":"Once a prison island for political dissidents, Lüdao has become an island of fishermen and divers that you tour by scooter, among reefs, hot springs and strangely shaped rocks.","r":"Don’t touch the large massive corals and avoid finning over the bottom. On the coast road, ride your scooter carefully: it is narrow and windy.","s":"May to September: calm sea and regular boats from Taitung; in winter, the monsoon often cancels the crossings.","a":[["Zhaori Hot Spring","A rare saltwater hot spring, with open-air pools facing the sunrise."],["White Terror Memorial Park","A former political prison turned place of remembrance of the martial-law era."],["Sleeping Beauty Rock","A volcanic rock shaped like a reclining woman, seen from the coast."],["Sika Deer Park","A park devoted to sika deer, once raised in large numbers on the island."]],"lbl":"Minsu"},"penghu":{"v":"An archipelago of basalt and coral where villages shelter their vegetable plots behind low stone walls against the wind, and where fishermen built stone fish traps on the foreshore.","f":["Brown sugar cake","A soft steamed cake made with brown sugar, Penghu’s emblematic sweet."],"r":"The winter wind is violent and the sea cold: never ride alone and tell someone before you head out. Keep clear of the swimming area.","s":"October to March: the north-east monsoon blows strong and steady, for experienced, well-equipped riders.","a":[["Penghu Tianhou Temple","A temple to the sea goddess Mazu, considered the oldest in Taiwan."],["Erkan Village","Traditional basalt-and-coral houses in a preserved village on Xiyu."],["Tongpan Island","A small island ringed by basalt columns, reached by boat from Magong."],["Xiyu Western Fort","A 19th-century artillery fort with vaulted galleries, facing the strait."]]},"hoihawan":{"v":"At the tip of the Sai Kung peninsula, old Hakka villages, lime kilns and coastal trails recall rural Hong Kong, far from the towers, with a protected bay as its showcase.","f":["Sai Kung seafood","Choose fish or crabs from the harbour’s tanks, cooked on the spot."],"r":"In the marine park, fishing and collecting are forbidden. Come by minibus from Sai Kung: the access road is closed to unauthorised cars.","s":"May to October: warm water and calm sea; the drier end of the season is best, and keep an eye out for typhoons.","a":[["Tai Long Wan","Four wild white-sand beaches linked by a coastal trail."],["Tap Mun Island","A fishing island with a Tin Hau temple and windswept meadows above the sea."],["Sheung Yiu Folk Museum","A 19th-century fortified Hakka village and its old lime kiln."],["Sharp Island","An islet in Kiu Tsui Country Park, with a pebble tombolo to cross at low tide."]]},"turquoisebay":{"v":"Between the ochre Cape Range and Ningaloo Reef, life revolves around the sea: whale sharks, manta rays and turtles draw van travellers and campers here from Exmouth.","r":"Enter the water at the south end of the bay and get out before the sand spit: beyond it, the current races out to sea. Don’t touch the coral; the area is a sanctuary.","s":"April to June: still-warm water, lighter winds and whale sharks passing along the reef.","a":[["Cape Range National Park","Limestone gorges such as Yardie Creek and Mandu Mandu, and rock wallabies."],["North West Cape","The tip of the peninsula, with Vlamingh Head Lighthouse and whales in winter."]]},"yongala":{"v":"Lost with all hands in a cyclone in the early 20th century, the steamship was only found decades later. Divers now set out from Alva Beach, amid the sugar cane fields of the Ayr region.","r":"The wreck is a grave and a protected historic site: entering it or touching the hull is forbidden. Descend along the mooring line.","s":"August to December: the trade winds ease, the sea calms down and visibility is often better."},"ladyelliot":{"v":"Once stripped bare by guano miners, this coral island has been replanted and now lives to the rhythm of seabirds, nesting turtles and an old lighthouse.","r":"Stay on the paths while birds and turtles are nesting, with no torch on the beach at night. Keep your distance from the manta rays.","s":"May to October: crystal-clear water and plenty of manta rays, even if the sea is cooler than in summer.","lbl":"Island eco-lodge"},"tangalooma":{"v":"A former whaling station turned holiday spot, Tangalooma lies on a sand island explored by 4WD, Quandamooka country, where dolphins and dugongs frequent Moreton Bay.","r":"Don’t climb on the rusty wrecks, and keep clear of the channel where boats anchor. On the island, you can only get around by 4WD and with a permit.","s":"October to April: warmer water and an often calm sea on the bay side; avoid very windy days.","a":[["Moreton Island National Park","Giant dunes for sandboarding, freshwater lakes and wild beaches."],["Flinders Reef","A small, isolated coral reef, a dive site rich in turtles and rays."],["Fort Cowan Cowan","Second World War gun batteries and bunkers in the dunes."]]},"pointlookout":{"v":"On Minjerribah, Quandamooka country, the village of Point Lookout overlooks the Pacific from its headland, where you watch for whales, dolphins and turtles. Here, life is lived barefoot between beach and bush.","r":"Main Beach has strong rip currents: swim between the flags. In the water, give priority to the headland’s locals.","s":"February to May: more consistent cyclone and trade-wind swells, still-warm water and the summer holidays over.","a":[["Point Lookout headland","The North Gorge walk, with turtles, rays and whales in season."],["Brown Lake","A perched lake with tea-coloured water, stained by tea trees."],["Blue Lake","A crystal-clear freshwater lake hidden in the bush, at the end of a trail."],["North Stradbroke Island Museum","Aboriginal history, shipwrecks and island life, in Dunwich."]]},"agneswater":{"v":"A small coastal town surrounded by national parks, near the spot where James Cook landed in 1770, hence the name of the neighbouring village. You learn to surf here before heading off to the islands of the southern Great Barrier Reef.","r":"In the nearby parks, turtles nest in summer: no torches on the beach at night. Swim between the flags, patrolled by the local club.","s":"December to April: cyclone swells and trade winds bring the most consistent waves, often gentle.","a":[["Deepwater National Park","Dunes, deserted beaches and coves, turtle nesting sites."],["Seventeen Seventy","A village on a peninsula, with sunsets over the water and trips out to the islands."],["Captain Cook’s landing site","A monument on the beach where Cook came ashore on his 1770 voyage."],["Eurimbula National Park","Mangroves, coastal forests and wild beaches reachable by 4WD."]]},"crescenthead":{"v":"A small village in Dunghutti country where longboarding is almost a religion: long boards glide along the point, and kangaroos graze on the headland grass at sunset.","r":"The line-up is crowded and full of longboards: one person per wave, and never drop in on someone else’s wave. Don’t feed the headland’s kangaroos.","s":"March to June: south-east and cyclone swells wrapping around the headland, often with offshore winds in the morning.","a":[["Hat Head National Park","Dunes, swamps and rocky headlands, with a trail to Smoky Cape Lighthouse."],["Hat Head","A quiet village beside a clear river, ideal for swimming or paddling."],["Limeburners Creek National Park","Wild beaches, flowering heathland and sand tracks between lagoons and ocean."]]},"angourie":{"v":"A National Surfing Reserve, Angourie drew shortboard pioneers from the 1960s, who came to live near the point. The village keeps a low-key spirit, away from the bustle of Yamba.","f":["Yamba prawns","Clarence River prawns landed at the harbour, to enjoy by the water."],"r":"The point is for experienced surfers only, and the local line-up is demanding: watch for a long time before paddling out, and let the regulars go.","s":"March to July: southerly swells and offshore south-westerly winds make the point peel along the rocks.","a":[["Yamba","A small fishing town at the mouth of the Clarence, with cafés and varied beaches."],["Iluka Nature Reserve","World Heritage-listed littoral rainforest, with a trail to the sea."],["Bundjalung National Park","Long beaches, dark rivers and Aboriginal sites north of the Clarence."],["Yamba Lighthouse","A lighthouse on Yamba’s headland, a lookout over the ocean and the whales in winter."]]},"narrabeen":{"v":"Sung about by the Beach Boys, Narrabeen has produced generations of champions in one of Sydney’s most demanding line-ups. The neighbourhood lives between lagoon, surf life saving club and suburban surf culture.","r":"The local line-up is very sharp: no room for beginners at the peak. After heavy rain, avoid surfing near the mouth: the lagoon water is polluted.","s":"March to June: more frequent autumn swells, offshore wind in the morning and still-mild water.","a":[["Barrenjoey Lighthouse","A steep climb up to the lighthouse, with views over Palm Beach and Pittwater."],["Long Reef headland","A protected rock platform, teeming with rock-pool life at low tide."],["Manly Dam Reserve","A lake in the bush, with swimming, a loop walk and mountain-bike trails."],["Rose Seidler House","A 1950s modernist house turned museum, in the northern bush."]]},"flypoint":{"v":"On Port Stephens, Worimi country, Nelson Bay lives off fishing, dolphin-watching trips and oysters. Underwater, the marine sanctuary teems with small, colourful creatures.","f":["Port Stephens oysters","Rock oysters farmed in the estuary, served natural or with just a squeeze of lemon."],"r":"Fly Point is a sanctuary: take nothing. Dive at high-tide slack water, because the current quickly becomes very strong in the channel.","s":"May to September: the water cools, but visibility is often the best of the year.","a":[["Tomaree National Park","A climb to the top of Tomaree Head, with views over the islands and whales in winter."],["Tomaree Head fortifications","Second World War guns and bunkers on the headland."],["Hawks Nest","A village of river and dunes, with koalas in the trees and calm beaches."],["Cabbage Tree Island","A reserve for the rare Gould’s petrel, to be observed only from the boat."]]},"woolamai":{"v":"On Phillip Island, Bunurong country, shearwaters nest in their thousands on Cape Woolamai and little penguins come home every evening. You surf here in cold water from Bass Strait.","r":"The rip currents are fearsome: don’t surf alone, and swim only between the flags. On the cape, stay on the path to spare the shearwater burrows.","s":"March to May: consistent south-westerly swells, calmer winds and the water at its mildest of the year.","a":[["Phillip Island Nature Parks","The Penguin Parade at dusk, koalas and a seal-watching centre."],["Churchill Island","A historic farm, old gardens and sheep-shearing demonstrations, linked by a bridge."],["Seal Rocks","Offshore rocks home to a large colony of fur seals."],["San Remo","A fishing village where you can watch the pelicans being fed on the jetty."]]},"squeaky":{"v":"At the tip of the mainland, the Prom is a land of granite, bush and empty beaches, where wombats and emus wander among the tents at Tidal River. Melbourne families have camped here for generations.","r":"Never feed wombats, emus or ravens, and store your food away. The beach is unpatrolled: don’t stray far from the shore.","s":"January to March: the water reaches its peak, still cool, and the days are long and bright.","a":[["Wilsons Promontory National Park","Trails up Mount Oberon, deserted beaches and bush wildlife."],["South Point","The southernmost point of the Australian mainland, at the end of a long walk."],["Wilsons Promontory Marine National Park","Granite seabeds, kelp forests and fur seals, for experienced divers."]]},"wineglass":{"v":"On Tasmania’s east coast, pink granite and orange lichen, the fishing shacks of Coles Bay and oyster farms: here, life follows the rhythm of the tides, the kayaks and the unshy wallabies.","f":["Freycinet oysters","Farmed in the cool waters around Coles Bay, best tasted freshly shucked."],"r":"A Tasmanian parks pass is required at Freycinet. Stay on the marked trails and never feed the wallabies.","s":"December to March: long southern-summer days, less cold water and an often calmer sea for paddling.","a":[["The Hazards","A granite range towering over the bay, crossed by the park’s trails."],["Cape Tourville Lighthouse","A clifftop boardwalk with views over the coast and the granite islets."],["Honeymoon Bay","A small sheltered cove between granite boulders, ideal for a calm swim."],["Friendly Beaches","Long, wild white-sand beaches pounded by the Tasman Sea."],["Schouten Island","A wild island south of the peninsula, reached by boat or sea kayak."]]},"busselton":{"v":"A quiet seaside town on Geographe Bay, built around its historic jetty. Families come here for picnics on the grass, fishing trips and the whales that cruise along the bay in the southern spring.","f":["Margaret River wines","The neighbouring wine region pairs beautifully with the bay’s fish."],"r":"Along the jetty, don’t touch or pull anything off the pylons: the soft corals and sponges are fragile. Stay within the marked swimming area.","s":"December to April: warmer water and a calm bay in the morning, before the afternoon sea breeze.","a":[["Tuart Forest National Park","A rare forest of tuarts, tall coastal eucalypts, to explore on foot."],["Wonnerup House","A 19th-century settlers’ farm, restored and open to visitors."],["Vasse-Wonnerup Estuary","A wetland where you can watch black swans and waterbirds in great numbers."],["Dunsborough","A small coastal town, the gateway to Cape Naturaliste and its coves."]]},"lancelin":{"v":"A crayfishing village turned haunt of windsurfers and kiters, facing its islets. Between fishing boats, white dunes and riders’ vans, the mood stays simple and all about the wind.","f":["Western rock lobster","Caught in pots by the Lancelin boats, best tasted grilled during the season."],"r":"Respect the launch zones and give way to windsurfers already out on the water. Off the permitted tracks, don’t drive on the dunes.","s":"November to March: a strong, steady sea breeze every afternoon of the southern summer.","a":[["Lancelin Island","A protected islet facing the village, sheltering the lagoon from the ocean waves."],["Ledge Point","A small neighbouring coastal village, another spot loved by windsurfers and kiters."]]},"goatisland":{"v":"The country’s first marine reserve, created on the initiative of researchers at the Leigh marine laboratory. The small fishing harbour nearby now also welcomes families who come to see the fish that have returned to the shore.","r":"A no-take marine reserve: take nothing, neither fish nor shellfish, and don’t feed the fish, it’s forbidden.","s":"January to April: the warmest water of the year and often good visibility close to shore.","a":[["Tāwharanui Regional Park","A peninsula fenced off to protect native birds, with beaches and trails."],["Pākiri Regional Park","A long white-sand beach lined with dunes, ideal for walking."],["Kawau Island","A wooded island in the Hauraki Gulf, with Governor Grey’s historic mansion."],["Mathesons Bay","A small neighbouring cove lined with rocks, for a quiet picnic."],["Leigh","A fishing village on Omaha Cove, with its little harbour and cafés."]]},"taupo":{"v":"In the heart of Ngāti Tūwharetoa country, the lake lives between trout fishing, steaming vents and pumice beaches. The Mine Bay carvings are a reminder that this lake is first and foremost Māori land.","r":"The lakebed belongs to Ngāti Tūwharetoa: be discreet in front of the Mine Bay carvings and don’t land on Motutaiko Island.","s":"December to March: milder water, settled weather and a lake often glassy in the morning before the wind.","a":[["Craters of the Moon","Boardwalks among steaming craters and active geothermal ground."],["Mount Tauhara","A volcanic dome whose summit looks out over the whole lake."],["Wairakei","A geothermal area north of Taupō, all steam and hot pools."],["Kinloch","A peaceful little village on the lake’s northernmost bay."]]},"milford":{"v":"Piopiotahi to the Māori, this fjord’s only village is a small harbour and a few wharves. Rain, waterfalls and cliffs set the pace of life here, where fur seals and dolphins cross paths with kayaks.","r":"Keep your distance from fur seals and dolphins, and stay close to the walls, out of the boats’ path. Bring repellent against the sandflies.","s":"December to March: long days and less cold water; after rain, the waterfalls multiply.","a":[["Mitre Peak","An iconic peak soaring out of the fjord, to admire from the water or the shore."],["Piopiotahi Marine Reserve","The fjord’s dark waters, where black corals and deep-sea life thrive."],["Routeburn Track","A famous alpine hike through lakes, ridges and glacial valleys."],["Hollyford Valley","A wild rainforest valley with trails and waterfalls."]]},"stclair":{"v":"The beach of a city founded by Scottish settlers, with its esplanade, cafés and surf club. Dunedin’s students brave water here that comes straight from the Deep South.","r":"Keep at least 20 m between you and the sea lions resting on the sand. In the line-up, wait your turn: there are plenty of regulars.","s":"March to June: consistent southern swells and more frequent offshore winds, with a thick wetsuit.","a":[["Larnach Castle","A castle-style mansion on the Otago Peninsula, with gardens and views."],["Toitū Otago Settlers Museum","Otago’s Māori and colonial history told in a lively museum."],["The Octagon","Dunedin’s central square, lined by the cathedral and the town hall."],["Orokonui Ecosanctuary","A fenced forest where you can spot rare native birds and tuatara."],["Dunedin Chinese Garden","A classical Chinese garden, a tribute to Otago’s first Chinese immigrants."]]},"natadola":{"v":"On the south coast of Viti Levu, Fijian villages still live from fishing and their gardens. Children on horseback on the sand, fishers in the lagoon and kava shared in the evening set the tone.","r":"Stay inside the lagoon: near the reef passes, the current gets strong. Horse rides offered by villagers are negotiated politely.","s":"May to October: dry season, moderate trade winds and an often crystal-clear lagoon.","a":[["Robinson Crusoe Island","A small sandy islet offered as a day trip from the coast."],["Sigatoka","A lively Coral Coast town with its fruit and vegetable market."]]},"drawaqa":{"v":"In the Yasawas, small fishing villages and bungalow resorts share roadless volcanic islands. Evening singing, kava and boat trips back and forth set the rhythm of life.","r":"Don’t touch the mantas or dive down onto them: stay at the surface, off to the side. To visit a village, bring kava as sevusevu.","s":"May to October: manta ray season in the channel, drawn by plankton carried on the currents.","a":[["Naviti","A large neighbouring volcanic island, with traditional villages and hills to climb."],["Waya Island","An island with spectacular ridges, criss-crossed by trails to its summits."],["Kuata","A small rocky island in the southern Yasawas, popular for snorkelling."]],"lbl":"Small island resort"},"tikehau":{"v":"Around the village of Tuherahera, people live from copra and fishing: the lagoon’s stone fish parks feed the atoll. Deserted motu, pink sand and ukulele parties do the rest.","l":"French, Tahitian, Paumotu","r":"Dive the pass with the local club, on the incoming current. Don’t touch the fishers’ fish parks and don’t feed the sharks.","s":"April to November: drier season, moderate trade winds and excellent visibility in the pass.","lbl":"Family guesthouse"},"iledespins":{"v":"Land of the Kunié people, where outrigger canoes still glide across Upi Bay. Columnar pines, traditional huts and the remains of the penal colony where the Communards were deported tell a singular story.","l":"French, Kwênyii","f":["Isle of Pines snails","The island’s big snails, served grilled with parsley butter in the small restaurants."],"r":"The path to the natural pool crosses customary land: stay on the trail, don’t walk on the coral and don’t pick anything up.","s":"September to December: fairly dry weather, warming water and clear light, before the cyclones."},"jinek":{"v":"On Lifou, life follows the custom of the chiefdoms: round huts, yam fields and vanilla grown by the tribe. At Easo, fishers and children share the bay with the turtles.","r":"The bay belongs to the Easo tribe: pay the access fee asked on site and never set foot on the coral.","s":"September to December: drier weather, clear water and an often calm sea, before the cyclone season.","a":[["Jokin Cliffs","High limestone cliffs in the north of the island, with a plunging view over the lagoon."],["Easo","A coastal village overlooked by a chapel perched on a panoramic headland."]],"lbl":"Tribal homestay"},"amedee":{"v":"The metal lighthouse, made in France and reassembled here piece by piece, watches over the Boulari Pass. At weekends, Nouméa locals arrive by boat to picnic and swim.","r":"The banded sea kraits, sea snakes very common on the islet, are venomous: watch them without touching. Fishing is forbidden in the reserve.","s":"September to December: fairly dry weather, warming water and an often calm lagoon.","a":[["Aquarium des Lagons","Fluorescent corals, nautiluses and New Caledonian lagoon fish shown in tanks."],["Museum of New Caledonia","Kanak and Oceanian collections: carvings, currencies and everyday objects."],["Place des Cocotiers","Nouméa’s shady heart, with its bandstand and fountain."],["Ouen Toro","A wooded hill by the lagoon, with views over the islets and the bay."],["Île aux Canards","A small islet facing Anse Vata, with an underwater snorkelling trail."]]},"champagnebeach":{"v":"Coconut plantations, ni-Vanuatu villages and roadside stalls: Santo’s east coast keeps a slow pace, between copra, fishing from dugout canoes and family swims on Sundays.","r":"The beach belongs to customary landowners: pay the entry fee requested and, if possible, avoid cruise-ship days.","s":"May to October: dry season, gentler heat and crystal-clear water.","a":[["Port Olry","A French-speaking village on a long beach, with islets you can reach on foot."],["Hog Harbour","A neighbouring village on Santo’s east coast, between plantations and beaches."]],"lbl":"Bungalow"},"bonegi":{"v":"On Guadalcanal’s north coast, the remains of the Pacific War are part of the everyday landscape. Families swim on the black sand between two wrecks that have become reefs.","r":"The wrecks are places of remembrance: take nothing away. An access fee is charged by the beach’s customary landowners.","s":"May to November: drier season, an often calm sea on the north coast and good visibility on the wrecks.","a":[["Honiara Central Market","The capital’s big market for fish, fruit, vegetables and betel nut."],["Solomon Islands National Museum","Traditional objects, shell money and the history of the archipelago."],["Edson’s Ridge (Bloody Ridge)","The hill of a 1942 battle, with a memorial and views over the plain."]]},"kimbe":{"v":"Between forested volcanoes and oil palm plantations, the bay’s villages live from fishing by canoe. Divers come here for some of the richest reefs in the Coral Triangle.","r":"On the pinnacles, keep your distance from gorgonians and sea fans, with no gloves and no contact. The dive fees paid to villages help protect the reefs.","s":"April to June and September to November: an often calm sea and excellent visibility on the pinnacles.","a":[["Willaumez Peninsula","A volcanic peninsula with hot springs and a caldera lake at its tip."],["Kimbe","A lively provincial capital with its market of local produce."]]},"lalomanu":{"v":"Village families run beach fale here, rebuilt after the 2009 tsunami. Shared meals, songs and fiafia nights keep fa’a Samoa, the Samoan way of life, alive.","r":"At dusk, respect sa, the evening prayer: lower your voice and don’t walk through the village. Ask before settling in front of a fale.","s":"May to October: dry season, refreshing trade winds and an often crystal-clear lagoon.","a":[["Namu’a Island","A small island off the coast, with beach fale and a trail to the summit."],["Uafato","A remote village renowned for its woodcarvers and its protected forest."],["Aleipata Islands","Uninhabited volcanic islets off the eastern tip, rich in seabirds."]],"lbl":"Beach fale"},"muri":{"v":"On the Ngatangiia side, tradition holds that voyaging canoes set off from Avana for Aotearoa. Today, outrigger canoes, kites and Sunday barbecues share the lagoon.","r":"Launch well away from swimmers and paddlers. Watch out for the current in the Avana passage, which can pull you towards the reef.","s":"May to October: steady south-easterly trade winds over the lagoon and drier weather.","a":[["Te Rua Manga (The Needle)","A rocky spire in the heart of the island, a stop on the cross-island hike of Rarotonga."],["Avarua","A small capital by the harbour, with its Punanga Nui market on Saturdays."],["Te Ara Museum","A small museum on the culture, crafts and navigation of the Cook Islands."],["Cook Islands National Museum","Canoes, carvings and traditional objects from the archipelago’s different islands."]]},"chuuk":{"v":"Among the lagoon’s volcanic islands, Chuukese people live from fishing and their gardens. Kimiuo Aisek, a witness to the 1944 bombing, guided the first divers to the sunken fleet.","l":"English, Chuukese","r":"The wrecks are protected war graves: take nothing, and don’t touch human remains or munitions.","s":"December to April: drier season, a calm lagoon and good visibility on the wrecks.","a":[["Tonowas (Dublon)","The lagoon’s former Japanese headquarters, dotted with wartime remains."],["Kimiuo Aisek Museum","Objects and stories from the Pacific War and the fleet sunk in the lagoon."],["Tonotan Guns and Caves","Tunnels dug into the hill on Weno, where Japanese guns remain."]]},"yap":{"v":"Yap keeps its customs: giant stone money lined up along the paths, men’s houses on stilts and traditional loincloths. Here, tradition comes before tourism.","l":"English, Yapese","r":"Ask permission before entering a village or taking photos. Never touch the stone money and cover your thighs.","s":"December to April: dry season and the mantas’ mating display in the reef channels.","a":[["Bechiyal","A traditional village with a men’s house, stone money and a beach."],["Yap Living History Museum","Traditional houses, stone money and craft demonstrations."],["Rull Men’s House","A large traditional faluw with a thatched roof, built at the water’s edge."],["O’Keefe’s Island","A harbour islet linked to the 19th-century stone money trade."]]},"germanchannel":{"v":"Palauans have long practised bul, a system of fishing bans that inspired the protection of their waters. Painted bai, clans and wartime remains mark the archipelago.","r":"Reef-toxic sunscreens are banned in Palau: bring a mineral one. At the cleaning station, keep your distance from the mantas.","s":"November to April: drier season, an often calm sea and excellent visibility.","a":[["Rock Islands","Hundreds of forest-covered, mushroom-shaped limestone islets above a turquoise lagoon."],["Jellyfish Lake","A marine lake filled with golden jellyfish, to explore with mask and snorkel."],["Blue Corner","A famous current-swept drop-off, frequented by sharks and schools of fish."],["Peleliu","An island marked by a 1944 battle, with military remains and memorials."]]},"lencois":{"v":"In Barreirinhas, boats head down the Rio Preguiças to fishing villages, and rugged 4x4s take visitors into the dunes. In the evening, the squares come alive to the sound of forró.","f":["Arroz de cuxá","Maranhão rice with vinagreira and dried shrimp, served with fish."],"r":"Only enter the park with a licensed guide and leave no litter: the rainwater lagoons are fragile.","s":"June to September: lagoons filled by the rains, with warm turquoise water."},"abrolhos":{"v":"Caravelas, a small fishing town on the edge of a mangrove, lives to the rhythm of the whales and the boats to the archipelago, which Darwin visited aboard the Beagle.","r":"Only Siriba Island can be visited on land, with the park rangers. Don’t touch the chapeirões and keep your distance from the whales.","s":"December to March: an often calmer sea and good visibility; from July to November, it’s whale time.","lbl":"Pousada"},"ilhadomel":{"v":"No cars, sandy trails and caiçara communities who still live from small-scale fishing. In the evening, forró echoes through the villages of Encantadas and Nova Brasília.","f":["Barreado","Beef slow-cooked for hours in a sealed clay pot, a speciality of the Paraná coast."],"r":"The trails cross protected restinga: don’t stray from the paths and take your rubbish back to the mainland.","s":"April to September: more consistent southern swells and empty beaches, with cool water.","a":[["Paranaguá","Paraná’s oldest town, with a colonial centre and the port the boats leave from."],["Ilhas dos Currais Marine Park","Rocky islets offshore, seabird colonies and protected waters."]]},"silveira":{"v":"A coast of Azorean heritage where small-scale mullet (tainha) fishing still sets the rhythm of winter, and where southern right whales come to calve offshore while surfers watch for the southern swells.","f":["Sequência de camarão","Shrimp served in several dishes one after another, a ritual of the Santa Catarina coast’s tables."],"r":"During the tainha fishing season, some beaches close to surfing: respect the flags and leave room for the fishers’ nets.","s":"April to September: more consistent southern swells, cool water (wetsuit) and less crowded line-ups than in summer.","a":[["Garopaba","A fishing village of Azorean origin on its bay, where whale-watching trips set off."],["Imbituba","A port and wild beaches to the south, on the coast protected for southern right whales."],["Praia de Naufragados","An isolated beach at the southern tip of Santa Catarina Island, reached by a forest trail."]]},"guardaembau":{"v":"A car-free surf village where you cross the river with your board under your arm: a bohemian vibe, fishers on the sandbar and the park’s Atlantic Forest tumbling down to the sand.","f":["Florianópolis oysters","Farmed in the bays south of the island, eaten raw or gratinated."],"r":"Cross the Rio da Madre where the current is weak, and at the river-mouth wave, wait your turn: the locals surf it every day.","s":"April to August: well-lined-up southern swells on the river-mouth sandbanks and a quieter village than in summer.","a":[["Serra do Tabuleiro State Park","A large park of Atlantic Forest, restinga and mountains, with trails and protected beaches."],["Morro do Cambirela","A steep climb to a summit overlooking the bay and Florianópolis Island."],["Praia de Naufragados","A wild beach in the south of Santa Catarina Island, reached by a forest trail."],["Garopaba","A fishing village of Azorean origin, the starting point for watching southern right whales."]]},"arpoador":{"v":"Where Cariocas discovered surfing, the Arpoador rock gathers a crowd every evening that applauds the sunset behind the Dois Irmãos.","f":["Biscoito Globo and mate gelado","Puffed cassava biscuits and iced mate tea, hawked on the sand."],"r":"The peak is crowded and has a pecking order: wait your turn, don’t take wave after wave and stay well away from the swimming zone.","s":"May to September: southern swells wrapping along the rock, milder air and fewer storms.","a":[["Sugarloaf Mountain","A cable car to the summit for views over Guanabara Bay and Rio’s beaches."],["Corcovado","The summit of Christ the Redeemer, reached by a little train through the Tijuca Forest."],["Rio de Janeiro Botanical Garden","An avenue of imperial palms, greenhouses and rainforest at the foot of Corcovado."],["Lagoa Rodrigo de Freitas","A lagoon lined with a cycle path and kiosks, ringed by mountains."],["Copacabana Fort","A fort at the tip of Copacabana, with an army museum and cafés facing the sea."]]},"taiba":{"v":"A village where jangadas, the sail rafts of Ceará’s fishers, still come back onto the sand below ochre cliffs, while kites zip across the lagoon.","f":["Baião de dois","Rice and beans mixed with coalho cheese, an iconic dish of Ceará cuisine."],"r":"Give way to the jangadas heading out or coming back, and never rig your kite among the boats pulled up on the beach.","s":"July to December: strong, steady trade winds, dry skies and constant heat, peaking from August to November.","lbl":"Pousada"},"barragrande":{"v":"A sandy village on Piauí’s short coastline where kiters cross paths with crab fishers, and where you head out by boat into the mangroves in search of seahorses.","f":["Caranguejo","Mangrove crab cooked in a spicy broth, cracked with a mallet at the barracas’ tables."],"r":"On seahorse trips, leave them in the water and don’t touch the mangrove; at low tide, ride well away from the nets.","s":"July to December: steady trade winds every afternoon and flat lagoons at low tide, perfect for learning.","a":[["Cajueiro da Praia","A small neighbouring coastal town, between beaches, mangroves and the delta’s protected areas."],["Timonha Estuary","A mangrove-lined estuary to explore by boat, rich in birds and crabs."]],"lbl":"Pousada"},"guajiru":{"v":"A Ceará fishing coast dotted with currais, wooden fish traps planted in the water, where a small kite community has settled facing the sand island.","f":["Baião de dois","Rice and beans mixed with coalho cheese, an iconic dish of Ceará cuisine."],"r":"Spot the fishing currais and nets before you ride, and keep your lines well away from them: they are the village’s livelihood.","s":"July to December: strong, steady trade winds, flat water in the lagoon and skies that are almost always dry.","lbl":"Pousada"},"lagoaparaiso":{"v":"A rainwater lagoon surrounded by dunes where life happens in the water: hammocks and tables just above the surface, barracas on the shore and kite sails in the afternoon.","f":["Tapioca","A cassava-starch pancake filled with coalho cheese or coconut, sold all over Jijoca."],"r":"The hammocks in the water belong to the barracas: order something to use them, and leave nothing in the lagoon.","s":"July to November: the lagoon still full after the rains, dry skies and steady wind for kiting in the afternoon.","a":[["Jericoacoara National Park","Dunes, lagoons and sculpted rocks like the Pedra Furada, to explore by buggy or on foot."],["Jericoacoara","A village of sandy streets where you climb the big dune for sunset."]]},"morrosp":{"v":"A car-free village where luggage travels by wheelbarrow from the pier, between the ruins of a small fort, a lighthouse on the hill and numbered beaches where Bahian music plays late into the night.","f":["Acarajé","A black-eyed-bean fritter fried in dendê oil, filled with shrimp and vatapá."],"r":"At low tide, don’t walk on the coral to get into the natural pools, and wear mineral sunscreen.","s":"September to March: dry season, calm sea and clear water in the natural pools at low tide.","a":[["Morro de São Paulo village","Lanes, ruins of the colonial fort and a hilltop lighthouse with views over the beaches and the island."],["Tinharé Island","An island of mangroves and coconut palms to discover by boat, with villages and deserted beaches."]]},"praiadoforte":{"v":"A former fishing village overlooked by the ruins of Garcia d’Ávila’s castle, now a stronghold of sea turtle protection, where the TAMAR base monitors nesting on the beaches.","f":["Acarajé","A black-eyed-bean fritter fried in dendê oil, filled with shrimp and vatapá."],"r":"From September to March, the turtles nest: no torches or fires on the beach at night, and don’t go near the marked nests.","s":"September to March: dry season, calm sea and crystal-clear natural pools at low tide."},"paraty":{"v":"A port on the old gold route, with cobbled streets that the spring tides sometimes lap, surrounded by caiçara communities who still live from fishing and dugout canoes.","f":["Azul-marinho","A caiçara stew of fish and green banana that turns bluish as it cooks."],"r":"In the protected bays, drop anchor well away from the seagrass beds and take your rubbish back: the caiçara communities live from this sea.","s":"April to October: drier season, calm water and light wind, away from the summer thunderstorms.","a":[["Paraty historic centre","Cobbled streets, baroque churches and white colonial houses with colourful doors."],["Saco do Mamanguá","A narrow inlet lined with mangroves and mountains, to explore by kayak."],["Juatinga Ecological Reserve","A wild peninsula of caiçara beaches linked by trails through the Atlantic Forest."]]},"maresias":{"v":"São Paulo’s weekend beach, between parties and Atlantic Forest, and a breeding ground for Brazilian surfing: Gabriel Medina grew up here, and the local kids train on the same sandbanks.","f":["Azul-marinho","A caiçara stew of fish and green banana that turns bluish as it cooks."],"r":"The rip currents are strong and the line-up very local: surf near the lifeguard towers and leave the waves to the regulars.","s":"April to September: more powerful southern swells and a far less crowded beach than in summer.","a":[["Ilhabela","A mountainous, forest-covered island with beaches, waterfalls and wrecks to explore."],["Ilhabela State Park","Preserved Atlantic Forest, with trails to waterfalls and secluded beaches."],["São Sebastião","A small colonial historic centre facing the channel and the ferry to Ilhabela."],["Praia de Juqueí","A long beach with calmer water, popular with families for swimming."]]},"piramides":{"v":"A village of sandy streets facing the gulf, living to the rhythm of southern right whales and sea lions: you head out to sea with fishers turned guides.","f":["Patagonian lamb","Cordero slow-roasted on a cross by the embers, a speciality of Chubut’s estancias."],"r":"A protected reserve: by kayak or when diving, keep your distance from whales and sea lions, and leave the approach to authorised boats.","s":"December to March: the least cold water and playful sea lions; the whales are here from June to December."},"nahuelhuapi":{"v":"An Andean shore of wood and stone houses inherited from Swiss and German settlers, where you paddle facing snowy peaks before heading back to taste Bariloche chocolate.","f":["Bariloche chocolate","Chocolate branches from the town’s chocolate shops, also to enjoy as hot chocolate."],"r":"The wind picks up fast and the water stays icy: set off in the morning, stick to the shore and wear a buoyancy aid even in calm weather.","s":"December to March: southern summer, often light wind in the morning and slightly less cold water for paddling.","a":[["Nahuel Huapi National Park","Argentina’s oldest national park: lakes, coihue forests and mountain trails."],["Cerro Otto","A cable car to a summit with a panoramic view over the lake and the Andes."],["Bariloche","A stone-and-wood civic centre, chocolate shops and a lively lakefront."],["Colonia Suiza","A village of Swiss settlers, known for its craft fair and its curanto."],["Lake Gutiérrez","A smaller, sheltered lake at the foot of Cerro Catedral, with beaches and trails."]]},"marmol":{"v":"A land of ranchers and pioneers along the Carretera Austral, where the glacial lake has carved the marble into blue-veined vaults that you visit from a tiny village.","f":["Cordero al palo","Patagonian lamb roasted on a spit in front of the fire, the festive dish of Aysén."],"r":"Don’t touch or climb the fragile marble walls, and only go out by kayak with a guide: the lake’s wind shifts fast.","s":"December to March: southern summer, a turquoise lake that is calmer early in the morning, before the wind picks up."},"puntachoros":{"v":"A caleta of loco and seaweed fishers at the gateway to the desert, now the base for boats heading out to see the reserve’s Humboldt penguins, marine otters and bottlenose dolphins.","r":"On Isla Damas, stay on the marked trails; Isla Choros can only be seen from the boat, landing there is forbidden.","s":"December to March: a more manageable sea, slightly less cold water and regular crossings to the island.","a":[["Caleta Chañaral de Aceituno","A fishing village where whale- and dolphin-watching trips set off."],["Chañaral Island","A rocky island in the reserve, with penguin and seabird colonies seen from the boat."]]},"totoralillo":{"v":"A fishing cove among rocks and cacti, where surfers from La Serena and Coquimbo come looking for the reef peaks when the swell arrives, facing surprisingly clear water.","f":["Tongoy ostiones","Pacific scallops gratinated with parmesan, a speciality of the nearby caletas."],"r":"The beach is small and popular in summer: take your rubbish with you, and on the reef peaks, let the locals have their waves.","s":"April to September: more consistent south-westerly swells; in summer, an often calm, clear sea for swimming.","a":[["Cross of the Third Millennium","A huge concrete cross on a hill in Coquimbo, with a lookout over the bay."],["Guanaqueros Bay","A large sheltered bay, a sandy beach and a caleta where you eat fresh fish."],["La Serena Monumental Lighthouse","An iconic lighthouse at the end of Avenida del Mar, symbol of La Serena."],["Mohammed VI Mosque","An Andalusian-Moroccan-style mosque with a minaret, a surprise on Coquimbo’s hill."]]},"futaleufu":{"v":"A town of wooden houses right by the Argentine border, where farmers, rafting guides and kayakers from all over the world meet around a turquoise river of rare power.","f":["Cordero al palo","Patagonian lamb roasted on a spit in front of the fire, the festive dish of southern Chile."],"r":"Only run the rapids with a local guide and a safety kayaker: the class V sections leave no margin for error.","s":"December to March: southern summer, favourable water levels, less icy water and long days on the river."},"mompiche":{"v":"A fishing village on the Afro-Ecuadorian coast of Esmeraldas, lulled by the marimba, where canoes come back onto the sand while surfers watch for the left at the end of the bay.","f":["Encocado de pescado","Fish simmered in coconut milk, the emblem of Esmeraldas’ Afro-Ecuadorian cuisine."],"r":"The point-break left draws a crowd: respect priority, paddle out through the channel and watch out for the rocks at low tide.","s":"December to April: north-westerly swells that make the point peel, warm water and a lively beach.","a":[["Mache-Chindul Ecological Reserve","Hills of tropical rainforest, rich in birds and howler monkeys."],["Muisne","A town on a mangrove island, with a long beach and fishing boats."]]},"caboblanco":{"v":"A fishing hamlet at the foot of desert cliffs, once famous for marlin fishing and its club where Hemingway stayed, now known for its legendary left-hand barrel.","f":["Seco de chabelo","Green plantain and dried meat sautéed together, a typical dish of Piura."],"r":"A shallow reef wave with a narrow peak: don’t paddle to the peak without the level for it, and give priority to the locals.","s":"December to March: north-westerly swells and offshore wind in the morning, for the best barrels of the year."},"pacasmayo":{"v":"An old port in northern Peru with its long pier and republican-era houses, battered by the desert wind, where surfers come for a left that seems never to end.","f":["Arroz con pato","Green rice with coriander and beer cooked with duck, a classic of northern Peru."],"r":"On such a long left, one surfer per wave: don’t steal priority by paddling back up around the point.","s":"April to October: southern swells that light up the point and often offshore wind in the morning.","a":[["Pacatnamú","A vast pre-Hispanic archaeological site of adobe pyramids, facing the ocean."],["Pacasmayo","The malecón, the old iron pier and the colonial houses of the former port."]]},"isladelaplata":{"v":"A dry, wild island where blue-footed boobies and frigatebirds nest, reached from Puerto López, a fishing village that also lives to the rhythm of the humpback whales of the southern winter.","f":["Viche de pescado","A Manabí soup of fish, peanut, green plantain and corn, very filling."],"r":"Stay on the trails with the Machalilla park guide, don’t go near the nests and never put your fins on the coral.","s":"December to April: warmer, clearer water; from June to September, humpback whales during the crossing."},"tortugabay":{"v":"At the end of a trail lined with opuntia cacti, a beach where marine iguanas, reef sharks and pelicans share the water with Puerto Ayora locals who come for a swim after work.","f":["Pescado brujo","A local Galápagos fish served fried or as encocado at the kiosks of Puerto Ayora."],"r":"Only swim at Playa Mansa, as the main beach has strong currents; let the iguanas pass and respect the park’s opening hours.","s":"December to May: warm season, milder water and a calm sea, ideal for swimming and paddling in the cove.","a":[["Santa Cruz Island","Highlands where giant tortoises roam free, lava tunnels and twin craters."],["Puerto Ayora","A small port town, with its fish market frequented by sea lions and pelicans."],["Academy Bay","Santa Cruz’s natural harbour, home to the Charles Darwin Research Station."]]},"puntacarola":{"v":"The beach of the Galápagos capital’s residents, where sea lions sleep on the sand, frigatebirds circle above the hill and a small crew of surfers watches the reef.","f":["Pescado brujo","A local Galápagos fish served fried or as encocado in the port’s restaurants."],"r":"Sea lions share the beach and the water: keep two metres away and never pass between a mother and her pup.","s":"December to April: northern swells that wake up the reef, warmer water and good visibility.","a":[["Laguna El Junco","A freshwater crater lake in the highlands, where frigatebirds come to bathe."],["Puerto Baquerizo Moreno","A lively malecón where sea lions take over the benches and the docks."],["San Cristóbal Island","The easternmost island: Kicker Rock, sea lion beaches and a giant tortoise reserve."]]},"rosario":{"v":"A string of coral islets home to Afro-Caribbean fishing communities, between mangroves, boats from Cartagena and little houses on the water.","f":["Cocadas","Coconut sweets sold by Cartagena’s palenqueras and on the beaches."],"r":"Never stand on the coral, use mineral sunscreen and buy fish and crafts from the islanders.","s":"December to April: dry season and clear water, even if the trade winds sometimes make the crossing choppy."},"providencia":{"v":"A Raizal island that has stayed away from mass tourism: colourful wooden houses, English creole, reggae and small-scale fishing around a lagoon of a thousand shades of blue.","l":"Spanish, English, Raizal Creole","f":["Rondón","A stew of fish, conch and root vegetables simmered in coconut milk, a Raizal dish."],"r":"Stay in the navigation lanes on the lagoon and never walk on the reef: it lies at the heart of the Seaflower reserve.","s":"February to May: dry season, gentler trade winds and the best visibility on the reef.","a":[["Old Providence McBean Lagoon National Park","Mangroves, lagoon and Crab Cay, where you snorkel among reef fish."],["Santa Catalina Island","An islet linked by a floating bridge, with remains of small forts and the Morgan’s Head rock."]],"lbl":"Native posada"},"malpelo":{"v":"A bare volcanic rock pounded by the Pacific, uninhabited apart from a small military post: here, life is underwater, in schools of sharks as far as the eye can see.","r":"Landing is forbidden without a permit. Underwater, stay with your group, with no gloves and no contact, and let the sharks come to you without chasing them.","s":"January to April for a calmer sea; June to November for silky sharks and sometimes the whale shark.","lbl":"Hotel at the departure port"},"capurgana":{"v":"A car-free Afro-Colombian village where you get around on foot or on horseback between jungle and coves, and where life follows the arrival of the lanchas from Necoclí and Turbo.","r":"The village has no reliable cash machine: bring cash. To walk to La Miel, on the Panamanian side, keep your passport on you.","s":"December to April: dry season, fewer downpours over the jungle, but a sometimes rough lancha crossing."},"cabopolonio":{"v":"A hamlet of fishers’ and artists’ shacks with no grid electricity, where the lighthouse beam sweeps the dunes at night and where sea lions were once hunted.","f":["Buñuelos de algas","Fried seaweed fritters, the speciality of the shacks on the Rocha coast."],"r":"Keep your distance from the sea lions at the foot of the lighthouse and stay on the trails: the park’s dunes are protected.","s":"March to June: more consistent southern swells, water still mild at first and a village returned to calm.","a":[["Cabo Polonio Lighthouse","A lighthouse on the rocky point, with views over the islands and the sea lion colony."],["Aguas Dulces","A small, rustic seaside village of shacks on a long beach of the Rocha coast."]]},"marialagorda":{"v":"A Cuban land’s end beyond the tobacco fields of Pinar del Río, named after a pirate legend, with only a handful of buildings for a village, facing the drop-offs.","r":"You are in Guanahacabibes National Park: take away no coral or shells, and leave the turtles that nest in summer in peace.","s":"November to April: dry season, a glassy sea in the bay and good visibility, away from the cyclones."},"jardinesreina":{"v":"A maze of cays and mangroves named in honour of the Queen of Spain, kept almost untouched thanks to strict protection and the complete absence of any village.","r":"Don’t feed the mangrove crocodiles or the sharks, and take nothing away: corals and shells stay in the park.","s":"November to April: dry season, a calmer sea for outings and a cyclone-free period.","lbl":"Casa particular"},"puntaperdiz":{"v":"A stretch of the Zapata Swamp coast marked by the 1961 landing, with memorial stones along the road, between marshes, crystal-clear sinkholes and fishing villages.","r":"In spring, thousands of crabs cross the coast road: drive slowly. Enter the water via the designated access points.","s":"November to April: dry season, a calm sea on the bay and fewer mosquitoes from the marshes.","lbl":"Casa particular"},"negril":{"v":"A former fishing village turned hippie hideaway and then beach resort, where reggae drifts out of the beach bars and everyone gathers on the cliffs for sunset.","r":"With beach vendors, a smiling “no thanks” is enough. Before jumping off the cliffs, watch the locals and check the depth.","s":"December to April: dry season, calm, clear sea, away from the hurricane season.","a":[["Negril Lighthouse","A white late-19th-century lighthouse on the cliffs of the island’s western point."],["Xtabi","A cove in the West End cliffs, riddled with caves and passages over clear water."]]},"bostonbay":{"v":"A green, rainy corner of Portland parish, where smoke from the jerk pits rises by the roadside and the village kids learn to ride waves in the little bay.","f":["Boston jerk pork","Pork marinated in chilli and smoked over pimento wood, in the roadside shacks."],"r":"The line-up is tiny and very local: say hello, wait your turn and rent your board from the guys on the beach.","s":"December to March: more consistent north-easterly swells and a drier season on the coast.","a":[["Blue and John Crow Mountains National Park","UNESCO-listed mountain forests, trails and Blue Mountain coffee plantations."],["Rio Grande","A peaceful trip down the river on a bamboo raft steered by a raftsman."],["Port Antonio","A banana port with twin bays, a market, old houses and a laid-back feel."],["Winnifred Beach","A public beach dear to Jamaicans, with grilled-fish shacks and calm water."],["Long Bay Beach","A wide, wild crescent of white sand on the east coast, lined with coconut palms."]]},"catalina_do":{"v":"Off a town born from sugar cane, the uninhabited island welcomes the boats of Bayahíbe’s fishers, now turned ferrymen to the reef, every day.","r":"Never stand on the Aquarium reef, choose a mineral sunscreen and avoid the times when the big catamarans arrive.","s":"December to April: dry season, calm sea and good visibility, away from the hurricanes.","a":[["Bayahíbe","A fishing village where boats leave for the islands and East National Park."],["Río Chavón","A river carved deep into the jungle, admired from the village of Altos de Chavón."]]},"playarincon":{"v":"A coconut-palm peninsula partly settled by descendants of freed African Americans, where cooking is done with coconut milk and whales come to calve in winter.","f":["Pescado con coco","Fish simmered in a coconut-milk sauce, the signature dish of the Samaná peninsula."],"r":"For grilled fish on the beach, agree the price before it’s cooked. Take your rubbish back: there are hardly any bins.","s":"February to April: drier season, calm sea and humpback whales still present in Samaná Bay.","a":[["Las Galeras","A fishing village at the end of the peninsula, the starting point for the wild beaches."],["Samaná","A small port town where humpback whale-watching trips set off."],["Cayo Levantado","A white-sand islet in the middle of Samaná Bay, for a quiet swim."]]},"jobos":{"v":"A north-west coast nicknamed the island’s surfers’ corner, where Puerto Rican families come at weekends and the chiringuitos serve alcapurrias and cold beers facing the right-hander.","r":"The right breaks over a shallow reef and a current pulls out to sea: give priority to the locals and swim in the sheltered part.","s":"November to March: consistent northern Atlantic swells and trade winds that are often calm in the morning.","a":[["Pozo de Jacinto","A blowhole in the coastal rock, linked to the legend of a farmer and his cow."],["Guajataca Tunnel","An old railway tunnel on the edge of the cliffs, with a lookout over the Atlantic."],["Punta Borinquen Lighthouse","Ruins of the old Spanish lighthouse and the current lighthouse at the island’s north-west tip."],["Palacete Los Moreau","A planters’ mansion turned house museum, amid former cane fields."]]},"flamenco":{"v":"An island municipality that got the US Navy to leave after years of protest, still peaceful with its rental jeeps, its little town of Dewey and its turtle bays.","r":"Turtles nest on the island’s beaches: don’t walk on marked nests and avoid any light on the sand at night.","s":"April to August: winter swells are over, and the water is calm and clear at the head of the bay; avoid long weekends.","a":[["Culebrita Island","Wild islet with natural pools, beaches and a lighthouse from the Spanish era."],["Cayo Luis Peña","Protected islet reached by kayak or water taxi, for snorkelling among turtles."],["Culebra National Wildlife Refuge","Refuge for seabirds and nesting beaches, spread across the island and its cays."],["Dewey","The island’s little town, with its ferry port, colourful houses and restaurants."]]},"mosquitobay":{"v":"An island long occupied by the US Navy and handed back to its residents in the early 2000s, where free-roaming horses wander between the villages and still-wild beaches.","r":"Swimming is forbidden in the bay: no sunscreen or insect repellent on your skin before the trip, and no flash.","s":"All year round, but pick a night close to the new moon: without moonlight, the blue glow stands out far better.","a":[["Vieques National Wildlife Refuge","Former military land turned into a refuge, with wild beaches and lagoons."],["La Chiva Beach","Long beach on the south coast, with turquoise water and an islet you can swim to."],["Fortín Conde de Mirasol","The last Spanish fort built in the Americas, now a museum of the island’s history."],["Esperanza","Small coastal village with a lively malecón, the starting point for kayak trips."]]},"thebaths":{"v":"On the island Columbus is said to have named “the fat virgin” for its outline, charter yachts anchor off the boulders and locals live to the rhythm of the sea.","r":"Arrive early, before the cruise-ship excursions, and give up on swimming if the red flag warns of swell.","s":"April to July: winter swells have eased, the sea is calm between the rocks and there are fewer people.","a":[["Copper Mine","Stone ruins of a 19th-century copper mine, perched above the waves."],["Fallen Jerusalem Island","Protected islet with granite boulders like those at the Baths, explored by boat."],["Spanish Town","The island’s town, with its marina, small eateries and market."],["Sage Mountain National Park","Rainforest trails up to the highest peak in the islands, on Tortola."]]},"rhone":{"v":"In the heart of the charter-yacht archipelago, the wreck made famous by the film The Deep tells of the hurricanes that have always shaped life on these islands.","r":"Take nothing from the wreck, not even a scrap of copper: moor to the park buoys and don’t touch the corals covering it.","s":"March to July: the sea is often calm, visibility good and the water warm, before the peak of hurricane season.","a":[["Salt Island","Islet with natural salt ponds, where salt was once harvested, and the Rhone’s graveyard."],["Cooper Island","Small, quiet island with a beach, snorkelling and a popular stop for yachts."],["Dead Chest Island","Wild islet linked to the pirate song “Fifteen men on a dead man’s chest”."],["Sage Mountain National Park","Rainforest trails up to the highest peak in the islands, on Tortola."]]},"trunkbay":{"v":"An island two-thirds protected since Laurance Rockefeller’s gift of land, where the ruins of Danish sugar mills sleep under the forest above the bays.","r":"On the underwater trail, stay at the surface without resting on the coral, and pay your park entry fee when you arrive.","s":"April to July: calm sea on the north coast, clear water and the winter crowds gone home.","a":[["Virgin Islands National Park","Coastal trails, sheltered bays and dry forest over most of the island."],["Reef Bay Sugar Mill","Ruins of a sugar mill at the end of a trail that passes Taíno petroglyphs."],["Cinnamon Bay Plantation","Remains of a beachside Danish plantation, with a short waymarked trail."],["Coral Bay","Bohemian hamlet in the east of the island, with its sailors’ bars and sheltered bay."]]},"carlislebay":{"v":"Below the UNESCO-listed capital, racehorses from the Garrison sometimes come down for a morning swim and yachts anchor above the wrecks.","r":"Swim with the turtles without touching or feeding them, and stay within the marked areas: boats and jet skis cross the bay.","s":"December to May: dry season, a glassy sea in the bay and great visibility over the wrecks.","a":[["Historic Garrison","Former British garrison listed by UNESCO, with its racecourse."],["Bridgetown","Colonial capital with its neo-Gothic Parliament, markets and rum shops."],["Nidhe Israel Synagogue","One of the oldest synagogues in the Americas, with a museum and ritual bath."],["George Washington House","House where the young George Washington stayed, now a small museum."]]},"tobagocays":{"v":"A yacht anchorage where the boat boys paddle out to sell fruit and bread, and where lobster is grilled in the evening on the beach at Petit Bateau.","r":"Bargain politely with the boat boys before buying anything, and never stand on Horseshoe Reef, which shelters the islets.","s":"December to June: dry season, steady trade winds and clear water on the reef, before the hurricanes.","a":[["Mayreau","Tiny inhabited island, with its hilltop village, its church and Salt Whistle Bay."],["Union Island","Mountainous island with the port of Clifton, a base for boats to the cays and a kitesurfing spot."]]},"moliniere":{"v":"On the Spice Island, among the fishing villages of the west coast and the scent of nutmeg, the underwater statues tell Caribbean history as they slowly become covered in coral.","r":"Don’t stand on the statues or touch them: the coral colonising them is fragile and part of the artwork.","s":"January to May: dry season, calm sea on the leeward coast and the best visibility.","a":[["Fort George","18th-century fort perched above the harbour, with views over the Carenage."],["Grenada National Museum","Small museum in an old colonial building, on the island’s history and spices."],["Cathedral of the Immaculate Conception","Stone cathedral overlooking St George’s, restored after Hurricane Ivan."]]},"champagnereef":{"v":"A volcanic island that has stayed green and lightly built, where people speak Kwéyòl, where the Kalinago live and where the earth’s heat escapes even under the sea.","r":"Enter from the pebble beach without trampling the sponges, and stay above the bubbles without touching the bottom.","s":"February to May: dry season, calm sea on the leeward coast and maximum visibility.","a":[["Scotts Head","Fishing village at the end of an isthmus between the Atlantic and the Caribbean Sea."],["Soufrière","Village with sulphurous hot springs, where you can bathe in natural pools."],["Trafalgar","Village in the Roseau Valley, the gateway to the twin Trafalgar Falls."],["Dominica Botanic Gardens","Colonial garden at the foot of the hills, with tropical trees and endemic parrots."],["Roseau Old Market","Former market square in the heart of the capital, with craft stalls."]]},"ansechastanet":{"v":"At the foot of the Pitons, around the island’s former French capital, people speak Kwéyòl, live from fishing and farming, and the earth still steams at the sulphur springs.","r":"Swim in the marked zone off the beach: dive boats manoeuvre right next to the reef.","s":"February to May: dry season, calm sea in the lee of the Pitons and good visibility on the drop-off.","a":[["The Pitons","Two UNESCO-listed volcanic peaks; the guided climb of Gros Piton is superb."],["Sulphur Springs","Steaming geothermal field and hot mud pools where you can bathe."],["Soufrière Botanical Gardens","Tropical garden around the Diamond waterfall and its old mineral baths."],["Soufrière","Colourful little town on the bay, with its church and fishermen’s market."],["Anse Mamin","Small neighbouring beach of dark sand, lined by a former plantation."]]},"thousandsteps":{"v":"An island where shore diving is a way of life: yellow stones mark the sites along the coast road, among pink salt pans, flamingos and cacti.","f":["Stoba di kabritu","Goat stew slow-cooked with spices, a classic of Bonaire’s kitchens."],"r":"Don’t leave anything valuable in your car at the car park, and take the steps down carefully with your tank on your back.","s":"All year round: warm water, remarkable visibility and an island generally spared by hurricanes.","a":[["Washington Slagbaai National Park","The island’s wild north, with dirt tracks, flamingo lagoons and snorkelling coves."],["Rincon","The island’s oldest village, with colourful houses nestled in a valley."],["Pekelmeer Salt Pans","Pink salt flats, salt pyramids and flamingos in the south of the island."],["Kralendijk","Small capital with a colourful seafront, cafés, a fort and shops."]]},"deansbluehole":{"v":"A rural, peaceful island where people live from fishing and small-scale farming, and where the white churches built by Father Jerome watch over Clarence Town.","r":"Never freedive alone, and give space to the freedivers training on the lines above the hole.","s":"April to June: very calm sea in the bay, water already warm, before hurricane season."},"smithsreef":{"v":"Around Turtle Cove, Provo’s small historic marina, local life still revolves around conch, caught, cooked into fritters and salads, and the boats that speed off to the cays.","r":"Keep clear of the Turtle Cove entrance channel, used by boats, and never stand on the coral heads.","s":"April to June: the sea is often calm, the water warm and visibility good, before hurricane season begins."},"bloodybay":{"v":"On Little Cayman, the smallest and quietest of the three islands, the day revolves around the dive boats; on the roads, iguanas have right of way.","r":"Moor only to the marine park buoys, control your buoyancy along the wall and touch neither sponges nor corals.","s":"April to October: the north coast is generally calm and visibility excellent; in winter, northerly gales can close the site.","a":[["Booby Pond Nature Reserve","Protected lagoon home to a large colony of red-footed boobies and frigatebirds."],["Teignmouth Electron wreck","Hull of Donald Crowhurst’s trimaran, beached on Cayman Brac after a solo race."]]},"losislotes":{"v":"From the La Paz malecón, pangas set off every morning for the desert islands of the gulf; here, the Sea of Cortez is lived between small-scale fishing and encounters with sea lions.","r":"Let the sea lions come to you without touching or chasing them, and stay well away from the rocks where the males defend their territory.","s":"September to November: the water is still warm, the young sea lions playful and the sea calm before the winter northerlies.","a":[["Espíritu Santo Island","Protected desert island with turquoise coves and red cliffs, ideal by kayak."],["Partida Island","Espíritu Santo’s neighbour, separated by a shallow channel, with beaches and sheltered anchorages."]]},"grancenote":{"v":"For the Maya, cenotes were both the only water sources in a Yucatán without rivers and gateways to the underworld; today Tulum lives between ruins, jungle and cave diving.","f":["Cochinita pibil","Pork marinated in annatto and bitter orange, slow-cooked and served in tacos."],"r":"Shower before getting in the water, with no sunscreen or repellent, and touch neither the stalactites nor the turtles that live in the pool.","s":"All year round, the water stays cool and crystal clear; come early in the morning, outside the winter holidays, to avoid the crowds.","a":[["Tulum Maya site","Fortified Maya city perched on a cliff facing the Caribbean Sea."],["Boca Paila Peninsula (Sian Ka’an)","Wild strip between lagoons and sea, in the Sian Ka’an Biosphere Reserve."]]},"barradelacruz":{"v":"A village of fishers and farmers on the Oaxaca coast, Barra de la Cruz chose to manage its own beach: no concrete, a few palapas and a right-hander that made its name.","f":["Tlayuda","Large crispy tortilla topped with beans, quesillo and meat, an emblem of Oaxaca."],"r":"The beach is run by the community: pay the access fee at the entrance and give priority to the village surfers at the peak.","s":"May to September: south swells get the right-hander peeling, especially in the morning before the breeze.","a":[["Río Copalita","River run by raft, which meets the sea near Huatulco."],["La Crucecita","The lively heart of Huatulco, with its zócalo, church and small restaurants."]],"lbl":"Cabañas"},"loreto":{"v":"The first capital of the Californias, Loreto keeps its stone mission and quiet streets; across from the malecón, the islands of the marine park draw fishers, kayakers and, in winter, whales.","f":["Almejas chocolatas","Brown-shelled clams from the bay, often roasted under embers, known as tatemadas."],"r":"The marine park requires an access wristband: get it before you set off, don’t fish without a permit and keep your distance from the sea lions.","s":"October to May: mild temperatures; watch for days without the north wind, common in winter, to cross to the islands.","a":[["Mission of Our Lady of Loreto","The first Jesuit mission in the Californias, founded in 1697, with a small museum next door."],["Mission San Bruno","Remains of the short-lived first Jesuit settlement on the coast, north of Loreto."]]},"bacalar":{"v":"A former Spanish stronghold against pirates, Bacalar lives to the slow rhythm of its lagoon, among wooden jetties, balnearios and stromatolites, some of the oldest forms of life.","f":["Cochinita pibil","Pork marinated in annatto and bitter orange, slow-cooked and served in tacos."],"r":"Don’t walk on or touch the stromatolites: these living formations are fragile. Swim without sunscreen.","s":"November to April: the dry season and clear skies bring out the lagoon’s shades of blue.","a":[["Bacalar","Village on the lagoon shore, overlooked by the San Felipe fort and its museum."],["Chetumal","Capital of Quintana Roo, with its bayfront and its museum of Maya culture."]]},"santamaria":{"v":"Between two resorts, this cove recalls the Baja California of old: cactus desert plunging into a turquoise sea and pelicans skimming the rocks.","r":"The bay is a protected area: no fishing or collecting, and stay in the marked swimming area, away from the boats.","s":"June to November: warm water and good visibility, but keep an eye on hurricane alerts in late summer.","a":[["Cabo San Lucas","Lively town at the tip of the peninsula, the departure point for boats to El Arco."],["Cabo San Lucas Natural History Museum","Small museum on the wildlife, plants and fossils of the Cape region."]]},"atitlan":{"v":"Around the lake, each Maya village keeps its own language and dress; in San Marcos, fishermen in wooden cayucos cross paths with yoga lovers who have come in search of calm.","l":"Spanish, Kaqchikel, Tz’utujil","f":["Patín","Small lake fish cooked in a spicy tomato sauce, wrapped in leaves."],"r":"In San Marcos, keep a low profile in the Maya village, cover up out of the water and use the jetties or the reserve to swim.","s":"November to April: dry season; paddle in the morning, before the Xocomil, the afternoon wind, whips up waves.","a":[["Santiago Atitlán","Large Tz’utujil town, with its market, colonial church and the cult of Maximón."],["San Juan La Laguna","Village of weaving cooperatives using natural dyes, with painted streets."],["San Pedro Volcano","Guided climb through coffee fields to a bird’s-eye view of the lake."],["Panajachel","Gateway to the lake, with its craft market and boats to the villages."]]},"elparedon":{"v":"A black-sand village at the end of a road lined with plantations, El Paredón lives between fishing from small boats, the nearby mangroves and a small surf scene that has stayed simple.","r":"Make room for the fishermen hauling in their boats and respect the sea turtle nests; the black sand burns at midday, so keep your sandals on.","s":"March to October: regular south swells; surf early in the morning, before the wind picks up.","a":[["Sipacate-Naranjo National Park","Mangroves and channels to explore by boat or kayak, rich in birdlife."],["Sipacate","Neighbouring seaside town, another base for surfers on the Pacific coast."]]},"eltunco":{"v":"A former fishing hamlet turned El Salvador’s surfer hangout, El Tunco comes alive at night around its beach bars, while cobblestone peaks line up along the coast.","r":"On the cobblestone peaks, watch out for sea urchins and let everyone take their turn, especially at Sunzal, where the line-up fills up fast.","s":"March to October: regular South Pacific swells and offshore winds in the morning, in water that’s warm all year round.","a":[["El Zonte","Neighbouring surf village, with its right-hander over rock and its laid-back feel."],["Port of La Libertad","Port town with a fishing pier where you can buy fish and seafood."],["Tamanique","Hill village known for its waterfalls, reached on a guided hike."]]},"lasflores":{"v":"In eastern El Salvador, far from the bustle of La Libertad, the fishermen of El Cuco and a few surfers share a coast that is still rural and lined with coconut palms.","r":"On this point break, don’t paddle into the surfers’ path: go round via the channel and respect priority at the peak.","s":"March to October: south swells get the right-hander peeling along its full length, in warm water."},"utila":{"v":"An island of English-speaking islanders with Caribbean roots, Utila has become a haven for trainee divers; on the Utila Cays, fishermen’s houses crowd onto tiny islets.","f":["Coconut bread","Soft bread made with coconut milk, an Anglo-Caribbean legacy of the Bay Islands."],"r":"With whale sharks, don’t touch them, no flash, and slip into the water calmly, at a distance, following the boat’s instructions.","s":"March to September: calmer sea and good visibility, with peak whale shark sightings in spring.","lbl":"Dive club dorm"},"maderas":{"v":"Once a stop on the gold-seekers’ route between the two oceans, the bay of San Juan del Sur mixes fishing boats, surfing and lively nights; Maderas is its wild beach.","r":"At the peak, give priority to the locals and avoid paddling through the many surf school students in the inside.","s":"April to October: regular South Pacific swells and morning offshore winds blowing across from Lake Nicaragua.","a":[["San Juan del Sur","Fishing town at the head of a curved bay, with its market and wooden houses."],["Christ of Mercy","Giant statue on the hill, with a wide view over the bay of San Juan del Sur."]]},"apoyo":{"v":"Around the crater, the “pueblos blancos” live from pottery, plant nurseries and crafts; down below, the warm lake is shared by swimmers, howler monkeys and birds.","f":["Vigorón","Yuca, chicharrón and cabbage salad served on a banana leaf, a Granada speciality."],"r":"In the reserve, no soap or greasy cream in the lake, and carry your rubbish back up: the wooded shore is home to monkeys and birds.","s":"December to April: dry season, clear and warm water; late in the day, the breeze can ripple the surface.","a":[["Granada","Colonial city with colourful façades, churches and horse-drawn carriages on the shore of Lake Nicaragua."],["Masaya Volcano","Active volcano whose crater sometimes reveals a glowing lava lake."],["Catarina","Village of plant nurseries with a mirador looking straight down on the lagoon."],["Isletas de Granada","Archipelago of volcanic islets in Lake Nicaragua, to explore by boat."],["San Juan de Oriente","Potters’ village where you can visit workshops making decorated ceramics."]]},"isladelcoco":{"v":"Only park rangers live on this island covered in forest and waterfalls, which has fuelled legends of pirate treasure; here, everything happens from the boat.","r":"Stay with your dive group, don’t touch or chase the sharks, and only land on the island with the park rangers’ permission.","s":"June to November: rainy season, nutrient-rich water and larger schools of hammerheads; calmer sea from December to May.","lbl":"Before boarding"},"bahiasalinas":{"v":"In this corner of Guanacaste near Nicaragua, the sabanero culture of the cattle ranches sits alongside a handful of kite schools, and the papagayo wind sets the rhythm of the dry season.","f":["Rosquillas","Small crunchy rings of corn and cheese, a traditional Guanacaste snack."],"r":"Leave the beach free for swimmers when rigging and launching, and sail well away from the fishing boats at anchor.","s":"December to April: a strong, steady papagayo wind through the dry season, over water that’s often flat in the bay.","a":[["Santa Rosa National Park","Tropical dry forest, trails and the historic La Casona hacienda."],["Junquillal Bay Wildlife Refuge","Small protected bay with calm water, for swimming and snorkelling."],["Guanacaste National Park","Forest corridor climbing from dry forest to the slopes of the volcanoes."]]},"salsabrava":{"v":"Puerto Viejo carries the Afro-Caribbean heritage of the Limón coast: English creole, calypso, coconut-milk cooking and local surfers proud of their reef.","l":"Spanish, Limón Creole (Mekatelyu)","f":["Rice and beans","Rice and red beans cooked in coconut milk, a pillar of local Caribbean cooking."],"r":"Salsa Brava is a locals’ wave over a very shallow reef: if you don’t have the level, watch from the beach and never drop in.","s":"December to March: Caribbean winter swells get the reef working, with a possible second window in June and July.","a":[["Gandoca-Manzanillo Wildlife Refuge","Rainforest, wild beaches and reefs all the way to the Panamanian border."],["Cahuita","Caribbean village and its coastal national park, with a forest trail and coral reef."],["Jaguar Rescue Center","Rehabilitation centre for wild animals, open for guided visits."],["Kéköldi Bribri Territory","Indigenous community offering guided walks in the rainforest."]]},"santacatalina":{"v":"Long isolated at the end of a dirt track, this fishing village in Veraguas made its name with its wave, then with the boats that head out to explore the islands of Coiba.","r":"The point breaks over volcanic rock: work out the way in and out before you go, and respect the locals at the peak.","s":"April to October: the most regular South Pacific swells, which wake up the point."},"coiba":{"v":"Long home to a penal colony, Coiba stayed out of reach of people and industrial fishing; today only park rangers live there, and boats come over from Santa Catalina.","r":"In the park, no fishing or collecting, no chemical sunscreen, and register at the ranger station before landing.","s":"December to April: dry season and an often calmer sea for the long boat crossing from the coast."},"sanblas":{"v":"An autonomous territory of the Guna people, the archipelago lives from fishing, coconut palms and the craft of molas, the layered embroidery that women wear every day.","l":"Spanish, Guna","f":["Tule masi","Guna stew of fish, plantain and coconut milk, cooked over a wood fire."],"r":"Each island belongs to a community: pay the access fees, don’t pick coconuts and ask before taking any photo.","s":"March to June: the trade winds ease, the sea is calmer for crossings and the water crystal clear over the reefs.","a":[["Gardi Sugdub (Cartí)","Densely populated Guna island, with lanes of bamboo houses and a small cultural museum."],["Cayos Limones","Islets of sand and coconut palms in turquoise water, sheltered by a reef."]],"lbl":"Hut on a Guna island"},"holchan":{"v":"A former lobster-fishing village, San Pedro is explored by golf cart; people switch constantly between Spanish, Kriol and English, between trips out to the barrier reef.","r":"Don’t touch the nurse sharks or rays at Shark Ray Alley, even when they come close, and follow your guide, who is compulsory in the reserve.","s":"April to June: the sea is often calm, trade winds moderate and visibility good before the rainy season.","a":[["Caye Caulker","Small car-free island with a slow pace, and its swimming channel, The Split."],["San Pedro","Colourful town on Ambergris Caye, with sandy streets, seafood restaurants and piers."],["Mexico Rocks","Shallow coral heads in the north of the island, ideal for snorkelling."]]},"sandharbor":{"v":"Ancestral land of the Washoe, the lake later supplied timber for the Comstock silver mines; its Nevada shore is still wooded, scattered with granite boulders and lightly built.","r":"Clean, drain and dry your paddleboard or kayak before launching so you don’t introduce invasive species, and arrive early: the car park fills up fast.","s":"July to September: the least cold water and windless mornings; paddle early, before the afternoon breeze.","a":[["Thunderbird Lodge","1930s stone mansion on the lakeshore, open for guided tours."],["Mount Rose","Summit in the Carson Range, a day hike with views over the lake and the desert."],["Spooner Lake","Small mountain lake, the start of the famous Flume Trail above Lake Tahoe."],["Nevada State Museum","Housed in the old mint, it tells the story of Nevada’s mining, nature and history."]]},"crystalriver":{"v":"A small town that has stayed very “Old Florida”, Crystal River lives from fishing and the summer scallop harvest; in winter, manatees swim upriver in search of the springs’ warmth.","r":"Watch the manatees without touching or chasing them, float at the surface without kicking hard and never enter the marked sanctuary areas.","s":"November to March: manatees gather in the warm springs of Kings Bay as soon as the Gulf cools down.","a":[["Crystal River Archaeological State Park","Mounds and shell middens left by pre-Columbian Native American peoples."],["Homosassa Springs Wildlife State Park","Spring where you can see manatees and Florida wildlife, with an underwater observatory."],["Crystal River Preserve","Salt marshes and riverside trails, rich in coastal birds."],["Yulee Sugar Mill Ruins","Remains of a 19th-century plantation sugar mill, in stone and cast iron."]]},"ginnie":{"v":"In the heart of north Florida, a land of cypress trees and springs, the banks of the Santa Fe draw families on inner tubes as well as cave divers from all over the world who come to explore the flooded passages.","r":"Never enter the caves without a cave-diving certification: these caverns have already killed divers. In the pools, avoid stirring up the sand to keep the water clear.","s":"April to May and September to October: the water stays at 22 °C all year round, the air is mild and the summer crowds are gone.","a":[["Ichetucknee Springs State Park","Crystal-clear spring-fed river to float down on an inner tube or by kayak under the cypresses."],["Poe Springs","Turquoise spring in a wooded park on the Santa Fe, ideal for a swim."],["River Rise Preserve","Forest trails where the Santa Fe disappears underground before resurfacing further on."],["Historic downtown High Springs","Small railway town with old shopfronts, antique shops and dive stores."]]},"scorpionanchorage":{"v":"Santa Cruz is the Limuw of the Chumash, whose plank canoes sometimes come back to land here; at Scorpion, the old ranch buildings recall sheep farming, on an island with no shops at all.","r":"Store your food in the campground’s fox-proof boxes: the island fox is protected. You are in a national park with no services, so take all your rubbish back on the boat.","s":"August to October: the warmest water of the year, a sea that’s often calm in the morning and good visibility in the kelp.","a":[["Santa Cruz Island","Trails to Cavern Point and Potato Harbor, with the island’s endemic scrub-jay along the way."],["Anacapa Island","Volcanic islet with gull-covered cliffs, famous for its Arch Rock."],["Anacapa Marine Reserve","Protected kelp forests where garibaldi, sea lions and sometimes dolphins cruise by."],["Anacapa Lighthouse","White lighthouse perched on the island, watching over the entrance to the Santa Barbara Channel."]],"lbl":"Scorpion Ranch Campground"},"shortsands":{"v":"The park is named after the governor who made Oregon’s beaches public; you walk down a trail under the spruces, board under your arm, to surf cold water in a thick wetsuit.","f":["Clam chowder and Tillamook cheddar","Creamy clam soup and cheese from the nearby Tillamook creamery."],"r":"Parking along the 101 fills up fast: never park outside the marked spaces. The line-up is packed, so wait your turn and beware of currents near the rocks.","s":"September to November: clean autumn swells, wind more often calm in the morning and fewer people than in summer.","a":[["Ecola State Park","Wooded cliffs and coastal viewpoints, with trails down to wild coves."],["Neahkahnie Mountain","Switchback hike to a summit overlooking the ocean and Nehalem Bay."],["Cannon Beach","Village of galleries facing Haystack Rock, a huge rock teeming with seabirds."],["Hug Point","Beach with a waterfall and caves, and an old road cut into the rock at low tide."],["Nehalem Bay State Park","Sand spit between ocean and bay, great for walks and seal watching."]]},"sanjuanpark":{"v":"A border island known for the “Pig War” between the British and the Americans, San Juan lives to the rhythm of the ferries and the resident orcas, which locals recognise one by one.","r":"Keep your distance from the orcas, the law is strict: never cut across their path. Haro Strait has strong currents and icy water, so check the tides before setting off.","s":"June to September: calmer sea in the morning, long days and the best chance of meeting orcas along the west coast.","a":[["Lime Kiln Point State Park","Small lighthouse on the rocks, one of the best spots for watching orcas from shore."],["The Whale Museum","Museum dedicated to the orcas of the Salish Sea and their families."],["Friday Harbor","Ferry port with wooden houses, a marina and small seafood restaurants."],["San Juan Island","The British and American camps of the Pig War, with meadows and farms along the roads."]]},"molokini":{"v":"A crescent-shaped islet that Hawaiian legends link to the goddess Pele, Molokini long served as a navy target before becoming a sanctuary; every morning, a flotilla sets off from Māʻalaea and Kihei.","l":"English, Hawaiian","h":["Aloha","“hello” and “love” in Hawaiian, said warmly"],"f":["Poke","Cubes of raw tuna marinated in soy sauce and sesame oil, sold by weight."],"r":"Boats moor to buoys, never on the reef. Don’t touch the coral, don’t feed the fish and use mineral sunscreen, as required by Hawaiian law.","s":"May to September: weaker trade winds in the morning, a calm sea inside the crater and visibility that’s often exceptional.","a":[["ʻĀhihi-Kīnaʻu Natural Area Reserve","Black lava flow and protected reef coves, to explore with a mask."],["Mākena State Park","Big Beach, a long golden beach at the foot of the Puʻu Ōlaʻi volcanic cone."],["La Perouse Bay","Bay of raw lava at the end of the road, the start of a trail along the old King’s Highway."],["Maui Ocean Center","Aquarium devoted to Hawaiian reefs, with a tunnel under a large tank."],["Kealia Pond National Wildlife Refuge","Coastal wetland for watching Hawaiian stilts and migratory birds."]]},"honolua":{"v":"A lush valley and a bay sacred to Hawaiian surfers, Honolua long rallied residents against property developments; in winter, the point gathers Maui’s best surfers.","l":"English, Hawaiian","h":["Aloha","“hello” and “love” in Hawaiian, said warmly"],"f":["Shave ice","Shaved ice drenched in tropical syrups, a sweet ritual after the session."],"r":"The bay is a marine reserve: no fishing or collecting of any kind, and mineral sunscreen is compulsory. At the peak, locals have priority, so stay humble and keep your distance.","s":"November to March: northwest swells get the right-hander peeling below the point, for experienced surfers only.","a":[["Nakalele Point","Rugged lava coast and a spectacular blowhole, to be watched from a distance in big seas."],["Kahakuloa","Taro village tucked at the head of a valley, on a narrow, dizzying coastal road."],["Mokuleʻia Beach","Small neighbouring sandy cove, also protected, good for snorkelling in summer."],["D.T. Fleming Beach","Lifeguarded, tree-lined beach, handy for a family swim."]]},"twostep":{"v":"In Kona coffee country, Hōnaunau keeps the memory of kapu: anyone who broke the sacred law could find absolution by swimming to the nearby place of refuge.","l":"English, Hawaiian","h":["Aloha","“hello” and “love” in Hawaiian, said warmly"],"f":["Kona coffee","Grown on the slopes above the bay, to taste straight from the farms."],"r":"Don’t settle on the park’s sacred grounds for a picnic. Stay well away from turtles resting on the lava and from spinner dolphins, protected by law in the bay.","s":"April to October: the Kona coast is sheltered, the sea often glassy in the morning and the water crystal clear at around 26 °C.","a":[["Puʻuhonua o Hōnaunau","Ancient sacred place of refuge with its great stone wall, rebuilt temple and wooden statues."],["Kealakekua Bay","Historic bay where James Cook died, with very rich reefs reached by kayak."],["Painted Church","Small wooden church whose walls are entirely painted with naive biblical scenes."],["Kona Coffee Living History Farm","Japanese pioneers’ farm where you can discover coffee growing as it used to be."],["Greenwell Ethnobotanical Garden","Plants used by the ancient Hawaiians, grown as they were before European contact."]]},"tunnels":{"v":"At the foot of the Nā Pali cliffs, Hāʻena lives among taro valleys, ancestral hula sites and beaches where monk seals come to rest, far from the bustle of southern Kauaʻi.","l":"English, Hawaiian","h":["Aloha","“hello” and “love” in Hawaiian, said warmly"],"f":["Taro poi","Fermented taro purée, from the taro fields of the Hanalei Valley."],"r":"Keep your distance from monk seals lying on the sand. The car park is tiny: don’t park on private driveways, and the nearby state park must be booked in advance.","s":"May to September: the sea is often glassy and the water crystal clear; in winter, north swells make the reef dangerous.","a":[["Kalalau Trail","Legendary trail on the Nā Pali coast, between dizzying cliffs and wild valleys."],["Limahuli Garden","Garden of ancient taro terraces and endemic plants, facing the mountains."],["Hanalei Bay","Large half-moon bay with its pier, mountain backdrop and surf village."],["Waiʻoli Mission","19th-century mission house and green church in the heart of Hanalei."],["Kīlauea Point National Wildlife Refuge","Clifftop lighthouse and colonies of boobies, frigatebirds and albatrosses."]]},"sandybeach":{"v":"At the foot of Koko Crater, Sandy’s is the playground of Oahu’s bodyboarders and bodysurfers, who come to take on the shore break after work under the lifeguards’ watchful eye.","l":"English, Hawaiian","h":["Aloha","“hello” and “love” in Hawaiian, said warmly"],"f":["Plate lunch","Rice, macaroni salad and grilled meat, the Hawaiians’ everyday favourite meal."],"r":"Listen to the lifeguards and respect the flags: the shore break causes serious injuries every year. If you’re a beginner, watch from the sand and leave room for the locals.","s":"May to September: south swells and trade winds hollow out the shore break; in winter, the coast is often calmer.","a":[["Hanauma Bay","Collapsed crater flooded by the sea, a marine reserve renowned for snorkelling."],["Makapuʻu Point","Easy trail to a lighthouse and a viewpoint over the windward coast and its islets."],["Hālona Blowhole","Spout of water bursting from a lava tube in rough seas, beside the coast road."],["Koko Crater Botanical Garden","Dryland plants, plumerias and cacti in the hollow of an old crater."],["Lanikai Beach","Fine sand and turquoise water facing the two Mokulua islets."]]},"apostle":{"v":"Land of the Ojibwe and of fishermen from Scandinavia, the Bayfield Peninsula lives from the lake: whitefish fishing, apple orchards and, in winter, walks out to the frozen caves when the ice holds.","f":["Lake Superior whitefish","White fish caught locally, smoked or grilled in Bayfield’s restaurants."],"r":"The lake is icy and gets rough quickly: only set out in calm weather, well equipped, as the cliffs offer no shelter for kilometres. Don’t climb into the fragile caves.","s":"July to August: slightly less cold water and a lake that’s more often calm; set off early, as the wind strengthens in the afternoon.","a":[["Bayfield","Small harbour town with Victorian houses, orchards all around and a ferry to Madeline Island."],["Raspberry Island Lighthouse","Restored lighthouse with a keeper’s house, reached by boat from Bayfield."],["Sand Island Lighthouse","Brown sandstone lighthouse at the north tip of the island, facing the sculpted cliffs."]]},"northbar":{"v":"According to an Anishinaabe legend, a mother bear waits here for her cubs, who became the Manitou Islands; around it, life goes on among dunes, cherry orchards and old life-saving stations.","f":["Cherry pie","The region’s tart cherries fill the farms’ pies and jams."],"r":"Stay on the marked trails, as the dune vegetation is fragile. The channel linking the small lake to Lake Michigan sometimes creates a current: keep an eye on children.","s":"Mid-July to late August: the small lake warms up quickly and becomes pleasant, while Lake Michigan stays cool.","a":[["Sleeping Bear Dunes","Tall dunes to climb and a scenic drive with overlooks on Lake Michigan."],["South Manitou Island","Wild island reached by ferry, with a lighthouse, old farms and giant cedars."],["Glen Haven Life-Saving Station","Former life-saving station turned maritime museum, with demonstrations."],["Glen Lake","Lake with turquoise water surrounded by wooded hills, good for swimming and paddleboarding."]]},"looekey":{"v":"The reef is named after a British frigate that ran aground here in the 18th century; the Lower Keys live to the slow rhythm of fishermen, tiny deer and an underwater music festival.","f":["Key lime pie","Key lime tart on a biscuit base, born in the archipelago."],"r":"Boats moor to buoys, never anchoring on the coral. The heart of the reef is a protected zone: touch nothing, don’t fish and don’t feed the fish.","s":"May to September: the sea is often flat and the water warm; keep an eye on the weather during hurricane season, especially from August.","a":[["National Key Deer Refuge","Pine forests and mangroves where you may spot the tiny Key deer, endemic to the archipelago."],["Blue Hole","Flooded former quarry for watching alligators, turtles and waterbirds."],["Adolphus Busch wreck","Freighter sunk deliberately to form an artificial reef, a dive for experienced divers."]]},"drytortugas":{"v":"Named for their turtles and their lack of fresh water, the Tortugas are home to a never-finished fort that served as a prison, notably for the doctor convicted after Lincoln’s assassination.","f":["Conch fritters","Fried conch fritters, a Key West speciality, served with a spicy sauce."],"r":"The neighbouring islet of Bush Key closes for part of the year for nesting terns: respect the buoys. There are no services or fresh water here, so bring everything and take your rubbish back.","s":"April to June: the sea is often calm for the crossing, the water already warm and hurricane season not yet under way.","a":[["Loggerhead Key","Wild island where loggerhead turtles nest, fringed by reefs to explore with a mask."],["Dry Tortugas Lighthouse","Tall black-and-white lighthouse towering over Loggerhead Key and the archipelago."]],"lbl":"Garden Key Campground"},"southpadre":{"v":"A barrier island at the very south of Texas, South Padre lives between Port Isabel’s shrimp fleet, the Kemp’s ridley turtles that return to nest on its beaches and the influx of students in spring.","f":["Gulf shrimp","Shrimp from the Port Isabel fleet, grilled, fried or in tacos."],"r":"Pump up and launch your kite in the designated zones, away from swimmers. Shuffle your feet as you walk and wear booties: the lagoon is home to stingrays and sharp shells.","s":"March to May: the most regular southeasterly winds and warming water, before the crushing heat of summer.","a":[["Sea Turtle, Inc.","Rehabilitation centre for injured sea turtles, open to the public and very educational."],["Port Isabel Lighthouse","Historic lighthouse to climb for views over the lagoon, the bridge and the island."],["Port Isabel","Fishing port with colourful shrimp boats and seafood restaurants on the quay."]]},"sebastian":{"v":"On the “Treasure Coast”, where a Spanish fleet laden with gold sank in 1715, Sebastian is the benchmark wave for Florida surfers, while anglers occupy the jetty day and night.","r":"The peak is tight and very local: wait your turn and don’t drop in on anyone. Keep clear of the anglers’ lines on the jetty and beware of the current in the inlet.","s":"October to December: swells from the northeast and from distant hurricanes, more frequent offshore winds and water that’s still warm.","a":[["Pelican Island National Wildlife Refuge","The first national wildlife refuge in the United States, with brown pelicans on the lagoon."],["McLarty Treasure Museum","Small museum on the wreck of the 1715 fleet, on the site of a survivors’ camp."],["Archie Carr National Wildlife Refuge","Some of the most important beaches in the world for loggerhead turtle nesting."],["Mel Fisher’s Treasure Museum","Gold coins, jewellery and objects recovered from wrecked Spanish galleons."]]},"blueheron":{"v":"Here, everything revolves around high tide: at slack water, divers and photographers gather under the bridge in search of tiny creatures, in an urban lagoon facing the yachts of Palm Beach.","r":"Deploy your dive buoy and stay out of the channel, as boats pass very close. Don’t handle seahorses or frogfish, not even for a photo.","s":"All year round at high tide; from May to September, the water is very warm and the lagoon often clearer.","a":[["Palm Beach","Island of billionaires’ grand villas, with Worth Avenue and its lakeside promenade."],["Juno Beach","Long turtle-nesting beach, with a rehabilitation centre and a fishing pier."],["Singer Island","Barrier island with wild beaches, and John D. MacArthur State Park to the north."],["1928 Hurricane Memorial","Mass grave of the hurricane’s Black victims, a moving place of remembrance."]]},"resurrectionbay":{"v":"The terminus of the Alaska Railroad, Seward lives from fishing and the boats that head out to the glaciers; every 4th of July, the whole town turns out to watch runners race up and down Mount Marathon.","f":["Alaskan salmon and halibut","Caught in the bay, served grilled or as fish and chips on the harbour."],"r":"Keep your distance from otters and seals: if they change their behaviour, you’re too close. The water is icy, so paddle suitably equipped and close to shore.","s":"June to August: long days, calmer sea and active wildlife; the wind often picks up in the afternoon in the fjord.","a":[["Alaska SeaLife Center","Aquarium and rehabilitation centre for marine mammals: sea lions, seals and seabirds."],["Mount Marathon","Steep trail above the town, with a bird’s-eye view over the whole bay."],["Fort McGilvray","Second World War bunkers perched on Caines Head, reached on foot or by kayak."],["Seward","Small harbour town with murals, a marina and cruise departures to the fjords."]]},"tadoussac":{"v":"A fur-trading post from the earliest days of New France, Tadoussac lives to the rhythm of the whales, among dunes, an old wooden chapel and whale-watching boats leaving from the wharf.","l":"French","h":["Bonjour","“hello” in French, the language of Quebec, said without fuss"],"f":["Tourtière du Lac-Saint-Jean","Deep pie of meats and potatoes, a speciality of the neighbouring region."],"r":"In the marine park, approach distances for whales are regulated, even more so for the beluga, an endangered species: never chase them. Currents and fog change quickly.","s":"July to September: slightly less cold water, plenty of whales at the river mouth and a sea that’s often calm in the morning.","a":[["Saguenay Fjord","Fjord with tall wooded cliffs, to explore by boat or along the trails on its banks."],["Tadoussac","Village on the bay with its Indian chapel, its grand white hotel and its dunes."],["Baie-Sainte-Catherine","Village on the opposite shore, with a lookout for spotting belugas at the river mouth."]]},"grotto":{"v":"On the territory of the Anishinaabeg of the Saugeen Ojibway Nation, the tip of the peninsula is a mecca for freshwater diving: its cold, clear waters preserve sunken schooners.","f":["Whitefish fish and chips","Fried Great Lakes whitefish, served on the harbour in Tobermory."],"r":"In summer, parking at Cyprus Lake must be booked in advance through Parks Canada. Cliff jumping causes injuries every year: get into the water from the rocks.","s":"Mid-July to late August: Georgian Bay finally reaches a bearable temperature; come early to avoid the crowds.","a":[["Tobermory","Harbour village at the tip of the peninsula, the departure point for glass-bottom boats."],["Sweepstakes wreck","19th-century schooner sunk in a shallow cove, visible from the surface."],["Cove Island Lighthouse","Stone lighthouse on an island in Fathom Five National Marine Park, surrounded by wrecks."]]},"algonquin":{"v":"The historic heart of canoeing in Ontario, Canoe Lake is also the lake where the painter Tom Thomson disappeared, whose canvases fixed the image of this land of pines, loons and portages.","r":"In the backcountry, a permit is compulsory and cans and glass bottles are banned. Hang your food out of bears’ reach and take all your rubbish out with you.","s":"Late August to September: fewer blackflies, water still pleasant and maples blazing by the end of the month.","lbl":"Algonquin Park campsite"},"squamish":{"v":"On the lands of the Squamish Nation, the town has become a haven for climbers and mountain bikers; in summer, a riders’ association runs the spit and the launches at the foot of the Chief.","r":"The spit is run by an association: buy your day pass and follow its launching rules. The fjord water is icy, so a full wetsuit is compulsory.","s":"May to September: on sunny days, the thermal breeze funnels up the fjord in the afternoon, strong and steady.","a":[["Stawamus Chief","Huge granite dome, a climbing paradise, with a steep trail to its summits."],["Shannon Falls","Spectacular waterfall tumbling down the cliff in several drops, a stone’s throw from the road."],["Britannia Mine Museum","Former copper mine toured by underground train, at the foot of the huge mill."],["Brackendale Eagles Provincial Park","In winter, hundreds of bald eagles gather for the salmon."],["Alice Lake Provincial Park","Forest-fringed lake, ideal for swimming and family walks."]]},"hopewell":{"v":"Here the sea rises and falls by more than ten metres twice a day; all around, the Acadians once reclaimed the salt marshes from the sea with their aboiteaux, and life goes on between farms and mudflats.","f":["Poutine râpée","Ball of grated potato stuffed with pork, an Acadian speciality of the southeast."],"r":"Respect the posted cut-off time for climbing back up from the ocean floor: the tide comes in fast and cuts off the beaches. Never climb on the “flowerpots”, which are fragile and unstable.","s":"June to September: less cold water and outings at high tide; always plan your day around the tide times.","a":[["Joggins Fossil Cliffs","UNESCO-listed cliffs where the tide reveals fossil trees from the Carboniferous."],["Fort Beauséjour","Star-shaped French fort, taken by the British in 1755, with views over the marshes."],["Memramcook","Cradle of the Acadian renaissance, with the Monument-Lefebvre and its farms."],["Shepody Bay","Mudflats where huge flocks of migrating sandpipers land in summer."]]}}};
